/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-app-studio/commit/1aa63e9f3115a49ab373a78cba7ae9d78d95626d","node_id":"C_kwDOS5hKY9oAKDFhYTYzZTlmMzExNWE0OWFiMzczYTc4Y2JhN2FlOWQ3OGQ5NTYyNmQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-app-studio/commit/2113e5e94b442bf87ed8748373936cc55eceb2a8","sha":"2113e5e94b442bf87ed8748373936cc55eceb2a8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-app-studio/commits/2113e5e94b442bf87ed8748373936cc55eceb2a8"}],"sha":"1aa63e9f3115a49ab373a78cba7ae9d78d95626d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-app-studio/commits/1aa63e9f3115a49ab373a78cba7ae9d78d95626d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3225744?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-app-studio/commits/2113e5e94b442bf87ed8748373936cc55eceb2a8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-26T08:37:11Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-26T08:37:11Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Adjust providers machinery (#777)\n\n* Fix nerw provider struct\n\n* Adjust providers logic\n\n* Move to better providers machinery\n\n* move provider contract\n\n* fix CI\n\n* Claim the kind a required verb coordinate hangs off\n\nkcp refuses an APIExport whose permission claim names a custom\nsubresource without a claim on the resource that serves it. App Studio\nrequired code.railgrid.ai connections/mint-registry-token but never\nconnections, so `init` died at admission:\n\n  spec.permissionClaims[13].resource: Invalid value:\n  \"connections/mint-registry-token\": a claim on a custom subresource\n  requires a claim on \"connections\", which is what serves it\n\nApp Studio does read those Connections — it lists them when a project\npicks one and gets one back when a view resolves its connectionRef — so\nthe claim was simply missing, not deliberately absent. Declare it\nread-only and regenerate the export.\n\nValidateProviderRequirement now rejects a verb coordinate whose parent is\nnot declared in the same requirement, in either order, so the next one\nfails in `make verify` with the coordinate named rather than at a\nprovider''s own bootstrap.\n\nThe infrastructure provider e2e reported \"provider init failed: exit\nstatus 1\" and left the reason in a temp file CI does not upload; tail it\nto stderr.\n\nAgents ran with the WRONG identity: run-provider-agents resolved\nRAILGRID_PROVIDER_KUBECONFIG by taking the first file that existed out of\nthe provider''s own kubeconfig, kcp''s admin kubeconfig and the Tilt front\nproxy''s. Started before `agents-init` mints the first, it silently bound\nto kcp-admin, which authenticates but holds nothing in the provider\nworkspace, so every data-plane verb failed the gate:\n\n  dataplane: reading APIExportEndpointSlice agents.railgrid.ai:\n  Unauthorized\n\nserving HTTP 500 to the portal''s model discovery, and the controllers\nlogged Unauthorized lease errors on a loop. A verb runs as the provider,\nso no other identity will do: use the provider''s own kubeconfig or refuse\nto start, and watch the file in Tilt so init''s write restarts serve —\nwhat kuery and infrastructure already do.\n\n* Accept every requirement kuery declares when enabling it\n\nTestKueryAggregatesEdgeObjects failed with kuery unable to discover the\nedge''s Kubernetes API:\n\n  kuery engage: discovery failed for cluster 1vqly0gm36vbqs0b/kuery-k8s:\n  unknown\n\nThe edges provider never saw the request. kcp refused it first:\n\n  authorization step reason=\"failed to find suitable reason to allow\n  access in APIBinding\" decision=\"Denied\" resource=\"kubernetesclusters\"\n  subresource=\"k8s\" verb=\"get\" ... resp=403\n\nThe tenant''s APIBinding asked for three claims and accepted one. Its\nexportPermissionClaims carried kubernetesclusters, kubernetesclusters/k8s\nand subjectaccessreviews; appliedPermissionClaims carried only\nkubernetesclusters, because the test''s Enable ticked that one kind by\nname. A claim the binding does not accept is a coordinate the virtual\nworkspace does not serve, so the k8s verb was never reachable and the\ndiscovery client rendered the 403 as \"unknown\".\n\nRead the declaration from the hub catalog and accept all of it, which is\nwhat the portal and `railgrid dev` already do. A hardcoded list stops\nmatching spec.requires the moment a requirement is added, which is how\nthis test came to enable a kind without the verb on it.\n\n* Read the e2e bootstrap logs before cleanup deletes them\n\nThe tail added for the infrastructure provider e2e ran after cleanup(),\nwhich removes the whole data dir, so the failure reported\n\n  provider init failed: exit status 1 (log: /tmp/.../init.log)\n  (open /tmp/.../init.log: no such file or directory)\n\nTake the tail first. Give the serve readiness check the same treatment:\nits log is in the same directory and would be just as gone.\n\n* Claim the infrastructure Templates App Studio reads\n\nProject details failed with:\n\n  templates.infrastructure.railgrid.ai is forbidden: User\n  \"system:serviceaccount:default:provider\" cannot list resource\n  \"templates\" in API group \"infrastructure.railgrid.ai\" at the cluster\n  scope: access denied\n\nApp Studio reads that catalog in three places — preparing a project''s\ndetail view, resolving the template a binding names, and the assistant''s\ntemplate tool — but spec.requires named only instances and its verb\ncoordinates, so the generated claim never covered the kind and kcp served\nnothing for it. Declare it read-only.\n\nAudited every provider for the same class both ways a GVR is spelled in\nthis tree, the composite literal and GroupVersion.WithResource: no other\nkind is read from a group its provider does not declare. The one other\ncross-group read, agents reading the workspace''s MCPServer, is deliberate\n— it runs as the CALLER, on their bearer, so it needs no claim.\n\n* Revendor kcp at the custom-subresource parent gate\n\nmjudeikis/kcp 3b4bd7a70 (\"fix getter\"), one commit on from ba9e0a6a4.\nIt adds gateOnParent to the APIExport virtual workspace''s custom\nsubresource storage: before a verb runs, the object it hangs off is read\nback through the storage that serves the parent, label selector and all,\nso a claimer cannot reach a subresource of an object its claim does not\ncover.\n\nThat makes the parent claim load-bearing at request time rather than only\nat APIExport admission. Every verb coordinate this repo declares already\nsits under a claimed parent, including the code.railgrid.ai/connections\none added earlier in this branch, so the gate has an object to read in\neach case.\n\nAll twelve pins move together: the six replace targets in the root module\nplus the apimachinery/v2 pin the SDK and four providers carry. Every\nmodule builds and its tests pass.\n\n* rebase\n\n* Run the infrastructure provider e2e init as the provider, not as admin\n\nThe suite bootstrapped with kcp''s admin kubeconfig, and on this branch''s\nkcp that is refused:\n\n  init: provider workspace bootstrap: install: apply APIExport: creating\n  APIExport infrastructure.providers.railgrid.ai is forbidden:\n  spec.resources[0].group: Forbidden: API group\n  \"infrastructure.railgrid.ai\" is reserved by PermissionClaimPolicy\n  \"railgrid\"; only its providers may export it\n\nThe platform''s PermissionClaimPolicy reserves every API group it names and\nlists the subjects that may export one, and kcp-admin is not among them.\nmain never hit this because main binds upstream kcp v0.33.0, which has no\nsuch admission.\n\nMint the provider ServiceAccount''s kubeconfig from the provider-token\nSecret the hub''s Provider controller publishes, and hand init that. It is\nwhat `make init-provider-infrastructure` and the chart''s init container\nalready do, and what every other provider suite already does; this suite\nwas the only one reading no provider-token at all.\n\n* Treat an unresolved admin virtual workspace as an absent API\n\nThe External KCP suite deploys the hub against a released kcp image\n(ghcr.io/kcp-dev/kcp:v0.33.0, pinned in create_kcp.go), while the hub\nbinary is built from the vendored fork. The hub crash-looped there for 45\nminutes until Helm''s wait expired:\n\n  Error: installing PermissionClaimPolicy: discovering admin.kcp.io/v1alpha1:\n  forbidden: User \"railgrid-e2e-admin\" cannot get path\n  \"/services/admin/clusters/root/apis/admin.kcp.io/v1alpha1\":\n  Path not resolved to a valid virtual workspace\n\nPermissionClaimPolicyServed is meant to make this a no-op — its comment\nsays one info log on every kcp that does not serve admin.kcp.io, which is\ntoday''s — and it tolerated NotFound and a group-discovery failure. But the\ngroup lives behind a virtual workspace, and a path no virtual workspace\nclaims comes back 403: the framework''s authorizer has no opinion on a path\nit cannot resolve, and no opinion becomes a denial. So absence arrives as\nForbidden, which was neither tolerated case, and the caller treats a\nreturned error as fatal.\n\nTolerate that one shape, matched on the reason rather than on Forbidden\nalone: a denial from a kcp that does serve the group is a credential\nproblem, and reading it as absence would silently skip installing a policy\nwhose job is to reserve API groups.\n\n* Revendor kcp with the subresource path fix\n\nmjudeikis/kcp 20f1d300c. The APIExport virtual workspace now forwards the\npath a client addressed below a custom subresource, instead of carrying\nover only the query.\n\nThat is what the kuery edge aggregation needed. kuery reaches an edge''s\nKubernetes API through the k8s verb on kubernetesclusters, so every request\nis \"<edge>/k8s/<kube path>\". Inbound the virtual workspace saw\n.../kuery-k8s/k8s/apis/apiextensions.k8s.io/v1/customresourcedefinitions;\noutbound it sent .../kubernetesclusters:<identity>/kuery-k8s/k8s?watch=true.\nWith no tail the edges provider resolves /k8s/, the agent proxies the edge\napiserver''s root, and the root answers 200 with its paths document -- so\nclient-go reported \"Object ''Kind'' is missing\" and the CRD informer never\nsynced. Nothing else caught it because every other path through the k8s\nverb goes via the shard, which preserves the inbound path.\n\nAll twelve pins move together. Every module builds and its tests pass.\n\n* Report why a provider e2e bootstrap failed, in every suite\n\nThe kuery provider suite failed with\n\n  kuery init failed: exit status 1 (log: /tmp/railgrid-e2e-kuery-.../init.log)\n\nand that directory is removed by cleanup and never uploaded, so the reason\nwas gone. The infrastructure suite had the same blindness and it cost three\nCI rounds to read one admission error.\n\nTail the log to stderr, taken before cleanup, in the four suites that still\nhid it: edges, edgesconn, kueryprovider and provider.\n\n* Tell the kuery provider e2e where its data plane is\n\n  init: provider workspace bootstrap: install: DataPlaneEndpointSlice: no\n  data-plane base URL (set Options.DataPlaneURL, or point\n  Options.CatalogEntryFile at a CatalogEntry with spec.serving.backend.url)\n\nkuery''s APIExport declares \"<resource>/<verb>\" entries, so init writes the\nDataPlaneEndpointSlice they resolve through, and it refuses to guess the\naddress. The suite deliberately gave init no CatalogEntry, because it\nregisters the entry itself.\n\nPass the address directly, as the quickstart and infrastructure suites do.\nIt is the one applyKueryManifests already rewrites spec.serving.backend.url\nto, so the slice and the entry agree on where the provider is; reading the\ncommitted manifest instead would record the dev-loop port.\n\n* Report what the provider said when the edge-proxy gate refuses\n\nTestDEdgeProxyAuthBoundary fails with\n\n  wait timeout after 30s; last status: status=404 (want 502)\n\nand 404 is what the gate answers for every refusal, deliberately: a denial\nmust not disclose whether the addressed edge exists. So the status cannot\nsay whether the caller was denied, the object was unreadable as the\nprovider, or the review never ran, and the three have different causes.\n\nKeep the provider log path in suite state and dump its tail when the probe\nnever reaches 502.\n\n* Name the hop that refused the edge-proxy probe\n\nThe provider log tail showed only startup, because one line in these logs can\nbe a whole API object, so forty lines cover a few milliseconds. The useful\nfact was in what was absent: nothing in the provider log mentions the edge\nthe probe addresses, so the 404 comes from before the provider and the gate\nis not what refused.\n\nMatch on the edge name instead of tailing, over the hub''s log as well as the\nprovider''s, so the next run says which hop answered.\n\n* Forward a ServiceAccount request to the workspace it addresses\n\nTestDEdgeProxyAuthBoundary read 404 where it wanted 502, before and after\nthe grant, and nothing in the edges provider''s log ever mentioned the edge\nthe probe addresses: the request never got past kcp. The hub''s proxy was\nsending it to a path that cannot exist.\n\n  inbound    /clusters/{tenant}/apis/edges.railgrid.ai/v1alpha1/\n             kubernetesclusters/e2e-engage/k8s/api\n  forwarded  /clusters/{sa}/clusters/{tenant}/apis/...\n\nserveServiceAccount resolves the workspace from the token''s clusterName\nclaim, which is where the ServiceAccount LIVES, not where the request is\naimed. It de-doubled the prefix only when the path named the SA''s own\ncluster, so a request addressing another workspace -- which a data-plane\nverb on another provider''s object always does -- got a second prefix\nprepended. main never hit this because the probe took the old grammar,\nwhich carried no cluster prefix.\n\nHonour a cluster named in the path and forward it unchanged. That is safe\nbecause the SA token travels as it arrived: kcp authenticates it natively\nand authorizes it in THAT workspace, so reaching another one still needs a\ngrant there, which is what this test grants. Both checks the claim gets --\nthe traversal regex and the O-10 Org refusal -- are applied to the\naddressed cluster too, so naming one in the path walks around neither. The\nagent-kubeconfig case the old strip existed for falls out of the same\nbranch.","tree":{"sha":"045349f5ee074969bc38ea46c50854d1ddc3c519","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-app-studio/git/trees/045349f5ee074969bc38ea46c50854d1ddc3c519"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-app-studio/git/commits/2113e5e94b442bf87ed8748373936cc55eceb2a8","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-app-studio/commit/2113e5e94b442bf87ed8748373936cc55eceb2a8","node_id":"C_kwDOS5hKY9oAKDIxMTNlNWU5NGI0NDJiZjg3ZWQ4NzQ4MzczOTM2Y2M1NWVjZWIyYTg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-app-studio/commit/9b2c799dcbf43083a3fd8aae9af89f764db67814","sha":"9b2c799dcbf43083a3fd8aae9af89f764db67814","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-app-studio/commits/9b2c799dcbf43083a3fd8aae9af89f764db67814"}],"sha":"2113e5e94b442bf87ed8748373936cc55eceb2a8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-app-studio/commits/2113e5e94b442bf87ed8748373936cc55eceb2a8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5685031?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/cwilhit","id":5685031,"login":"cwilhit","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU2ODUwMzE=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-app-studio/commits/9b2c799dcbf43083a3fd8aae9af89f764db67814/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-24T23:11:34Z","email":"cdwilhite0601@gmail.com","name":"Craig D Wilhite"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-24T23:11:34Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Add shared table multi-selection and bulk settings actions (#775)\n\n* Add resource table selection and bulk workspace deletion\n\n* Polish resource selection toolbar and tooltip behavior\n\n* Clarify selection toolbar placement in the table contract\n\n* Preserve focus when clearing off-page resource selection\n\n* Fix resource selection layout, focus, and live tooltip updates\n\n* Keep cleared selection focus visible and fit narrow toolbars\n\n* Fix table action tooltip clipping and progress animation\n\n* Add bulk actions to workspace settings resource tables\n\n* Add organization member bulk removal and compact empty tables\n\n* Preserve retries for incomplete organization member removals\n\n* Fix bulk settings stack CI dependencies and bundle budgets","tree":{"sha":"a5058a99ef56424b61aff099cff2b22e308907de","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-app-studio/git/trees/a5058