{"account":{"login":"khmilevoi","providerType":"User"},"email":"khmilevoi@gmail.com","name":"khmilevoi"},"observedAt":"2026-10-08T23:26:37.713Z","repositoryId":1271990646,"sha":"63dc2f329c34da905995fd1d90e31daf3d34b026"},{"author":{"account":{"login":"khmilevoi","providerType":"User"},"email":"khmilevoi@gmail.com","name":"khmilevoi"},"authoredDate":"2026-07-12T23:10:09Z","committedDate":"2026-07-12T23:10:09Z","committer":{"account":{"login":"khmilevoi","providerType":"User"},"email":"khmilevoi@gmail.com","name":"khmilevoi"},"observedAt":"2026-10-08T23:26:37.713Z","repositoryId":1271990646,"sha":"eaabcaa95a9ca60ed843c0598d3c85e5c88014ea"}],"observedAt":"2026-10-08T23:26:37.713Z","raw":[{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24359879?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi","id":24359879,"login":"khmilevoi","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI0MzU5ODc5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/c33be9e8bcffd690589e7a70bb4a765deb0eb3ee/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-09T16:44:41Z","email":"Khmilevoi@gmail.com","name":"khmilevoi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-09T16:44:41Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"release: ofelia debt no longer compounds while someone is away (#43)\n\n* fix(browser-automation): hide CDP/webdriver signals from the recovery browser\n\nCloudflare Turnstile treats navigator.webdriver and the AutomationControlled\nblink feature as bot signals even while a real human is solving the\nchallenge through the noVNC recovery view, since the page stays a\nPlaywright-owned CDP target throughout. Add the launch flag and patch\nnavigator.webdriver so a genuine human click can actually clear the\nchallenge.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01TnS6zUKuxGfbj1YcpkdNgx\n\n* fix(ofelia-poop-duty): stop unpaid repayment days from compounding debt\n\nA debt day is a repayment slot carved out of the creditor''s own rotation\nday. Recording \"went into debt\" on such a day charged the debtor a second\ntime, even though the creditor was cleaning that day regardless — so one\nmissed turn minted a fresh debt day, which minted another, and the balance\ngrew unbounded while the debtor was away. Production reached six days\nagainst four real misses.\n\nCharge a missed turn only when the day was naturally the debtor''s own.\n`foldDebt` and the history list''s per-entry badge carried separate copies\nof these branches and could drift apart; both now share the exported\n`debtDeltaFor`.\n\nThe drafts fixtures put their outstanding debt on a day that was not the\ndebtor''s, which no longer creates any debt to repay or forgive — moved to\nher own rotation day, preserving each scenario''s shape.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ofelia-poop-duty): drop stored rows whose date is not a real day\n\n`foldDebt` now parses `entry.date`, and `Temporal.PlainDate.from` reports a\nbad string by throwing. `LedgerEntrySchema.date` was a bare `z.string()`, so a\nrow hand-written through the generic storage endpoint reached that parse and\nthrew out of the balance computation — bypassing the per-element tolerance\n`LedgerEntriesSchema` exists to provide, and blanking the widget. The same\nexposure already sat in `toHistoryGroups`, which has parsed `entry.date` all\nalong.\n\nValidate at the parse boundary instead, where every reader is covered:\n`foldDebt`, the history view, the draft builders and the nightly cron all\nreceive their entries through `LedgerEntriesSchema`.\n\nThe shape check alone is not enough — `2026-13-45` and `2026-02-30` match\n`/^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$/` and still throw — so the new `CalendarDateSchema`\nrefines it with the parse itself. It is deliberately separate from\n`IsoDateSchema` rather than replacing it: request payloads keep the shape-only\ncheck, because the server already answers a calendar-invalid date with an\nError value via `errore.try` in `readDraftInput`, and that is asserted\nbehavior.\n\nReported by the Codex reviewer on PR #42.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"8aeea7daab31e1c4dcb281e1e1485bd75aa4380b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/git/trees/8aeea7daab31e1c4dcb281e1e1485bd75aa4380b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/git/commits/c33be9e8bcffd690589e7a70bb4a765deb0eb3ee","verification":{"payload":"tree 8aeea7daab31e1c4dcb281e1e1485bd75aa4380b\nparent b3a139409e8032a99df27b699980a7cb87fbda20\nauthor khmilevoi <Khmilevoi@gmail.com> 1786293881 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786293881 +0200\n\nrelease: ofelia debt no longer compounds while someone is away (#43)\n\n* fix(browser-automation): hide CDP/webdriver signals from the recovery browser\n\nCloudflare Turnstile treats navigator.webdriver and the AutomationControlled\nblink feature as bot signals even while a real human is solving the\nchallenge through the noVNC recovery view, since the page stays a\nPlaywright-owned CDP target throughout. Add the launch flag and patch\nnavigator.webdriver so a genuine human click can actually clear the\nchallenge.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01TnS6zUKuxGfbj1YcpkdNgx\n\n* fix(ofelia-poop-duty): stop unpaid repayment days from compounding debt\n\nA debt day is a repayment slot carved out of the creditor''s own rotation\nday. Recording \"went into debt\" on such a day charged the debtor a second\ntime, even though the creditor was cleaning that day regardless — so one\nmissed turn minted a fresh debt day, which minted another, and the balance\ngrew unbounded while the debtor was away. Production reached six days\nagainst four real misses.\n\nCharge a missed turn only when the day was naturally the debtor''s own.\n`foldDebt` and the history list''s per-entry badge carried separate copies\nof these branches and could drift apart; both now share the exported\n`debtDeltaFor`.\n\nThe drafts fixtures put their outstanding debt on a day that was not the\ndebtor''s, which no longer creates any debt to repay or forgive — moved to\nher own rotation day, preserving each scenario''s shape.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ofelia-poop-duty): drop stored rows whose date is not a real day\n\n`foldDebt` now parses `entry.date`, and `Temporal.PlainDate.from` reports a\nbad string by throwing. `LedgerEntrySchema.date` was a bare `z.string()`, so a\nrow hand-written through the generic storage endpoint reached that parse and\nthrew out of the balance computation — bypassing the per-element tolerance\n`LedgerEntriesSchema` exists to provide, and blanking the widget. The same\nexposure already sat in `toHistoryGroups`, which has parsed `entry.date` all\nalong.\n\nValidate at the parse boundary instead, where every reader is covered:\n`foldDebt`, the history view, the draft builders and the nightly cron all\nreceive their entries through `LedgerEntriesSchema`.\n\nThe shape check alone is not enough — `2026-13-45` and `2026-02-30` match\n`/^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$/` and still throw — so the new `CalendarDateSchema`\nrefines it with the parse itself. It is deliberately separate from\n`IsoDateSchema` rather than replacing it: request payloads keep the shape-only\ncheck, because the server already answers a calendar-invalid date with an\nError value via `errore.try` in `readDraftInput`, and that is asserted\nbehavior.\n\nReported by the Codex reviewer on PR #42.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqeK55CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAg2MQAIMuFAqUtwwy6VMzirhuwtlU\nnCrnS75w/1J3nKciodZQzR6oYjWN54znvl2hPjMOwoDsKC3zSVw74hlPVIMtQbRI\nenhosM26NkvMXZKSXS6zSZ5G9wKdaoPh8yU+0hJBnMBOY5k+dJHhfALfYsEAWksC\nkGwawJYyBIs6B3Q+fGF3fSVDxGuuwFkNJnh7OId+GBEy5Pefjh5+rdnKOqQQWZb/\ntkYzFLA+Q9ukhDuX66nnZ2cp3tS24wbq0Q7vrRtLp1VEC4EgWi6/X1N5hkDQR4xB\n0BrPvilqpY7PviGPFvpIX8LIUu9ERRl7Im73YK1Q8vbEDFevx0pOBbuubhlkmUsA\nl7aTi8jpeGyRno10YJSzKi7L1n270T8Ua0WshlAUSH3sVNuauu6A7/Z1wKkLDNJF\nFYMol3cT+lxDWC/ZRLvOse/otPDsNm87l7jwBvNUz5TzOsvuzFMz9OhDvVipy3Ov\nfJ6YLf2NWsKsjGefYdDZRKZ75EHfnD12c4TS5+77k+6DQwyyVRENRsompxUP9rM6\n0qKE/2Mmq4ioXX2A4C4+tPhS6aHLhd1ju6x5CBsKiCYdlQI9iL2ujkyKp9tkuCVh\nqpwmh6DkoAzePICXzinJtKJ9ZQs84AayIK30yQkcvl/OcOQWuot9A9HQH2H3qCKa\nVC+GsRh9FDUv+6aX8LYC\n=5yZL\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-21T12:39:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi/myboard/commit/c33be9e8bcffd690589e7a70bb4a765deb0eb3ee","node_id":"C_kwDOS9EJdtoAKGMzM2JlOWU4YmNmZmQ2OTA1ODllN2E3MGJiNGE3NjVkZWIwZWIzZWU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi/myboard/commit/b3a139409e8032a99df27b699980a7cb87fbda20","sha":"b3a139409e8032a99df27b699980a7cb87fbda20","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/b3a139409e8032a99df27b699980a7cb87fbda20"}],"sha":"c33be9e8bcffd690589e7a70bb4a765deb0eb3ee","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/c33be9e8bcffd690589e7a70bb4a765deb0eb3ee"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24359879?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi","id":24359879,"login":"khmilevoi","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI0MzU5ODc5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/b3a139409e8032a99df27b699980a7cb87fbda20/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-05T10:46:04Z","email":"Khmilevoi@gmail.com","name":"khmilevoi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-05T10:46:04Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(browser-automation): hide CDP/webdriver signals from the recovery browser (#41)\n\nCloudflare Turnstile treats navigator.webdriver and the AutomationControlled\nblink feature as bot signals even while a real human is solving the\nchallenge through the noVNC recovery view, since the page stays a\nPlaywright-owned CDP target throughout. Add the launch flag and patch\nnavigator.webdriver so a genuine human click can actually clear the\nchallenge.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01TnS6zUKuxGfbj1YcpkdNgx\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"c92c774d028c160b4c6d21bed492e891dc289db9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/git/trees/c92c774d028c160b4c6d21bed492e891dc289db9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/git/commits/b3a139409e8032a99df27b699980a7cb87fbda20","verification":{"payload":"tree c92c774d028c160b4c6d21bed492e891dc289db9\nparent 1156dd175867d11a66a824e789f9d7848320a7f8\nauthor khmilevoi <Khmilevoi@gmail.com> 1785926764 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785926764 +0200\n\nfix(browser-automation): hide CDP/webdriver signals from the recovery browser (#41)\n\nCloudflare Turnstile treats navigator.webdriver and the AutomationControlled\nblink feature as bot signals even while a real human is solving the\nchallenge through the noVNC recovery view, since the page stays a\nPlaywright-owned CDP target throughout. Add the launch flag and patch\nnavigator.webdriver so a genuine human click can actually clear the\nchallenge.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01TnS6zUKuxGfbj1YcpkdNgx\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqcxRsCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAa2kQAJZz9LxUcxCccRDK7MnJ8BQ6\n/x5kQlfHLR6hfL4EI1ma0rmRWjqmvH5wo/sol6jfIjq5kXpJE0PwKeAYA0hFH7eu\nXILGHoePH5odgEX8n2icg8KUPLHmsB2CNMvmVUgO5OFHHt+kYBPUEH87HEXG26NK\nC8A+Yd7lEeOgHHM0r81h5k+5Rg6syl1eSGZ8sm9U0wnzTjDl3bJTnGoYbqKnhcB7\nYOewW4SB2YXpDr6SVgDchIlgnZjwe1SLZUJTW7yea+JVTwHPBPDNVr/nbU6B98mS\n9Wjgg4ExiAuhhyXPD1AvlpOZlJdw3aKD+/wC+POdeWo80+ZDiI3GxgOJxwJEBzbG\nX+KGJ9ILHK8KhCWhuH48Y8dGe/idegrOSNKfDv2xY7Qtyza5vTkrBvAnmQW3YpRD\nKTrt5skxUK94kj7M3EoEfdLiWgngeRldbTSJcf4adaAD28uT3F5bKi5FMzwhmv9R\nrmcVDotnYLBJD0MW2ZuyDkAZAOXhuAbD4Otsv3mT/3xy6vzkYwjPfuwUY+o3DZff\nVHq3bBnkss02KsjfLBnLcQ3hUSsKf/cg0X7MnQcYTQ3hrF+tOL2V1kwPYCaL9tPg\nCKwWr2H2F7eBTifa9MqAAn7Fk4/aKUxbWnu59TZT7ih+M++Fra4p8OolSK3S4rWq\nqOgTKbAQDbbOJFbqKPmw\n=WL7q\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-05T11:10:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi/myboard/commit/b3a139409e8032a99df27b699980a7cb87fbda20","node_id":"C_kwDOS9EJdtoAKGIzYTEzOTQwOWU4MDMyYTk5ZGYyN2I2OTk5ODBhN2NiODdmYmRhMjA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi/myboard/commit/1156dd175867d11a66a824e789f9d7848320a7f8","sha":"1156dd175867d11a66a824e789f9d7848320a7f8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/1156dd175867d11a66a824e789f9d7848320a7f8"}],"sha":"b3a139409e8032a99df27b699980a7cb87fbda20","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/b3a139409e8032a99df27b699980a7cb87fbda20"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24359879?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi","id":24359879,"login":"khmilevoi","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI0MzU5ODc5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/1156dd175867d11a66a824e789f9d7848320a7f8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-05T10:06:35Z","email":"Khmilevoi@gmail.com","name":"khmilevoi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-05T10:06:35Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Dev (#40)\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the modal focusout guard against narrowing\n\nMount the underlying Radix dialog in its own pass, as production does, and\ncover the intra-modal focus move that the proposed guard narrowing would have\nbroken silently. The regression test dispatches the underlying focusout event\ndirectly rather than chaining native .focus() calls: jsdom (like real\nbrowsers) always completes an outer .focus() call on its own target once its\nhandler stack unwinds, so a settled document.activeElement assertion can''t\nobserve a reclaim a nested handler only ever wins transiently — verified\nempirically against the narrowed guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): close a cross-test focus-restore leak in the Radix stack suite\n\nRadix''s FocusScope defers its unmount focus-restore to a real setTimeout(0),\nand testing-library''s auto cleanup() never waits for it, so a prior test''s\ndeferred restore (and an unawaited NoVncCanvas state update from that same\ntest''s async recovery flow) can bleed into the next test — confirmed by\ninstrumentation. A formal trace of the reentrant focus cascade shows the\n''pulls focus back...'' assertion itself can''t actually be flipped by this\n(no yield point exists between its synchronous .focus() call and its\nexpect()), but the leak is real and pre-existing to any suite that\nmounts+unmounts a trapped FocusScope, so close it defensively: flush one\nreal tick after each test''s cleanup so Radix''s deferred work always\nfinishes before the next test starts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): drain Radix''s focus-restore timer from beforeEach, not afterEach\n\ne797d50''s message overstated what that change achieved. It claimed the added\nafterEach ran \"strictly after cleanup()\" and closed the cross-test focus-restore\nleak. It did neither. Vitest resolves sequence.hooks to \"stack\", so same-suite\nafterEach hooks run in REVERSE registration order, and testing-library''s\ncleanup() -- registered first, at setupFiles import time -- therefore runs last.\nThe file-level afterEach ran BEFORE cleanup(), i.e. before the unmount that\nschedules Radix''s setTimeout(0) focus restore, so it drained nothing.\n\nInstrumenting setTimeout and the hook boundaries on this file shows it directly:\nat afterEach entry the modal is still in the DOM and no FocusScope timer is\npending; the timer is scheduled only after the hook returns; the next test''s\nbeforeEach starts with one still queued. Moving the same one-tick flush to\nbeforeEach takes that count to zero before the test body runs. beforeEach is\ncorrect by construction -- it always follows the previous test''s afterEach\nhooks whatever the hook-order mode.\n\nCorrect the scope of the claim too. The restore is focus-neutral in this\nfixture: the previouslyFocusedElement it captured is either document.body,\nwhich jsdom refuses to focus, or an already-detached node, and every observed\nfiring left document.activeElement unchanged. The intermittent failure this was\nchasing did not reproduce at e797d50 in 30 consecutive single-file runs or 6\nwhole-package runs. So this closes a scheduling leak, not an observed failure,\nand the comment now says exactly that instead of claiming otherwise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): link the tier-shared-state follow-up\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): drop the phantom rebuild from the instance store''s connect hook\n\nThe connect hook carried `current ??= { value: make() }` behind a comment\nclaiming it gave a reconnect after disposal a fresh value. Measured against\n@reatom/core@1001.1.0 with the shipped code:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 2\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"],[{\"id\":2},\"a\"]]   built = 2\n\nThe reconnect still returned the DISPOSED value — a dependency-free `computed`\nnever recomputes, so its cached result is what every later read gets. The line\nonly built a second model graph nobody could reach and then handed it to\n`dispose` a second time. Same run with the line removed:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n\nThe comment now states what is true: a stale reconnect reads the cached,\nalready-disposed value, and a fresh value comes only from a new `store()`\nlookup — which is what every real caller does, because it calls `store()`\nduring render and the cleanup has already evicted the stale handle.\n\nWith the phantom build gone, `if (held)` is what prevents the double dispose,\nso a test pins it: replacing it with an unconditional `dispose?.(held?.value,\nkey)` now fails with a second teardown recorded as `[undefined, \"a\"]`.\n\nAlso corrects the module doc''s cost claim for reading atoms inside `make`.\nIt does not \"silently rebuild the whole value\": measured, after such a\ndependency changes the compute function re-runs once, `current ??=`\nshort-circuits it so `make` does not re-run (built stayed 1, the identical\nobject was returned, no subscriber notified), and because the re-run never\nreads the atom again the edge is dropped. The cost is a pointless dependency\nedge, not lost state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): align the tier-shared-state spec with what shipped\n\nThree claims in the design spec described work that was never delivered:\n\n- The instance atom was said to carry `withDisconnectHook`. It carries\n  `withConnectHook`, whose cleanup calls `teardownIfDisconnected()` — which\n  re-checks `isConnected` before disposing and evicts the map entry only while\n  it still points at the handle being torn down.\n- Section 4 \"StrictMode removal\" claimed `<StrictMode>` was dropped from\n  `packages/client/src/app/main.tsx` and replaced by a comment explaining its\n  absence. None of it shipped: `git diff cfff8d2..HEAD -- packages/client/` is\n  empty and the wrapper is still there. Measurement showed the store survives\n  the remount cycle, because the cleanup''s `isConnected` re-check returns\n  without touching a value a live mount still holds. Section 4 is rewritten as\n  \"StrictMode kept, tolerance pinned by test\", stating why the plan''s original\n  reasoning does not apply.\n- The Testing gate, the Documentation list and the Decision Summary repeated\n  the removal. They now say StrictMode stays and that the tolerance is pinned\n  by `describe(''makeWidgetInstanceStore under StrictMode'')`.\n\nThat last claim is measured, not assumed: deleting the `isConnected` re-check\nfrom the cleanup turns two of the three StrictMode tests red, along with the\nnon-StrictMode same-tick-resubscribe test.\n\nThe plan file is left untouched — it is the historical record of what was\nintended.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): narrow unmeasured claims and close two coverage gaps\n\nComment/claim accuracy — all of these stated more than was ever measured:\n\n- `ui/recovery-flow.test.tsx`: the \"roughly 1 in 15-40 runs\" hang frequency\n  was applied to every Escape-closing test in the file. It was measured for\n  exactly one of them, ''does not collapse when recovery opens from the tile''\n  (task-4-report.md, Fix round 3). The others carry the same wait by\n  inspection — identical shape, identical race — with no observed rate. The\n  comment now says so.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"at `beforeEach` entry one (later\n  two) FocusScope timer is pending\" — only the 1 -> 0 transition was ever\n  recorded, so the \"(later two)\" is dropped.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"jsdom (like real browsers) always\n  completes an outer `.focus()` call on its own target\" — true and measured\n  for jsdom, never measured against a real browser. Scoped to jsdom.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: ''keeps focus inside when it moves\n  between two of our own controls'' promised a focus invariant the test does\n  not assert — it asserts that our guard swallows the `focusout` before it\n  reaches `document`. Renamed to say that.\n\nCoverage:\n\n- `model/instance-store.test.ts` (new) covers `dispose: (models) =>\n  models.recoveryModel.teardown()`, the safety net against leaking a live VNC\n  session when the modal outlived the widget. It had no test at all: replacing\n  the callback with a no-op left the whole widget suite green. Verified both\n  new tests fail against that no-op.\n- `ui/PassportChecker.test.tsx`: two assertions used\n  `expect(await screen.findAllBy*(…)).toHaveLength(2)`, which cannot retry —\n  `findAllBy*` resolves on the first snapshot with >= 1 match, so it settles on\n  one mount and never waits for the second. Both are now a re-querying\n  `waitFor`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(backlog): record the two-modal focus ping-pong as a deferred defect\n\nBoth `useModalIsolation` instances register focus containment on `window` in\nthe capture phase, so two mounted modals each see the other''s `focusin` before\nthe root-level `stopPropagation()` can stop it, and each pulls focus into its\nown root — forever.\n\nReproduced in jsdom 29.1.1 with two `useModalIsolation` roots mounted at once:\none modal performs 1 focus pull on mount; two mounted together never settle,\nand a hard test guard at 400 pulls had to stop the run (402 recorded).\n\nDeferred rather than fixed because it is unreachable through the UI: the\npassport checker is the only owner of such a modal, renders at most one\n(non-fullscreen mount only), and its overlay is a full-viewport\n`pointer-events: auto` layer whose `pointerdown` handler closes the modal, so\na second one cannot be opened over the first.\n\nThe entry notes the likely fix — a module-level stack of active modal roots\nwith containment applied only by the topmost.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): correct five unverified claims left by code review\n\nBring wording in the tier-shared-state spec, BACKLOG.md, and a test\ncomment in line with what was actually measured/read: the instance\nstore''s dependency-edge cost (not a rebuilt value), a stale\nuse-modal-isolation.ts line citation (:53 -> :55, two places), the\nroot focusin stopper''s actual bubble phase (not capture), and which\nrecovery-flow tests keep the shared timeout default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay at this branch\n\nPins rpi.dev.toml''s source.branch to feat/passport-checker-widget so the dev\nenvironment runs this PR instead of the dev branch.\n\nREVERT BEFORE MERGING: once this PR lands on dev, source.branch must go back to\n\"dev\" or the dev overlay stays pinned to a branch that no longer moves.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): drop the stale Chromium singleton lock before launch\n\nThe Chromium profile is a named Docker volume, so `SingletonLock` keeps the\n`<hostname>-<pid>` of whichever container wrote it. After a redeploy the new\ncontainer has a new hostname, Chromium reads a lock owned by \"another computer\"\nit cannot probe, and `launchPersistentContext` fails on every task until the\nvolume is touched by hand.\n\nRemove `SingletonLock`, `SingletonCookie`, and `SingletonSocket` before each\nlaunch. `fs.rm` unlinks the symlinks themselves instead of following them, and\n`force` keeps a fresh profile from failing; a removal that does fail is returned\nas a value, logged, and the launch still proceeds. Unconditional removal is safe\nbecause `getPersistentContext` is the only caller of `launch` and the image\nentrypoint runs a single `node dist/index.cjs` - no check for a live Chromium is\nperformed or implied.\n\nAlso asserts that a failed launch keeps its cause, which is what makes the\nPlaywright message reachable from the service log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): log the error behind an internal task failure\n\nInternal failures were logged as `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` only, so the\ntagged error and its cause chain never reached the container log. A Chromium\nlaunch failure therefore showed up as a bare `internal` code with nothing to\ndiagnose. Add the error to the logged fields; `toEnvelopeError` still answers\nthe client with the redacted `internal` code, so only the log gains detail.\n\nNote the trade-off: a handler error whose message carries task data now reaches\nthe container log, which the previous field list deliberately avoided.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): redact handler failures in the internal task log\n\nfa87a47c logged too much. It added `error: outcome` to the internal-failure log\nso a Chromium launch failure would stop being invisible, but that field carries\nwhatever the handler threw. errore''s `toJSON()` serialises `message`, `stack`,\nand the whole `cause` chain, Playwright quotes `page.evaluate` arguments into\nits messages, and the passport widget passes the secret identity as one — so a\nhandler failure could print the passport series and number into the container\nlog. The wrapper''s own `stack` repeats the cause under `Caused by:`, so the\nidentity landed there twice.\n\nThat contradicts the design spec: raw Playwright, network, Zod, and upstream\nresponse details are logged only after redaction, request and response bodies\nare never logged, and logs redact task payloads and secret values\n(docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md\n:350-353, :684).\n\nThe rule now, verified against dispatch.ts:\n\n- `BrowserExecutorError` wraps a failed `executor.acquire(...)`. Acquire runs\n  before the handler and is passed only `(signal, widgetId)` — never the payload\n  or the widget secrets — so its cause chain is Chromium/Playwright launch\n  detail. Still logged in full; this is the stale singleton lock failure\n  fa87a47c existed to surface.\n- Every other internal failure wraps handler output and is reduced to\n  `errorChain`: the names/tags of the error and its `cause` chain and nothing\n  else. No `message`, no `stack`, no foreign properties.\n\nThe log message, the `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` fields, and the\n`code === ''internal''` condition are unchanged.\n\nTests split the single fa87a47c case in two: one pins that an executor failure''s\ncause chain still reaches the log, one asserts a secret-looking cause string is\nabsent from the serialised logged payload. Both were confirmed to fail before\nthe fix and to discriminate after it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add an env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe passport widget''s recovery path (retain the page, issue a single-use\ncapability, stream the live browser over noVNC, let a human solve the\nchallenge, retry) has never been exercised on real hardware, because it only\ntriggers when pasport.org.ua serves a Cloudflare challenge and none has been\nencountered — the smoke test on the Pi returned a normal result. Subproject 8\n(`passport-checker-rpi-integration` in\ndocs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md)\nhas \"embedded noVNC Cloudflare recovery, SSH fallback, and real checker smoke\ntest on the Raspberry Pi\" as an acceptance item and had no way to reach it on\ndemand. PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY is that way.\n\nContract: `1` and nothing else enables it — absent, empty, `0`, `true`, ` 1 `\nare all off (isForcedRecoveryEnabled does exact string equality, no trimming,\nno truthiness coercion). Compose passes it through with a `:-` default so an\nunset variable stays off, and it must never be set in production.\n\nSemantics: at most once per process. The latch lives in the closure\nmakePassportCheckHandler returns, so each handler owns its own and the unit\ntests stay isolated. It fires after a successful goto and after\ncollectNavigationEvidence — the retained page is the real checker page with the\nsite''s own UI loaded, so a human can drive it over noVNC — and returns exactly\nthe retainPageForRecovery() + BrowserSessionRequiredError pair the genuine\nCloudflare branch returns, making the downstream flow byte-identical to a real\nchallenge. The latch is consumed only when the forced path actually fires, so a\nfailed navigation returns its own error untouched and leaves the lever armed:\nan infrastructure hiccup must not burn the single forced run an operator is\nwaiting on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): apply the design-system widget redesign\n\nBring the tile and the recovery modal in line with Паспорт-чекер.dc.html.\n\nBoth tiers now lay out as header -> flexible middle -> action, so the\naction sits on the tile''s bottom edge in every state instead of trailing\nwhatever content happens to be above it. Status banners pick up the\ndesign''s per-state hues, the filled success disc, and the CircleAlert /\nTriangleAlert split that separates a retryable failure from a session\nprompt. The modal gains the access-pill dot, a pulsing LIVE dot, framed\nconnecting/disconnected/expired states, and the shell-style SSH block.\n\nDark theme was effectively missing: the --pc-* hues chained to\n--success-soft / --destructive-soft, which the host token set never\ndefines, and to --success, which is a dark surface green rather than an\nicon green under [data-theme=''dark'']. Every hue is now declared per\ntheme the way ofelia-poop-duty does it. The modal portals to\ndocument.body, outside .widget, so it declares the one hue it needs on\nits own overlay.\n\nThree deliberate departures from the mockup:\n- invalidConfig copy stays server-side (\"Обратитесь к администратору\"),\n  since this widget has no per-instance settings — its config lives in\n  server secrets.\n- the expired frame keeps \"Переподключиться\"; without it an expired\n  capability can only be recovered by closing and reopening the modal.\n- the sessionRequired tiny tile only fills amber, no border: the tile\n  border belongs to the host card, and drawing one from inside the\n  widget would render a second, inset contour.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xwGNctwqXcYi5p4QSSFhA\n\n* docs(browser-automation): design widget user-input detection\n\nMove the \"a human must act\" mechanism out of passport-checker and into the\nautomation platform. The handler gets context.detectUserInput(detector), which\nruns the detector, optionally prepares the page, retains it and returns the\ncanonical error as one indivisible step; retainPageForRecovery leaves the public\ncontext so the halves cannot be separated. Cloudflare becomes a reusable helper\n(probe and predicate together, not just the predicate) that any widget can pass\nin. Also records the removal of the PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY lever.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): correct the user-input spec''s test-seam claims\n\nmakeFakeExecutor fakes BrowserExecutor<FakeContext> with FakeContext = { id,\nsignal }; it never builds a BrowserTaskContext, so it cannot host a recording\ndetectUserInput and needs no change at all. Name the seams that do exist: the\nsix retainPageForRecovery call sites in chromium-executor.test.ts and the three\nBrowserTaskContext literals in diagnostics.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): plan the widget user-input detection refactor\n\nSeven tasks, each green on its own: retire the forced-recovery lever, add the\ndetector and error contracts, move Cloudflare detection into the platform with\nits probe reunited with its predicate, run escalation from the task context,\nrewire the widget, then withdraw retainPageForRecovery from the public context so\nthe retain/escalate pair cannot be taken apart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): make the Cloudflare task a move, not a copy\n\nTask 3 previously left the classifier duplicated between the widget and the\nplatform until Task 5, which any reviewer would rightly flag as verbatim\nduplication of a logic block. The widget now switches to the moved module in the\nsame task, so the logic never exists in two places.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* revert(passport-checker): drop the env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe lever forced one trip through the Cloudflare recovery path per service\nstart so Subproject 8 could exercise noVNC on the Pi on demand. It is being\nretired deliberately ahead of moving user-input detection into the platform;\nthe Pi smoke test needs another answer.\n\nThis reverts the behaviour added in 45c604f1.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(browser-automation): add user-input detector and error contracts\n\nUserInputRequiredError carries the browser_session_required code and sshTarget\nmeta the recovery flow keys off, moved out of the passport-checker widget where\na platform contract had no business living. UserInputProbeError distinguishes\na broken check from a negative one.\n\n* feat(browser-automation): move Cloudflare detection into the platform\n\nThe classifier lived in the passport-checker widget while the DOM probe feeding\nit lived in that widget''s check handler, so the only reusable half was the half\nwithout the probe. Both now sit in browser-automation behind two detector\nfactories: one that probes the live page, one that classifies evidence already\nderived inside the page from a fetch response.\n\nThe widget imports the moved module rather than keeping a copy; it still calls\nthe classifier by hand, which the next commit changes.\n\n* @\ndocs: document the feature branch -> dev -> main workflow\n\nFeature work happens in a dedicated branch inside a ./.worktrees checkout,\nlands in dev through a PR, gets verified on the dev stack, and only then\nreaches main through a dev -> main PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* feat(browser-automation): run user-input detection from the task context\n\ndetectUserInput runs a widget-supplied detector and, on a match, prepares the\npage, retains it and returns UserInputRequiredError as one step, so the two\nhalves cannot drift apart. A detector that fails yields UserInputProbeError and\ndeliberately leaves the page unretained: an undecidable check is not a challenge,\nand pinning a browser page for a recovery nobody will perform is worse.\n\nAUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET moves into the service config, where an unusable value\ndegrades to null rather than failing config loading and killing the service.\n\n* docs(passport-checker): design last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* test(browser-automation): cover the prepare-before-retain order in detectUserInput directly\n\nThe executor-level test asserted hasRetainedPage() from inside the prepare\nmock, but that bookkeeping is only populated at release() time, so it passed\nregardless of call order. Add a direct unit test against makeDetectUserInput\nthat records prepare/retain into one ordered array — genuinely fails if the\norder in detect.ts is inverted (verified by temporarily inverting it).\n\nNo production behavior changes; detect.ts ends identical to before this commit.\n\n* @\ndocs: merge AGENTS.md into CLAUDE.md and symlink it\n\nAGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md had drifted into two partly overlapping rule sets.\nEverything unique to AGENTS.md (project structure, codegen commands, Windows\ntest-runner notes, coding style, UI gotchas, testing, security, failure modes)\nnow lives in CLAUDE.md, and AGENTS.md becomes a symlink so every agent reads\nthe same file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* docs(passport-checker): plan last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* feat(passport-checker): date-aware check timestamp label\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout design\n\nTwo width breakpoints instead of one hardcoded grid: one column below 768,\ntwelve above, with row height and margin scaled by the same factor as the\ncolumn width so the desktop grid zooms instead of stretching on one axis.\n\nThe mobile layout is derived from the desktop one and only persisted once the\nuser drags or resizes, stored as an additive optional `mobileLayout` field so\nno migration of `root:boards` is needed.\n\nAlso records the react-draggable touchstart defect: the whole card is the drag\nhandle today, which cancels page scrolling on touch — harmless on a short\n12-column board, blocking at one column.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): detect required user input through the platform\n\nThe handler no longer collects Cloudflare evidence, classifies it, retains the\npage and builds the escalation error by hand at every site; it passes a detector\nto context.detectUserInput and returns what comes back. browser/challenge.ts and\nthe widget-local BrowserSessionRequiredError are gone — the classifier now lives\nin browser-automation next to the probe that feeds it, and the\nbrowser_session_required contract belongs to the platform that owns the recovery\nflow.\n\n* fix(passport-checker): restore the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nAn automated amend from a concurrent session accidentally stripped this\nline out of 3a12494e. It belongs to the user-input-detection work; this\nrestores it verbatim.\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nRETRYABLE_MESSAGES.user_input_probe vanished twice with no test turning red,\nbecause nothing asserted the actual behaviour: with the entry gone,\nmapCheckError silently falls back to GENERIC_RETRYABLE_MESSAGE and the user\nsees the generic string instead of the specific one. Assert the real output\nof mapCheckError against a hardcoded expected string, not against the table\nitself, so a future deletion or typo of the entry fails this test instead of\ncomparing the map against itself.\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron design spec\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout implementation plan\n\n* feat(board): resolve grid metrics from the container width\n\n* feat(passport-checker): persist the last successful check\n\n* feat(board): derive and reconcile the one-column mobile layout\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron implementation plan\n\nAlso corrects the spec: the jsdom Temporal shims must stay, only the\n--harmony-temporal execArgv becomes redundant on Node 26.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin which detector goes to which detectUserInput call\n\nNothing previously checked that the navigation check gets the page detector\nand the submission check gets the evidence detector -- only the second call''s\nprepare option was asserted. A handler that swapped the two factories would\nleave every test in this file green; only the gated real-browser suite would\nnotice, and it isn''t run in CI. Pull the captured detector off each mock call\nand invoke it directly: the page detector must reach for page.evaluate, the\nevidence detector must ignore its page argument and classify the real\nevidence. Also drops the now-inert hasChallengeForm evidence field from the\nsubmission-escalation test, whose result was always driven by the mock''s\nescalation queue regardless of that field.\n\n* feat(board): add mobile layout materialize, update and reset actions\n\n* refactor(browser-automation): expose only detectUserInput on the task context\n\nretainPageForRecovery was half of a pair a handler had to assemble correctly by\nhand: retaining without escalating strands a browser page, escalating without\nretaining hands the UI a recovery session with nothing behind it. With the retain\nflag reachable only through detectUserInput, neither mistake is expressible.\n\n* test(passport-checker): cover restore-on-mount and cross-placement sync\n\nAdd two widget-level cases pinning storage-backed persistence through\nReact: a fresh mount renders a previously stored result without\ninvoking a check, and a check made in one placement is observed by a\nsecond placement sharing the same storage. Rename the pre-existing\n\"gives separate instance ids separate state\" case to \"gives separate\nstorages separate state\" (it isolates by storage, not instanceId) and\nupdate its comment to point at the new cross-placement case below it.\nPin PASSPORT_LAST_RESULT_KEY''s literal value in check-model.test.ts so\na silent rename cannot slip through green while orphaning deployed\nusers'' stored data under the old key.\n\n* feat(board): switch the grid to resolved metrics and a mobile drag grip\n\n* docs(browser-automation): document how a widget requests manual input\n\n* feat(board): offer a mobile layout reset in the board schema popover\n\n* fix(widgets): address passport-checker persistence review findings\n\n- TinyTier''s success chip now shows checkedAtLabel, so a restored (possibly\n  stale) result reads honestly in the compact tile too, not just standard.\n- Harden the two-placement isolation test: assert placement two stays idle\n  instead of a length-1 findAllByText that can pass mid-race.\n- Clarify why lastResult/transient set order is safe in checkPassport\n  (Reatom notifies after the sync block, not \"order for readability\").\n- Add a test exercising lastResultSchema.safeParse over the exact shape\n  checkPassport writes, since createFakeStorage skips schema validation.\n- Document lastResultSchema as a persistence contract: new fields must be\n  optional/defaulted or every stored value fails validation on next deploy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* fix(passport-checker): remove dead AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET threading\n\nrecoverySshTarget was never read by check.ts''s handler after the\ndetectUserInput refactor moved sshTarget sourcing entirely into the\nplatform config. Delete the widget''s leftover env read and option\nthreading so the README''s \"widgets neither read nor pass it\" claim is\nactually true, and drop the now-meaningless SSH-sanitization test in\nfavor of the equivalent (and broader) coverage in browser-automation''s\nconfig.test.ts.\n\n* docs(ofelia): design the authored ledger and comments\n\nRecords get the authenticated account instead of the per-device \"Я:\"\ntoggle, the write path moves into the widget''s own server.ts, and the\nhistory row is rebuilt: human dates, plain-language phrases, explicit\ndebt effect, visible supersession, no IP.\n\nPulls the Node 26 prerequisite out of the widget-server-cron spec, since\nthe shared domain module it enables is needed here first.\n\nAlso imports the Claude Design workspace under design/ (documents only,\nscreenshots and uploads stripped) and keeps the brief that produced the\nOfelia mock.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs(ofelia): note the cron plan overlap and landing order\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): cover the mobile layout and touch-scroll behaviour end to end\n\n* fix(board): keep the measured grid container mounted\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a 1920 viewport\n\n* docs(ofelia): add the authored ledger implementation plan\n\n15 tasks: Node 26, the pure domain split, the members directory, widget\nidentity, the server-resolved viewer, record schemas, draft construction,\nthe widget server definition, the model rewire, the history and comment\nview models, the account avatar, and the two rebuilt panels.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): wait for the grid relayout to settle before measuring\n\n* fix(browser-automation): bundle workspace imports instead of externalizing them\n\nThe rspack externals predicate was an allow-list whose default was\n\"externalize\". When the user-input refactor introduced the first runtime\nbare-specifier import of a workspace package into the service module graph\n(browser-automation/user-input/cloudflare, from the passport-checker browser\ntask), it was silently externalized: the build stayed green, all tests stayed\ngreen, and the Cloudflare module was simply absent from dist/index.cjs. The\nruntime stage copies only package.json and dist/, so Node resolved the\nspecifier through the exports map to a .ts path that does not exist in the\nimage and the container died on `exec node dist/index.cjs`.\n\nInvert the predicate into a deny-list derived from the package''s own\ndependencies (minus errore, which is ESM-only and must be bundled) plus Node\nbuiltins. An unrecognised bare specifier is now bundled, so the same mistake\nsurfaces as a build-time resolution error rather than a startup crash, and\nadding a real runtime dependency externalizes it with no config edit.\n\nAdd scripts/check-bundle.ts, chained onto `build` so it runs inside the image\nbuild the Dockerfile already performs. It asserts that every require() in the\nbundle is a builtin or a declared dependency, that the scan still matches the\nemitted shape at all, and that the compiled evidenceFromResponseText the widget\nsplices into Chromium still evaluates and runs in a bare vm realm. That last\ncheck is what the BROWSER_IT suite could never prove: it runs under vitest,\nwhere the specifier resolves to .ts through esbuild and the swc path is never\nexercised. Verified: the compiled form is ES5-lowered but self-contained --\ndestructuring became inline property reads, no swc helpers, no hoisted regexes.\n\nAlso:\n- detect.ts: tag the prepare outcome instead of comparing against null, so a\n  prepare that rejects with exactly null is a failure rather than a silent\n  success, matching the detector branch''s `typeof detected !== ''boolean''`.\n- user-input/errors.test.ts: cover toEnvelopeError for UserInputRequiredError\n  with and without an ssh target, pinning with toStrictEqual that `meta` is\n  omitted rather than set to undefined. contracts.test.ts already claimed this\n  coverage lived here.\n- chromium-executor.test.ts: drop an assertion inside a prepare callback that\n  could not fail the test and was vacuous, and rename the case to what it\n  actually verifies; the ordering contract is owned by detect.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* test(board): settle past the grid transition and stop assuming card order\n\n* test(board): correct the stale comment above the breakpoint wait\n\n* chore(node): align every runtime and image on Node 26\n\nnode:26-alpine and node:26-bookworm-slim no longer bundle corepack\n(removed from Node core), so `npm install -g corepack@latest` is added\nahead of every `corepack enable` in the three Dockerfiles and\ndocker-compose.dev.yml. scripts/infra.test.ts''s assertion on the\nbrowser-automation Dockerfile''s base image is updated to match.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(node): unshadow jsdom localStorage and bump the e2e image to Node 26\n\nNode 26 ships its own unflagged global localStorage/sessionStorage (Web\nStorage API), which resolves to undefined without --localstorage-file\nand shadows jsdom''s window.localStorage for bare-identifier reads in\npackages/client''s jsdom test environment (40 failing tests). Disable\nNode''s own globals via execArgv: [''--no-experimental-webstorage''] in\npackages/client/vite.config.ts so jsdom''s storage wins; verified via\nObject.getOwnPropertyDescriptor that the flag actually removes the\nglobal rather than just suppressing the warning. widget-runtime and\nwidget-sdk don''t touch localStorage in tests today, so the flag is not\nadded to their execArgv arrays (narrowest correct remedy).\n\nAlso bumps packages/client/e2e.Dockerfile (used by pnpm test:e2e:docker\nvia docker-compose.e2e.yml) from node:22-bookworm-slim to\nnode:26-bookworm-slim, missed by the original brief''s Files list, with\nthe same corepack@latest install this Node 26 base image now needs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): make the relayout wait actually poll\n\n* refactor(ofelia): extract the pure duty domain out of the Reatom model\n\n* fix(lint): make the widget domain import barrier a real allowlist\n\nThe regex denylist let ''../model/ofelia-duty'' through — domain/ and model/\nare siblings, so a single-''../'' import was both the most likely accidental\nescape and completely unguarded. It also only enumerated known packages, so\nany new npm dependency passed silently.\n\nReplace it with a gitignore-style group allowlist (''**'' minus zod, @shared/*\nand ''./'' siblings), which oxlint evaluates natively. A negative-lookahead\nregex was tried first and rejected: oxlint''s Rust regex engine has no\nlookaround and silently drops such a pattern, disabling the whole barrier.\n\nDomain tests get a second, later override that additionally allows vitest;\neverything else stays blocked there too.\n\n* chore(lint): keep the design mock exports out of lint and format\n\ndesign/ holds vendored Claude Design exports (support.js, *.dc.html)\nthat break the repo''s only gate: oxlint reports 9 errors in\ndesign/support.js, and oxfmt aborts (exit 2) on non-standard markup\nin design/Design System.dc.html before it can check anything else.\n\nAdd design/** to the existing ignorePatterns glob array in both\n.oxlintrc.json and .oxfmtrc.json, the same mechanism already used for\ndocs/**. This is a plain glob matcher (not the Rust regex engine\nbehind no-restricted-imports), so it isn''t subject to that engine''s\nlookaround gap, and it leaves every other rule/override untouched.\n\nFixing the oxfmt abort also uncovered pre-existing, unrelated\nformatting drift in CLAUDE.md, package.json and\nscripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts that the crash had been masking from\nthe format:check summary. Reformatted them (whitespace/ordering only,\nno logic changes) so pnpm format:check is actually green end to end.\n\n* feat(auth): expose the board members directory\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): expose the board identity to widgets\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): stop identity fetch from leaking an unhandled rejection and a discarded error\n\nHandle the AbortError that wrap()''s promise rejects with when the last\nwidget reading identity unmounts mid-fetch, and stop discarding\nBoardMembersError whole: a 401 stays silent (expected on an\nunauthenticated host), everything else is surfaced via console.warn so\nschema drift or a server fault is not silently swallowed.\n\n* feat(widgets): resolve the signed-in viewer for widget server events\n\n* feat(ofelia): carry the authoring account on ledger and comment records\n\n* feat(ofelia): move draft construction and event schemas into the domain\n\n* feat(ofelia): write the ledger and comments from the widget server\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): harden server date parsing and unforgeability coverage\n\n- Wrap Temporal.PlainDate.from in errore.try so a calendar-invalid but\n  regex-valid date (e.g. 2026-02-30) returns an Error instead of\n  throwing through a function typed Error | T.\n- Add regression coverage: forged createdBy/actor in the payload is\n  ignored, the debt handler is exercised, storage-read failures are\n  asserted by identity with no write, and the invalid-date case is\n  asserted as a value.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): split the unforgeability guard into schema and handler halves\n\nThe round-1 forged-payload test went through run(), which parses the\npayload via zod''s default strip mode before the handler ever sees it —\nso it only proved the schema strips forged fields, twice over, and\nwould not catch a handler that starts spreading its raw payload into\nthe draft. Replace it with two schema-level tests (clean + comment\npayloads drop a forged createdBy/actor) and one handler-level test\nthat calls ofeliaServer.handlers.clean directly, bypassing the schema,\nto prove the handler itself never reads those fields even if they\nreach it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* feat(ofelia): drive ledger and comment writes through widget server events\n\nThe client no longer appends to the ledger or the comment key. All four\nday actions collapse into one `invokeDay` that sends `{ date }` to the\nmatching widget-server event, and `send` sends `{ weekStart, text }` —\nwho the actor is, whose debt moves, whether the event applies at all and\nwhich account signed it are now derived and stamped server-side, so a\nclient cannot forge authorship.\n\n`selectedDate()` and `today()` (and `viewWeekStart()`) are read before\nthe first await so they resolve against the widget''s own Reatom context.\n\nThe per-device \"Я: Леша/Карина\" toggle is deleted rather than hidden:\nauthorship comes from the session, so there is nothing left to pick.\n`currentUser` is gone from the duty model, the widget context, the\nfixture and the view-model''s `onSetUser`.\n\nBoth model factories now take `api` and `identity`; `identity` is the\ndirectory the history and comment views read in the follow-up tasks.\nThe view projections drop `ip`/`by`, whose schema fields Tasks 5-8\nremoved, and `authorName` falls back through `createdBy.name`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* style: format the ofelia server test\n\n* feat(ofelia): group history by duty day and mark superseded records\n\n* feat(ofelia): resolve comment authors through the members directory\n\n* fix(ofelia): flush the comment thread''s connect hook via vi.waitFor, not a bare microtask\n\nReview round 1: the timing fix for the comment-thread subscribe/emit race\nassumed a single-microtask flush of Reatom''s connect-hook queue. Match the\nsibling comments-subscription tests in the same file instead — wait for the\nstorage mock''s subscribe call and wrap the whole body in context.start, so\nthe assertion is condition-based rather than scheduling-internals-based.\n\n* feat(ofelia): add the account avatar and its palette\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the history list around duty-day groups\n\n* fix(ofelia): announce the history phrase subject to assistive tech\n\nThe duty circle is the sentence subject in the rebuilt history phrase, and\nAvatar is aria-hidden, so the column announced as bare verbs with no subject.\nPair every circle with a visually hidden name, attribute the debt pill to its\nperson, and separate the chips with real spaces as the mock does.\n\nAlso correct the text-decoration comment to name the real mechanism (atomic\ninline, not descendant cancellation) and cover the three untested branches.\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the comment thread around account authors\n\n* fix(ofelia): restore composer coverage and mute the unknown comment author\n\n* fix(ofelia): break ledger day ties by append order\n\nRouting the writes through the widget server changed where `ts` comes from.\n`/api/storage/:key/append` stamped `Date.now()`, so consecutive appends always\ngot a strictly increasing timestamp. The widget dispatch stamps the app''s\ninjectable `now()`, and the e2e harness pins that clock — so a clean-then-undo\npair on the same day produced two entries with an identical `ts`, and\n`latestOutcomesByDate`''s strict `>` kept the earlier `cleaned` entry as the\nday''s outcome. The `reset` was written but never counted, and the day never\nreopened; `pnpm test:e2e:docker` caught it as a failing \"undo — returns the day\nto pending\".\n\nThe ledger is append-only and iterated in insertion order, so `>=` is the\ncorrect tie-break: on an equal timestamp the later-appended entry is the newer\ndecision. This also hardens production, where two writes can still land in the\nsame millisecond.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: describe server-authored widget records\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): fail widget dispatch when the session account is unreadable\n\nresolveWidgetViewer folded every getAccount failure into `null`, so a Valkey\nfault, a corrupt account record and a deleted account all produced a record\nstamped `createdBy: null` plus a `{ ok: true }` response. The ledger is\nappend-only, so that authorship could never be recovered.\n\nThe three outcomes are now distinct: no session still returns `null`, which is\nwhat the standalone harnesses and the e2e stack (no nginx, so no session cookie)\nrely on to keep writing unattributed records; a failed lookup returns a\nWidgetViewerLookupError, which the dispatch route turns into a fault instead of\na false success.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): order superseded history records by append order\n\n`superseded` sorted on `right.ts - left.ts`, which returns 0 on a tie; the\nstable sort then left tied records in append order, oldest first, contradicting\nthe \"newest first\" contract on HistoryDayGroup. Ties became a supported case\nwhen latestOutcomesByDate started breaking them with `>=`, and the dockerized\ne2e stack freezes the clock, so there every record ties.\n\nThe ledger is append-only, so append order is chronological order: reversing the\nday''s bucket is both correct and cheaper than sorting.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): warn when listAccounts skips an unreadable record\n\nA corrupt or schema-mismatched account record was dropped silently, so the\nroster simply came back short and the symptom read as \"an account vanished\"\nrather than as the data corruption it is. Name the key in a console.warn.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* build: pin corepack to 0.35.0 across images and dev compose\n\n`npm install -g corepack@latest` ran in five image build stages and four dev\ncompose services. There is no CI and the Pi deploy is manual with a 30-minute\nbuild timeout, so a breaking corepack release would only ever surface as a\nfailed deploy. Pin one explicit version everywhere.\n\nVerified: `docker build -f packages/server/Dockerfile` succeeds and the built\nimage reports corepack 0.35.0 with pnpm 10.28.2.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* refactor(ofelia): delete the dead short-date formatter\n\nformatDateShort and MONTHS_SHORT lost their last production consumer when the\ncomment thread moved to its own formatter; only the test still called them. It\nalso formatted through `new Date(ts)` in the local timezone, while every current\nformatter goes through plainDateIn(DUTY_TIME_ZONE, ...).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): drop the tier guards that cannot fail, hoist the env directive\n\nStandardTier and CompactTier each asserted they do not render UserToggle. The\ntoggle only ever lived in RichLayout and the component is now deleted outright,\nso neither assertion could ever fail — they only looked like coverage.\n\nAlso move the `@vitest-environment node` directive in server.test.ts back to\nline 1, where a reader expects it and where model/history-view.test.ts keeps it.\nVitest 4 matches the directive anywhere in the file content, so the file was\nalready running under node; this is readability, not a behaviour fix.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: state the authorship guarantee the code actually provides\n\nCLAUDE.md claimed a server-stamped author \"cannot be forged\". Nothing enforces\nthat: POST /api/storage/:key/append does no session check in Node, and nginx''s\nauth_request only proves a session exists, not whose it is, so any signed-in\nhousehold member can still hand-write a record carrying an arbitrary author.\n\nState what is true instead — the UI can no longer sign a record with someone\nelse''s name, and the generic storage route remains a shared trusted channel\ninside the board. Closing that route is a separate task.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(board): never render the desktop grid below its 1920 baseline\n\nThe desktop zoom is now one-directional: raise the lower clamp on the\ngrid scale from 0.75 to 1, so every container width between the mobile\nbreakpoint and BASE_WIDTH resolves to exactly the historical metrics\n(rowHeight: 30, margin: [10, 10]) and only wider displays scale up.\n\nUntil this branch, useContainerWidth never attached its observer and\npinned the reported width at its 1280 default on every screen. With the\ncontainer measured for real, a 0.75 floor would have shrunk every board\non a 1280/1366/1440 laptop below the size it has always had, dropping an\nh:6 card from 230px to 172.5px and pushing widgets under their own tier\nthresholds. Nothing shrinks now; the 4K aspect drift the scale was added\nfor is still fixed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling record\n\nBring the design doc in line with what shipped: the lower clamp is 1,\nthe 1280 table row shows the baseline metrics, and the paragraph\njustifying the clamp says plainly that it is active across the whole\n768-1920 band rather than a narrow 768-1100 one.\n\nAlso correct the claim that existing 1080p boards \"do not shift by a\npixel\". The formula at 1920 does reproduce the old constants, but the\nmeasured width was previously pinned at 1280 on every screen by the\nuseContainerWidth bug this branch fixes, so wider screens legitimately\nre-flow to use the whole window.\n\nDrop the 1920 viewport pin from ofelia-duty.spec.ts: it only worked\naround the 0.75 clamp, and removing it restores default-viewport\ncoverage. Recompute the footprint arithmetic in the ofelia client test\ncomment, which assumed a container of exactly 1280 rather than the\nviewport minus the board padding and the scrollbar gutter.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a viewport its tier needs\n\nEvery assertion in the duty spec is StandardTier UI, and the widget only\nresolves `standard` at a frame width of 400px or more. Playwright''s\ndefault 1280 viewport is about 7px short: the board measures its own\ncontainer, which is 1225px there once the stable scrollbar gutter and the\nboard''s 20px padding on each side come off, so a w:4 card is ~395px and\nits frame ~393px. `compact` is the correct rendering at that width, so\nthe spec has to declare the width its assertions need.\n\nThe dependency was previously invisible because the board reported a\nhardcoded 1280px container on every screen, which made the card 413px and\nleft the threshold only 13px of headroom against a measurement that was\nwrong by 55px.\n\nCorrect the footprint comment in the widget''s client test to match: the\ntier is measured on the frame inside the card''s 1px border, so a 1280px\nviewport yields ~393x228 and resolves to `compact`, not `standard`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling claims to match what ships\n\nThree statements asserted the opposite of what this branch established,\nall written before the width axis was measured.\n\nThe grid-metrics JSDoc claimed the clamp keeps 1280/1366/1440 boards\n\"the size they have always been\" and widgets \"above their own tier\nthresholds\". Both are false at 1280: the container is 1225 there, so a\nw:4 card is 395px where the old code drew 413px, and ofelia falls under\nits 400px standard threshold into compact. The clamp pins the metrics -\nrowHeight 30 and margin [10, 10] at every desktop width - not the\nrendered geometry, which still follows the measured container.\n\nThe design doc claimed \"no board shrinks and no widget loses a tier\".\nThe 1366 and 1440 frames (422px, 446px) clear the widget threshold; the\n1280 frame (393px) does not. Corrected to draw the metrics/geometry\ndistinction the document already draws two paragraphs earlier.\n\nThe \"do not shift by a pixel\" claim survived in the doc''s Testing\nsection and in the test''s own comment. At 1080p the container is now\n1865, so colWidth is 144.58 rather than the 95.83 the pinned-1280 bug\nproduced. Both now state what the test guarantees: the formula at\nBASE_WIDTH reproduces the previous hardcoded constants.\n\nAlso record the shipped consequence in Risks - ofelia renders\nCompactTier below ~1300px, correct behaviour on a genuinely 393px frame,\nwith recalibration left as a follow-up - and fix three figures: the tier\nparagraph attributed the frame inset to a card padding that does not\nexist (it is a 1px border; the heights are 148px and 298px, not 110 and\n260), the e2e headroom was 11px on the frame rather than 13px on the\ncard, the 1920 card/frame are 608/606, and useContainerWidth is at\nBoard.tsx:28.\n\nComments and prose only; no executable line changed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* build(deploy): add a per-branch rpi environment and deploy scripts\n\nrpi.branch.toml is a disposable stack for trying the checked-out branch on\nreal hardware, with ttl = \"72h\" so a forgotten one reaps itself. It takes the\nbranch through ${BRANCH_NAME}, the only variable rpi 0.26 accepts — hence the\nliteral hostname rather than a derived one.\n\nscripts/deploy-branch.ts reads the branch from git so nobody retypes it, and\nrefuses to deploy without .env.branch, which would otherwise produce a stack\nwhose WebAuthn gate fails with nothing in the deploy log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(deploy): send the branch secrets bundle before the build\n\nEvery branch is a fresh rpi project with an empty secrets store, so the very\nfirst deploy of any branch died after the ~3 minute image build with an opaque\nCompose \"secret file ... does not exist\". deploy:branch now checks\n`rpi secrets ls` and sends the bundle itself when there is none.\n\nAlso strip the `--` that `pnpm run x -- --flag` forwards verbatim: rpi''s parser\nreads a bare `--` as end-of-flags, so the flag after it was rejected as a\npositional.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(browser-automation): read the spliced callback when swc stops inlining it\n\ncheck-bundle extracted the page callback by slicing between `.concat(` and\n`.toString()`, which is the whole function expression only while swc inlines it.\nA second consumer of evidenceFromResponseText — precisely what the public\nbrowser-automation/user-input/cloudflare subpath exists to allow — makes swc emit\na named declaration instead, leaving a bare identifier at the splice site. The\nguard then evaluated that identifier in an empty realm, got a ReferenceError, and\nfailed the image build accusing a perfectly correct bundle of shipping a\nnon-self-contained callback.\n\nResolve the identifier to its declaration, and let the JavaScript parser find\nwhere that declaration ends: counting braces by hand would have to understand\nregex literals, and this body is full of regexes containing quote characters.\nExtraction failures now say so instead of reporting a self-containment defect.\n\nVerified by building all four ways: inlined and named shapes both pass; the\noriginal externals regression and a regex hoisted out of the callback are both\nstill caught.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* fix(ofelia): centre the initial in avatars re-cast to inline-flex\n\nBoth avatars centre their initial with `place-items: center`, but the\nhistory phrase re-casts the box to `inline-flex` so the circle stays in\nthe inline run, and a flex container ignores `justify-items`. The glyph\nwas left-aligned: in an 18px circle it sat 0px from the inline start and\n12.97px from the end, roughly a third of the diameter off centre.\n\nName the inline axis outright so the centring survives whichever display\na caller imposes. `.debt > [data-tone]` re-casts the same way and was hit\nby the same bug.\n\n* fix(ofelia): reserve room for the viewer ring in scrolling columns\n\nThe signed-in viewer''s avatar carries a 3px ring drawn as a spread\nshadow, so it paints outside the border box. Every avatar sits flush\nagainst the inline start of a container that clips: `.historyCol` and\n`.list` scroll (and `overflow-y: auto` clips the inline axis too), and\n`.commentsCol` is `overflow: hidden`. The ring lost its whole left edge —\nmeasured at exactly 3.0px — leaving a flat-sided avatar in the history,\nthe comment list and the composer, in both themes and on mobile.\n\nWiden each clip box outward by the ring width with a padding/negative\nmargin pair, which moves no content, and hold the width in\n`--ofelia-viewer-ring` so the shadow and the reserved room cannot drift.\n\n* fix(ofelia): size the account initial like the duty initial\n\n`MemberAvatar` scaled its initial to half the box while `Avatar` uses\n0.4, and the history phrase renders both at `px={18}` on the same line —\n9px next to 7.2px, which reads as two mismatched type sizes rather than\none set.\n\nShare the ratio through `AVATAR_INITIAL_RATIO`. Taking `Avatar`''s value\nkeeps every pre-existing avatar (the 62px hero, the 26px week strip)\npixel-identical and moves only the account squares this branch added.\n\n* fix(ofelia): raise the standard tier to the height it actually needs\n\nStandardTier lays out to 298px of content, but `tiers.standard` asked for\nonly 200px and the widget card clips instead of scrolling. At the default\nplacement (413x230px) the tier therefore resolved and then cut its own\naction row in half: \"Уборка подтверждена\" was sliced through and \"В долг\"\n/ \"Простить\" never appeared at all.\n\nState the height the tier needs, so anything shorter degrades to\n`compact` rather than rendering truncated, and raise `defaultSize` to\n`{ w: 4, h: 8 }` (~413x310px) so a freshly-placed widget still clears it\n— the same fix shape as the earlier default-placement regression.\n\n* fix(ofelia): let the pinned day header cover the reserved ring strip\n\nThe history column reserves `--ofelia-viewer-ring` of inline-start room\nso the viewer ring is not clipped, and records paint into that strip. The\nsticky day header''s own box stops at the content edge, so the strip stayed\nuncovered: scrolling avatars slid past the pinned header through a 3px\ngap, measured against the column''s border box.\n\nStretch the header back over the strip with a negative inline-start margin\nand return the width as padding, so its background spans the full\nscrollport while nothing inside it moves.\n\n* feat(ofelia): scroll the history to the day picked in the week strip\n\nSelecting a day highlighted it in the strip and changed the panel, but\nleft the history where it was, so the records for that day had to be found\nby hand.\n\n`HistoryList` now keeps a node per duty day and scrolls the matching group\nto the top of its scrollport when the selection changes. Exactly one\nancestor scrolls in each layout — the column on desktop, the dialog body\non mobile — so the native `scrollIntoView` cannot drag two scrollports at\nonce. The first run only records the mounted day: scrolling on mount would\npull the panel above the history off screen in the mobile layout before\nthe reader touched anything.\n\nThe column subscribes to a newly exposed `view.selectedIso` rather than\n`view.selected`, so confirming or forgiving the selected day still does\nnot re-render the history.\n\n* fix(ofelia): scroll only a box the reader can scroll back\n\n`scrollIntoView` moves *every* scroll container in the ancestor chain, and\n`overflow: hidden` boxes are scroll containers too — they just have no\nscrollbar. The widget frame is one: measured at 149px of overflow in the\nmobile layout, `overflow-y: hidden`. Switching a day therefore displaced\nthe whole widget, and with no scrollbar there the reader could not put it\nback.\n\nWalk up to the innermost box that is genuinely user-scrollable\n(`auto`/`scroll` *and* overflowing) and scroll that one by hand, aligning\nthe group with the scrollport top where the sticky header pins. When\nnothing in the chain scrolls there is nothing to reveal, so the layout is\nleft alone rather than nudged.\n\n* fix(ofelia): keep the mobile tab pane from clipping the viewer ring\n\nBounding the tab pane needs `overflow: hidden`, which clips the inline axis\ntoo — `overflow-x` cannot stay `visible` once the other axis is not. So in\nthe mobile layout `.split` becomes a second clipper, outside the columns\nand at the same inline start, and it shaved off the 3px viewer ring that\nthe columns had already reserved room for. Measured at `cutL: 3` on the\ncomment and record avatars; on desktop `.split` is `overflow: visible`, so\nthis only ever showed on narrow screens.\n\nReach outward by the ring width there too, the same padding/negative-margin\npair the columns use.\n\n* docs(cron): rebase the cron spec and plan onto dev\n\ndev landed Node 26, the widget domain/ boundary and Ofelia''s server.ts with\naccount authorship, so those tasks are gone. Authorship for cron records is now\na system arm on the persisted createdBy union rather than a `by: ''system''`\nfield, and the plan drops from 12 tasks to 8.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay back at dev\n\nThe overlay was temporarily aimed at this feature branch (5208c5c5) to test it on\nthe Pi before the `branch` environment existed. rpi.branch.toml now covers that\ncase per branch, so dev goes back to deploying dev — which is also what\nscripts/infra.test.ts asserts, and that test passes again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(widgets): add the cron job contract to widget server definitions\n\n* feat(server): validate widget cron schedules when the registry is built\n\n* feat(server): persist a per-job cron cursor in Valkey\n\n* feat(server): add the widget cron tick scheduler\n\n* fix(server): drain the cron scheduler on stop and pin the no-double-fire invariant\n\ninFlight was replaced on every interval fire instead of chained, so a tick\nthat outlived intervalMs was silently orphaned and stop() could return\nwhile it was still running. Chain onto the existing promise instead.\n\nAlso add a dueOccurrence test pin…","tree":{"sha":"b29de88199cf1f598138dba82b41437a0f7f5a16","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/git/trees/b29de88199cf1f598138dba82b41437a0f7f5a16"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/git/commits/1156dd175867d11a66a824e789f9d7848320a7f8","verification":{"payload":"tree b29de88199cf1f598138dba82b41437a0f7f5a16\nparent 0cfc5f1dbfd9533ac227db0453a6a82971a9072e\nauthor khmilevoi <Khmilevoi@gmail.com> 1785924395 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785924395 +0200\n\nDev (#40)\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the modal focusout guard against narrowing\n\nMount the underlying Radix dialog in its own pass, as production does, and\ncover the intra-modal focus move that the proposed guard narrowing would have\nbroken silently. The regression test dispatches the underlying focusout event\ndirectly rather than chaining native .focus() calls: jsdom (like real\nbrowsers) always completes an outer .focus() call on its own target once its\nhandler stack unwinds, so a settled document.activeElement assertion can''t\nobserve a reclaim a nested handler only ever wins transiently — verified\nempirically against the narrowed guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): close a cross-test focus-restore leak in the Radix stack suite\n\nRadix''s FocusScope defers its unmount focus-restore to a real setTimeout(0),\nand testing-library''s auto cleanup() never waits for it, so a prior test''s\ndeferred restore (and an unawaited NoVncCanvas state update from that same\ntest''s async recovery flow) can bleed into the next test — confirmed by\ninstrumentation. A formal trace of the reentrant focus cascade shows the\n''pulls focus back...'' assertion itself can''t actually be flipped by this\n(no yield point exists between its synchronous .focus() call and its\nexpect()), but the leak is real and pre-existing to any suite that\nmounts+unmounts a trapped FocusScope, so close it defensively: flush one\nreal tick after each test''s cleanup so Radix''s deferred work always\nfinishes before the next test starts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): drain Radix''s focus-restore timer from beforeEach, not afterEach\n\ne797d50''s message overstated what that change achieved. It claimed the added\nafterEach ran \"strictly after cleanup()\" and closed the cross-test focus-restore\nleak. It did neither. Vitest resolves sequence.hooks to \"stack\", so same-suite\nafterEach hooks run in REVERSE registration order, and testing-library''s\ncleanup() -- registered first, at setupFiles import time -- therefore runs last.\nThe file-level afterEach ran BEFORE cleanup(), i.e. before the unmount that\nschedules Radix''s setTimeout(0) focus restore, so it drained nothing.\n\nInstrumenting setTimeout and the hook boundaries on this file shows it directly:\nat afterEach entry the modal is still in the DOM and no FocusScope timer is\npending; the timer is scheduled only after the hook returns; the next test''s\nbeforeEach starts with one still queued. Moving the same one-tick flush to\nbeforeEach takes that count to zero before the test body runs. beforeEach is\ncorrect by construction -- it always follows the previous test''s afterEach\nhooks whatever the hook-order mode.\n\nCorrect the scope of the claim too. The restore is focus-neutral in this\nfixture: the previouslyFocusedElement it captured is either document.body,\nwhich jsdom refuses to focus, or an already-detached node, and every observed\nfiring left document.activeElement unchanged. The intermittent failure this was\nchasing did not reproduce at e797d50 in 30 consecutive single-file runs or 6\nwhole-package runs. So this closes a scheduling leak, not an observed failure,\nand the comment now says exactly that instead of claiming otherwise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): link the tier-shared-state follow-up\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): drop the phantom rebuild from the instance store''s connect hook\n\nThe connect hook carried `current ??= { value: make() }` behind a comment\nclaiming it gave a reconnect after disposal a fresh value. Measured against\n@reatom/core@1001.1.0 with the shipped code:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 2\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"],[{\"id\":2},\"a\"]]   built = 2\n\nThe reconnect still returned the DISPOSED value — a dependency-free `computed`\nnever recomputes, so its cached result is what every later read gets. The line\nonly built a second model graph nobody could reach and then handed it to\n`dispose` a second time. Same run with the line removed:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n\nThe comment now states what is true: a stale reconnect reads the cached,\nalready-disposed value, and a fresh value comes only from a new `store()`\nlookup — which is what every real caller does, because it calls `store()`\nduring render and the cleanup has already evicted the stale handle.\n\nWith the phantom build gone, `if (held)` is what prevents the double dispose,\nso a test pins it: replacing it with an unconditional `dispose?.(held?.value,\nkey)` now fails with a second teardown recorded as `[undefined, \"a\"]`.\n\nAlso corrects the module doc''s cost claim for reading atoms inside `make`.\nIt does not \"silently rebuild the whole value\": measured, after such a\ndependency changes the compute function re-runs once, `current ??=`\nshort-circuits it so `make` does not re-run (built stayed 1, the identical\nobject was returned, no subscriber notified), and because the re-run never\nreads the atom again the edge is dropped. The cost is a pointless dependency\nedge, not lost state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): align the tier-shared-state spec with what shipped\n\nThree claims in the design spec described work that was never delivered:\n\n- The instance atom was said to carry `withDisconnectHook`. It carries\n  `withConnectHook`, whose cleanup calls `teardownIfDisconnected()` — which\n  re-checks `isConnected` before disposing and evicts the map entry only while\n  it still points at the handle being torn down.\n- Section 4 \"StrictMode removal\" claimed `<StrictMode>` was dropped from\n  `packages/client/src/app/main.tsx` and replaced by a comment explaining its\n  absence. None of it shipped: `git diff cfff8d2..HEAD -- packages/client/` is\n  empty and the wrapper is still there. Measurement showed the store survives\n  the remount cycle, because the cleanup''s `isConnected` re-check returns\n  without touching a value a live mount still holds. Section 4 is rewritten as\n  \"StrictMode kept, tolerance pinned by test\", stating why the plan''s original\n  reasoning does not apply.\n- The Testing gate, the Documentation list and the Decision Summary repeated\n  the removal. They now say StrictMode stays and that the tolerance is pinned\n  by `describe(''makeWidgetInstanceStore under StrictMode'')`.\n\nThat last claim is measured, not assumed: deleting the `isConnected` re-check\nfrom the cleanup turns two of the three StrictMode tests red, along with the\nnon-StrictMode same-tick-resubscribe test.\n\nThe plan file is left untouched — it is the historical record of what was\nintended.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): narrow unmeasured claims and close two coverage gaps\n\nComment/claim accuracy — all of these stated more than was ever measured:\n\n- `ui/recovery-flow.test.tsx`: the \"roughly 1 in 15-40 runs\" hang frequency\n  was applied to every Escape-closing test in the file. It was measured for\n  exactly one of them, ''does not collapse when recovery opens from the tile''\n  (task-4-report.md, Fix round 3). The others carry the same wait by\n  inspection — identical shape, identical race — with no observed rate. The\n  comment now says so.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"at `beforeEach` entry one (later\n  two) FocusScope timer is pending\" — only the 1 -> 0 transition was ever\n  recorded, so the \"(later two)\" is dropped.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"jsdom (like real browsers) always\n  completes an outer `.focus()` call on its own target\" — true and measured\n  for jsdom, never measured against a real browser. Scoped to jsdom.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: ''keeps focus inside when it moves\n  between two of our own controls'' promised a focus invariant the test does\n  not assert — it asserts that our guard swallows the `focusout` before it\n  reaches `document`. Renamed to say that.\n\nCoverage:\n\n- `model/instance-store.test.ts` (new) covers `dispose: (models) =>\n  models.recoveryModel.teardown()`, the safety net against leaking a live VNC\n  session when the modal outlived the widget. It had no test at all: replacing\n  the callback with a no-op left the whole widget suite green. Verified both\n  new tests fail against that no-op.\n- `ui/PassportChecker.test.tsx`: two assertions used\n  `expect(await screen.findAllBy*(…)).toHaveLength(2)`, which cannot retry —\n  `findAllBy*` resolves on the first snapshot with >= 1 match, so it settles on\n  one mount and never waits for the second. Both are now a re-querying\n  `waitFor`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(backlog): record the two-modal focus ping-pong as a deferred defect\n\nBoth `useModalIsolation` instances register focus containment on `window` in\nthe capture phase, so two mounted modals each see the other''s `focusin` before\nthe root-level `stopPropagation()` can stop it, and each pulls focus into its\nown root — forever.\n\nReproduced in jsdom 29.1.1 with two `useModalIsolation` roots mounted at once:\none modal performs 1 focus pull on mount; two mounted together never settle,\nand a hard test guard at 400 pulls had to stop the run (402 recorded).\n\nDeferred rather than fixed because it is unreachable through the UI: the\npassport checker is the only owner of such a modal, renders at most one\n(non-fullscreen mount only), and its overlay is a full-viewport\n`pointer-events: auto` layer whose `pointerdown` handler closes the modal, so\na second one cannot be opened over the first.\n\nThe entry notes the likely fix — a module-level stack of active modal roots\nwith containment applied only by the topmost.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): correct five unverified claims left by code review\n\nBring wording in the tier-shared-state spec, BACKLOG.md, and a test\ncomment in line with what was actually measured/read: the instance\nstore''s dependency-edge cost (not a rebuilt value), a stale\nuse-modal-isolation.ts line citation (:53 -> :55, two places), the\nroot focusin stopper''s actual bubble phase (not capture), and which\nrecovery-flow tests keep the shared timeout default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay at this branch\n\nPins rpi.dev.toml''s source.branch to feat/passport-checker-widget so the dev\nenvironment runs this PR instead of the dev branch.\n\nREVERT BEFORE MERGING: once this PR lands on dev, source.branch must go back to\n\"dev\" or the dev overlay stays pinned to a branch that no longer moves.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): drop the stale Chromium singleton lock before launch\n\nThe Chromium profile is a named Docker volume, so `SingletonLock` keeps the\n`<hostname>-<pid>` of whichever container wrote it. After a redeploy the new\ncontainer has a new hostname, Chromium reads a lock owned by \"another computer\"\nit cannot probe, and `launchPersistentContext` fails on every task until the\nvolume is touched by hand.\n\nRemove `SingletonLock`, `SingletonCookie`, and `SingletonSocket` before each\nlaunch. `fs.rm` unlinks the symlinks themselves instead of following them, and\n`force` keeps a fresh profile from failing; a removal that does fail is returned\nas a value, logged, and the launch still proceeds. Unconditional removal is safe\nbecause `getPersistentContext` is the only caller of `launch` and the image\nentrypoint runs a single `node dist/index.cjs` - no check for a live Chromium is\nperformed or implied.\n\nAlso asserts that a failed launch keeps its cause, which is what makes the\nPlaywright message reachable from the service log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): log the error behind an internal task failure\n\nInternal failures were logged as `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` only, so the\ntagged error and its cause chain never reached the container log. A Chromium\nlaunch failure therefore showed up as a bare `internal` code with nothing to\ndiagnose. Add the error to the logged fields; `toEnvelopeError` still answers\nthe client with the redacted `internal` code, so only the log gains detail.\n\nNote the trade-off: a handler error whose message carries task data now reaches\nthe container log, which the previous field list deliberately avoided.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): redact handler failures in the internal task log\n\nfa87a47c logged too much. It added `error: outcome` to the internal-failure log\nso a Chromium launch failure would stop being invisible, but that field carries\nwhatever the handler threw. errore''s `toJSON()` serialises `message`, `stack`,\nand the whole `cause` chain, Playwright quotes `page.evaluate` arguments into\nits messages, and the passport widget passes the secret identity as one — so a\nhandler failure could print the passport series and number into the container\nlog. The wrapper''s own `stack` repeats the cause under `Caused by:`, so the\nidentity landed there twice.\n\nThat contradicts the design spec: raw Playwright, network, Zod, and upstream\nresponse details are logged only after redaction, request and response bodies\nare never logged, and logs redact task payloads and secret values\n(docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md\n:350-353, :684).\n\nThe rule now, verified against dispatch.ts:\n\n- `BrowserExecutorError` wraps a failed `executor.acquire(...)`. Acquire runs\n  before the handler and is passed only `(signal, widgetId)` — never the payload\n  or the widget secrets — so its cause chain is Chromium/Playwright launch\n  detail. Still logged in full; this is the stale singleton lock failure\n  fa87a47c existed to surface.\n- Every other internal failure wraps handler output and is reduced to\n  `errorChain`: the names/tags of the error and its `cause` chain and nothing\n  else. No `message`, no `stack`, no foreign properties.\n\nThe log message, the `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` fields, and the\n`code === ''internal''` condition are unchanged.\n\nTests split the single fa87a47c case in two: one pins that an executor failure''s\ncause chain still reaches the log, one asserts a secret-looking cause string is\nabsent from the serialised logged payload. Both were confirmed to fail before\nthe fix and to discriminate after it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add an env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe passport widget''s recovery path (retain the page, issue a single-use\ncapability, stream the live browser over noVNC, let a human solve the\nchallenge, retry) has never been exercised on real hardware, because it only\ntriggers when pasport.org.ua serves a Cloudflare challenge and none has been\nencountered — the smoke test on the Pi returned a normal result. Subproject 8\n(`passport-checker-rpi-integration` in\ndocs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md)\nhas \"embedded noVNC Cloudflare recovery, SSH fallback, and real checker smoke\ntest on the Raspberry Pi\" as an acceptance item and had no way to reach it on\ndemand. PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY is that way.\n\nContract: `1` and nothing else enables it — absent, empty, `0`, `true`, ` 1 `\nare all off (isForcedRecoveryEnabled does exact string equality, no trimming,\nno truthiness coercion). Compose passes it through with a `:-` default so an\nunset variable stays off, and it must never be set in production.\n\nSemantics: at most once per process. The latch lives in the closure\nmakePassportCheckHandler returns, so each handler owns its own and the unit\ntests stay isolated. It fires after a successful goto and after\ncollectNavigationEvidence — the retained page is the real checker page with the\nsite''s own UI loaded, so a human can drive it over noVNC — and returns exactly\nthe retainPageForRecovery() + BrowserSessionRequiredError pair the genuine\nCloudflare branch returns, making the downstream flow byte-identical to a real\nchallenge. The latch is consumed only when the forced path actually fires, so a\nfailed navigation returns its own error untouched and leaves the lever armed:\nan infrastructure hiccup must not burn the single forced run an operator is\nwaiting on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): apply the design-system widget redesign\n\nBring the tile and the recovery modal in line with Паспорт-чекер.dc.html.\n\nBoth tiers now lay out as header -> flexible middle -> action, so the\naction sits on the tile''s bottom edge in every state instead of trailing\nwhatever content happens to be above it. Status banners pick up the\ndesign''s per-state hues, the filled success disc, and the CircleAlert /\nTriangleAlert split that separates a retryable failure from a session\nprompt. The modal gains the access-pill dot, a pulsing LIVE dot, framed\nconnecting/disconnected/expired states, and the shell-style SSH block.\n\nDark theme was effectively missing: the --pc-* hues chained to\n--success-soft / --destructive-soft, which the host token set never\ndefines, and to --success, which is a dark surface green rather than an\nicon green under [data-theme=''dark'']. Every hue is now declared per\ntheme the way ofelia-poop-duty does it. The modal portals to\ndocument.body, outside .widget, so it declares the one hue it needs on\nits own overlay.\n\nThree deliberate departures from the mockup:\n- invalidConfig copy stays server-side (\"Обратитесь к администратору\"),\n  since this widget has no per-instance settings — its config lives in\n  server secrets.\n- the expired frame keeps \"Переподключиться\"; without it an expired\n  capability can only be recovered by closing and reopening the modal.\n- the sessionRequired tiny tile only fills amber, no border: the tile\n  border belongs to the host card, and drawing one from inside the\n  widget would render a second, inset contour.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xwGNctwqXcYi5p4QSSFhA\n\n* docs(browser-automation): design widget user-input detection\n\nMove the \"a human must act\" mechanism out of passport-checker and into the\nautomation platform. The handler gets context.detectUserInput(detector), which\nruns the detector, optionally prepares the page, retains it and returns the\ncanonical error as one indivisible step; retainPageForRecovery leaves the public\ncontext so the halves cannot be separated. Cloudflare becomes a reusable helper\n(probe and predicate together, not just the predicate) that any widget can pass\nin. Also records the removal of the PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY lever.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): correct the user-input spec''s test-seam claims\n\nmakeFakeExecutor fakes BrowserExecutor<FakeContext> with FakeContext = { id,\nsignal }; it never builds a BrowserTaskContext, so it cannot host a recording\ndetectUserInput and needs no change at all. Name the seams that do exist: the\nsix retainPageForRecovery call sites in chromium-executor.test.ts and the three\nBrowserTaskContext literals in diagnostics.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): plan the widget user-input detection refactor\n\nSeven tasks, each green on its own: retire the forced-recovery lever, add the\ndetector and error contracts, move Cloudflare detection into the platform with\nits probe reunited with its predicate, run escalation from the task context,\nrewire the widget, then withdraw retainPageForRecovery from the public context so\nthe retain/escalate pair cannot be taken apart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): make the Cloudflare task a move, not a copy\n\nTask 3 previously left the classifier duplicated between the widget and the\nplatform until Task 5, which any reviewer would rightly flag as verbatim\nduplication of a logic block. The widget now switches to the moved module in the\nsame task, so the logic never exists in two places.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* revert(passport-checker): drop the env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe lever forced one trip through the Cloudflare recovery path per service\nstart so Subproject 8 could exercise noVNC on the Pi on demand. It is being\nretired deliberately ahead of moving user-input detection into the platform;\nthe Pi smoke test needs another answer.\n\nThis reverts the behaviour added in 45c604f1.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(browser-automation): add user-input detector and error contracts\n\nUserInputRequiredError carries the browser_session_required code and sshTarget\nmeta the recovery flow keys off, moved out of the passport-checker widget where\na platform contract had no business living. UserInputProbeError distinguishes\na broken check from a negative one.\n\n* feat(browser-automation): move Cloudflare detection into the platform\n\nThe classifier lived in the passport-checker widget while the DOM probe feeding\nit lived in that widget''s check handler, so the only reusable half was the half\nwithout the probe. Both now sit in browser-automation behind two detector\nfactories: one that probes the live page, one that classifies evidence already\nderived inside the page from a fetch response.\n\nThe widget imports the moved module rather than keeping a copy; it still calls\nthe classifier by hand, which the next commit changes.\n\n* @\ndocs: document the feature branch -> dev -> main workflow\n\nFeature work happens in a dedicated branch inside a ./.worktrees checkout,\nlands in dev through a PR, gets verified on the dev stack, and only then\nreaches main through a dev -> main PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* feat(browser-automation): run user-input detection from the task context\n\ndetectUserInput runs a widget-supplied detector and, on a match, prepares the\npage, retains it and returns UserInputRequiredError as one step, so the two\nhalves cannot drift apart. A detector that fails yields UserInputProbeError and\ndeliberately leaves the page unretained: an undecidable check is not a challenge,\nand pinning a browser page for a recovery nobody will perform is worse.\n\nAUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET moves into the service config, where an unusable value\ndegrades to null rather than failing config loading and killing the service.\n\n* docs(passport-checker): design last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* test(browser-automation): cover the prepare-before-retain order in detectUserInput directly\n\nThe executor-level test asserted hasRetainedPage() from inside the prepare\nmock, but that bookkeeping is only populated at release() time, so it passed\nregardless of call order. Add a direct unit test against makeDetectUserInput\nthat records prepare/retain into one ordered array — genuinely fails if the\norder in detect.ts is inverted (verified by temporarily inverting it).\n\nNo production behavior changes; detect.ts ends identical to before this commit.\n\n* @\ndocs: merge AGENTS.md into CLAUDE.md and symlink it\n\nAGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md had drifted into two partly overlapping rule sets.\nEverything unique to AGENTS.md (project structure, codegen commands, Windows\ntest-runner notes, coding style, UI gotchas, testing, security, failure modes)\nnow lives in CLAUDE.md, and AGENTS.md becomes a symlink so every agent reads\nthe same file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* docs(passport-checker): plan last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* feat(passport-checker): date-aware check timestamp label\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout design\n\nTwo width breakpoints instead of one hardcoded grid: one column below 768,\ntwelve above, with row height and margin scaled by the same factor as the\ncolumn width so the desktop grid zooms instead of stretching on one axis.\n\nThe mobile layout is derived from the desktop one and only persisted once the\nuser drags or resizes, stored as an additive optional `mobileLayout` field so\nno migration of `root:boards` is needed.\n\nAlso records the react-draggable touchstart defect: the whole card is the drag\nhandle today, which cancels page scrolling on touch — harmless on a short\n12-column board, blocking at one column.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): detect required user input through the platform\n\nThe handler no longer collects Cloudflare evidence, classifies it, retains the\npage and builds the escalation error by hand at every site; it passes a detector\nto context.detectUserInput and returns what comes back. browser/challenge.ts and\nthe widget-local BrowserSessionRequiredError are gone — the classifier now lives\nin browser-automation next to the probe that feeds it, and the\nbrowser_session_required contract belongs to the platform that owns the recovery\nflow.\n\n* fix(passport-checker): restore the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nAn automated amend from a concurrent session accidentally stripped this\nline out of 3a12494e. It belongs to the user-input-detection work; this\nrestores it verbatim.\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nRETRYABLE_MESSAGES.user_input_probe vanished twice with no test turning red,\nbecause nothing asserted the actual behaviour: with the entry gone,\nmapCheckError silently falls back to GENERIC_RETRYABLE_MESSAGE and the user\nsees the generic string instead of the specific one. Assert the real output\nof mapCheckError against a hardcoded expected string, not against the table\nitself, so a future deletion or typo of the entry fails this test instead of\ncomparing the map against itself.\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron design spec\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout implementation plan\n\n* feat(board): resolve grid metrics from the container width\n\n* feat(passport-checker): persist the last successful check\n\n* feat(board): derive and reconcile the one-column mobile layout\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron implementation plan\n\nAlso corrects the spec: the jsdom Temporal shims must stay, only the\n--harmony-temporal execArgv becomes redundant on Node 26.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin which detector goes to which detectUserInput call\n\nNothing previously checked that the navigation check gets the page detector\nand the submission check gets the evidence detector -- only the second call''s\nprepare option was asserted. A handler that swapped the two factories would\nleave every test in this file green; only the gated real-browser suite would\nnotice, and it isn''t run in CI. Pull the captured detector off each mock call\nand invoke it directly: the page detector must reach for page.evaluate, the\nevidence detector must ignore its page argument and classify the real\nevidence. Also drops the now-inert hasChallengeForm evidence field from the\nsubmission-escalation test, whose result was always driven by the mock''s\nescalation queue regardless of that field.\n\n* feat(board): add mobile layout materialize, update and reset actions\n\n* refactor(browser-automation): expose only detectUserInput on the task context\n\nretainPageForRecovery was half of a pair a handler had to assemble correctly by\nhand: retaining without escalating strands a browser page, escalating without\nretaining hands the UI a recovery session with nothing behind it. With the retain\nflag reachable only through detectUserInput, neither mistake is expressible.\n\n* test(passport-checker): cover restore-on-mount and cross-placement sync\n\nAdd two widget-level cases pinning storage-backed persistence through\nReact: a fresh mount renders a previously stored result without\ninvoking a check, and a check made in one placement is observed by a\nsecond placement sharing the same storage. Rename the pre-existing\n\"gives separate instance ids separate state\" case to \"gives separate\nstorages separate state\" (it isolates by storage, not instanceId) and\nupdate its comment to point at the new cross-placement case below it.\nPin PASSPORT_LAST_RESULT_KEY''s literal value in check-model.test.ts so\na silent rename cannot slip through green while orphaning deployed\nusers'' stored data under the old key.\n\n* feat(board): switch the grid to resolved metrics and a mobile drag grip\n\n* docs(browser-automation): document how a widget requests manual input\n\n* feat(board): offer a mobile layout reset in the board schema popover\n\n* fix(widgets): address passport-checker persistence review findings\n\n- TinyTier''s success chip now shows checkedAtLabel, so a restored (possibly\n  stale) result reads honestly in the compact tile too, not just standard.\n- Harden the two-placement isolation test: assert placement two stays idle\n  instead of a length-1 findAllByText that can pass mid-race.\n- Clarify why lastResult/transient set order is safe in checkPassport\n  (Reatom notifies after the sync block, not \"order for readability\").\n- Add a test exercising lastResultSchema.safeParse over the exact shape\n  checkPassport writes, since createFakeStorage skips schema validation.\n- Document lastResultSchema as a persistence contract: new fields must be\n  optional/defaulted or every stored value fails validation on next deploy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* fix(passport-checker): remove dead AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET threading\n\nrecoverySshTarget was never read by check.ts''s handler after the\ndetectUserInput refactor moved sshTarget sourcing entirely into the\nplatform config. Delete the widget''s leftover env read and option\nthreading so the README''s \"widgets neither read nor pass it\" claim is\nactually true, and drop the now-meaningless SSH-sanitization test in\nfavor of the equivalent (and broader) coverage in browser-automation''s\nconfig.test.ts.\n\n* docs(ofelia): design the authored ledger and comments\n\nRecords get the authenticated account instead of the per-device \"Я:\"\ntoggle, the write path moves into the widget''s own server.ts, and the\nhistory row is rebuilt: human dates, plain-language phrases, explicit\ndebt effect, visible supersession, no IP.\n\nPulls the Node 26 prerequisite out of the widget-server-cron spec, since\nthe shared domain module it enables is needed here first.\n\nAlso imports the Claude Design workspace under design/ (documents only,\nscreenshots and uploads stripped) and keeps the brief that produced the\nOfelia mock.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs(ofelia): note the cron plan overlap and landing order\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): cover the mobile layout and touch-scroll behaviour end to end\n\n* fix(board): keep the measured grid container mounted\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a 1920 viewport\n\n* docs(ofelia): add the authored ledger implementation plan\n\n15 tasks: Node 26, the pure domain split, the members directory, widget\nidentity, the server-resolved viewer, record schemas, draft construction,\nthe widget server definition, the model rewire, the history and comment\nview models, the account avatar, and the two rebuilt panels.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): wait for the grid relayout to settle before measuring\n\n* fix(browser-automation): bundle workspace imports instead of externalizing them\n\nThe rspack externals predicate was an allow-list whose default was\n\"externalize\". When the user-input refactor introduced the first runtime\nbare-specifier import of a workspace package into the service module graph\n(browser-automation/user-input/cloudflare, from the passport-checker browser\ntask), it was silently externalized: the build stayed green, all tests stayed\ngreen, and the Cloudflare module was simply absent from dist/index.cjs. The\nruntime stage copies only package.json and dist/, so Node resolved the\nspecifier through the exports map to a .ts path that does not exist in the\nimage and the container died on `exec node dist/index.cjs`.\n\nInvert the predicate into a deny-list derived from the package''s own\ndependencies (minus errore, which is ESM-only and must be bundled) plus Node\nbuiltins. An unrecognised bare specifier is now bundled, so the same mistake\nsurfaces as a build-time resolution error rather than a startup crash, and\nadding a real runtime dependency externalizes it with no config edit.\n\nAdd scripts/check-bundle.ts, chained onto `build` so it runs inside the image\nbuild the Dockerfile already performs. It asserts that every require() in the\nbundle is a builtin or a declared dependency, that the scan still matches the\nemitted shape at all, and that the compiled evidenceFromResponseText the widget\nsplices into Chromium still evaluates and runs in a bare vm realm. That last\ncheck is what the BROWSER_IT suite could never prove: it runs under vitest,\nwhere the specifier resolves to .ts through esbuild and the swc path is never\nexercised. Verified: the compiled form is ES5-lowered but self-contained --\ndestructuring became inline property reads, no swc helpers, no hoisted regexes.\n\nAlso:\n- detect.ts: tag the prepare outcome instead of comparing against null, so a\n  prepare that rejects with exactly null is a failure rather than a silent\n  success, matching the detector branch''s `typeof detected !== ''boolean''`.\n- user-input/errors.test.ts: cover toEnvelopeError for UserInputRequiredError\n  with and without an ssh target, pinning with toStrictEqual that `meta` is\n  omitted rather than set to undefined. contracts.test.ts already claimed this\n  coverage lived here.\n- chromium-executor.test.ts: drop an assertion inside a prepare callback that\n  could not fail the test and was vacuous, and rename the case to what it\n  actually verifies; the ordering contract is owned by detect.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* test(board): settle past the grid transition and stop assuming card order\n\n* test(board): correct the stale comment above the breakpoint wait\n\n* chore(node): align every runtime and image on Node 26\n\nnode:26-alpine and node:26-bookworm-slim no longer bundle corepack\n(removed from Node core), so `npm install -g corepack@latest` is added\nahead of every `corepack enable` in the three Dockerfiles and\ndocker-compose.dev.yml. scripts/infra.test.ts''s assertion on the\nbrowser-automation Dockerfile''s base image is updated to match.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(node): unshadow jsdom localStorage and bump the e2e image to Node 26\n\nNode 26 ships its own unflagged global localStorage/sessionStorage (Web\nStorage API), which resolves to undefined without --localstorage-file\nand shadows jsdom''s window.localStorage for bare-identifier reads in\npackages/client''s jsdom test environment (40 failing tests). Disable\nNode''s own globals via execArgv: [''--no-experimental-webstorage''] in\npackages/client/vite.config.ts so jsdom''s storage wins; verified via\nObject.getOwnPropertyDescriptor that the flag actually removes the\nglobal rather than just suppressing the warning. widget-runtime and\nwidget-sdk don''t touch localStorage in tests today, so the flag is not\nadded to their execArgv arrays (narrowest correct remedy).\n\nAlso bumps packages/client/e2e.Dockerfile (used by pnpm test:e2e:docker\nvia docker-compose.e2e.yml) from node:22-bookworm-slim to\nnode:26-bookworm-slim, missed by the original brief''s Files list, with\nthe same corepack@latest install this Node 26 base image now needs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): make the relayout wait actually poll\n\n* refactor(ofelia): extract the pure duty domain out of the Reatom model\n\n* fix(lint): make the widget domain import barrier a real allowlist\n\nThe regex denylist let ''../model/ofelia-duty'' through — domain/ and model/\nare siblings, so a single-''../'' import was both the most likely accidental\nescape and completely unguarded. It also only enumerated known packages, so\nany new npm dependency passed silently.\n\nReplace it with a gitignore-style group allowlist (''**'' minus zod, @shared/*\nand ''./'' siblings), which oxlint evaluates natively. A negative-lookahead\nregex was tried first and rejected: oxlint''s Rust regex engine has no\nlookaround and silently drops such a pattern, disabling the whole barrier.\n\nDomain tests get a second, later override that additionally allows vitest;\neverything else stays blocked there too.\n\n* chore(lint): keep the design mock exports out of lint and format\n\ndesign/ holds vendored Claude Design exports (support.js, *.dc.html)\nthat break the repo''s only gate: oxlint reports 9 errors in\ndesign/support.js, and oxfmt aborts (exit 2) on non-standard markup\nin design/Design System.dc.html before it can check anything else.\n\nAdd design/** to the existing ignorePatterns glob array in both\n.oxlintrc.json and .oxfmtrc.json, the same mechanism already used for\ndocs/**. This is a plain glob matcher (not the Rust regex engine\nbehind no-restricted-imports), so it isn''t subject to that engine''s\nlookaround gap, and it leaves every other rule/override untouched.\n\nFixing the oxfmt abort also uncovered pre-existing, unrelated\nformatting drift in CLAUDE.md, package.json and\nscripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts that the crash had been masking from\nthe format:check summary. Reformatted them (whitespace/ordering only,\nno logic changes) so pnpm format:check is actually green end to end.\n\n* feat(auth): expose the board members directory\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): expose the board identity to widgets\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): stop identity fetch from leaking an unhandled rejection and a discarded error\n\nHandle the AbortError that wrap()''s promise rejects with when the last\nwidget reading identity unmounts mid-fetch, and stop discarding\nBoardMembersError whole: a 401 stays silent (expected on an\nunauthenticated host), everything else is surfaced via console.warn so\nschema drift or a server fault is not silently swallowed.\n\n* feat(widgets): resolve the signed-in viewer for widget server events\n\n* feat(ofelia): carry the authoring account on ledger and comment records\n\n* feat(ofelia): move draft construction and event schemas into the domain\n\n* feat(ofelia): write the ledger and comments from the widget server\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): harden server date parsing and unforgeability coverage\n\n- Wrap Temporal.PlainDate.from in errore.try so a calendar-invalid but\n  regex-valid date (e.g. 2026-02-30) returns an Error instead of\n  throwing through a function typed Error | T.\n- Add regression coverage: forged createdBy/actor in the payload is\n  ignored, the debt handler is exercised, storage-read failures are\n  asserted by identity with no write, and the invalid-date case is\n  asserted as a value.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): split the unforgeability guard into schema and handler halves\n\nThe round-1 forged-payload test went through run(), which parses the\npayload via zod''s default strip mode before the handler ever sees it —\nso it only proved the schema strips forged fields, twice over, and\nwould not catch a handler that starts spreading its raw payload into\nthe draft. Replace it with two schema-level tests (clean + comment\npayloads drop a forged createdBy/actor) and one handler-level test\nthat calls ofeliaServer.handlers.clean directly, bypassing the schema,\nto prove the handler itself never reads those fields even if they\nreach it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* feat(ofelia): drive ledger and comment writes through widget server events\n\nThe client no longer appends to the ledger or the comment key. All four\nday actions collapse into one `invokeDay` that sends `{ date }` to the\nmatching widget-server event, and `send` sends `{ weekStart, text }` —\nwho the actor is, whose debt moves, whether the event applies at all and\nwhich account signed it are now derived and stamped server-side, so a\nclient cannot forge authorship.\n\n`selectedDate()` and `today()` (and `viewWeekStart()`) are read before\nthe first await so they resolve against the widget''s own Reatom context.\n\nThe per-device \"Я: Леша/Карина\" toggle is deleted rather than hidden:\nauthorship comes from the session, so there is nothing left to pick.\n`currentUser` is gone from the duty model, the widget context, the\nfixture and the view-model''s `onSetUser`.\n\nBoth model factories now take `api` and `identity`; `identity` is the\ndirectory the history and comment views read in the follow-up tasks.\nThe view projections drop `ip`/`by`, whose schema fields Tasks 5-8\nremoved, and `authorName` falls back through `createdBy.name`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* style: format the ofelia server test\n\n* feat(ofelia): group history by duty day and mark superseded records\n\n* feat(ofelia): resolve comment authors through the members directory\n\n* fix(ofelia): flush the comment thread''s connect hook via vi.waitFor, not a bare microtask\n\nReview round 1: the timing fix for the comment-thread subscribe/emit race\nassumed a single-microtask flush of Reatom''s connect-hook queue. Match the\nsibling comments-subscription tests in the same file instead — wait for the\nstorage mock''s subscribe call and wrap the whole body in context.start, so\nthe assertion is condition-based rather than scheduling-internals-based.\n\n* feat(ofelia): add the account avatar and its palette\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the history list around duty-day groups\n\n* fix(ofelia): announce the history phrase subject to assistive tech\n\nThe duty circle is the sentence subject in the rebuilt history phrase, and\nAvatar is aria-hidden, so the column announced as bare verbs with no subject.\nPair every circle with a visually hidden name, attribute the debt pill to its\nperson, and separate the chips with real spaces as the mock does.\n\nAlso correct the text-decoration comment to name the real mechanism (atomic\ninline, not descendant cancellation) and cover the three untested branches.\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the comment thread around account authors\n\n* fix(ofelia): restore composer coverage and mute the unknown comment author\n\n* fix(ofelia): break ledger day ties by append order\n\nRouting the writes through the widget server changed where `ts` comes from.\n`/api/storage/:key/append` stamped `Date.now()`, so consecutive appends always\ngot a strictly increasing timestamp. The widget dispatch stamps the app''s\ninjectable `now()`, and the e2e harness pins that clock — so a clean-then-undo\npair on the same day produced two entries with an identical `ts`, and\n`latestOutcomesByDate`''s strict `>` kept the earlier `cleaned` entry as the\nday''s outcome. The `reset` was written but never counted, and the day never\nreopened; `pnpm test:e2e:docker` caught it as a failing \"undo — returns the day\nto pending\".\n\nThe ledger is append-only and iterated in insertion order, so `>=` is the\ncorrect tie-break: on an equal timestamp the later-appended entry is the newer\ndecision. This also hardens production, where two writes can still land in the\nsame millisecond.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: describe server-authored widget records\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): fail widget dispatch when the session account is unreadable\n\nresolveWidgetViewer folded every getAccount failure into `null`, so a Valkey\nfault, a corrupt account record and a deleted account all produced a record\nstamped `createdBy: null` plus a `{ ok: true }` response. The ledger is\nappend-only, so that authorship could never be recovered.\n\nThe three outcomes are now distinct: no session still returns `null`, which is\nwhat the standalone harnesses and the e2e stack (no nginx, so no session cookie)\nrely on to keep writing unattributed records; a failed lookup returns a\nWidgetViewerLookupError, which the dispatch route turns into a fault instead of\na false success.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): order superseded history records by append order\n\n`superseded` sorted on `right.ts - left.ts`, which returns 0 on a tie; the\nstable sort then left tied records in append order, oldest first, contradicting\nthe \"newest first\" contract on HistoryDayGroup. Ties became a supported case\nwhen latestOutcomesByDate started breaking them with `>=`, and the dockerized\ne2e stack freezes the clock, so there every record ties.\n\nThe ledger is append-only, so append order is chronological order: reversing the\nday''s bucket is both correct and cheaper than sorting.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): warn when listAccounts skips an unreadable record\n\nA corrupt or schema-mismatched account record was dropped silently, so the\nroster simply came back short and the symptom read as \"an account vanished\"\nrather than as the data corruption it is. Name the key in a console.warn.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* build: pin corepack to 0.35.0 across images and dev compose\n\n`npm install -g corepack@latest` ran in five image build stages and four dev\ncompose services. There is no CI and the Pi deploy is manual with a 30-minute\nbuild timeout, so a breaking corepack release would only ever surface as a\nfailed deploy. Pin one explicit version everywhere.\n\nVerified: `docker build -f packages/server/Dockerfile` succeeds and the built\nimage reports corepack 0.35.0 with pnpm 10.28.2.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* refactor(ofelia): delete the dead short-date formatter\n\nformatDateShort and MONTHS_SHORT lost their last production consumer when the\ncomment thread moved to its own formatter; only the test still called them. It\nalso formatted through `new Date(ts)` in the local timezone, while every current\nformatter goes through plainDateIn(DUTY_TIME_ZONE, ...).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): drop the tier guards that cannot fail, hoist the env directive\n\nStandardTier and CompactTier each asserted they do not render UserToggle. The\ntoggle only ever lived in RichLayout and the component is now deleted outright,\nso neither assertion could ever fail — they only looked like coverage.\n\nAlso move the `@vitest-environment node` directive in server.test.ts back to\nline 1, where a reader expects it and where model/history-view.test.ts keeps it.\nVitest 4 matches the directive anywhere in the file content, so the file was\nalready running under node; this is readability, not a behaviour fix.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: state the authorship guarantee the code actually provides\n\nCLAUDE.md claimed a server-stamped author \"cannot be forged\". Nothing enforces\nthat: POST /api/storage/:key/append does no session check in Node, and nginx''s\nauth_request only proves a session exists, not whose it is, so any signed-in\nhousehold member can still hand-write a record carrying an arbitrary author.\n\nState what is true instead — the UI can no longer sign a record with someone\nelse''s name, and the generic storage route remains a shared trusted channel\ninside the board. Closing that route is a separate task.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(board): never render the desktop grid below its 1920 baseline\n\nThe desktop zoom is now one-directional: raise the lower clamp on the\ngrid scale from 0.75 to 1, so every container width between the mobile\nbreakpoint and BASE_WIDTH resolves to exactly the historical metrics\n(rowHeight: 30, margin: [10, 10]) and only wider displays scale up.\n\nUntil this branch, useContainerWidth never attached its observer and\npinned the reported width at its 1280 default on every screen. With the\ncontainer measured for real, a 0.75 floor would have shrunk every board\non a 1280/1366/1440 laptop below the size it has always had, dropping an\nh:6 card from 230px to 172.5px and pushing widgets under their own tier\nthresholds. Nothing shrinks now; the 4K aspect drift the scale was added\nfor is still fixed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling record\n\nBring the design doc in line with what shipped: the lower clamp is 1,\nthe 1280 table row shows the baseline metrics, and the paragraph\njustifying the clamp says plainly that it is active across the whole\n768-1920 band rather than a narrow 768-1100 one.\n\nAlso correct the claim that existing 1080p boards \"do not shift by a\npixel\". The formula at 1920 does reproduce the old constants, but the\nmeasured width was previously pinned at 1280 on every screen by the\nuseContainerWidth bug this branch fixes, so wider screens legitimately\nre-flow to use the whole window.\n\nDrop the 1920 viewport pin from ofelia-duty.spec.ts: it only worked\naround the 0.75 clamp, and removing it restores default-viewport\ncoverage. Recompute the footprint arithmetic in the ofelia client test\ncomment, which assumed a container of exactly 1280 rather than the\nviewport minus the board padding and the scrollbar gutter.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a viewport its tier needs\n\nEvery assertion in the duty spec is StandardTier UI, and the widget only\nresolves `standard` at a frame width of 400px or more. Playwright''s\ndefault 1280 viewport is about 7px short: the board measures its own\ncontainer, which is 1225px there once the stable scrollbar gutter and the\nboard''s 20px padding on each side come off, so a w:4 card is ~395px and\nits frame ~393px. `compact` is the correct rendering at that width, so\nthe spec has to declare the width its assertions need.\n\nThe dependency was previously invisible because the board reported a\nhardcoded 1280px container on every screen, which made the card 413px and\nleft the threshold only 13px of headroom against a measurement that was\nwrong by 55px.\n\nCorrect the footprint comment in the widget''s client test to match: the\ntier is measured on the frame inside the card''s 1px border, so a 1280px\nviewport yields ~393x228 and resolves to `compact`, not `standard`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling claims to match what ships\n\nThree statements asserted the opposite of what this branch established,\nall written before the width axis was measured.\n\nThe grid-metrics JSDoc claimed the clamp keeps 1280/1366/1440 boards\n\"the size they have always been\" and widgets \"above their own tier\nthresholds\". Both are false at 1280: the container is 1225 there, so a\nw:4 card is 395px where the old code drew 413px, and ofelia falls under\nits 400px standard threshold into compact. The clamp pins the metrics -\nrowHeight 30 and margin [10, 10] at every desktop width - not the\nrendered geometry, which still follows the measured container.\n\nThe design doc claimed \"no board shrinks and no widget loses a tier\".\nThe 1366 and 1440 frames (422px, 446px) clear the widget threshold; the\n1280 frame (393px) does not. Corrected to draw the metrics/geometry\ndistinction the document already draws two paragraphs earlier.\n\nThe \"do not shift by a pixel\" claim survived in the doc''s Testing\nsection and in the test''s own comment. At 1080p the container is now\n1865, so colWidth is 144.58 rather than the 95.83 the pinned-1280 bug\nproduced. Both now state what the test guarantees: the formula at\nBASE_WIDTH reproduces the previous hardcoded constants.\n\nAlso record the shipped consequence in Risks - ofelia renders\nCompactTier below ~1300px, correct behaviour on a genuinely 393px frame,\nwith recalibration left as a follow-up - and fix three figures: the tier\nparagraph attributed the frame inset to a card padding that does not\nexist (it is a 1px border; the heights are 148px and 298px, not 110 and\n260), the e2e headroom was 11px on the frame rather than 13px on the\ncard, the 1920 card/frame are 608/606, and useContainerWidth is at\nBoard.tsx:28.\n\nComments and prose only; no executable line changed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* build(deploy): add a per-branch rpi environment and deploy scripts\n\nrpi.branch.toml is a disposable stack for trying the checked-out branch on\nreal hardware, with ttl = \"72h\" so a forgotten one reaps itself. It takes the\nbranch through ${BRANCH_NAME}, the only variable rpi 0.26 accepts — hence the\nliteral hostname rather than a derived one.\n\nscripts/deploy-branch.ts reads the branch from git so nobody retypes it, and\nrefuses to deploy without .env.branch, which would otherwise produce a stack\nwhose WebAuthn gate fails with nothing in the deploy log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(deploy): send the branch secrets bundle before the build\n\nEvery branch is a fresh rpi project with an empty secrets store, so the very\nfirst deploy of any branch died after the ~3 minute image build with an opaque\nCompose \"secret file ... does not exist\". deploy:branch now checks\n`rpi secrets ls` and sends the bundle itself when there is none.\n\nAlso strip the `--` that `pnpm run x -- --flag` forwards verbatim: rpi''s parser\nreads a bare `--` as end-of-flags, so the flag after it was rejected as a\npositional.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(browser-automation): read the spliced callback when swc stops inlining it\n\ncheck-bundle extracted the page callback by slicing between `.concat(` and\n`.toString()`, which is the whole function expression only while swc inlines it.\nA second consumer of evidenceFromResponseText — precisely what the public\nbrowser-automation/user-input/cloudflare subpath exists to allow — makes swc emit\na named declaration instead, leaving a bare identifier at the splice site. The\nguard then evaluated that identifier in an empty realm, got a ReferenceError, and\nfailed the image build accusing a perfectly correct bundle of shipping a\nnon-self-contained callback.\n\nResolve the identifier to its declaration, and let the JavaScript parser find\nwhere that declaration ends: counting braces by hand would have to understand\nregex literals, and this body is full of regexes containing quote characters.\nExtraction failures now say so instead of reporting a self-containment defect.\n\nVerified by building all four ways: inlined and named shapes both pass; the\noriginal externals regression and a regex hoisted out of the callback are both\nstill caught.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* fix(ofelia): centre the initial in avatars re-cast to inline-flex\n\nBoth avatars centre their initial with `place-items: center`, but the\nhistory phrase re-casts the box to `inline-flex` so the circle stays in\nthe inline run, and a flex container ignores `justify-items`. The glyph\nwas left-aligned: in an 18px circle it sat 0px from the inline start and\n12.97px from the end, roughly a third of the diameter off centre.\n\nName the inline axis outright so the centring survives whichever display\na caller imposes. `.debt > [data-tone]` re-casts the same way and was hit\nby the same bug.\n\n* fix(ofelia): reserve room for the viewer ring in scrolling columns\n\nThe signed-in viewer''s avatar carries a 3px ring drawn as a spread\nshadow, so it paints outside the border box. Every avatar sits flush\nagainst the inline start of a container that clips: `.historyCol` and\n`.list` scroll (and `overflow-y: auto` clips the inline axis too), and\n`.commentsCol` is `overflow: hidden`. The ring lost its whole left edge —\nmeasured at exactly 3.0px — leaving a flat-sided avatar in the history,\nthe comment list and the composer, in both themes and on mobile.\n\nWiden each clip box outward by the ring width with a padding/negative\nmargin pair, which moves no content, and hold the width in\n`--ofelia-viewer-ring` so the shadow and the reserved room cannot drift.\n\n* fix(ofelia): size the account initial like the duty initial\n\n`MemberAvatar` scaled its initial to half the box while `Avatar` uses\n0.4, and the history phrase renders both at `px={18}` on the same line —\n9px next to 7.2px, which reads as two mismatched type sizes rather than\none set.\n\nShare the ratio through `AVATAR_INITIAL_RATIO`. Taking `Avatar`''s value\nkeeps every pre-existing avatar (the 62px hero, the 26px week strip)\npixel-identical and moves only the account squares this branch added.\n\n* fix(ofelia): raise the standard tier to the height it actually needs\n\nStandardTier lays out to 298px of content, but `tiers.standard` asked for\nonly 200px and the widget card clips instead of scrolling. At the default\nplacement (413x230px) the tier therefore resolved and then cut its own\naction row in half: \"Уборка подтверждена\" was sliced through and \"В долг\"\n/ \"Простить\" never appeared at all.\n\nState the height the tier needs, so anything shorter degrades to\n`compact` rather than rendering truncated, and raise `defaultSize` to\n`{ w: 4, h: 8 }` (~413x310px) so a freshly-placed widget still clears it\n— the same fix shape as the earlier default-placement regression.\n\n* fix(ofelia): let the pinned day header cover the reserved ring strip\n\nThe history column reserves `--ofelia-viewer-ring` of inline-start room\nso the viewer ring is not clipped, and records paint into that strip. The\nsticky day header''s own box stops at the content edge, so the strip stayed\nuncovered: scrolling avatars slid past the pinned header through a 3px\ngap, measured against the column''s border box.\n\nStretch the header back over the strip with a negative inline-start margin\nand return the width as padding, so its background spans the full\nscrollport while nothing inside it moves.\n\n* feat(ofelia): scroll the history to the day picked in the week strip\n\nSelecting a day highlighted it in the strip and changed the panel, but\nleft the history where it was, so the records for that day had to be found\nby hand.\n\n`HistoryList` now keeps a node per duty day and scrolls the matching group\nto the top of its scrollport when the selection changes. Exactly one\nancestor scrolls in each layout — the column on desktop, the dialog body\non mobile — so the native `scrollIntoView` cannot drag two scrollports at\nonce. The first run only records the mounted day: scrolling on mount would\npull the panel above the history off screen in the mobile layout before\nthe reader touched anything.\n\nThe column subscribes to a newly exposed `view.selectedIso` rather than\n`view.selected`, so confirming or forgiving the selected day still does\nnot re-render the history.\n\n* fix(ofelia): scroll only a box the reader can scroll back\n\n`scrollIntoView` moves *every* scroll container in the ancestor chain, and\n`overflow: hidden` boxes are scroll containers too — they just have no\nscrollbar. The widget frame is one: measured at 149px of overflow in the\nmobile layout, `overflow-y: hidden`. Switching a day therefore displaced\nthe whole widget, and with no scrollbar there the reader could not put it\nback.\n\nWalk up to the innermost box that is genuinely user-scrollable\n(`auto`/`scroll` *and* overflowing) and scroll that one by hand, aligning\nthe group with the scrollport top where the sticky header pins. When\nnothing in the chain scrolls there is nothing to reveal, so the layout is\nleft alone rather than nudged.\n\n* fix(ofelia): keep the mobile tab pane from clipping the viewer ring\n\nBounding the tab pane needs `overflow: hidden`, which clips the inline axis\ntoo — `overflow-x` cannot stay `visible` once the other axis is not. So in\nthe mobile layout `.split` becomes a second clipper, outside the columns\nand at the same inline start, and it shaved off the 3px viewer ring that\nthe columns had already reserved room for. Measured at `cutL: 3` on the\ncomment and record avatars; on desktop `.split` is `overflow: visible`, so\nthis only ever showed on narrow screens.\n\nReach outward by the ring width there too, the same padding/negative-margin\npair the columns use.\n\n* docs(cron): rebase the cron spec and plan onto dev\n\ndev landed Node 26, the widget domain/ boundary and Ofelia''s server.ts with\naccount authorship, so those tasks are gone. Authorship for cron records is now\na system arm on the persisted createdBy union rather than a `by: ''system''`\nfield, and the plan drops from 12 tasks to 8.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay back at dev\n\nThe overlay was temporarily aimed at this feature branch (5208c5c5) to test it on\nthe Pi before the `branch` environment existed. rpi.branch.toml now covers that\ncase per branch, so dev goes back to deploying dev — which is also what\nscripts/infra.test.ts asserts, and that test passes again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(widgets): add the cron job contract to widget server definitions\n\n* feat(server): validate widget cron schedules when the registry is built\n\n* feat(server): persist a per-job cron cursor in Valkey\n\n* feat(server): add the widget cron tick scheduler\n\n* fix(server): drain the cron scheduler on stop and pin the no-double-fire invariant\n\ninFlight was replaced on every interval fire instead of chained, so a tick\nthat outlived intervalMs was silently orphaned and stop() could return\nwhile it was still running. Chain onto the existing promise instead.\n\nAlso add a dueOccurrence test pinning that an occurrence already recorded\nin the cursor never re-fires.\n\n* style(server): rewrap the cron registry validation to the formatter''s width\n\n* fix(client): stop an edge cache from serving a stale host federation entry\n\n/remoteEntry.js is the one release file whose URL never changes while its\ncontents do: it names the hashed chunk that calls createInstance. Cached by URL,\nan intermediary keeps serving the previous release''s copy, whose chunk 404s in\nthe new release. The host then never initialises and every loadRemote fails with\n\"#RUNTIME-009 Please call createInstance first\" — a board that renders its shell\nand not one widget.\n\nObserved today on the branch stack: Cloudflare answered /remoteEntry.js with\ncf-cache-status HIT, age 6503, under its default max-age=14400, from a build\nnearly three hours older than the deployed one, while /index.html was DYNAMIC\nand the hashed assets were current. It survived a browser site-data clear, a\ncontainer recreate, a builder-cache prune and a cold rebuild, because none of\nthose touch the edge.\n\nWidget entries already carried no-cache for this exact reason; the host''s own\nentry never did. Give it the same header and pin both halves in the nginx smoke\ntest: the header, and that the chunk the entry names is one this release ships.\n\nThis is not specific to the throwaway branch host — production serves the same\nfile under the same TTL, so any release that changes the board bundle could\nshow an empty board for up to four hours.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(server): start the cron scheduler with the app and expose a test tick\n\n* feat(ofelia): give server-written records a system author\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the harness and tests the required widget identity\n\nThe ofelia authored-ledger work made `identity` a required field of\nWidgetRuntimeProps; the passport-checker widget was written before that\nfield existed. Both landed on dev without a textual conflict, so the\nsemantic conflict only surfaced as four TS2741 errors — all in a dev\nharness and three test call sites, none of them part of the production\nbuild, which is why `pnpm build` kept passing.\n\nFollow the convention the widgets updated in that PR already use: the\nharness takes the live `runtime.identity`, the tests take the\n`makeStaticWidgetIdentity()` double. passport-checker never reads the\nroster, so the empty default keeps every assertion unchanged, and the\nstatic double issues no request — which matters in recovery-flow.test,\nwhere fetch is stubbed with a never-resolving promise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* feat(ofelia): auto-approve unresolved duty days on a nightly cron\n\n* test(e2e): cover the auto-approve cron reaching an open board\n\n* test(server): cover the cron interval path and a retried partial run\n\nClose the three test-coverage gaps from the final whole-branch review:\nexercise makeCronScheduler''s start()/stop() interval path (the only\nproduction entry point, previously untested) with fake timers, cover a\nretry after a partially failed ledger append end to end in the ofelia\ncron suite, and assert append was never called in the widget''s cron\nread-error test to match its manual-path siblings.\n\n* fix(server): fence the generic storage routes off the auth keyspace\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`GET /api/storage?prefix=` passed an unvalidated prefix straight to `scanKeys`, and\n`session:<id>` embeds the session cookie''s own value in the key name, so any\nsigned-in device could enumerate live session tokens and then act as anyone —\nvoiding the server-stamped authorship this release is built on. PUT/DELETE were\nequally unbounded, which also left the new `cron:` cursor client-writable.\n\nRestrict all five generic storage routes to `root:`/`w:i:`/`w:t:`, rejecting a\npartial prefix (including the empty one) so enumeration cannot walk in sideways.\n\nAlongside: re-stamp `ip: ''''` on append as a one-release shim for pre-release\nreaders whose schema requires the field, log every `status >= 500` with its cause\nchain instead of only 500, and cover the dispatch route''s session→viewer wiring,\nwhich had no test at all — replacing it with a literal null left the suite green.\n\n* fix(ofelia): let the nightly auto-close actually discharge a debt\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`getDebtDays` scans forward from its origin, so every day it yields is >= today,\nwhile the cron only ever asks about days 1..7 in the past — `debtDayFor` was\nstructurally unreachable on that path. The auto-close therefore credited the\nrotation duty person instead of the debtor, omitted `onBehalfOf`, and `foldDebt`\nnever decremented: the debt walked forward to the next open day, forever, one\nmis-attributed record per night.\n\nAnchor the scan at `min(target, today)` behind an explicit `allowPastDebtDays`\nthat only `autoApproveDrafts` sets, so the interactive handlers stay anchored at\n`today` and keep matching what the week strip showed the user.\n\nAlongside: keep writing `by`/`author` for one release (pre-release readers require\nthem and their `z.array` fails wholesale on one missing field), drop unparseable\nelements instead of rejecting the whole array, skip the run when the ledger key is\nabsent so unused stacks stop accruing fabricated history, and re-read each day''s\nresolution before appending so a manual close landing mid-run is not overwritten.\n\n* fix(ofelia): surface failures the widget used to swallow\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\nEvery duty action captured its error into an atom nothing read, so a failed tap\nlooked identical to a successful one — and with the nightly cron now closing\nunresolved days, a silently failed «В долг» becomes a system-signed `cleaned`\nrecord at 00:05. A failed comment send additionally discarded the typed text and\nraised an unhandled rejection.\n\nTrack the last failure in write order rather than merging four per-action atoms in\na fixed order (the first non-null shadowed the rest forever), render it in Russian\nrather than leaking `WidgetApiError`''s internal message, and keep the composer''s\ntext on failure.\n\nAlongside: show a retry instead of an endless skeleton when the ledger read fails,\nstop attributing the previous week''s comments to the selected one, keep\nscroll-to-day inside the history column instead of scrolling the whole card, and\nstop the account avatar announcing a name the adjacent text already carries.\n\n* fix(board): migrate stale widget heights once, not on every read\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`tiers.standard.minHeightPx` rose 200→300 and `defaultSize.h` 6→8, but nothing\nmigrated the `h` already persisted, so every placed ofelia card would render\nCompactTier after deploy and lose its week strip and debt chips.\n\nRun the bump once, recorded in a `root:`-scoped marker key a pre-release client\ncannot strip on write-back — clamping on every read instead would have made every\nwidget unshrinkable below its package default, since `minH` sits well under it.\n\nAlongside: preserve unknown keys on the shared board snapshot, so a client that\ndoes not know a field can no longer delete it when it writes the board back.\n\n* fix(client): stop the edge from serving a stale service worker\n\nThis release already gave `/remoteEntry.js` a `no-cache` header after Cloudflare\nwas caught serving a three-hour-old copy of it. `/sw.js` has the identical\nproperty — a stable URL whose contents change every release — and fell through to\nthe catch-all block with no Cache-Control at all, so an installed PWA kept getting\nthe previous release''s worker from the edge for up to four hours and never\ninstalled the new one. That is also what would have made the ofelia record-shape\nincompatibility outlast the deploy by hours rather than a reload.\n\nGive the worker its own location alongside the federation entries, and pin it in\nthe nginx smoke spec the same way the host entry is pinned.\n\nAlongside: raise `proxy_read_timeout` for widget dispatch above the automation\nbudget — the generic `/api/` block inherited nginx''s 60s default, which would have\ncut every long passport check before its own deadline could fire — and re-point\nthe two gate probes at allowlisted keys so they keep isolating the auth and CSRF\ngates instead of tripping the new storage allowlist.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): let the members directory recover from a failed first fetch\n\nThe roster was fetched once from a connect hook that fires only on the 0→1\nsubscriber edge. On the board the identity atoms stay connected for the life of\nthe page, so a transient failure at first paint — routine on the Pi, where the\nservice worker serves the shell while the server container is still starting —\npinned the roster at null for the whole session: no viewer avatar, no viewer ring,\nuntil someone reloaded. An account created later never appeared either.\n\nRetry with backoff on a transport failure, leave a 401 alone, and refresh so a\nlater-created account arrives without a reload. Every await carries its own\nrejection handler: the abort that `wrap` raises on teardown was otherwise an\nunhandled rejection on each disconnect.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add a delete control, and stop leaking recovery sessions\n\nThe board already hands every widget a `requestDelete` callback and clock and\nofelia both use it; passport-checker was the only widget with no way to remove it\nfrom the board. Wire it through the shared `WidgetControls`, in both tiers and in\nthe dev harness.\n\nAlongside, three fixes to the recovery and check paths:\n\n`teardown()` disposed the session but never invalidated an in-flight `start()`, so\nclosing the modal during the ~1s issue request still built the noVNC RFB against a\ndetached node. The recovery slot is a single global, so that orphan answered every\nlater attempt with 409 until its TTL expired.\n\nThe 25s client deadline sat far below the pipeline''s own budget (30s queue + 60s\nexecution, 100s at the board server), so on the Pi a normal check timed out in the\nUI while the invoke resolved later with a real result that nobody read — the\nstatus could never be persisted while the upstream site was slow. Raise it past\nthe server budget, keep a late success instead of dropping it, and ignore a\nstraggler from a superseded attempt so it cannot overwrite a newer result.\n\nThe SSH fallback hardcoded noVNC''s port 6080 after compose made it per-stack, so\nan operator recovering the dev stack tunnelled into production''s live Chromium —\npossibly mid check, with a real passport number on screen. It now uses the port\nthe stack actually published.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* fix(browser-automation): report the stack''s own noVNC host port\n\nThe widget renders an `ssh -L` command so an operator can take over a stuck\nbrowser session, and it reads the port from the rejection''s meta. Nothing ever\nput a port there: `UserInputRequiredError.publicMeta` carried only `sshTarget`,\nso the widget always fell back to 6080 — production''s published port — and an\noperator on dev or branch was told to tunnel into the wrong stack.\n\nThread `NOVNC_HOST_PORT` from the same compose variable the publish line uses\ninto the service config and out through `publicMeta`, so both ends of that\ncontract now agree and each is pinned by a test.\n\nAlso set `AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET` in the dev and branch env templates: it was\nunset there, so the fallback the operator is meant to reach did not render at\nall on exactly the stacks that collide.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(backlog): record the findings this branch deliberately did not fix\n\nThe PR #27 review surfaced nine issues that were judged not worth acting on now:\nsome are contested (one review lens refuted them), some are the release''s own\ndocumented known gaps, and some are pre-existing shapes this branch only made\nvisible. They were about to be lost with the review transcript.\n\nEach entry states what exists today, why it is tolerable, the shape a fix would\ntake, and what would trigger doing the work — matching the two entries already\nhere. The facts in every entry were re-verified against the code rather than\ncopied from the review.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(rpi): move the env configs onto rpi 0.27 groups and ${git.branch}\n\nDeliver secrets through named groups instead of one bundle per environment:\n`prod` for production, `dev` for the passport-checker files the dev stack and\nthe branch stand share, `branch` for the stand''s own .env.branch. Both overlays\nclear [secrets].files, so nothing carries a second copy, and [secrets].env in\neach file is now documented as the local push source it has always been.\n\nResolve the branch stand''s source.branch through ${git.branch} and drop the\nBRANCH_NAME variable everywhere. Since 0.27 the environment key only gets a\nper-branch suffix when the configuration references ${env.slug}, so the stand is\none shared key by design; say so where the comments claimed the opposite.\n\nDelete scripts/deploy-branch.ts. It existed to push .env.branch before a deploy\ncould come up unconfigured, which a missing key bundle does silently. A declared\ngroup that is missing or empty instead fails the deploy at the layer-load step,\nnaming the group and the push that fixes it, so `rpi deploy --env branch` needs\nno wrapper.\n\nLock the layout with infra tests and refresh the docs that still said\n`rpi secrets send` and `--vars BRANCH_NAME=...`.\n\nVerified: rpi config show for base/dev/branch, oxfmt --check, oxlint and\ntsc --noEmit. The new infra assertions were run through an equivalent script —\nroot Vitest cannot start in this checkout, where rolldown fails to resolve any\nconfig entry, including untouched ones.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(rpi): drop the prod secret group and the dead PASSPORT_* variables\n\nProduction would be the only project attaching a `prod` group, and a keyless\npush always writes this file''s env + files into the deploy key''s own bundle,\nwhich is the last layer — so the group could only ever be an identical shadowed\ncopy, and a rotation pushed to it alone would silently never reach production.\nKeep the group mechanism where it earns its place: `dev`, shared by the dev\nstack and the branch stand.\n\nPASSPORT_SERIES/PASSPORT_NUMBER are gone from .env (untracked, so only its\ncomment header records this): nothing in docker-compose.yml reads them, the\nwidget takes those values from the file-backed secrets, and they were riding\ninto every stack''s containers through both groups.\n\nAgent state after this change: groups `dev` (0 keys, 2 files) and `branch`\n(7 keys, 0 files); production''s bundle 0 keys + 2 files; dev''s 7 keys + 0 files,\nwith the passport files finally resolving `<- dev` instead of a stale `<- key`\ncopy that was overriding the group.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: spec the unified widget card controls\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: plan the unified widget card controls\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: require codegen in the plan''s worktree setup\n\n* feat(widget-sdk): make WidgetControls the one widget chrome component\n\n* fix(widget-host): stop hiding card controls where there is no hover\n\n* test(client): assert the touch controls on the element that carries the style\n\n* docs: add the e2e typecheck the workspace gate does not cover\n\n* refactor(clock): take the card chrome from useWidgetChrome\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): take the delete control from useWidgetChrome\n\n* refactor(ofelia): drop OfeliaActionControls for the shared WidgetControls\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): mount the tiny tier chrome once\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): narrow the chrome policy and guard the tiny-tier switch\n\nWhole-branch code review fix wave:\n\n- useWidgetChrome now allows expand+delete only on mode === ''small'' and\n  falls through to close-only otherwise, instead of denying only on\n  ''large''. Equivalent today (WidgetMode is ''small'' | ''large''), but a\n  future third mode now defaults to no card-management chrome rather\n  than silently gaining full expand+delete.\n- TinyTier''s switch gains a `default: view satisfies never` branch.\n  ReactNode already includes undefined, so the prior bare `let body:\n  ReactNode` never tripped TS''s used-before-assignment check on a\n  missing case; the plan''s claim that it did was wrong and is corrected.\n- widget-interactions.spec.ts asserts the desktop half of the\n  hover-visibility rule (opacity 0 -> 1 on the overlay controls\n  container), which mobile-board.spec.ts cannot reach since it forces\n  hasTouch: true.\n- Corrects the spec/plan claim that a standalone dev/ harness shows\n  widget controls: every harness hardcodes mode: ''large'', so\n  useWidgetChrome() returns only onClose there and no controls render.\n\n* feat(widget-host): let a loading card name itself and be deleted\n\nA card whose widget is still loading showed an anonymous shimmer and no\ncontrols, so a component chunk that never resolved left a tile that could\nnot be removed. It now draws the widget''s icon and title — both known\nsynchronously from the codegen catalog before the remote lands — with the\nshared WidgetControls alongside them.\n\nThe controls are placement=\"inline\", not the board card''s usual overlay:\nan overlay is hover-revealed, and hover-hiding the only useful control on\na stuck card is the trap being fixed. Inline already means always-visible,\nso no new visibility rule was needed.\n\nAlso fixes a guard that never fired: the frame''s own onDelete is a\nuseEvent handle and so always truthy, which made the error card''s\n`{onDelete && ...}` unconditional. Both it and the error boundary now read\nthe raw prop.\n\n* fix(e2e): give the auto-approve cron test a ledger to close\n\nThe job returns early on an empty ledger by design — a stack where nobody\never used the widget must not grow a fabricated `cleaned` record a night\n(server.ts, F11). That guard landed in b1fffc0e, six hours after this test\nwas written in ecdd1cd0, and the test was never updated: it resets the\ndatabase, places the widget, touches nothing, and then expects the cron to\nclose a day. The ledger was empty, so the job no-opped and the plaque never\narrived.\n\nSeed one settled earlier day, the way the neighbouring forgive test already\ndoes. 2026-06-15 is a genuine Карина duty day and the entry closes it, so\nthe run skips it and auto-approves 2026-06-16 — the day the card shows.\n\nVerified: pnpm test:e2e:docker now reports 27/27, against 26/27 both on\nthis branch and on origin/dev before the change.\n\n* chore(lint): stop formatting markdown with oxfmt\n\nHand-written tables and wrapped prose in CLAUDE.md and the agent guides read\nbetter than oxfmt''s column padding, which also left the table separator row\nwider than the rows around it. docs/** and design/** were already ignored, so\nthis only extends the same treatment to the root markdown and the skill files.\n\nThe oxlint entry is symmetry: oxlint does not lint markdown today, but the two\nignore lists are maintained together.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* docs(agents): split CLAUDE.md into on-demand guides\n\nCLAUDE.md is read in full at the start of every session, and it had grown to\n~3.4k words. Move the parts that are only needed some of the time next to the\ncode they describe, and leave an index behind:\n\n- docs/agent/{deployment,test-troubleshooting,widget-server,radix-stacked-dialogs}.md,\n  each fronted by a thin .claude/skills wrapper so Claude Code loads it on its\n  own trigger, while Codex and the rest follow the table in CLAUDE.md\n- docs/architecture/overview.md for the descriptive architecture prose\n- the storage-key contract, including the incident that proves it, now lives in\n  the header comment of packages/shared/storage/scope.ts, so it reaches whoever\n  is actually editing the derivation\n\nAlso drop the generic rpi CLI mechanics already covered by the rpi:rpi-cli and\nrpi:rpi-toml skills, and fix what had drifted: pi.toml -> rpi.toml, the\nscopeWithColon normalization lives in host-runtime.ts and not widget-api.ts,\nbrowser-automation is no longer \"the future browser service\", the command list\nmatches package.json, and the hardcoded per-machine skill paths are gone.\n\nCLAUDE.md: 3368 -> 1382 words.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* fix(client): replace the deprecated apple PWA capability meta\n\nChrome flags <meta name=\"apple-mobile-web-app-capable\"> as deprecated and\nasks for the standard mobile-web-app-capable instead. Keeping both would\nkeep the warning, and standalone mode no longer depends on the apple tag:\nthe VitePWA manifest already declares display: standalone, which Safari\nhonours since iOS 16.4.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* fix(passport-checker): load noVNC lazily so the widget can mount\n\n@novnc/novnc@1.7.0 ends core/util/browser.js in a module-scope top-level\nawait: it builds a real VideoDecoder, configures it for 1920x1080\navc1.42401f, decodes a frame and awaits decoder.flush(). That probe\nresolves in milliseconds on desktop Chrome and never settles at all on\nsome devices, and a top-level await that never settles parks every\nimporter in \"evaluating\" forever.\n\nui/PassportChecker.tsx imported model/rfb.ts statically for the prod RFB\nadapter, so the chain was mount-time: import(''./ui/PassportChecker'') never\nresolved, React.lazy never got a payload, and the board rendered this\nwidget''s loading card forever. Nothing was thrown, so no error boundary\nfired and the card was indistinguishable from a slow load.\n\nReach the adapter through model/load-rfb.ts''s dynamic import instead. The\nrecovery model now takes loadRfb and awaits it alongside transport.issue:\nthe store supersedes an unused capability on the next issue() and only\ncounts a live socket as busy, so running them in parallel costs nothing\nand keeps the modal''s time to first frame unchanged. A viewer that fails\nto load lands in the new viewerUnavailable state with a reconnect button\nrather than a silent spinner.\n\nVerified on the built output: avc1.42401f/EncodedVideoChunk now appear\nonly in rfb-*.js, and the widget chunk''s single reference to it is a\ndynamic import(). ui/passport-checker-novnc-graph.test.ts guards the\nchain — it goes red if the static import comes back.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(agent): record the machine and deploy failures that lived only in memory\n\nThe CLAUDE.md split moved detail into docs/agent/* but left ten machine- and\ndeploy-specific failure modes recorded nowhere in the repository. Each of them\nreads as a regression you just caused, and each has cost multiple sessions.\n\ntest-troubleshooting.md gains the worktree symlink failure, the rotten\nnode_modules in the main checkout, pnpm check''s kill-others-on-fail behaviour,\nthe docker e2e run that exits 0 without starting, and the stale vite preview\nthat serves another checkout''s build. It also corrects the Vitest filter: the\ndocumented `--filter client test -- <file>` does not scope, `exec vitest run`\ndoes.\n\ndeployment.md gains the three caches that sit between a build and what runs -\nthe service worker and IndexedDB that survive a redeploy on the shared branch\nhostname, the edge cache on URL-stable files like /remoteEntry.js and /sw.js,\nand the aborted deploy that strands a container on a dead network.\n\n* docs: stop CLAUDE.md repeating itself, and give it a worktree command that works\n\nTesting and Failure modes said nothing the file had not already said. The gate,\nthe PR rules, the commit style and the test locations were each stated twice;\nof the five failure modes, four restated the feature workflow or the global\nrules and the fifth is now in docs/agent/test-troubleshooting.md.\n\nThe worktree command in step 1 has never worked on this machine: AGENTS.md is a\ncommitted symlink Windows cannot materialize, so the documented command fails\noutright. It now carries both required git flags.\n\n* docs(backlog): record three follow-ups the shipped releases left behind\n\nOfelia''s tier thresholds now sit within 10px of their own frames, so any chrome\nadded to the card silently drops it to compact and takes five e2e tests with it.\nThe z.array record schemas mean one new field hangs every still-open client, and\nboth shipped releases papered over it with a compat shim. And an ofelia comment\nfrom an account outside DUTY_ROTATION loses its author on pre-release clients.\n\nAll three were only ever recorded in agent memory, which is not where a\nfollow-up survives.\n\n* docs(context): commit the session review snapshots\n\nThey have been untracked for three sessions running, so each review has had\nnothing to diff against.\n\n* docs: log the PWA update path in the backlog\n\nregisterType: ''autoUpdate'' does reload the page on its own, but the same\nbranch of vite-plugin-pwa''s register.js never calls onNeedRefresh and\nmakes updateServiceWorker() a no-op — so needRefreshAtom, applyUpdate and\nUpdateBanner are dead code, and an already-open client only re-checks once\nan hour. Records what exists, why it is tolerable, and what would trigger\nthe work.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: drop the typescript 7 migration benchmark report\n\nThe migration is done and the numbers have served their purpose. The\ngenerator, scripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts, stays — the report and its\nbenchmarks.json are reproducible from it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(context): add the session-4 startup capture\n\nFollows 3d327e45, which committed the earlier snapshots.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: record the passport-checker loading-skeleton handoff\n\nRoot cause, the fix on feat/widget-controls, what is verified and what is\nnot, plus the device console probes and the list of dead ends not worth\nredoing. Delete it once that branch is released.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: spec the fullscreen close control and back-gesture dismissal\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* docs: plan the fullscreen close control and back-gesture dismissal\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the tiny tier a way out of a restored success\n\nA card narrower than the widget''s 321px `standard` threshold renders\nTinyTier, and its `success` state drew a status chip and no action at all —\nthe only state in that tier without one. That was survivable while the\nwidget always started in `idle`. Once it began restoring the last\nsuccessful check on mount, a narrow card came up in `success` and stayed\nthere: passport-checker deliberately offers no expand affordance, so the\nonly button left on the card was \"Удалить\". Seen on the branch stand at\n314px, where 4 of 12 columns miss the threshold by 7px.\n\nThe chip is now the action rather than gaining one below it, so the state\ncosts no extra height and still fits the smallest tile. It keeps the\nstatus and timestamp it already carried, and its accessible name repeats\nthat text after the action rather than replacing it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* chore(docs): keep only agent and architecture guides in git\n\ndocs/context/ session logs and docs/superpowers/ plans, specs and\nartifacts churn every session and had nothing durable to diff against;\nuntrack them and ignore docs/* except docs/agent/ and docs/architecture/,\nthe canonical checked-in references.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(docs): ignore docs/* except agent and architecture\n\nFollow-up to the previous commit — the .gitignore rule itself was left\nunstaged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* doc: add .playwright-mcp to .gitignore\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): mirror open overlays onto browser history\n\n* chore(docs): drop this branch''s plan and spec from git\n\ndev now keeps only the agent and architecture guides in the repository and\nignores the rest of docs/ (0a17cdaf, 96b2c041). This branch predates that\nrule and still carried its own plan and design spec, which the merge left\ntracked because dev never had those two paths to delete. Untracked here;\nboth files stay on disk, now covered by the ignore rule.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* feat(widget-sdk): add the overlay back-dismiss hook\n\n* test(passport-checker): widen the noVNC graph test''s budget\n\nThe test''s only await is a cold import of the widget''s whole mount graph in\na fresh module registry, which overran the default 5s budget under\n`pnpm check` — five workspace gates running concurrently. It failed there\ntwice and passed every isolated run. Nothing in it waits on behaviour, so\n20s cannot mask a regression: a static noVNC import still fails the\nassertion, not the timer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* test(widget-sdk): add StrictMode and fallback tests for overlay back-dismiss hook\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): coalesce a dropped overlay''s traversal with the push behind it\n\nA history traversal is asynchronous. Measured in real Chromium,\nhistory.go(-1) lands 25-40ms later and a pushState issued in the same task\ndoes not cancel it — jsdom does, which is why the unit tests never caught\nthis. So dropOverlay''s eager go() fired after the following push, rolled the\nbrowser back past the new entry and delivered a spontaneous popstate at the\npre-overlay depth, which reconciled as \"close the overlay that opened 30ms\nago\". Both live call paths hit it: StrictMode''s mount -> cleanup -> mount,\nand any commit that closes one overlay and opens another.\n\ndropOverlay now records the depth history must land on and schedules the\ntraversal on the next task. A pushOverlay arriving first reuses one of the\nowed entries — depth taken from the debt rather than stack.length + 1, so it\ncannot collide with an overlay still waiting above a mid-stack drop — and\nthe flush is cancelled once the debt is spent. A drop with nothing behind it\ntraverses exactly as before.\n\nThe debt is a target depth, not a step count: two drops in one task each\nname the entry they want and the lower wins, where summing their steps\ntraversed past the pre-overlay entry and out of the application. Confirmed\nin Chromium against the module at HEAD, which left the probe page on\nabout:blank after one back press.\n\nThe new tests drive real navigation instead of dispatching a hand-built\nPopStateEvent, since a faked popstate cannot tell one registered entry from\ntwo that happen to carry the same depth.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* chore(widget-sdk): clear the pre-existing lint and type errors in the overlay tests\n\nBoth predate this branch''s overlay work and were failing `pnpm lint` and\n`pnpm typecheck` at HEAD: an unused `e2` binding in overlay-history.test.ts,\nand a `let capturedDismiss` that control-flow analysis narrows to `never`\nbecause it never sees the assignment made inside the component.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): derive a pushed overlay''s depth from history, not the stack\n\n`stack.length + 1` is only correct while depth counts entries one for one. It\nstops doing so in the duplicate-depth mode a mid-stack drop can leave behind,\nand then the number it hands out skips one: from [C(1), E(2), B(2)] the next\npush is stamped 4 on an entry history places directly after the one stamped 2.\nflushOwedTraversal computes `readDepth() - target`, which counts entries, so a\nlater drop of E issues go(-3) where two entries separate it from its target and\ntakes C down with it.\n\n`pushState` inserts its entry immediately after the current one, so the depth is\n`readDepth() + 1` by construction. Identical whenever history and the stack\nagree, which is every ordinary case.\n\nAlso drops the `replaceState` from the coalescing branch — that branch runs only\nwhen the claimed depth equals the current one, so the spread rewrote identical\nstate. Confirmed in Chromium: the four-scenario probe is byte-identical with and\nwithout it. The claim path now writes nothing to history at all, which is what\nthe comments (here and on the hook) now say.\n\nThe two residuals move from the report into the module doc, where the tasks that\nwill wire real overlays to it can see them: two overlays mounted in the same\ncommit as a middle drop, and dropOverlay racing an eager dismissOverlay in one\ntask.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* feat(client): add the app-env registry, palette derivation and OKLCH conversion\n\n* feat(client): close the topmost dialog on the platform back gesture\n\n* feat(client): generate and commit per-environment app icons\n\n* test(client): pin the Escape close to history.back, not just the outcome\n\nwaitFor(() => expandedInstanceId() === null) alone cannot distinguish\n\"routed through requestDismiss/history\" from \"closed onOpenChange(false)\ndirectly\" -- both reach the same null. Spy on history.back and assert it\nwas called synchronously right after the Escape keydown, so a future\nchange that bypasses requestDismiss fails this test instead of staying\ngreen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* feat(client): brand the document title, tokens and icons per environment at build time\n\n* feat(client): show the environment badge in the board header\n\n* build: deliver the rpi environment name into the client image build\n\n* fix(client): serve the environment icons publicly so the login page is branded\n\n* feat(client): close the topmost popover on the platform back gesture\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* test(client): pin the popover Escape close to history.back, not just the outcome\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(client): pin the environment-branding wiring with tests and share the accent constants\n\nGuard the two things nothing in the gate actually exercised: the Header rendering\nEnvBadge, and both Vite configs registering appEnvBranding. Both were verified to\nfail when the guarded code is deleted.\n\nAlso: export ACCENT_L/ACCENT_C from registry.ts so the fixed accent lightness and\nchroma stop being copied across tokens.ts, generate-env-icons.ts and\nicons-lock.test.ts; derive generate-env-icons.ts''s PRODUCTION_FILL from them\ninstead of a fourth hardcoded #645fd1; give the env badge pill flex: none so it\ncan''t be squeezed by BoardSelect; and tighten a handful of comments/test anchors\n(dev-only activation branding limitation, a VITE_APP_ENV override caveat, a hook\ntier name, a looser Dockerfile layer-order assertion, three deployment.md claims).\n\n* feat(clock): give the fullscreen clock a close control\n\n* feat(passport-checker): give the fullscreen widget a close control\n\n* feat(passport-checker): dismiss the recovery modal on the back gesture\n\n* test(passport-checker): assert the async close mechanism, not just its effect\n\nThe three tests asserting a synchronous close on Escape/backdrop pointerdown\nwent stale the moment the recovery modal''s close paths started routing\nthrough history.back(), which is async in jsdom (and ~25-40ms later in real\nChromium). Update them to pin the mechanism (spy on history.back and assert\nit fired) rather than only awaiting the eventual state flip, and to wait for\nour own modal to actually close before checking the underlying Radix dialog\nsurvived, so the survivor assertion cannot pass on a race that hasn''t landed\nyet.\n\n* test: reset overlay-history between tests in the shared widget and client setups\n\nRecoveryModal.tsx''s useOverlayBackDismiss(true, close) now runs on every\nmount, so any test that mounts a Dialog/Popover root or RecoveryModal\ntouches overlay-history.ts''s file-scoped singletons (the entry stack, the\npopstate listener, a pending owed-traversal timer). recovery-flow.test.tsx\ngot its own beforeEach for this; RecoveryModal.test.tsx and\nrecovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx did not, and neither did any client test\nthat mounts a Dialog/Popover (AddWidgetMenu.test.tsx,\nBoardSchemaSelect.test.tsx, etc.) — a gap that let one test''s still-open\noverlay leak state into the next.\n\nReset once per package instead of scattering beforeEach hooks: widget-sdk''s\nshared `./test-setup` (used by every widget package via\ndefineWidgetVitestConfig) and the client package''s own vitest.setup.ts.\nBoth guard the reset to jsdom, since @vitest-environment node files in\nthese packages have no window/history at all.\n\nAdded a proof test per setup file with no reset of its own, so the shared\nhook''s effect is directly observable rather than inferred from a green\nsuite: one test leaks an overlay entry on purpose, the next asserts the\nsingleton started clean.\n\n* test(e2e): cover the fullscreen close control and the back gesture\n\n* fix(client): degrade the env badge to a dot, then hide it, on narrow viewports\n\n.brand has no overflow clipping, so once the header runs out of room it\nslides its content under .actions instead of clipping it. Give the badge\nthe same breakpoint ladder BoardSchemaSelect already uses: at <=640px\n(where the select goes full-width) drop the label text and shrink to a\ndot-only circle; at <=430px (where the select collapses to an icon) hide\nit entirely. The label now lives in its own span so CSS can hide it\nindependently of the dot, while the pill''s own aria-label keeps the\nenvironment announced in the dot-only state.\n\n* fix(client): collapse the theme toggle to one cycling button on narrow viewports\n\nThe three-item ToggleGroup is 102px; below 768px (MOBILE_BREAKPOINT, right\nat the measured collision onset) that alone accounts for a big share of\nthe header overflow. Render both controls and let CSS pick one via a\nmedia query, same approach the rest of the header''s responsiveness uses.\nThe cycle order (light -> dark -> system -> light) moves into the model\nas cycleThemeMode next to themeMode, since the order is domain knowledge,\nnot view glue; the view''s OPTIONS list is now derived from it instead of\nrepeating it. The view-transition DOM interop (--vt-x/--vt-y, startViewTransition)\nis factored into one applyWithViewTransition helper shared by both controls.\n\nThe cycling button''s accessible name (\"Сменить тему\") is deliberately\ngeneric and distinct from the ToggleGroupItem radio names, since jsdom\nrenders both controls at once (media queries don''t evaluate there) and a\ncolliding name would break the existing getByRole(''radio'', ...) tests.\n\nAdds an e2e spec covering the group/button swap at two viewports, the\nonly layer where the media query is real.\n\n* fix(client): let the board select fill the remaining header row, not the whole row\n\n.trigger (or .skeleton, while loading) is BoardSchemaSelect''s own top-level\nelement -- it has no wrapper -- so it''s a direct flex item of .brand\nalongside the logo and EnvBadge. Its <=640px width: 100% therefore resolved\nagainst .brand''s own content box, claiming the entire row for itself as if\nthose siblings weren''t there and pushing their content under .actions.\nflex: 1 1 auto fills only the space actually left after the siblings,\nwhich is what this rule always meant to do.\n\n(.root, the class that width: 100% previously also applied to at this\nbreakpoint, is never applied to any element in BoardSchemaSelect.tsx --\ndead CSS, not the flex item in question. Left untouched, out of scope.)\n\nVerified with a 900->320px real-Chromium sweep: the header now has zero\noverlap at every width down to 320px, closing the residual 545-435px band\nleft after the EnvBadge/ThemeToggle responsive work.\n\n* fix(client): let the board select shrink instead of overflowing the header row\n\nAbove 640px .trigger/.skeleton were width: 220px -- rigid. When the header\nrow ran short (the deployed stands render ''dev'' and ''branch'' in the env\nbadge; ''branch'' is the longest label the dev server never builds locally),\na rigid flex item has nowhere to give, so the surplus went past .actions.\n.triggerText already has overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis, so the\nselect was built to shrink -- nothing let it.\n\nSwitched to width: 220px + flex-shrink: 1 + min-width: 0. A bare\nflex-basis (flex: 0 1 220px) was tried first and doesn''t compose the same\nway: .brand''s own width is auto (content-based), and Chrome''s content-based\nsizing pass for an ancestor like that measures a flex-basis-only child by\nits intrinsic content size, not its flex-basis -- it silently discarded\nthe 220px preference and left the trigger permanently shrunk to ~123px\neven at 1280px with room to spare. An explicit width is what a\ncontent-based ancestor actually measures, so it''s kept alongside\nflex-shrink instead of being replaced by it.\n\nVerified this composes with the two media queries below it, unchanged:\n<=640px (flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0, from d4c7c1af) and <=430px (fixed\n36px icon) -- selectW is exactly 220 at 1280/900 and exactly 36 at every\nwidth <=430, for all three non-production env-badge labels (''local'',\n''dev'', ''branch'').\n\n* docs(widget-runtime): correct overlay-history and MyDevicesDialog comments\n\noverlay-history.ts''s module header claimed every close path routes through\ndismissOverlay and a traversal, but a widget''s fullscreen close control and\nAddWidgetMenu''s close button drop the overlay directly (open set to false,\nunmount) without ever calling dismissOverlay. Rewrite the header to name the\ntwo legitimate shapes -- dismiss and drop -- and clarify that the\n\"only popstate calls close\" invariant is about close specifically, not about\nevery close path routing through history.\n\nDocument two more constraints on callers in dropOverlay''s doc block,\nalongside the two already recorded: a push landing while a flush''s own\nhistory.go is still in flight reads a stale depth (unreachable today, but\nundocumented), and a dismiss cannot be vetoed by its caller once history has\nalready forwarded it to reconcile.\n\nMyDevicesDialog''s belt-and-braces setTimeout comment described a same-tick\nrace with Radix''s deferred pointerDownOutside check. Since the overlay-history\nwork, onOpenChange(false) no longer runs inside the click -- it arrives from\npopstate tens of milliseconds later -- so the described mechanism is stale,\neven though the guard it protects still holds (more strongly, in fact).\nCorrect the explanation without changing the guard.\n\nNo behavior change; comments and doc blocks only.\n\n* docs(agent): preserve the browser history traversal measurement\n\n.superpowers/ is git-ignored and gets deleted once this branch''s work\nfinishes, so the real-Chromium measurement behind overlay-history.ts''s\ndeferred-traversal design (browser-history-probe.md) would otherwise be lost\nentirely, leaving only the numbers already quoted in the module header with\nno reproducible method behind them.\n\nCapture the question, method, measured numbers, and the jsdom/Chromium\ndivergence in docs/agent/browser-history-traversal.md, written as durable\nguidance rather than a session log.\n\nPoint at it from CLAUDE.md. This worktree branched before the CLAUDE.md\nguide-table refactor landed on dev, so there is no existing table to add a\nrow to here; add a new one-row \"Related guides\" table in the same format,\nto be reconciled with dev''s table on the next merge.\n\n* docs: fold the history-traversal guide into the main guide table\n\nThe dev merge brought in the guide table this branch predated, leaving two\ntables side by side. One table, one row.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(client): announce the compact theme button''s state and tighten the header tests\n\nThe compact theme button''s fixed aria-label (\"Сменить тему\") left the\ncurrent mode reaching a screen-reader user only through `title` (not\nreliably announced, least of all by touch screen readers on a <=768px\nviewport) -- a regression against the tri-state group it replaces, which\nannounces the active mode through the radio''s name plus aria-pressed.\nThe accessible name now composes the current mode in: `Тема: ${label}.\nСменить`. It names the state and the action, and can''t drift from\ncycleThemeMode since it describes the current mode, not the next one.\ntitle is kept for the hover affordance. Updated the two vitest queries and\nthe e2e HeaderPage locator that pinned the old fixed name to match on the\nconstant \"Сменить\" suffix instead (regex), since the name now changes\nwith every click.\n\nEnvBadge.test.tsx''s \"label in its own element\" guard couldn''t actually\nfail: getByText matches an element''s direct text-node children and\nreturns the closest such element, so reverting the label back to bare\ntext inside .badge would make getByText(''dev'') resolve to the pill\nitself -- still a <span>, still !== the dot, so both assertions passed on\nold and new markup alike. Rewrote it to compare against the outer pill\n(screen.getByRole(''status'')) instead, the one element getByText can only\nresolve to on the old markup. Verified by temporarily reverting the label\nto bare text: the rewritten test fails on it, passes once restored.\nFolded the now-redundant \"announces via aria-label\" test into the\nexisting \"labels itself for assistive technology\" test, which already\ncovered the same aria-label via a role query.\n\nDeleted BoardSchemaSelect.module.css''s dead .root class entirely (base\nrule and the <=430px block) instead of leaving two more live-looking\nreferences to a class the file''s own comment already called unused.\n\n@media (max-width: 768px) on the compact theme button disagreed by one\nwith the MOBILE_BREAKPOINT constant it''s commented as pairing with\n(grid-metrics.ts treats width < 768 as mobile, i.e. 768 itself is\ndesktop). Changed to max-width: 767px so both agree. Verified first: a\n1280->320px sweep (transitions disabled, 300ms settle) for all three\nnon-production env-badge labels (''local'', ''dev'', ''branch'') shows zero\noverlap at every width including exactly 768px, where the three-item\ngroup is still visible under the new threshold, so 767px doesn''t\nreintroduce the overlap 768px was chosen to avoid.\n\n* test(client): expect one Zakryt control in the fullscreen dialog, not zero\n\nClock now draws its own fullscreen close button (feat(clock): give the\nfullscreen clock a close control), so the \"widget can be expanded without\nduplicate fullscreen or close controls\" e2e test and the FullscreenOverlay\nheader comment -- both inherited unchanged from dev -- were asserting and\ndocumenting the old, no-longer-true premise that Clock draws none. Update\nthe assertion to expect exactly one such button (the widget''s own) and\ncorrect the comment''s example, while keeping the real guarantee both were\nprotecting: no *duplicate* chrome stacked on top of the widget''s.\n\n* chore(docs): drop this branch''s plan and spec from git\n\ndocs/* became local-only except the agent and architecture guides. These two\npredate that change on this branch; the files stay on disk.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* build: add a compose overlay for the local nginx e2e stack\n\ndocker-compose.yml defaults the WebAuthn triple to production\n(board.iiskelo.com, https) while playwright.nginx.config.ts drives\nhttp://localhost:8080. A stack started without overrides fails every passkey\njourney inside navigator.credentials.create(), surfacing only as the generic\nclient-side \"сбой процедуры регистрации\" -- register/options returns 200 and\nno verify call ever follows. README told readers to park the overrides in\n.env, which is unavailable here: rpi.toml uses that same file as its\n[secrets].env bundle, so localhost values would ride into production on the\nnext `rpi secrets push`.\n\nThe overlay carries those three plus ALLOW_TEST_DB_RESET, so\n`pnpm start:docker:nginx-e2e` is a single command that cannot be run half-way.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* fix(e2e): pin the gate logout journey to the logout response and navigation\n\n`page.waitForURL(''/'')` synchronized nothing: the board already IS at ''/'', so\nit resolved in the same tick as the logout click -- measured at 3 ms after it\nand ~20 ms before the model''s own navigation landed. Both halves of the\nresulting race were observed: the session probe reading the pre-logout 200,\nand page.reload() overtaking the navigation, which then re-entered the\nstill-controlling service worker and re-rendered the precached board shell\n(title \"myboard\" instead of \"myboard — активация\").\n\naccount.logout runs POST /api/auth/logout -> purge -> hard navigate, so arming\na waitForResponse and a framenavigated waiter before the click pins the whole\nsequence down.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* fix(server): skip the create-invite entry test when its bundle is missing\n\nThe test guarded only one of its two preconditions. Valkey reachability was\nchecked; the built dist/scripts/create-invite.cjs it executes was not, even\nthough dist/ is gitignored and nothing in `pnpm test` builds it. So the test\nwas green only while nothing listened on 6379, and turned `pnpm check` red\nwith a MODULE_NOT_FOUND the moment any stack was up -- in a file the diff\nnever touched. `pnpm start:docker` publishes 6379 and `pnpm test:e2e:nginx`\nrequires that stack, so running the gate after the e2e suite hit it every time.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* **fix(passport-checker): unify series/number secrets and validate identity via Zod schema**\n\n- Combined series and number secrets into a single `number` field, maintaining format consistency.\n- Introduced strict validation for passport identity using a Zod schema for improved error handling.\n- Adjusted regular expressions to parse series/number from a single input field.\n- Updated browser and test implementations to reflect merged secret logic and schema integration.\n\n* fix(rpi.toml): remove series secrets path from configuration\n\n* fix(docker): remove unused `series` secrets from compose files\n\n* fix: formatting\n\n* fix(passport-checker): align combined secret contracts\n\n* docs: design dual passport checker results\n\n* feat(passport-checker): define dual document results\n\n* feat(passport-checker): check both documents sequentially\n\n* test(passport-checker): cover dual browser submissions\n\n* feat(passport-checker): persist independent document results\n\n* fix(passport-checker): defer partial writes until hydration\n\n* feat(passport-checker): render dual document results\n\n* fix(passport-checker): resolve final review findings\n\n* fix(passport-checker): close final rereview findings\n\n* fix(passport-checker): resolve final release blockers\n\n* fix(passport-checker): support mixed-version PWA rollout\n\n* fix(passport-checker): preserve concurrent document results\n\n* docs: remove passport checker design spec\n\n* fix(passport-checker): use compact layout for short cards\n\n* chore: add dev:docker:browser command\n\n* feat(passport-checker): redesign tiny status card\n\n* feat(passport-checker): redesign tiny, standard and fullscreen per Claude Design (#37)\n\n* docs: replace design/ with the new Claude Design export\n\nFull myboard redesign export superseding the previous one; the\npassport-checker mockup (design/06) now specs distinct tiny/standard/\nfullscreen layouts instead of fullscreen being an enlarged standard.\n\n* feat(passport-checker): redesign the tiny tier per design/06\n\nPer-document icon chips (ID card + passport, each with its own\nsuccess/error badge) replace the single rolled-up status label, so a\npartial result (\"1 из 2\") is visible without opening the widget.\nLayout moves from a header row to a centered column, matching the\nClaude Design mockup; widget chrome switches from inline to the\nshared overlay placement.\n\nStandard and fullscreen are unchanged — fullscreen still needs a\ncheck-history/schedule data-model decision before its mockup (embedded\nrecovery, \"Журнал последних проверок\") can be implemented.\n\n* feat(passport-checker): give fullscreen its own layout per design/06\n\nFullscreen stops being an enlarged Standard card and becomes its own\ntier: both documents render full-detail side by side (StatusBanner in\na bigger frame, no truncation), a static \"what is checked and why\"\npanel explains the widget, and sessionRequired recovery embeds inline\n(noVNC canvas + SSH fallback + countdown) instead of collapsing\nfullscreen to reopen the tile''s portal modal.\n\nThat last part reworks recovery-flow.ts: `recoverySurface` (modal |\ninline) replaces the old fromFullscreen/collapse/restorePending dance,\nsince fullscreen no longer needs to close itself to show recovery —\nthe tile and the fullscreen mount of one instance can be alive at the\nsame time, so PassportChecker.tsx gates the portal modal on `tier !==\n''fullscreen''` in addition to the surface, or both would try to render\nit.\n\nStandard gets one content addition to match: GlobalStatusBanner''s\nsessionRequired banner now explains *why* (\"Автоматика уткнулась в\nкапчу/вход\"), not just that a login is needed.\n\nLeft out of fullscreen on purpose, vs. the mockup: the \"Журнал\nпоследних проверок\" (check history) and \"Расписание\" (schedule)\npanels, and invalidConfig''s \"чего не хватает\" field list — none of\nthat data exists in check-model.ts today (only the latest result per\ndocument is stored, there''s no schedule concept), and fabricating it\nwould be worse than not showing it.\n\ndev/harness.tsx''s `?tier=` now accepts any tier, not just `tiny`, so\nevery layout can be previewed standalone.\n\n* fix: passport-checker browser-automation stuck on a stale X display lock (#39)\n\n* fix(browser-automation): clear the stale X99 lock before Xvfb starts\n\n`docker restart`/`restart: unless-stopped` reuse this container''s writable\nlayer, so a lock Xvfb left behind from a previous crash survives into the\nnext boot. Xvfb then refuses to rebind display :99 (\"Server is already\nactive for display 99\"), Chromium has nowhere to launch, and every\npassport-checker browser task fails until someone recreates the container\nfrom scratch. Observed in production: the lock had been stale for four\ndays and survived several in-place restarts, silently failing every\npassport check while the container reported healthy.\n\n* fix: restore the dev overlay''s cleared passport secrets file list\n\nrpi.dev.toml''s [secrets] table declared groups but not files, so per the\noverlay merge rules (absent array field = inherit from base, not clear)\n`rpi secrets push --env dev` silently inherited production''s\npassport-checker secrets/number path instead of relying solely on the\n`dev` group, contradicting the file''s own comment. rpi.branch.toml already\nhad the explicit `files = []`; restate it in the dev overlay too.\n\n* test(client): update passport-checker layout floor tests to w3/h3\n\nd1c7e870 deliberately shrank passport-checker''s defaultSize from\n{w:4,h:4,minW:4,minH:4} to {w:3,h:3,minW:3,minH:3} but left these tests\nasserting the old floor. board-model.test.ts and mobile-layout.test.ts\nread the floor from the live widget catalog, so only the expected values\nwere stale, not the migration logic itself.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqcwsrCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAdcUQAE7oQvr8lElIVRYIJAP5H4wf\nws8IuLAgzC9QrDs3HV1Mz65MGsfsmFoKJg1f/tEvmLF0tzSO/bmvSaEoCk9tO3Yb\npPB4mQNwRdSb2ZdfL87HSZUmhBi7hzKp+thlbXJEpdn5V9p3GfNegEy08yMOKtsI\nmY2i1w8sP1j5/bBu9EDyToR+TnaVy/TfxNYy3u0O2vPLt2IrqLsXpPvXqNKfoY84\nyZgpTF4dQAuZ8gsI0nNGdoGm8I8zzNV/6Rrw2vWWuVwFowLKD6xDqDkitBExOfoF\nXukMjNad2H472vQFv1AklS1gMdmM74JJOwaAV/C/JQKiZ5e76/4FO+3wW5AnqLCa\nx/OnXErCuNL7q321SWRtEZ/84C1y0JJnJJZWFIMLBhQdFvk+VyK8P4qAJJpiE9j2\nv4TTDnDg+JUHi/ySmfgRshDrbKzGsxGvcwSoNDdUp0L9FOrwRm+bCpQ+Lp2bJPXS\nAYRAa1g8ve61uA4P++RRbRPYTjnFm/uGo6CvbSNJADF2EfM+KgIm2v6yD7Bc/Xm4\nJ4fm4sOeEWAEEehPkqt//ffq/XRKj9a0xjamwdmr1zwweSRLh1M2iwjTZh7nipJD\nkjoAIiydnVZbcLSOQbu6/EY2XYMybczeaj+VuuL6B7HuDr4cD27CzSkjrOnxJYv1\nNdn/Ewcrq6zOHquX1NWC\n=n2t9\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-05T10:08:13Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi/myboard/commit/1156dd175867d11a66a824e789f9d7848320a7f8","node_id":"C_kwDOS9EJdtoAKDExNTZkZDE3NTg2N2QxMWE2NmE4MjRlNzg5ZjlkNzg0ODMyMGE3Zjg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi/myboard/commit/0cfc5f1dbfd9533ac227db0453a6a82971a9072e","sha":"0cfc5f1dbfd9533ac227db0453a6a82971a9072e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/0cfc5f1dbfd9533ac227db0453a6a82971a9072e"}],"sha":"1156dd175867d11a66a824e789f9d7848320a7f8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/1156dd175867d11a66a824e789f9d7848320a7f8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24359879?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi","id":24359879,"login":"khmilevoi","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI0MzU5ODc5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/0cfc5f1dbfd9533ac227db0453a6a82971a9072e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-01T23:57:18Z","email":"Khmilevoi@gmail.com","name":"khmilevoi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-01T23:57:18Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"release: passport-checker tiny/standard/fullscreen redesign (#38)\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the unmount-cleanup test teeth and fix a flaky Escape assert\n\nRecoveryModal.test.tsx''s `leaves focus alone once the modal is gone` test\npassed even when the cleanup''s `removeEventListener(''focusin'', ...)` was\ndeleted, or the whole effect cleanup was zeroed out: after unmount the\nportal root is detached, so a leaked focusin listener calling `.focus()` on\na detached descendant is a jsdom/browser no-op and the activeElement assert\nstays green regardless. Pin the actual cleanup contract instead: spy on\nwindow.addEventListener/removeEventListener and assert the exact `focusin`\nlistener useModalIsolation registers on mount is the one removed on unmount,\nalongside the original behavioral assert.\n\nrecovery-flow.test.tsx''s first test asserted\n`dialog.contains(document.activeElement)` synchronously right after\n`findByRole(''dialog'')` resolves on DOM commit, before dispatching Escape.\n`useModalIsolation`''s mount effect (which moves focus in and attaches the\nEscape listener) is passive and can still be pending a tick later, so\nEscape could fire before the listener existed and the test would hang out\nits timeout — the same race Task 4 root-caused and fixed in this file''s\nother two Escape-closing tests by waiting for focus-in-dialog first. Apply\nthe same `await waitFor(...)` wrap here and correct the file-level comment,\nwhich had claimed only two tests reach the Escape-close race when this one\ndoes too.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the modal focusout guard against narrowing\n\nMount the underlying Radix dialog in its own pass, as production does, and\ncover the intra-modal focus move that the proposed guard narrowing would have\nbroken silently. The regression test dispatches the underlying focusout event\ndirectly rather than chaining native .focus() calls: jsdom (like real\nbrowsers) always completes an outer .focus() call on its own target once its\nhandler stack unwinds, so a settled document.activeElement assertion can''t\nobserve a reclaim a nested handler only ever wins transiently — verified\nempirically against the narrowed guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): close a cross-test focus-restore leak in the Radix stack suite\n\nRadix''s FocusScope defers its unmount focus-restore to a real setTimeout(0),\nand testing-library''s auto cleanup() never waits for it, so a prior test''s\ndeferred restore (and an unawaited NoVncCanvas state update from that same\ntest''s async recovery flow) can bleed into the next test — confirmed by\ninstrumentation. A formal trace of the reentrant focus cascade shows the\n''pulls focus back...'' assertion itself can''t actually be flipped by this\n(no yield point exists between its synchronous .focus() call and its\nexpect()), but the leak is real and pre-existing to any suite that\nmounts+unmounts a trapped FocusScope, so close it defensively: flush one\nreal tick after each test''s cleanup so Radix''s deferred work always\nfinishes before the next test starts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): drain Radix''s focus-restore timer from beforeEach, not afterEach\n\ne797d50''s message overstated what that change achieved. It claimed the added\nafterEach ran \"strictly after cleanup()\" and closed the cross-test focus-restore\nleak. It did neither. Vitest resolves sequence.hooks to \"stack\", so same-suite\nafterEach hooks run in REVERSE registration order, and testing-library''s\ncleanup() -- registered first, at setupFiles import time -- therefore runs last.\nThe file-level afterEach ran BEFORE cleanup(), i.e. before the unmount that\nschedules Radix''s setTimeout(0) focus restore, so it drained nothing.\n\nInstrumenting setTimeout and the hook boundaries on this file shows it directly:\nat afterEach entry the modal is still in the DOM and no FocusScope timer is\npending; the timer is scheduled only after the hook returns; the next test''s\nbeforeEach starts with one still queued. Moving the same one-tick flush to\nbeforeEach takes that count to zero before the test body runs. beforeEach is\ncorrect by construction -- it always follows the previous test''s afterEach\nhooks whatever the hook-order mode.\n\nCorrect the scope of the claim too. The restore is focus-neutral in this\nfixture: the previouslyFocusedElement it captured is either document.body,\nwhich jsdom refuses to focus, or an already-detached node, and every observed\nfiring left document.activeElement unchanged. The intermittent failure this was\nchasing did not reproduce at e797d50 in 30 consecutive single-file runs or 6\nwhole-package runs. So this closes a scheduling leak, not an observed failure,\nand the comment now says exactly that instead of claiming otherwise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): link the tier-shared-state follow-up\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): drop the phantom rebuild from the instance store''s connect hook\n\nThe connect hook carried `current ??= { value: make() }` behind a comment\nclaiming it gave a reconnect after disposal a fresh value. Measured against\n@reatom/core@1001.1.0 with the shipped code:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 2\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"],[{\"id\":2},\"a\"]]   built = 2\n\nThe reconnect still returned the DISPOSED value — a dependency-free `computed`\nnever recomputes, so its cached result is what every later read gets. The line\nonly built a second model graph nobody could reach and then handed it to\n`dispose` a second time. Same run with the line removed:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n\nThe comment now states what is true: a stale reconnect reads the cached,\nalready-disposed value, and a fresh value comes only from a new `store()`\nlookup — which is what every real caller does, because it calls `store()`\nduring render and the cleanup has already evicted the stale handle.\n\nWith the phantom build gone, `if (held)` is what prevents the double dispose,\nso a test pins it: replacing it with an unconditional `dispose?.(held?.value,\nkey)` now fails with a second teardown recorded as `[undefined, \"a\"]`.\n\nAlso corrects the module doc''s cost claim for reading atoms inside `make`.\nIt does not \"silently rebuild the whole value\": measured, after such a\ndependency changes the compute function re-runs once, `current ??=`\nshort-circuits it so `make` does not re-run (built stayed 1, the identical\nobject was returned, no subscriber notified), and because the re-run never\nreads the atom again the edge is dropped. The cost is a pointless dependency\nedge, not lost state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): align the tier-shared-state spec with what shipped\n\nThree claims in the design spec described work that was never delivered:\n\n- The instance atom was said to carry `withDisconnectHook`. It carries\n  `withConnectHook`, whose cleanup calls `teardownIfDisconnected()` — which\n  re-checks `isConnected` before disposing and evicts the map entry only while\n  it still points at the handle being torn down.\n- Section 4 \"StrictMode removal\" claimed `<StrictMode>` was dropped from\n  `packages/client/src/app/main.tsx` and replaced by a comment explaining its\n  absence. None of it shipped: `git diff cfff8d2..HEAD -- packages/client/` is\n  empty and the wrapper is still there. Measurement showed the store survives\n  the remount cycle, because the cleanup''s `isConnected` re-check returns\n  without touching a value a live mount still holds. Section 4 is rewritten as\n  \"StrictMode kept, tolerance pinned by test\", stating why the plan''s original\n  reasoning does not apply.\n- The Testing gate, the Documentation list and the Decision Summary repeated\n  the removal. They now say StrictMode stays and that the tolerance is pinned\n  by `describe(''makeWidgetInstanceStore under StrictMode'')`.\n\nThat last claim is measured, not assumed: deleting the `isConnected` re-check\nfrom the cleanup turns two of the three StrictMode tests red, along with the\nnon-StrictMode same-tick-resubscribe test.\n\nThe plan file is left untouched — it is the historical record of what was\nintended.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): narrow unmeasured claims and close two coverage gaps\n\nComment/claim accuracy — all of these stated more than was ever measured:\n\n- `ui/recovery-flow.test.tsx`: the \"roughly 1 in 15-40 runs\" hang frequency\n  was applied to every Escape-closing test in the file. It was measured for\n  exactly one of them, ''does not collapse when recovery opens from the tile''\n  (task-4-report.md, Fix round 3). The others carry the same wait by\n  inspection — identical shape, identical race — with no observed rate. The\n  comment now says so.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"at `beforeEach` entry one (later\n  two) FocusScope timer is pending\" — only the 1 -> 0 transition was ever\n  recorded, so the \"(later two)\" is dropped.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"jsdom (like real browsers) always\n  completes an outer `.focus()` call on its own target\" — true and measured\n  for jsdom, never measured against a real browser. Scoped to jsdom.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: ''keeps focus inside when it moves\n  between two of our own controls'' promised a focus invariant the test does\n  not assert — it asserts that our guard swallows the `focusout` before it\n  reaches `document`. Renamed to say that.\n\nCoverage:\n\n- `model/instance-store.test.ts` (new) covers `dispose: (models) =>\n  models.recoveryModel.teardown()`, the safety net against leaking a live VNC\n  session when the modal outlived the widget. It had no test at all: replacing\n  the callback with a no-op left the whole widget suite green. Verified both\n  new tests fail against that no-op.\n- `ui/PassportChecker.test.tsx`: two assertions used\n  `expect(await screen.findAllBy*(…)).toHaveLength(2)`, which cannot retry —\n  `findAllBy*` resolves on the first snapshot with >= 1 match, so it settles on\n  one mount and never waits for the second. Both are now a re-querying\n  `waitFor`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(backlog): record the two-modal focus ping-pong as a deferred defect\n\nBoth `useModalIsolation` instances register focus containment on `window` in\nthe capture phase, so two mounted modals each see the other''s `focusin` before\nthe root-level `stopPropagation()` can stop it, and each pulls focus into its\nown root — forever.\n\nReproduced in jsdom 29.1.1 with two `useModalIsolation` roots mounted at once:\none modal performs 1 focus pull on mount; two mounted together never settle,\nand a hard test guard at 400 pulls had to stop the run (402 recorded).\n\nDeferred rather than fixed because it is unreachable through the UI: the\npassport checker is the only owner of such a modal, renders at most one\n(non-fullscreen mount only), and its overlay is a full-viewport\n`pointer-events: auto` layer whose `pointerdown` handler closes the modal, so\na second one cannot be opened over the first.\n\nThe entry notes the likely fix — a module-level stack of active modal roots\nwith containment applied only by the topmost.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): correct five unverified claims left by code review\n\nBring wording in the tier-shared-state spec, BACKLOG.md, and a test\ncomment in line with what was actually measured/read: the instance\nstore''s dependency-edge cost (not a rebuilt value), a stale\nuse-modal-isolation.ts line citation (:53 -> :55, two places), the\nroot focusin stopper''s actual bubble phase (not capture), and which\nrecovery-flow tests keep the shared timeout default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay at this branch\n\nPins rpi.dev.toml''s source.branch to feat/passport-checker-widget so the dev\nenvironment runs this PR instead of the dev branch.\n\nREVERT BEFORE MERGING: once this PR lands on dev, source.branch must go back to\n\"dev\" or the dev overlay stays pinned to a branch that no longer moves.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): drop the stale Chromium singleton lock before launch\n\nThe Chromium profile is a named Docker volume, so `SingletonLock` keeps the\n`<hostname>-<pid>` of whichever container wrote it. After a redeploy the new\ncontainer has a new hostname, Chromium reads a lock owned by \"another computer\"\nit cannot probe, and `launchPersistentContext` fails on every task until the\nvolume is touched by hand.\n\nRemove `SingletonLock`, `SingletonCookie`, and `SingletonSocket` before each\nlaunch. `fs.rm` unlinks the symlinks themselves instead of following them, and\n`force` keeps a fresh profile from failing; a removal that does fail is returned\nas a value, logged, and the launch still proceeds. Unconditional removal is safe\nbecause `getPersistentContext` is the only caller of `launch` and the image\nentrypoint runs a single `node dist/index.cjs` - no check for a live Chromium is\nperformed or implied.\n\nAlso asserts that a failed launch keeps its cause, which is what makes the\nPlaywright message reachable from the service log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): log the error behind an internal task failure\n\nInternal failures were logged as `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` only, so the\ntagged error and its cause chain never reached the container log. A Chromium\nlaunch failure therefore showed up as a bare `internal` code with nothing to\ndiagnose. Add the error to the logged fields; `toEnvelopeError` still answers\nthe client with the redacted `internal` code, so only the log gains detail.\n\nNote the trade-off: a handler error whose message carries task data now reaches\nthe container log, which the previous field list deliberately avoided.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): redact handler failures in the internal task log\n\nfa87a47c logged too much. It added `error: outcome` to the internal-failure log\nso a Chromium launch failure would stop being invisible, but that field carries\nwhatever the handler threw. errore''s `toJSON()` serialises `message`, `stack`,\nand the whole `cause` chain, Playwright quotes `page.evaluate` arguments into\nits messages, and the passport widget passes the secret identity as one — so a\nhandler failure could print the passport series and number into the container\nlog. The wrapper''s own `stack` repeats the cause under `Caused by:`, so the\nidentity landed there twice.\n\nThat contradicts the design spec: raw Playwright, network, Zod, and upstream\nresponse details are logged only after redaction, request and response bodies\nare never logged, and logs redact task payloads and secret values\n(docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md\n:350-353, :684).\n\nThe rule now, verified against dispatch.ts:\n\n- `BrowserExecutorError` wraps a failed `executor.acquire(...)`. Acquire runs\n  before the handler and is passed only `(signal, widgetId)` — never the payload\n  or the widget secrets — so its cause chain is Chromium/Playwright launch\n  detail. Still logged in full; this is the stale singleton lock failure\n  fa87a47c existed to surface.\n- Every other internal failure wraps handler output and is reduced to\n  `errorChain`: the names/tags of the error and its `cause` chain and nothing\n  else. No `message`, no `stack`, no foreign properties.\n\nThe log message, the `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` fields, and the\n`code === ''internal''` condition are unchanged.\n\nTests split the single fa87a47c case in two: one pins that an executor failure''s\ncause chain still reaches the log, one asserts a secret-looking cause string is\nabsent from the serialised logged payload. Both were confirmed to fail before\nthe fix and to discriminate after it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add an env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe passport widget''s recovery path (retain the page, issue a single-use\ncapability, stream the live browser over noVNC, let a human solve the\nchallenge, retry) has never been exercised on real hardware, because it only\ntriggers when pasport.org.ua serves a Cloudflare challenge and none has been\nencountered — the smoke test on the Pi returned a normal result. Subproject 8\n(`passport-checker-rpi-integration` in\ndocs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md)\nhas \"embedded noVNC Cloudflare recovery, SSH fallback, and real checker smoke\ntest on the Raspberry Pi\" as an acceptance item and had no way to reach it on\ndemand. PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY is that way.\n\nContract: `1` and nothing else enables it — absent, empty, `0`, `true`, ` 1 `\nare all off (isForcedRecoveryEnabled does exact string equality, no trimming,\nno truthiness coercion). Compose passes it through with a `:-` default so an\nunset variable stays off, and it must never be set in production.\n\nSemantics: at most once per process. The latch lives in the closure\nmakePassportCheckHandler returns, so each handler owns its own and the unit\ntests stay isolated. It fires after a successful goto and after\ncollectNavigationEvidence — the retained page is the real checker page with the\nsite''s own UI loaded, so a human can drive it over noVNC — and returns exactly\nthe retainPageForRecovery() + BrowserSessionRequiredError pair the genuine\nCloudflare branch returns, making the downstream flow byte-identical to a real\nchallenge. The latch is consumed only when the forced path actually fires, so a\nfailed navigation returns its own error untouched and leaves the lever armed:\nan infrastructure hiccup must not burn the single forced run an operator is\nwaiting on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): apply the design-system widget redesign\n\nBring the tile and the recovery modal in line with Паспорт-чекер.dc.html.\n\nBoth tiers now lay out as header -> flexible middle -> action, so the\naction sits on the tile''s bottom edge in every state instead of trailing\nwhatever content happens to be above it. Status banners pick up the\ndesign''s per-state hues, the filled success disc, and the CircleAlert /\nTriangleAlert split that separates a retryable failure from a session\nprompt. The modal gains the access-pill dot, a pulsing LIVE dot, framed\nconnecting/disconnected/expired states, and the shell-style SSH block.\n\nDark theme was effectively missing: the --pc-* hues chained to\n--success-soft / --destructive-soft, which the host token set never\ndefines, and to --success, which is a dark surface green rather than an\nicon green under [data-theme=''dark'']. Every hue is now declared per\ntheme the way ofelia-poop-duty does it. The modal portals to\ndocument.body, outside .widget, so it declares the one hue it needs on\nits own overlay.\n\nThree deliberate departures from the mockup:\n- invalidConfig copy stays server-side (\"Обратитесь к администратору\"),\n  since this widget has no per-instance settings — its config lives in\n  server secrets.\n- the expired frame keeps \"Переподключиться\"; without it an expired\n  capability can only be recovered by closing and reopening the modal.\n- the sessionRequired tiny tile only fills amber, no border: the tile\n  border belongs to the host card, and drawing one from inside the\n  widget would render a second, inset contour.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xwGNctwqXcYi5p4QSSFhA\n\n* docs(browser-automation): design widget user-input detection\n\nMove the \"a human must act\" mechanism out of passport-checker and into the\nautomation platform. The handler gets context.detectUserInput(detector), which\nruns the detector, optionally prepares the page, retains it and returns the\ncanonical error as one indivisible step; retainPageForRecovery leaves the public\ncontext so the halves cannot be separated. Cloudflare becomes a reusable helper\n(probe and predicate together, not just the predicate) that any widget can pass\nin. Also records the removal of the PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY lever.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): correct the user-input spec''s test-seam claims\n\nmakeFakeExecutor fakes BrowserExecutor<FakeContext> with FakeContext = { id,\nsignal }; it never builds a BrowserTaskContext, so it cannot host a recording\ndetectUserInput and needs no change at all. Name the seams that do exist: the\nsix retainPageForRecovery call sites in chromium-executor.test.ts and the three\nBrowserTaskContext literals in diagnostics.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): plan the widget user-input detection refactor\n\nSeven tasks, each green on its own: retire the forced-recovery lever, add the\ndetector and error contracts, move Cloudflare detection into the platform with\nits probe reunited with its predicate, run escalation from the task context,\nrewire the widget, then withdraw retainPageForRecovery from the public context so\nthe retain/escalate pair cannot be taken apart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): make the Cloudflare task a move, not a copy\n\nTask 3 previously left the classifier duplicated between the widget and the\nplatform until Task 5, which any reviewer would rightly flag as verbatim\nduplication of a logic block. The widget now switches to the moved module in the\nsame task, so the logic never exists in two places.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* revert(passport-checker): drop the env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe lever forced one trip through the Cloudflare recovery path per service\nstart so Subproject 8 could exercise noVNC on the Pi on demand. It is being\nretired deliberately ahead of moving user-input detection into the platform;\nthe Pi smoke test needs another answer.\n\nThis reverts the behaviour added in 45c604f1.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(browser-automation): add user-input detector and error contracts\n\nUserInputRequiredError carries the browser_session_required code and sshTarget\nmeta the recovery flow keys off, moved out of the passport-checker widget where\na platform contract had no business living. UserInputProbeError distinguishes\na broken check from a negative one.\n\n* feat(browser-automation): move Cloudflare detection into the platform\n\nThe classifier lived in the passport-checker widget while the DOM probe feeding\nit lived in that widget''s check handler, so the only reusable half was the half\nwithout the probe. Both now sit in browser-automation behind two detector\nfactories: one that probes the live page, one that classifies evidence already\nderived inside the page from a fetch response.\n\nThe widget imports the moved module rather than keeping a copy; it still calls\nthe classifier by hand, which the next commit changes.\n\n* @\ndocs: document the feature branch -> dev -> main workflow\n\nFeature work happens in a dedicated branch inside a ./.worktrees checkout,\nlands in dev through a PR, gets verified on the dev stack, and only then\nreaches main through a dev -> main PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* feat(browser-automation): run user-input detection from the task context\n\ndetectUserInput runs a widget-supplied detector and, on a match, prepares the\npage, retains it and returns UserInputRequiredError as one step, so the two\nhalves cannot drift apart. A detector that fails yields UserInputProbeError and\ndeliberately leaves the page unretained: an undecidable check is not a challenge,\nand pinning a browser page for a recovery nobody will perform is worse.\n\nAUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET moves into the service config, where an unusable value\ndegrades to null rather than failing config loading and killing the service.\n\n* docs(passport-checker): design last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* test(browser-automation): cover the prepare-before-retain order in detectUserInput directly\n\nThe executor-level test asserted hasRetainedPage() from inside the prepare\nmock, but that bookkeeping is only populated at release() time, so it passed\nregardless of call order. Add a direct unit test against makeDetectUserInput\nthat records prepare/retain into one ordered array — genuinely fails if the\norder in detect.ts is inverted (verified by temporarily inverting it).\n\nNo production behavior changes; detect.ts ends identical to before this commit.\n\n* @\ndocs: merge AGENTS.md into CLAUDE.md and symlink it\n\nAGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md had drifted into two partly overlapping rule sets.\nEverything unique to AGENTS.md (project structure, codegen commands, Windows\ntest-runner notes, coding style, UI gotchas, testing, security, failure modes)\nnow lives in CLAUDE.md, and AGENTS.md becomes a symlink so every agent reads\nthe same file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* docs(passport-checker): plan last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* feat(passport-checker): date-aware check timestamp label\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout design\n\nTwo width breakpoints instead of one hardcoded grid: one column below 768,\ntwelve above, with row height and margin scaled by the same factor as the\ncolumn width so the desktop grid zooms instead of stretching on one axis.\n\nThe mobile layout is derived from the desktop one and only persisted once the\nuser drags or resizes, stored as an additive optional `mobileLayout` field so\nno migration of `root:boards` is needed.\n\nAlso records the react-draggable touchstart defect: the whole card is the drag\nhandle today, which cancels page scrolling on touch — harmless on a short\n12-column board, blocking at one column.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): detect required user input through the platform\n\nThe handler no longer collects Cloudflare evidence, classifies it, retains the\npage and builds the escalation error by hand at every site; it passes a detector\nto context.detectUserInput and returns what comes back. browser/challenge.ts and\nthe widget-local BrowserSessionRequiredError are gone — the classifier now lives\nin browser-automation next to the probe that feeds it, and the\nbrowser_session_required contract belongs to the platform that owns the recovery\nflow.\n\n* fix(passport-checker): restore the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nAn automated amend from a concurrent session accidentally stripped this\nline out of 3a12494e. It belongs to the user-input-detection work; this\nrestores it verbatim.\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nRETRYABLE_MESSAGES.user_input_probe vanished twice with no test turning red,\nbecause nothing asserted the actual behaviour: with the entry gone,\nmapCheckError silently falls back to GENERIC_RETRYABLE_MESSAGE and the user\nsees the generic string instead of the specific one. Assert the real output\nof mapCheckError against a hardcoded expected string, not against the table\nitself, so a future deletion or typo of the entry fails this test instead of\ncomparing the map against itself.\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron design spec\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout implementation plan\n\n* feat(board): resolve grid metrics from the container width\n\n* feat(passport-checker): persist the last successful check\n\n* feat(board): derive and reconcile the one-column mobile layout\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron implementation plan\n\nAlso corrects the spec: the jsdom Temporal shims must stay, only the\n--harmony-temporal execArgv becomes redundant on Node 26.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin which detector goes to which detectUserInput call\n\nNothing previously checked that the navigation check gets the page detector\nand the submission check gets the evidence detector -- only the second call''s\nprepare option was asserted. A handler that swapped the two factories would\nleave every test in this file green; only the gated real-browser suite would\nnotice, and it isn''t run in CI. Pull the captured detector off each mock call\nand invoke it directly: the page detector must reach for page.evaluate, the\nevidence detector must ignore its page argument and classify the real\nevidence. Also drops the now-inert hasChallengeForm evidence field from the\nsubmission-escalation test, whose result was always driven by the mock''s\nescalation queue regardless of that field.\n\n* feat(board): add mobile layout materialize, update and reset actions\n\n* refactor(browser-automation): expose only detectUserInput on the task context\n\nretainPageForRecovery was half of a pair a handler had to assemble correctly by\nhand: retaining without escalating strands a browser page, escalating without\nretaining hands the UI a recovery session with nothing behind it. With the retain\nflag reachable only through detectUserInput, neither mistake is expressible.\n\n* test(passport-checker): cover restore-on-mount and cross-placement sync\n\nAdd two widget-level cases pinning storage-backed persistence through\nReact: a fresh mount renders a previously stored result without\ninvoking a check, and a check made in one placement is observed by a\nsecond placement sharing the same storage. Rename the pre-existing\n\"gives separate instance ids separate state\" case to \"gives separate\nstorages separate state\" (it isolates by storage, not instanceId) and\nupdate its comment to point at the new cross-placement case below it.\nPin PASSPORT_LAST_RESULT_KEY''s literal value in check-model.test.ts so\na silent rename cannot slip through green while orphaning deployed\nusers'' stored data under the old key.\n\n* feat(board): switch the grid to resolved metrics and a mobile drag grip\n\n* docs(browser-automation): document how a widget requests manual input\n\n* feat(board): offer a mobile layout reset in the board schema popover\n\n* fix(widgets): address passport-checker persistence review findings\n\n- TinyTier''s success chip now shows checkedAtLabel, so a restored (possibly\n  stale) result reads honestly in the compact tile too, not just standard.\n- Harden the two-placement isolation test: assert placement two stays idle\n  instead of a length-1 findAllByText that can pass mid-race.\n- Clarify why lastResult/transient set order is safe in checkPassport\n  (Reatom notifies after the sync block, not \"order for readability\").\n- Add a test exercising lastResultSchema.safeParse over the exact shape\n  checkPassport writes, since createFakeStorage skips schema validation.\n- Document lastResultSchema as a persistence contract: new fields must be\n  optional/defaulted or every stored value fails validation on next deploy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* fix(passport-checker): remove dead AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET threading\n\nrecoverySshTarget was never read by check.ts''s handler after the\ndetectUserInput refactor moved sshTarget sourcing entirely into the\nplatform config. Delete the widget''s leftover env read and option\nthreading so the README''s \"widgets neither read nor pass it\" claim is\nactually true, and drop the now-meaningless SSH-sanitization test in\nfavor of the equivalent (and broader) coverage in browser-automation''s\nconfig.test.ts.\n\n* docs(ofelia): design the authored ledger and comments\n\nRecords get the authenticated account instead of the per-device \"Я:\"\ntoggle, the write path moves into the widget''s own server.ts, and the\nhistory row is rebuilt: human dates, plain-language phrases, explicit\ndebt effect, visible supersession, no IP.\n\nPulls the Node 26 prerequisite out of the widget-server-cron spec, since\nthe shared domain module it enables is needed here first.\n\nAlso imports the Claude Design workspace under design/ (documents only,\nscreenshots and uploads stripped) and keeps the brief that produced the\nOfelia mock.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs(ofelia): note the cron plan overlap and landing order\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): cover the mobile layout and touch-scroll behaviour end to end\n\n* fix(board): keep the measured grid container mounted\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a 1920 viewport\n\n* docs(ofelia): add the authored ledger implementation plan\n\n15 tasks: Node 26, the pure domain split, the members directory, widget\nidentity, the server-resolved viewer, record schemas, draft construction,\nthe widget server definition, the model rewire, the history and comment\nview models, the account avatar, and the two rebuilt panels.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): wait for the grid relayout to settle before measuring\n\n* fix(browser-automation): bundle workspace imports instead of externalizing them\n\nThe rspack externals predicate was an allow-list whose default was\n\"externalize\". When the user-input refactor introduced the first runtime\nbare-specifier import of a workspace package into the service module graph\n(browser-automation/user-input/cloudflare, from the passport-checker browser\ntask), it was silently externalized: the build stayed green, all tests stayed\ngreen, and the Cloudflare module was simply absent from dist/index.cjs. The\nruntime stage copies only package.json and dist/, so Node resolved the\nspecifier through the exports map to a .ts path that does not exist in the\nimage and the container died on `exec node dist/index.cjs`.\n\nInvert the predicate into a deny-list derived from the package''s own\ndependencies (minus errore, which is ESM-only and must be bundled) plus Node\nbuiltins. An unrecognised bare specifier is now bundled, so the same mistake\nsurfaces as a build-time resolution error rather than a startup crash, and\nadding a real runtime dependency externalizes it with no config edit.\n\nAdd scripts/check-bundle.ts, chained onto `build` so it runs inside the image\nbuild the Dockerfile already performs. It asserts that every require() in the\nbundle is a builtin or a declared dependency, that the scan still matches the\nemitted shape at all, and that the compiled evidenceFromResponseText the widget\nsplices into Chromium still evaluates and runs in a bare vm realm. That last\ncheck is what the BROWSER_IT suite could never prove: it runs under vitest,\nwhere the specifier resolves to .ts through esbuild and the swc path is never\nexercised. Verified: the compiled form is ES5-lowered but self-contained --\ndestructuring became inline property reads, no swc helpers, no hoisted regexes.\n\nAlso:\n- detect.ts: tag the prepare outcome instead of comparing against null, so a\n  prepare that rejects with exactly null is a failure rather than a silent\n  success, matching the detector branch''s `typeof detected !== ''boolean''`.\n- user-input/errors.test.ts: cover toEnvelopeError for UserInputRequiredError\n  with and without an ssh target, pinning with toStrictEqual that `meta` is\n  omitted rather than set to undefined. contracts.test.ts already claimed this\n  coverage lived here.\n- chromium-executor.test.ts: drop an assertion inside a prepare callback that\n  could not fail the test and was vacuous, and rename the case to what it\n  actually verifies; the ordering contract is owned by detect.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* test(board): settle past the grid transition and stop assuming card order\n\n* test(board): correct the stale comment above the breakpoint wait\n\n* chore(node): align every runtime and image on Node 26\n\nnode:26-alpine and node:26-bookworm-slim no longer bundle corepack\n(removed from Node core), so `npm install -g corepack@latest` is added\nahead of every `corepack enable` in the three Dockerfiles and\ndocker-compose.dev.yml. scripts/infra.test.ts''s assertion on the\nbrowser-automation Dockerfile''s base image is updated to match.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(node): unshadow jsdom localStorage and bump the e2e image to Node 26\n\nNode 26 ships its own unflagged global localStorage/sessionStorage (Web\nStorage API), which resolves to undefined without --localstorage-file\nand shadows jsdom''s window.localStorage for bare-identifier reads in\npackages/client''s jsdom test environment (40 failing tests). Disable\nNode''s own globals via execArgv: [''--no-experimental-webstorage''] in\npackages/client/vite.config.ts so jsdom''s storage wins; verified via\nObject.getOwnPropertyDescriptor that the flag actually removes the\nglobal rather than just suppressing the warning. widget-runtime and\nwidget-sdk don''t touch localStorage in tests today, so the flag is not\nadded to their execArgv arrays (narrowest correct remedy).\n\nAlso bumps packages/client/e2e.Dockerfile (used by pnpm test:e2e:docker\nvia docker-compose.e2e.yml) from node:22-bookworm-slim to\nnode:26-bookworm-slim, missed by the original brief''s Files list, with\nthe same corepack@latest install this Node 26 base image now needs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): make the relayout wait actually poll\n\n* refactor(ofelia): extract the pure duty domain out of the Reatom model\n\n* fix(lint): make the widget domain import barrier a real allowlist\n\nThe regex denylist let ''../model/ofelia-duty'' through — domain/ and model/\nare siblings, so a single-''../'' import was both the most likely accidental\nescape and completely unguarded. It also only enumerated known packages, so\nany new npm dependency passed silently.\n\nReplace it with a gitignore-style group allowlist (''**'' minus zod, @shared/*\nand ''./'' siblings), which oxlint evaluates natively. A negative-lookahead\nregex was tried first and rejected: oxlint''s Rust regex engine has no\nlookaround and silently drops such a pattern, disabling the whole barrier.\n\nDomain tests get a second, later override that additionally allows vitest;\neverything else stays blocked there too.\n\n* chore(lint): keep the design mock exports out of lint and format\n\ndesign/ holds vendored Claude Design exports (support.js, *.dc.html)\nthat break the repo''s only gate: oxlint reports 9 errors in\ndesign/support.js, and oxfmt aborts (exit 2) on non-standard markup\nin design/Design System.dc.html before it can check anything else.\n\nAdd design/** to the existing ignorePatterns glob array in both\n.oxlintrc.json and .oxfmtrc.json, the same mechanism already used for\ndocs/**. This is a plain glob matcher (not the Rust regex engine\nbehind no-restricted-imports), so it isn''t subject to that engine''s\nlookaround gap, and it leaves every other rule/override untouched.\n\nFixing the oxfmt abort also uncovered pre-existing, unrelated\nformatting drift in CLAUDE.md, package.json and\nscripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts that the crash had been masking from\nthe format:check summary. Reformatted them (whitespace/ordering only,\nno logic changes) so pnpm format:check is actually green end to end.\n\n* feat(auth): expose the board members directory\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): expose the board identity to widgets\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): stop identity fetch from leaking an unhandled rejection and a discarded error\n\nHandle the AbortError that wrap()''s promise rejects with when the last\nwidget reading identity unmounts mid-fetch, and stop discarding\nBoardMembersError whole: a 401 stays silent (expected on an\nunauthenticated host), everything else is surfaced via console.warn so\nschema drift or a server fault is not silently swallowed.\n\n* feat(widgets): resolve the signed-in viewer for widget server events\n\n* feat(ofelia): carry the authoring account on ledger and comment records\n\n* feat(ofelia): move draft construction and event schemas into the domain\n\n* feat(ofelia): write the ledger and comments from the widget server\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): harden server date parsing and unforgeability coverage\n\n- Wrap Temporal.PlainDate.from in errore.try so a calendar-invalid but\n  regex-valid date (e.g. 2026-02-30) returns an Error instead of\n  throwing through a function typed Error | T.\n- Add regression coverage: forged createdBy/actor in the payload is\n  ignored, the debt handler is exercised, storage-read failures are\n  asserted by identity with no write, and the invalid-date case is\n  asserted as a value.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): split the unforgeability guard into schema and handler halves\n\nThe round-1 forged-payload test went through run(), which parses the\npayload via zod''s default strip mode before the handler ever sees it —\nso it only proved the schema strips forged fields, twice over, and\nwould not catch a handler that starts spreading its raw payload into\nthe draft. Replace it with two schema-level tests (clean + comment\npayloads drop a forged createdBy/actor) and one handler-level test\nthat calls ofeliaServer.handlers.clean directly, bypassing the schema,\nto prove the handler itself never reads those fields even if they\nreach it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* feat(ofelia): drive ledger and comment writes through widget server events\n\nThe client no longer appends to the ledger or the comment key. All four\nday actions collapse into one `invokeDay` that sends `{ date }` to the\nmatching widget-server event, and `send` sends `{ weekStart, text }` —\nwho the actor is, whose debt moves, whether the event applies at all and\nwhich account signed it are now derived and stamped server-side, so a\nclient cannot forge authorship.\n\n`selectedDate()` and `today()` (and `viewWeekStart()`) are read before\nthe first await so they resolve against the widget''s own Reatom context.\n\nThe per-device \"Я: Леша/Карина\" toggle is deleted rather than hidden:\nauthorship comes from the session, so there is nothing left to pick.\n`currentUser` is gone from the duty model, the widget context, the\nfixture and the view-model''s `onSetUser`.\n\nBoth model factories now take `api` and `identity`; `identity` is the\ndirectory the history and comment views read in the follow-up tasks.\nThe view projections drop `ip`/`by`, whose schema fields Tasks 5-8\nremoved, and `authorName` falls back through `createdBy.name`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* style: format the ofelia server test\n\n* feat(ofelia): group history by duty day and mark superseded records\n\n* feat(ofelia): resolve comment authors through the members directory\n\n* fix(ofelia): flush the comment thread''s connect hook via vi.waitFor, not a bare microtask\n\nReview round 1: the timing fix for the comment-thread subscribe/emit race\nassumed a single-microtask flush of Reatom''s connect-hook queue. Match the\nsibling comments-subscription tests in the same file instead — wait for the\nstorage mock''s subscribe call and wrap the whole body in context.start, so\nthe assertion is condition-based rather than scheduling-internals-based.\n\n* feat(ofelia): add the account avatar and its palette\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the history list around duty-day groups\n\n* fix(ofelia): announce the history phrase subject to assistive tech\n\nThe duty circle is the sentence subject in the rebuilt history phrase, and\nAvatar is aria-hidden, so the column announced as bare verbs with no subject.\nPair every circle with a visually hidden name, attribute the debt pill to its\nperson, and separate the chips with real spaces as the mock does.\n\nAlso correct the text-decoration comment to name the real mechanism (atomic\ninline, not descendant cancellation) and cover the three untested branches.\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the comment thread around account authors\n\n* fix(ofelia): restore composer coverage and mute the unknown comment author\n\n* fix(ofelia): break ledger day ties by append order\n\nRouting the writes through the widget server changed where `ts` comes from.\n`/api/storage/:key/append` stamped `Date.now()`, so consecutive appends always\ngot a strictly increasing timestamp. The widget dispatch stamps the app''s\ninjectable `now()`, and the e2e harness pins that clock — so a clean-then-undo\npair on the same day produced two entries with an identical `ts`, and\n`latestOutcomesByDate`''s strict `>` kept the earlier `cleaned` entry as the\nday''s outcome. The `reset` was written but never counted, and the day never\nreopened; `pnpm test:e2e:docker` caught it as a failing \"undo — returns the day\nto pending\".\n\nThe ledger is append-only and iterated in insertion order, so `>=` is the\ncorrect tie-break: on an equal timestamp the later-appended entry is the newer\ndecision. This also hardens production, where two writes can still land in the\nsame millisecond.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: describe server-authored widget records\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): fail widget dispatch when the session account is unreadable\n\nresolveWidgetViewer folded every getAccount failure into `null`, so a Valkey\nfault, a corrupt account record and a deleted account all produced a record\nstamped `createdBy: null` plus a `{ ok: true }` response. The ledger is\nappend-only, so that authorship could never be recovered.\n\nThe three outcomes are now distinct: no session still returns `null`, which is\nwhat the standalone harnesses and the e2e stack (no nginx, so no session cookie)\nrely on to keep writing unattributed records; a failed lookup returns a\nWidgetViewerLookupError, which the dispatch route turns into a fault instead of\na false success.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): order superseded history records by append order\n\n`superseded` sorted on `right.ts - left.ts`, which returns 0 on a tie; the\nstable sort then left tied records in append order, oldest first, contradicting\nthe \"newest first\" contract on HistoryDayGroup. Ties became a supported case\nwhen latestOutcomesByDate started breaking them with `>=`, and the dockerized\ne2e stack freezes the clock, so there every record ties.\n\nThe ledger is append-only, so append order is chronological order: reversing the\nday''s bucket is both correct and cheaper than sorting.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): warn when listAccounts skips an unreadable record\n\nA corrupt or schema-mismatched account record was dropped silently, so the\nroster simply came back short and the symptom read as \"an account vanished\"\nrather than as the data corruption it is. Name the key in a console.warn.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* build: pin corepack to 0.35.0 across images and dev compose\n\n`npm install -g corepack@latest` ran in five image build stages and four dev\ncompose services. There is no CI and the Pi deploy is manual with a 30-minute\nbuild timeout, so a breaking corepack release would only ever surface as a\nfailed deploy. Pin one explicit version everywhere.\n\nVerified: `docker build -f packages/server/Dockerfile` succeeds and the built\nimage reports corepack 0.35.0 with pnpm 10.28.2.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* refactor(ofelia): delete the dead short-date formatter\n\nformatDateShort and MONTHS_SHORT lost their last production consumer when the\ncomment thread moved to its own formatter; only the test still called them. It\nalso formatted through `new Date(ts)` in the local timezone, while every current\nformatter goes through plainDateIn(DUTY_TIME_ZONE, ...).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): drop the tier guards that cannot fail, hoist the env directive\n\nStandardTier and CompactTier each asserted they do not render UserToggle. The\ntoggle only ever lived in RichLayout and the component is now deleted outright,\nso neither assertion could ever fail — they only looked like coverage.\n\nAlso move the `@vitest-environment node` directive in server.test.ts back to\nline 1, where a reader expects it and where model/history-view.test.ts keeps it.\nVitest 4 matches the directive anywhere in the file content, so the file was\nalready running under node; this is readability, not a behaviour fix.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: state the authorship guarantee the code actually provides\n\nCLAUDE.md claimed a server-stamped author \"cannot be forged\". Nothing enforces\nthat: POST /api/storage/:key/append does no session check in Node, and nginx''s\nauth_request only proves a session exists, not whose it is, so any signed-in\nhousehold member can still hand-write a record carrying an arbitrary author.\n\nState what is true instead — the UI can no longer sign a record with someone\nelse''s name, and the generic storage route remains a shared trusted channel\ninside the board. Closing that route is a separate task.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(board): never render the desktop grid below its 1920 baseline\n\nThe desktop zoom is now one-directional: raise the lower clamp on the\ngrid scale from 0.75 to 1, so every container width between the mobile\nbreakpoint and BASE_WIDTH resolves to exactly the historical metrics\n(rowHeight: 30, margin: [10, 10]) and only wider displays scale up.\n\nUntil this branch, useContainerWidth never attached its observer and\npinned the reported width at its 1280 default on every screen. With the\ncontainer measured for real, a 0.75 floor would have shrunk every board\non a 1280/1366/1440 laptop below the size it has always had, dropping an\nh:6 card from 230px to 172.5px and pushing widgets under their own tier\nthresholds. Nothing shrinks now; the 4K aspect drift the scale was added\nfor is still fixed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling record\n\nBring the design doc in line with what shipped: the lower clamp is 1,\nthe 1280 table row shows the baseline metrics, and the paragraph\njustifying the clamp says plainly that it is active across the whole\n768-1920 band rather than a narrow 768-1100 one.\n\nAlso correct the claim that existing 1080p boards \"do not shift by a\npixel\". The formula at 1920 does reproduce the old constants, but the\nmeasured width was previously pinned at 1280 on every screen by the\nuseContainerWidth bug this branch fixes, so wider screens legitimately\nre-flow to use the whole window.\n\nDrop the 1920 viewport pin from ofelia-duty.spec.ts: it only worked\naround the 0.75 clamp, and removing it restores default-viewport\ncoverage. Recompute the footprint arithmetic in the ofelia client test\ncomment, which assumed a container of exactly 1280 rather than the\nviewport minus the board padding and the scrollbar gutter.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a viewport its tier needs\n\nEvery assertion in the duty spec is StandardTier UI, and the widget only\nresolves `standard` at a frame width of 400px or more. Playwright''s\ndefault 1280 viewport is about 7px short: the board measures its own\ncontainer, which is 1225px there once the stable scrollbar gutter and the\nboard''s 20px padding on each side come off, so a w:4 card is ~395px and\nits frame ~393px. `compact` is the correct rendering at that width, so\nthe spec has to declare the width its assertions need.\n\nThe dependency was previously invisible because the board reported a\nhardcoded 1280px container on every screen, which made the card 413px and\nleft the threshold only 13px of headroom against a measurement that was\nwrong by 55px.\n\nCorrect the footprint comment in the widget''s client test to match: the\ntier is measured on the frame inside the card''s 1px border, so a 1280px\nviewport yields ~393x228 and resolves to `compact`, not `standard`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling claims to match what ships\n\nThree statements asserted the opposite of what this branch established,\nall written before the width axis was measured.\n\nThe grid-metrics JSDoc claimed the clamp keeps 1280/1366/1440 boards\n\"the size they have always been\" and widgets \"above their own tier\nthresholds\". Both are false at 1280: the container is 1225 there, so a\nw:4 card is 395px where the old code drew 413px, and ofelia falls under\nits 400px standard threshold into compact. The clamp pins the metrics -\nrowHeight 30 and margin [10, 10] at every desktop width - not the\nrendered geometry, which still follows the measured container.\n\nThe design doc claimed \"no board shrinks and no widget loses a tier\".\nThe 1366 and 1440 frames (422px, 446px) clear the widget threshold; the\n1280 frame (393px) does not. Corrected to draw the metrics/geometry\ndistinction the document already draws two paragraphs earlier.\n\nThe \"do not shift by a pixel\" claim survived in the doc''s Testing\nsection and in the test''s own comment. At 1080p the container is now\n1865, so colWidth is 144.58 rather than the 95.83 the pinned-1280 bug\nproduced. Both now state what the test guarantees: the formula at\nBASE_WIDTH reproduces the previous hardcoded constants.\n\nAlso record the shipped consequence in Risks - ofelia renders\nCompactTier below ~1300px, correct behaviour on a genuinely 393px frame,\nwith recalibration left as a follow-up - and fix three figures: the tier\nparagraph attributed the frame inset to a card padding that does not\nexist (it is a 1px border; the heights are 148px and 298px, not 110 and\n260), the e2e headroom was 11px on the frame rather than 13px on the\ncard, the 1920 card/frame are 608/606, and useContainerWidth is at\nBoard.tsx:28.\n\nComments and prose only; no executable line changed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* build(deploy): add a per-branch rpi environment and deploy scripts\n\nrpi.branch.toml is a disposable stack for trying the checked-out branch on\nreal hardware, with ttl = \"72h\" so a forgotten one reaps itself. It takes the\nbranch through ${BRANCH_NAME}, the only variable rpi 0.26 accepts — hence the\nliteral hostname rather than a derived one.\n\nscripts/deploy-branch.ts reads the branch from git so nobody retypes it, and\nrefuses to deploy without .env.branch, which would otherwise produce a stack\nwhose WebAuthn gate fails with nothing in the deploy log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(deploy): send the branch secrets bundle before the build\n\nEvery branch is a fresh rpi project with an empty secrets store, so the very\nfirst deploy of any branch died after the ~3 minute image build with an opaque\nCompose \"secret file ... does not exist\". deploy:branch now checks\n`rpi secrets ls` and sends the bundle itself when there is none.\n\nAlso strip the `--` that `pnpm run x -- --flag` forwards verbatim: rpi''s parser\nreads a bare `--` as end-of-flags, so the flag after it was rejected as a\npositional.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(browser-automation): read the spliced callback when swc stops inlining it\n\ncheck-bundle extracted the page callback by slicing between `.concat(` and\n`.toString()`, which is the whole function expression only while swc inlines it.\nA second consumer of evidenceFromResponseText — precisely what the public\nbrowser-automation/user-input/cloudflare subpath exists to allow — makes swc emit\na named declaration instead, leaving a bare identifier at the splice site. The\nguard then evaluated that identifier in an empty realm, got a ReferenceError, and\nfailed the image build accusing a perfectly correct bundle of shipping a\nnon-self-contained callback.\n\nResolve the identifier to its declaration, and let the JavaScript parser find\nwhere that declaration ends: counting braces by hand would have to understand\nregex literals, and this body is full of regexes containing quote characters.\nExtraction failures now say so instead of reporting a self-containment defect.\n\nVerified by building all four ways: inlined and named shapes both pass; the\noriginal externals regression and a regex hoisted out of the callback are both\nstill caught.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* fix(ofelia): centre the initial in avatars re-cast to inline-flex\n\nBoth avatars centre their initial with `place-items: center`, but the\nhistory phrase re-casts the box to `inline-flex` so the circle stays in\nthe inline run, and a flex container ignores `justify-items`. The glyph\nwas left-aligned: in an 18px circle it sat 0px from the inline start and\n12.97px from the end, roughly a third of the diameter off centre.\n\nName the inline axis outright so the centring survives whichever display\na caller imposes. `.debt > [data-tone]` re-casts the same way and was hit\nby the same bug.\n\n* fix(ofelia): reserve room for the viewer ring in scrolling columns\n\nThe signed-in viewer''s avatar carries a 3px ring drawn as a spread\nshadow, so it paints outside the border box. Every avatar sits flush\nagainst the inline start of a container that clips: `.historyCol` and\n`.list` scroll (and `overflow-y: auto` clips the inline axis too), and\n`.commentsCol` is `overflow: hidden`. The ring lost its whole left edge —\nmeasured at exactly 3.0px — leaving a flat-sided avatar in the history,\nthe comment list and the composer, in both themes and on mobile.\n\nWiden each clip box outward by the ring width with a padding/negative\nmargin pair, which moves no content, and hold the width in\n`--ofelia-viewer-ring` so the shadow and the reserved room cannot drift.\n\n* fix(ofelia): size the account initial like the duty initial\n\n`MemberAvatar` scaled its initial to half the box while `Avatar` uses\n0.4, and the history phrase renders both at `px={18}` on the same line —\n9px next to 7.2px, which reads as two mismatched type sizes rather than\none set.\n\nShare the ratio through `AVATAR_INITIAL_RATIO`. Taking `Avatar`''s value\nkeeps every pre-existing avatar (the 62px hero, the 26px week strip)\npixel-identical and moves only the account squares this branch added.\n\n* fix(ofelia): raise the standard tier to the height it actually needs\n\nStandardTier lays out to 298px of content, but `tiers.standard` asked for\nonly 200px and the widget card clips instead of scrolling. At the default\nplacement (413x230px) the tier therefore resolved and then cut its own\naction row in half: \"Уборка подтверждена\" was sliced through and \"В долг\"\n/ \"Простить\" never appeared at all.\n\nState the height the tier needs, so anything shorter degrades to\n`compact` rather than rendering truncated, and raise `defaultSize` to\n`{ w: 4, h: 8 }` (~413x310px) so a freshly-placed widget still clears it\n— the same fix shape as the earlier default-placement regression.\n\n* fix(ofelia): let the pinned day header cover the reserved ring strip\n\nThe history column reserves `--ofelia-viewer-ring` of inline-start room\nso the viewer ring is not clipped, and records paint into that strip. The\nsticky day header''s own box stops at the content edge, so the strip stayed\nuncovered: scrolling avatars slid past the pinned header through a 3px\ngap, measured against the column''s border box.\n\nStretch the header back over the strip with a negative inline-start margin\nand return the width as padding, so its background spans the full\nscrollport while nothing inside it moves.\n\n* feat(ofelia): scroll the history to the day picked in the week strip\n\nSelecting a day highlighted it in the strip and changed the panel, but\nleft the history where it was, so the records for that day had to be found\nby hand.\n\n`HistoryList` now keeps a node per duty day and scrolls the matching group\nto the top of its scrollport when the selection changes. Exactly one\nancestor scrolls in each layout — the column on desktop, the dialog body\non mobile — so the native `scrollIntoView` cannot drag two scrollports at\nonce. The first run only records the mounted day: scrolling on mount would\npull the panel above the history off screen in the mobile layout before\nthe reader touched anything.\n\nThe column subscribes to a newly exposed `view.selectedIso` rather than\n`view.selected`, so confirming or forgiving the selected day still does\nnot re-render the history.\n\n* fix(ofelia): scroll only a box the reader can scroll back\n\n`scrollIntoView` moves *every* scroll container in the ancestor chain, and\n`overflow: hidden` boxes are scroll containers too — they just have no\nscrollbar. The widget frame is one: measured at 149px of overflow in the\nmobile layout, `overflow-y: hidden`. Switching a day therefore displaced\nthe whole widget, and with no scrollbar there the reader could not put it\nback.\n\nWalk up to the innermost box that is genuinely user-scrollable\n(`auto`/`scroll` *and* overflowing) and scroll that one by hand, aligning\nthe group with the scrollport top where the sticky header pins. When\nnothing in the chain scrolls there is nothing to reveal, so the layout is\nleft alone rather than nudged.\n\n* fix(ofelia): keep the mobile tab pane from clipping the viewer ring\n\nBounding the tab pane needs `overflow: hidden`, which clips the inline axis\ntoo — `overflow-x` cannot stay `visible` once the other axis is not. So in\nthe mobile layout `.split` becomes a second clipper, outside the columns\nand at the same inline start, and it shaved off the 3px viewer ring that\nthe columns had already reserved room for. Measured at `cutL: 3` on the\ncomment and record avatars; on desktop `.split` is `overflow: visible`, so\nthis only ever showed on narrow screens.\n\nReach outward by the ring width there too, the same padding/negative-margin\npair t…","tree":{"sha":"45cf3360a63572b1e89fa5a903e36b116a627e9c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/git/trees/45cf3360a63572b1e89fa5a903e36b116a627e9c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/git/commits/0cfc5f1dbfd9533ac227db0453a6a82971a9072e","verification":{"payload":"tree 45cf3360a63572b1e89fa5a903e36b116a627e9c\nparent ea12a9cd55976786e9d268e5f8aa9de2d6bfd27f\nauthor khmilevoi <Khmilevoi@gmail.com> 1785628638 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785628638 +0200\n\nrelease: passport-checker tiny/standard/fullscreen redesign (#38)\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the unmount-cleanup test teeth and fix a flaky Escape assert\n\nRecoveryModal.test.tsx''s `leaves focus alone once the modal is gone` test\npassed even when the cleanup''s `removeEventListener(''focusin'', ...)` was\ndeleted, or the whole effect cleanup was zeroed out: after unmount the\nportal root is detached, so a leaked focusin listener calling `.focus()` on\na detached descendant is a jsdom/browser no-op and the activeElement assert\nstays green regardless. Pin the actual cleanup contract instead: spy on\nwindow.addEventListener/removeEventListener and assert the exact `focusin`\nlistener useModalIsolation registers on mount is the one removed on unmount,\nalongside the original behavioral assert.\n\nrecovery-flow.test.tsx''s first test asserted\n`dialog.contains(document.activeElement)` synchronously right after\n`findByRole(''dialog'')` resolves on DOM commit, before dispatching Escape.\n`useModalIsolation`''s mount effect (which moves focus in and attaches the\nEscape listener) is passive and can still be pending a tick later, so\nEscape could fire before the listener existed and the test would hang out\nits timeout — the same race Task 4 root-caused and fixed in this file''s\nother two Escape-closing tests by waiting for focus-in-dialog first. Apply\nthe same `await waitFor(...)` wrap here and correct the file-level comment,\nwhich had claimed only two tests reach the Escape-close race when this one\ndoes too.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the modal focusout guard against narrowing\n\nMount the underlying Radix dialog in its own pass, as production does, and\ncover the intra-modal focus move that the proposed guard narrowing would have\nbroken silently. The regression test dispatches the underlying focusout event\ndirectly rather than chaining native .focus() calls: jsdom (like real\nbrowsers) always completes an outer .focus() call on its own target once its\nhandler stack unwinds, so a settled document.activeElement assertion can''t\nobserve a reclaim a nested handler only ever wins transiently — verified\nempirically against the narrowed guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): close a cross-test focus-restore leak in the Radix stack suite\n\nRadix''s FocusScope defers its unmount focus-restore to a real setTimeout(0),\nand testing-library''s auto cleanup() never waits for it, so a prior test''s\ndeferred restore (and an unawaited NoVncCanvas state update from that same\ntest''s async recovery flow) can bleed into the next test — confirmed by\ninstrumentation. A formal trace of the reentrant focus cascade shows the\n''pulls focus back...'' assertion itself can''t actually be flipped by this\n(no yield point exists between its synchronous .focus() call and its\nexpect()), but the leak is real and pre-existing to any suite that\nmounts+unmounts a trapped FocusScope, so close it defensively: flush one\nreal tick after each test''s cleanup so Radix''s deferred work always\nfinishes before the next test starts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): drain Radix''s focus-restore timer from beforeEach, not afterEach\n\ne797d50''s message overstated what that change achieved. It claimed the added\nafterEach ran \"strictly after cleanup()\" and closed the cross-test focus-restore\nleak. It did neither. Vitest resolves sequence.hooks to \"stack\", so same-suite\nafterEach hooks run in REVERSE registration order, and testing-library''s\ncleanup() -- registered first, at setupFiles import time -- therefore runs last.\nThe file-level afterEach ran BEFORE cleanup(), i.e. before the unmount that\nschedules Radix''s setTimeout(0) focus restore, so it drained nothing.\n\nInstrumenting setTimeout and the hook boundaries on this file shows it directly:\nat afterEach entry the modal is still in the DOM and no FocusScope timer is\npending; the timer is scheduled only after the hook returns; the next test''s\nbeforeEach starts with one still queued. Moving the same one-tick flush to\nbeforeEach takes that count to zero before the test body runs. beforeEach is\ncorrect by construction -- it always follows the previous test''s afterEach\nhooks whatever the hook-order mode.\n\nCorrect the scope of the claim too. The restore is focus-neutral in this\nfixture: the previouslyFocusedElement it captured is either document.body,\nwhich jsdom refuses to focus, or an already-detached node, and every observed\nfiring left document.activeElement unchanged. The intermittent failure this was\nchasing did not reproduce at e797d50 in 30 consecutive single-file runs or 6\nwhole-package runs. So this closes a scheduling leak, not an observed failure,\nand the comment now says exactly that instead of claiming otherwise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): link the tier-shared-state follow-up\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): drop the phantom rebuild from the instance store''s connect hook\n\nThe connect hook carried `current ??= { value: make() }` behind a comment\nclaiming it gave a reconnect after disposal a fresh value. Measured against\n@reatom/core@1001.1.0 with the shipped code:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 2\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"],[{\"id\":2},\"a\"]]   built = 2\n\nThe reconnect still returned the DISPOSED value — a dependency-free `computed`\nnever recomputes, so its cached result is what every later read gets. The line\nonly built a second model graph nobody could reach and then handed it to\n`dispose` a second time. Same run with the line removed:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n\nThe comment now states what is true: a stale reconnect reads the cached,\nalready-disposed value, and a fresh value comes only from a new `store()`\nlookup — which is what every real caller does, because it calls `store()`\nduring render and the cleanup has already evicted the stale handle.\n\nWith the phantom build gone, `if (held)` is what prevents the double dispose,\nso a test pins it: replacing it with an unconditional `dispose?.(held?.value,\nkey)` now fails with a second teardown recorded as `[undefined, \"a\"]`.\n\nAlso corrects the module doc''s cost claim for reading atoms inside `make`.\nIt does not \"silently rebuild the whole value\": measured, after such a\ndependency changes the compute function re-runs once, `current ??=`\nshort-circuits it so `make` does not re-run (built stayed 1, the identical\nobject was returned, no subscriber notified), and because the re-run never\nreads the atom again the edge is dropped. The cost is a pointless dependency\nedge, not lost state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): align the tier-shared-state spec with what shipped\n\nThree claims in the design spec described work that was never delivered:\n\n- The instance atom was said to carry `withDisconnectHook`. It carries\n  `withConnectHook`, whose cleanup calls `teardownIfDisconnected()` — which\n  re-checks `isConnected` before disposing and evicts the map entry only while\n  it still points at the handle being torn down.\n- Section 4 \"StrictMode removal\" claimed `<StrictMode>` was dropped from\n  `packages/client/src/app/main.tsx` and replaced by a comment explaining its\n  absence. None of it shipped: `git diff cfff8d2..HEAD -- packages/client/` is\n  empty and the wrapper is still there. Measurement showed the store survives\n  the remount cycle, because the cleanup''s `isConnected` re-check returns\n  without touching a value a live mount still holds. Section 4 is rewritten as\n  \"StrictMode kept, tolerance pinned by test\", stating why the plan''s original\n  reasoning does not apply.\n- The Testing gate, the Documentation list and the Decision Summary repeated\n  the removal. They now say StrictMode stays and that the tolerance is pinned\n  by `describe(''makeWidgetInstanceStore under StrictMode'')`.\n\nThat last claim is measured, not assumed: deleting the `isConnected` re-check\nfrom the cleanup turns two of the three StrictMode tests red, along with the\nnon-StrictMode same-tick-resubscribe test.\n\nThe plan file is left untouched — it is the historical record of what was\nintended.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): narrow unmeasured claims and close two coverage gaps\n\nComment/claim accuracy — all of these stated more than was ever measured:\n\n- `ui/recovery-flow.test.tsx`: the \"roughly 1 in 15-40 runs\" hang frequency\n  was applied to every Escape-closing test in the file. It was measured for\n  exactly one of them, ''does not collapse when recovery opens from the tile''\n  (task-4-report.md, Fix round 3). The others carry the same wait by\n  inspection — identical shape, identical race — with no observed rate. The\n  comment now says so.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"at `beforeEach` entry one (later\n  two) FocusScope timer is pending\" — only the 1 -> 0 transition was ever\n  recorded, so the \"(later two)\" is dropped.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"jsdom (like real browsers) always\n  completes an outer `.focus()` call on its own target\" — true and measured\n  for jsdom, never measured against a real browser. Scoped to jsdom.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: ''keeps focus inside when it moves\n  between two of our own controls'' promised a focus invariant the test does\n  not assert — it asserts that our guard swallows the `focusout` before it\n  reaches `document`. Renamed to say that.\n\nCoverage:\n\n- `model/instance-store.test.ts` (new) covers `dispose: (models) =>\n  models.recoveryModel.teardown()`, the safety net against leaking a live VNC\n  session when the modal outlived the widget. It had no test at all: replacing\n  the callback with a no-op left the whole widget suite green. Verified both\n  new tests fail against that no-op.\n- `ui/PassportChecker.test.tsx`: two assertions used\n  `expect(await screen.findAllBy*(…)).toHaveLength(2)`, which cannot retry —\n  `findAllBy*` resolves on the first snapshot with >= 1 match, so it settles on\n  one mount and never waits for the second. Both are now a re-querying\n  `waitFor`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(backlog): record the two-modal focus ping-pong as a deferred defect\n\nBoth `useModalIsolation` instances register focus containment on `window` in\nthe capture phase, so two mounted modals each see the other''s `focusin` before\nthe root-level `stopPropagation()` can stop it, and each pulls focus into its\nown root — forever.\n\nReproduced in jsdom 29.1.1 with two `useModalIsolation` roots mounted at once:\none modal performs 1 focus pull on mount; two mounted together never settle,\nand a hard test guard at 400 pulls had to stop the run (402 recorded).\n\nDeferred rather than fixed because it is unreachable through the UI: the\npassport checker is the only owner of such a modal, renders at most one\n(non-fullscreen mount only), and its overlay is a full-viewport\n`pointer-events: auto` layer whose `pointerdown` handler closes the modal, so\na second one cannot be opened over the first.\n\nThe entry notes the likely fix — a module-level stack of active modal roots\nwith containment applied only by the topmost.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): correct five unverified claims left by code review\n\nBring wording in the tier-shared-state spec, BACKLOG.md, and a test\ncomment in line with what was actually measured/read: the instance\nstore''s dependency-edge cost (not a rebuilt value), a stale\nuse-modal-isolation.ts line citation (:53 -> :55, two places), the\nroot focusin stopper''s actual bubble phase (not capture), and which\nrecovery-flow tests keep the shared timeout default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay at this branch\n\nPins rpi.dev.toml''s source.branch to feat/passport-checker-widget so the dev\nenvironment runs this PR instead of the dev branch.\n\nREVERT BEFORE MERGING: once this PR lands on dev, source.branch must go back to\n\"dev\" or the dev overlay stays pinned to a branch that no longer moves.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): drop the stale Chromium singleton lock before launch\n\nThe Chromium profile is a named Docker volume, so `SingletonLock` keeps the\n`<hostname>-<pid>` of whichever container wrote it. After a redeploy the new\ncontainer has a new hostname, Chromium reads a lock owned by \"another computer\"\nit cannot probe, and `launchPersistentContext` fails on every task until the\nvolume is touched by hand.\n\nRemove `SingletonLock`, `SingletonCookie`, and `SingletonSocket` before each\nlaunch. `fs.rm` unlinks the symlinks themselves instead of following them, and\n`force` keeps a fresh profile from failing; a removal that does fail is returned\nas a value, logged, and the launch still proceeds. Unconditional removal is safe\nbecause `getPersistentContext` is the only caller of `launch` and the image\nentrypoint runs a single `node dist/index.cjs` - no check for a live Chromium is\nperformed or implied.\n\nAlso asserts that a failed launch keeps its cause, which is what makes the\nPlaywright message reachable from the service log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): log the error behind an internal task failure\n\nInternal failures were logged as `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` only, so the\ntagged error and its cause chain never reached the container log. A Chromium\nlaunch failure therefore showed up as a bare `internal` code with nothing to\ndiagnose. Add the error to the logged fields; `toEnvelopeError` still answers\nthe client with the redacted `internal` code, so only the log gains detail.\n\nNote the trade-off: a handler error whose message carries task data now reaches\nthe container log, which the previous field list deliberately avoided.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): redact handler failures in the internal task log\n\nfa87a47c logged too much. It added `error: outcome` to the internal-failure log\nso a Chromium launch failure would stop being invisible, but that field carries\nwhatever the handler threw. errore''s `toJSON()` serialises `message`, `stack`,\nand the whole `cause` chain, Playwright quotes `page.evaluate` arguments into\nits messages, and the passport widget passes the secret identity as one — so a\nhandler failure could print the passport series and number into the container\nlog. The wrapper''s own `stack` repeats the cause under `Caused by:`, so the\nidentity landed there twice.\n\nThat contradicts the design spec: raw Playwright, network, Zod, and upstream\nresponse details are logged only after redaction, request and response bodies\nare never logged, and logs redact task payloads and secret values\n(docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md\n:350-353, :684).\n\nThe rule now, verified against dispatch.ts:\n\n- `BrowserExecutorError` wraps a failed `executor.acquire(...)`. Acquire runs\n  before the handler and is passed only `(signal, widgetId)` — never the payload\n  or the widget secrets — so its cause chain is Chromium/Playwright launch\n  detail. Still logged in full; this is the stale singleton lock failure\n  fa87a47c existed to surface.\n- Every other internal failure wraps handler output and is reduced to\n  `errorChain`: the names/tags of the error and its `cause` chain and nothing\n  else. No `message`, no `stack`, no foreign properties.\n\nThe log message, the `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` fields, and the\n`code === ''internal''` condition are unchanged.\n\nTests split the single fa87a47c case in two: one pins that an executor failure''s\ncause chain still reaches the log, one asserts a secret-looking cause string is\nabsent from the serialised logged payload. Both were confirmed to fail before\nthe fix and to discriminate after it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add an env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe passport widget''s recovery path (retain the page, issue a single-use\ncapability, stream the live browser over noVNC, let a human solve the\nchallenge, retry) has never been exercised on real hardware, because it only\ntriggers when pasport.org.ua serves a Cloudflare challenge and none has been\nencountered — the smoke test on the Pi returned a normal result. Subproject 8\n(`passport-checker-rpi-integration` in\ndocs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md)\nhas \"embedded noVNC Cloudflare recovery, SSH fallback, and real checker smoke\ntest on the Raspberry Pi\" as an acceptance item and had no way to reach it on\ndemand. PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY is that way.\n\nContract: `1` and nothing else enables it — absent, empty, `0`, `true`, ` 1 `\nare all off (isForcedRecoveryEnabled does exact string equality, no trimming,\nno truthiness coercion). Compose passes it through with a `:-` default so an\nunset variable stays off, and it must never be set in production.\n\nSemantics: at most once per process. The latch lives in the closure\nmakePassportCheckHandler returns, so each handler owns its own and the unit\ntests stay isolated. It fires after a successful goto and after\ncollectNavigationEvidence — the retained page is the real checker page with the\nsite''s own UI loaded, so a human can drive it over noVNC — and returns exactly\nthe retainPageForRecovery() + BrowserSessionRequiredError pair the genuine\nCloudflare branch returns, making the downstream flow byte-identical to a real\nchallenge. The latch is consumed only when the forced path actually fires, so a\nfailed navigation returns its own error untouched and leaves the lever armed:\nan infrastructure hiccup must not burn the single forced run an operator is\nwaiting on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): apply the design-system widget redesign\n\nBring the tile and the recovery modal in line with Паспорт-чекер.dc.html.\n\nBoth tiers now lay out as header -> flexible middle -> action, so the\naction sits on the tile''s bottom edge in every state instead of trailing\nwhatever content happens to be above it. Status banners pick up the\ndesign''s per-state hues, the filled success disc, and the CircleAlert /\nTriangleAlert split that separates a retryable failure from a session\nprompt. The modal gains the access-pill dot, a pulsing LIVE dot, framed\nconnecting/disconnected/expired states, and the shell-style SSH block.\n\nDark theme was effectively missing: the --pc-* hues chained to\n--success-soft / --destructive-soft, which the host token set never\ndefines, and to --success, which is a dark surface green rather than an\nicon green under [data-theme=''dark'']. Every hue is now declared per\ntheme the way ofelia-poop-duty does it. The modal portals to\ndocument.body, outside .widget, so it declares the one hue it needs on\nits own overlay.\n\nThree deliberate departures from the mockup:\n- invalidConfig copy stays server-side (\"Обратитесь к администратору\"),\n  since this widget has no per-instance settings — its config lives in\n  server secrets.\n- the expired frame keeps \"Переподключиться\"; without it an expired\n  capability can only be recovered by closing and reopening the modal.\n- the sessionRequired tiny tile only fills amber, no border: the tile\n  border belongs to the host card, and drawing one from inside the\n  widget would render a second, inset contour.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xwGNctwqXcYi5p4QSSFhA\n\n* docs(browser-automation): design widget user-input detection\n\nMove the \"a human must act\" mechanism out of passport-checker and into the\nautomation platform. The handler gets context.detectUserInput(detector), which\nruns the detector, optionally prepares the page, retains it and returns the\ncanonical error as one indivisible step; retainPageForRecovery leaves the public\ncontext so the halves cannot be separated. Cloudflare becomes a reusable helper\n(probe and predicate together, not just the predicate) that any widget can pass\nin. Also records the removal of the PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY lever.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): correct the user-input spec''s test-seam claims\n\nmakeFakeExecutor fakes BrowserExecutor<FakeContext> with FakeContext = { id,\nsignal }; it never builds a BrowserTaskContext, so it cannot host a recording\ndetectUserInput and needs no change at all. Name the seams that do exist: the\nsix retainPageForRecovery call sites in chromium-executor.test.ts and the three\nBrowserTaskContext literals in diagnostics.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): plan the widget user-input detection refactor\n\nSeven tasks, each green on its own: retire the forced-recovery lever, add the\ndetector and error contracts, move Cloudflare detection into the platform with\nits probe reunited with its predicate, run escalation from the task context,\nrewire the widget, then withdraw retainPageForRecovery from the public context so\nthe retain/escalate pair cannot be taken apart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): make the Cloudflare task a move, not a copy\n\nTask 3 previously left the classifier duplicated between the widget and the\nplatform until Task 5, which any reviewer would rightly flag as verbatim\nduplication of a logic block. The widget now switches to the moved module in the\nsame task, so the logic never exists in two places.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* revert(passport-checker): drop the env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe lever forced one trip through the Cloudflare recovery path per service\nstart so Subproject 8 could exercise noVNC on the Pi on demand. It is being\nretired deliberately ahead of moving user-input detection into the platform;\nthe Pi smoke test needs another answer.\n\nThis reverts the behaviour added in 45c604f1.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(browser-automation): add user-input detector and error contracts\n\nUserInputRequiredError carries the browser_session_required code and sshTarget\nmeta the recovery flow keys off, moved out of the passport-checker widget where\na platform contract had no business living. UserInputProbeError distinguishes\na broken check from a negative one.\n\n* feat(browser-automation): move Cloudflare detection into the platform\n\nThe classifier lived in the passport-checker widget while the DOM probe feeding\nit lived in that widget''s check handler, so the only reusable half was the half\nwithout the probe. Both now sit in browser-automation behind two detector\nfactories: one that probes the live page, one that classifies evidence already\nderived inside the page from a fetch response.\n\nThe widget imports the moved module rather than keeping a copy; it still calls\nthe classifier by hand, which the next commit changes.\n\n* @\ndocs: document the feature branch -> dev -> main workflow\n\nFeature work happens in a dedicated branch inside a ./.worktrees checkout,\nlands in dev through a PR, gets verified on the dev stack, and only then\nreaches main through a dev -> main PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* feat(browser-automation): run user-input detection from the task context\n\ndetectUserInput runs a widget-supplied detector and, on a match, prepares the\npage, retains it and returns UserInputRequiredError as one step, so the two\nhalves cannot drift apart. A detector that fails yields UserInputProbeError and\ndeliberately leaves the page unretained: an undecidable check is not a challenge,\nand pinning a browser page for a recovery nobody will perform is worse.\n\nAUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET moves into the service config, where an unusable value\ndegrades to null rather than failing config loading and killing the service.\n\n* docs(passport-checker): design last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* test(browser-automation): cover the prepare-before-retain order in detectUserInput directly\n\nThe executor-level test asserted hasRetainedPage() from inside the prepare\nmock, but that bookkeeping is only populated at release() time, so it passed\nregardless of call order. Add a direct unit test against makeDetectUserInput\nthat records prepare/retain into one ordered array — genuinely fails if the\norder in detect.ts is inverted (verified by temporarily inverting it).\n\nNo production behavior changes; detect.ts ends identical to before this commit.\n\n* @\ndocs: merge AGENTS.md into CLAUDE.md and symlink it\n\nAGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md had drifted into two partly overlapping rule sets.\nEverything unique to AGENTS.md (project structure, codegen commands, Windows\ntest-runner notes, coding style, UI gotchas, testing, security, failure modes)\nnow lives in CLAUDE.md, and AGENTS.md becomes a symlink so every agent reads\nthe same file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* docs(passport-checker): plan last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* feat(passport-checker): date-aware check timestamp label\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout design\n\nTwo width breakpoints instead of one hardcoded grid: one column below 768,\ntwelve above, with row height and margin scaled by the same factor as the\ncolumn width so the desktop grid zooms instead of stretching on one axis.\n\nThe mobile layout is derived from the desktop one and only persisted once the\nuser drags or resizes, stored as an additive optional `mobileLayout` field so\nno migration of `root:boards` is needed.\n\nAlso records the react-draggable touchstart defect: the whole card is the drag\nhandle today, which cancels page scrolling on touch — harmless on a short\n12-column board, blocking at one column.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): detect required user input through the platform\n\nThe handler no longer collects Cloudflare evidence, classifies it, retains the\npage and builds the escalation error by hand at every site; it passes a detector\nto context.detectUserInput and returns what comes back. browser/challenge.ts and\nthe widget-local BrowserSessionRequiredError are gone — the classifier now lives\nin browser-automation next to the probe that feeds it, and the\nbrowser_session_required contract belongs to the platform that owns the recovery\nflow.\n\n* fix(passport-checker): restore the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nAn automated amend from a concurrent session accidentally stripped this\nline out of 3a12494e. It belongs to the user-input-detection work; this\nrestores it verbatim.\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nRETRYABLE_MESSAGES.user_input_probe vanished twice with no test turning red,\nbecause nothing asserted the actual behaviour: with the entry gone,\nmapCheckError silently falls back to GENERIC_RETRYABLE_MESSAGE and the user\nsees the generic string instead of the specific one. Assert the real output\nof mapCheckError against a hardcoded expected string, not against the table\nitself, so a future deletion or typo of the entry fails this test instead of\ncomparing the map against itself.\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron design spec\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout implementation plan\n\n* feat(board): resolve grid metrics from the container width\n\n* feat(passport-checker): persist the last successful check\n\n* feat(board): derive and reconcile the one-column mobile layout\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron implementation plan\n\nAlso corrects the spec: the jsdom Temporal shims must stay, only the\n--harmony-temporal execArgv becomes redundant on Node 26.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin which detector goes to which detectUserInput call\n\nNothing previously checked that the navigation check gets the page detector\nand the submission check gets the evidence detector -- only the second call''s\nprepare option was asserted. A handler that swapped the two factories would\nleave every test in this file green; only the gated real-browser suite would\nnotice, and it isn''t run in CI. Pull the captured detector off each mock call\nand invoke it directly: the page detector must reach for page.evaluate, the\nevidence detector must ignore its page argument and classify the real\nevidence. Also drops the now-inert hasChallengeForm evidence field from the\nsubmission-escalation test, whose result was always driven by the mock''s\nescalation queue regardless of that field.\n\n* feat(board): add mobile layout materialize, update and reset actions\n\n* refactor(browser-automation): expose only detectUserInput on the task context\n\nretainPageForRecovery was half of a pair a handler had to assemble correctly by\nhand: retaining without escalating strands a browser page, escalating without\nretaining hands the UI a recovery session with nothing behind it. With the retain\nflag reachable only through detectUserInput, neither mistake is expressible.\n\n* test(passport-checker): cover restore-on-mount and cross-placement sync\n\nAdd two widget-level cases pinning storage-backed persistence through\nReact: a fresh mount renders a previously stored result without\ninvoking a check, and a check made in one placement is observed by a\nsecond placement sharing the same storage. Rename the pre-existing\n\"gives separate instance ids separate state\" case to \"gives separate\nstorages separate state\" (it isolates by storage, not instanceId) and\nupdate its comment to point at the new cross-placement case below it.\nPin PASSPORT_LAST_RESULT_KEY''s literal value in check-model.test.ts so\na silent rename cannot slip through green while orphaning deployed\nusers'' stored data under the old key.\n\n* feat(board): switch the grid to resolved metrics and a mobile drag grip\n\n* docs(browser-automation): document how a widget requests manual input\n\n* feat(board): offer a mobile layout reset in the board schema popover\n\n* fix(widgets): address passport-checker persistence review findings\n\n- TinyTier''s success chip now shows checkedAtLabel, so a restored (possibly\n  stale) result reads honestly in the compact tile too, not just standard.\n- Harden the two-placement isolation test: assert placement two stays idle\n  instead of a length-1 findAllByText that can pass mid-race.\n- Clarify why lastResult/transient set order is safe in checkPassport\n  (Reatom notifies after the sync block, not \"order for readability\").\n- Add a test exercising lastResultSchema.safeParse over the exact shape\n  checkPassport writes, since createFakeStorage skips schema validation.\n- Document lastResultSchema as a persistence contract: new fields must be\n  optional/defaulted or every stored value fails validation on next deploy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* fix(passport-checker): remove dead AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET threading\n\nrecoverySshTarget was never read by check.ts''s handler after the\ndetectUserInput refactor moved sshTarget sourcing entirely into the\nplatform config. Delete the widget''s leftover env read and option\nthreading so the README''s \"widgets neither read nor pass it\" claim is\nactually true, and drop the now-meaningless SSH-sanitization test in\nfavor of the equivalent (and broader) coverage in browser-automation''s\nconfig.test.ts.\n\n* docs(ofelia): design the authored ledger and comments\n\nRecords get the authenticated account instead of the per-device \"Я:\"\ntoggle, the write path moves into the widget''s own server.ts, and the\nhistory row is rebuilt: human dates, plain-language phrases, explicit\ndebt effect, visible supersession, no IP.\n\nPulls the Node 26 prerequisite out of the widget-server-cron spec, since\nthe shared domain module it enables is needed here first.\n\nAlso imports the Claude Design workspace under design/ (documents only,\nscreenshots and uploads stripped) and keeps the brief that produced the\nOfelia mock.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs(ofelia): note the cron plan overlap and landing order\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): cover the mobile layout and touch-scroll behaviour end to end\n\n* fix(board): keep the measured grid container mounted\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a 1920 viewport\n\n* docs(ofelia): add the authored ledger implementation plan\n\n15 tasks: Node 26, the pure domain split, the members directory, widget\nidentity, the server-resolved viewer, record schemas, draft construction,\nthe widget server definition, the model rewire, the history and comment\nview models, the account avatar, and the two rebuilt panels.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): wait for the grid relayout to settle before measuring\n\n* fix(browser-automation): bundle workspace imports instead of externalizing them\n\nThe rspack externals predicate was an allow-list whose default was\n\"externalize\". When the user-input refactor introduced the first runtime\nbare-specifier import of a workspace package into the service module graph\n(browser-automation/user-input/cloudflare, from the passport-checker browser\ntask), it was silently externalized: the build stayed green, all tests stayed\ngreen, and the Cloudflare module was simply absent from dist/index.cjs. The\nruntime stage copies only package.json and dist/, so Node resolved the\nspecifier through the exports map to a .ts path that does not exist in the\nimage and the container died on `exec node dist/index.cjs`.\n\nInvert the predicate into a deny-list derived from the package''s own\ndependencies (minus errore, which is ESM-only and must be bundled) plus Node\nbuiltins. An unrecognised bare specifier is now bundled, so the same mistake\nsurfaces as a build-time resolution error rather than a startup crash, and\nadding a real runtime dependency externalizes it with no config edit.\n\nAdd scripts/check-bundle.ts, chained onto `build` so it runs inside the image\nbuild the Dockerfile already performs. It asserts that every require() in the\nbundle is a builtin or a declared dependency, that the scan still matches the\nemitted shape at all, and that the compiled evidenceFromResponseText the widget\nsplices into Chromium still evaluates and runs in a bare vm realm. That last\ncheck is what the BROWSER_IT suite could never prove: it runs under vitest,\nwhere the specifier resolves to .ts through esbuild and the swc path is never\nexercised. Verified: the compiled form is ES5-lowered but self-contained --\ndestructuring became inline property reads, no swc helpers, no hoisted regexes.\n\nAlso:\n- detect.ts: tag the prepare outcome instead of comparing against null, so a\n  prepare that rejects with exactly null is a failure rather than a silent\n  success, matching the detector branch''s `typeof detected !== ''boolean''`.\n- user-input/errors.test.ts: cover toEnvelopeError for UserInputRequiredError\n  with and without an ssh target, pinning with toStrictEqual that `meta` is\n  omitted rather than set to undefined. contracts.test.ts already claimed this\n  coverage lived here.\n- chromium-executor.test.ts: drop an assertion inside a prepare callback that\n  could not fail the test and was vacuous, and rename the case to what it\n  actually verifies; the ordering contract is owned by detect.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* test(board): settle past the grid transition and stop assuming card order\n\n* test(board): correct the stale comment above the breakpoint wait\n\n* chore(node): align every runtime and image on Node 26\n\nnode:26-alpine and node:26-bookworm-slim no longer bundle corepack\n(removed from Node core), so `npm install -g corepack@latest` is added\nahead of every `corepack enable` in the three Dockerfiles and\ndocker-compose.dev.yml. scripts/infra.test.ts''s assertion on the\nbrowser-automation Dockerfile''s base image is updated to match.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(node): unshadow jsdom localStorage and bump the e2e image to Node 26\n\nNode 26 ships its own unflagged global localStorage/sessionStorage (Web\nStorage API), which resolves to undefined without --localstorage-file\nand shadows jsdom''s window.localStorage for bare-identifier reads in\npackages/client''s jsdom test environment (40 failing tests). Disable\nNode''s own globals via execArgv: [''--no-experimental-webstorage''] in\npackages/client/vite.config.ts so jsdom''s storage wins; verified via\nObject.getOwnPropertyDescriptor that the flag actually removes the\nglobal rather than just suppressing the warning. widget-runtime and\nwidget-sdk don''t touch localStorage in tests today, so the flag is not\nadded to their execArgv arrays (narrowest correct remedy).\n\nAlso bumps packages/client/e2e.Dockerfile (used by pnpm test:e2e:docker\nvia docker-compose.e2e.yml) from node:22-bookworm-slim to\nnode:26-bookworm-slim, missed by the original brief''s Files list, with\nthe same corepack@latest install this Node 26 base image now needs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): make the relayout wait actually poll\n\n* refactor(ofelia): extract the pure duty domain out of the Reatom model\n\n* fix(lint): make the widget domain import barrier a real allowlist\n\nThe regex denylist let ''../model/ofelia-duty'' through — domain/ and model/\nare siblings, so a single-''../'' import was both the most likely accidental\nescape and completely unguarded. It also only enumerated known packages, so\nany new npm dependency passed silently.\n\nReplace it with a gitignore-style group allowlist (''**'' minus zod, @shared/*\nand ''./'' siblings), which oxlint evaluates natively. A negative-lookahead\nregex was tried first and rejected: oxlint''s Rust regex engine has no\nlookaround and silently drops such a pattern, disabling the whole barrier.\n\nDomain tests get a second, later override that additionally allows vitest;\neverything else stays blocked there too.\n\n* chore(lint): keep the design mock exports out of lint and format\n\ndesign/ holds vendored Claude Design exports (support.js, *.dc.html)\nthat break the repo''s only gate: oxlint reports 9 errors in\ndesign/support.js, and oxfmt aborts (exit 2) on non-standard markup\nin design/Design System.dc.html before it can check anything else.\n\nAdd design/** to the existing ignorePatterns glob array in both\n.oxlintrc.json and .oxfmtrc.json, the same mechanism already used for\ndocs/**. This is a plain glob matcher (not the Rust regex engine\nbehind no-restricted-imports), so it isn''t subject to that engine''s\nlookaround gap, and it leaves every other rule/override untouched.\n\nFixing the oxfmt abort also uncovered pre-existing, unrelated\nformatting drift in CLAUDE.md, package.json and\nscripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts that the crash had been masking from\nthe format:check summary. Reformatted them (whitespace/ordering only,\nno logic changes) so pnpm format:check is actually green end to end.\n\n* feat(auth): expose the board members directory\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): expose the board identity to widgets\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): stop identity fetch from leaking an unhandled rejection and a discarded error\n\nHandle the AbortError that wrap()''s promise rejects with when the last\nwidget reading identity unmounts mid-fetch, and stop discarding\nBoardMembersError whole: a 401 stays silent (expected on an\nunauthenticated host), everything else is surfaced via console.warn so\nschema drift or a server fault is not silently swallowed.\n\n* feat(widgets): resolve the signed-in viewer for widget server events\n\n* feat(ofelia): carry the authoring account on ledger and comment records\n\n* feat(ofelia): move draft construction and event schemas into the domain\n\n* feat(ofelia): write the ledger and comments from the widget server\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): harden server date parsing and unforgeability coverage\n\n- Wrap Temporal.PlainDate.from in errore.try so a calendar-invalid but\n  regex-valid date (e.g. 2026-02-30) returns an Error instead of\n  throwing through a function typed Error | T.\n- Add regression coverage: forged createdBy/actor in the payload is\n  ignored, the debt handler is exercised, storage-read failures are\n  asserted by identity with no write, and the invalid-date case is\n  asserted as a value.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): split the unforgeability guard into schema and handler halves\n\nThe round-1 forged-payload test went through run(), which parses the\npayload via zod''s default strip mode before the handler ever sees it —\nso it only proved the schema strips forged fields, twice over, and\nwould not catch a handler that starts spreading its raw payload into\nthe draft. Replace it with two schema-level tests (clean + comment\npayloads drop a forged createdBy/actor) and one handler-level test\nthat calls ofeliaServer.handlers.clean directly, bypassing the schema,\nto prove the handler itself never reads those fields even if they\nreach it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* feat(ofelia): drive ledger and comment writes through widget server events\n\nThe client no longer appends to the ledger or the comment key. All four\nday actions collapse into one `invokeDay` that sends `{ date }` to the\nmatching widget-server event, and `send` sends `{ weekStart, text }` —\nwho the actor is, whose debt moves, whether the event applies at all and\nwhich account signed it are now derived and stamped server-side, so a\nclient cannot forge authorship.\n\n`selectedDate()` and `today()` (and `viewWeekStart()`) are read before\nthe first await so they resolve against the widget''s own Reatom context.\n\nThe per-device \"Я: Леша/Карина\" toggle is deleted rather than hidden:\nauthorship comes from the session, so there is nothing left to pick.\n`currentUser` is gone from the duty model, the widget context, the\nfixture and the view-model''s `onSetUser`.\n\nBoth model factories now take `api` and `identity`; `identity` is the\ndirectory the history and comment views read in the follow-up tasks.\nThe view projections drop `ip`/`by`, whose schema fields Tasks 5-8\nremoved, and `authorName` falls back through `createdBy.name`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* style: format the ofelia server test\n\n* feat(ofelia): group history by duty day and mark superseded records\n\n* feat(ofelia): resolve comment authors through the members directory\n\n* fix(ofelia): flush the comment thread''s connect hook via vi.waitFor, not a bare microtask\n\nReview round 1: the timing fix for the comment-thread subscribe/emit race\nassumed a single-microtask flush of Reatom''s connect-hook queue. Match the\nsibling comments-subscription tests in the same file instead — wait for the\nstorage mock''s subscribe call and wrap the whole body in context.start, so\nthe assertion is condition-based rather than scheduling-internals-based.\n\n* feat(ofelia): add the account avatar and its palette\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the history list around duty-day groups\n\n* fix(ofelia): announce the history phrase subject to assistive tech\n\nThe duty circle is the sentence subject in the rebuilt history phrase, and\nAvatar is aria-hidden, so the column announced as bare verbs with no subject.\nPair every circle with a visually hidden name, attribute the debt pill to its\nperson, and separate the chips with real spaces as the mock does.\n\nAlso correct the text-decoration comment to name the real mechanism (atomic\ninline, not descendant cancellation) and cover the three untested branches.\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the comment thread around account authors\n\n* fix(ofelia): restore composer coverage and mute the unknown comment author\n\n* fix(ofelia): break ledger day ties by append order\n\nRouting the writes through the widget server changed where `ts` comes from.\n`/api/storage/:key/append` stamped `Date.now()`, so consecutive appends always\ngot a strictly increasing timestamp. The widget dispatch stamps the app''s\ninjectable `now()`, and the e2e harness pins that clock — so a clean-then-undo\npair on the same day produced two entries with an identical `ts`, and\n`latestOutcomesByDate`''s strict `>` kept the earlier `cleaned` entry as the\nday''s outcome. The `reset` was written but never counted, and the day never\nreopened; `pnpm test:e2e:docker` caught it as a failing \"undo — returns the day\nto pending\".\n\nThe ledger is append-only and iterated in insertion order, so `>=` is the\ncorrect tie-break: on an equal timestamp the later-appended entry is the newer\ndecision. This also hardens production, where two writes can still land in the\nsame millisecond.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: describe server-authored widget records\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): fail widget dispatch when the session account is unreadable\n\nresolveWidgetViewer folded every getAccount failure into `null`, so a Valkey\nfault, a corrupt account record and a deleted account all produced a record\nstamped `createdBy: null` plus a `{ ok: true }` response. The ledger is\nappend-only, so that authorship could never be recovered.\n\nThe three outcomes are now distinct: no session still returns `null`, which is\nwhat the standalone harnesses and the e2e stack (no nginx, so no session cookie)\nrely on to keep writing unattributed records; a failed lookup returns a\nWidgetViewerLookupError, which the dispatch route turns into a fault instead of\na false success.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): order superseded history records by append order\n\n`superseded` sorted on `right.ts - left.ts`, which returns 0 on a tie; the\nstable sort then left tied records in append order, oldest first, contradicting\nthe \"newest first\" contract on HistoryDayGroup. Ties became a supported case\nwhen latestOutcomesByDate started breaking them with `>=`, and the dockerized\ne2e stack freezes the clock, so there every record ties.\n\nThe ledger is append-only, so append order is chronological order: reversing the\nday''s bucket is both correct and cheaper than sorting.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): warn when listAccounts skips an unreadable record\n\nA corrupt or schema-mismatched account record was dropped silently, so the\nroster simply came back short and the symptom read as \"an account vanished\"\nrather than as the data corruption it is. Name the key in a console.warn.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* build: pin corepack to 0.35.0 across images and dev compose\n\n`npm install -g corepack@latest` ran in five image build stages and four dev\ncompose services. There is no CI and the Pi deploy is manual with a 30-minute\nbuild timeout, so a breaking corepack release would only ever surface as a\nfailed deploy. Pin one explicit version everywhere.\n\nVerified: `docker build -f packages/server/Dockerfile` succeeds and the built\nimage reports corepack 0.35.0 with pnpm 10.28.2.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* refactor(ofelia): delete the dead short-date formatter\n\nformatDateShort and MONTHS_SHORT lost their last production consumer when the\ncomment thread moved to its own formatter; only the test still called them. It\nalso formatted through `new Date(ts)` in the local timezone, while every current\nformatter goes through plainDateIn(DUTY_TIME_ZONE, ...).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): drop the tier guards that cannot fail, hoist the env directive\n\nStandardTier and CompactTier each asserted they do not render UserToggle. The\ntoggle only ever lived in RichLayout and the component is now deleted outright,\nso neither assertion could ever fail — they only looked like coverage.\n\nAlso move the `@vitest-environment node` directive in server.test.ts back to\nline 1, where a reader expects it and where model/history-view.test.ts keeps it.\nVitest 4 matches the directive anywhere in the file content, so the file was\nalready running under node; this is readability, not a behaviour fix.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: state the authorship guarantee the code actually provides\n\nCLAUDE.md claimed a server-stamped author \"cannot be forged\". Nothing enforces\nthat: POST /api/storage/:key/append does no session check in Node, and nginx''s\nauth_request only proves a session exists, not whose it is, so any signed-in\nhousehold member can still hand-write a record carrying an arbitrary author.\n\nState what is true instead — the UI can no longer sign a record with someone\nelse''s name, and the generic storage route remains a shared trusted channel\ninside the board. Closing that route is a separate task.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(board): never render the desktop grid below its 1920 baseline\n\nThe desktop zoom is now one-directional: raise the lower clamp on the\ngrid scale from 0.75 to 1, so every container width between the mobile\nbreakpoint and BASE_WIDTH resolves to exactly the historical metrics\n(rowHeight: 30, margin: [10, 10]) and only wider displays scale up.\n\nUntil this branch, useContainerWidth never attached its observer and\npinned the reported width at its 1280 default on every screen. With the\ncontainer measured for real, a 0.75 floor would have shrunk every board\non a 1280/1366/1440 laptop below the size it has always had, dropping an\nh:6 card from 230px to 172.5px and pushing widgets under their own tier\nthresholds. Nothing shrinks now; the 4K aspect drift the scale was added\nfor is still fixed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling record\n\nBring the design doc in line with what shipped: the lower clamp is 1,\nthe 1280 table row shows the baseline metrics, and the paragraph\njustifying the clamp says plainly that it is active across the whole\n768-1920 band rather than a narrow 768-1100 one.\n\nAlso correct the claim that existing 1080p boards \"do not shift by a\npixel\". The formula at 1920 does reproduce the old constants, but the\nmeasured width was previously pinned at 1280 on every screen by the\nuseContainerWidth bug this branch fixes, so wider screens legitimately\nre-flow to use the whole window.\n\nDrop the 1920 viewport pin from ofelia-duty.spec.ts: it only worked\naround the 0.75 clamp, and removing it restores default-viewport\ncoverage. Recompute the footprint arithmetic in the ofelia client test\ncomment, which assumed a container of exactly 1280 rather than the\nviewport minus the board padding and the scrollbar gutter.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a viewport its tier needs\n\nEvery assertion in the duty spec is StandardTier UI, and the widget only\nresolves `standard` at a frame width of 400px or more. Playwright''s\ndefault 1280 viewport is about 7px short: the board measures its own\ncontainer, which is 1225px there once the stable scrollbar gutter and the\nboard''s 20px padding on each side come off, so a w:4 card is ~395px and\nits frame ~393px. `compact` is the correct rendering at that width, so\nthe spec has to declare the width its assertions need.\n\nThe dependency was previously invisible because the board reported a\nhardcoded 1280px container on every screen, which made the card 413px and\nleft the threshold only 13px of headroom against a measurement that was\nwrong by 55px.\n\nCorrect the footprint comment in the widget''s client test to match: the\ntier is measured on the frame inside the card''s 1px border, so a 1280px\nviewport yields ~393x228 and resolves to `compact`, not `standard`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling claims to match what ships\n\nThree statements asserted the opposite of what this branch established,\nall written before the width axis was measured.\n\nThe grid-metrics JSDoc claimed the clamp keeps 1280/1366/1440 boards\n\"the size they have always been\" and widgets \"above their own tier\nthresholds\". Both are false at 1280: the container is 1225 there, so a\nw:4 card is 395px where the old code drew 413px, and ofelia falls under\nits 400px standard threshold into compact. The clamp pins the metrics -\nrowHeight 30 and margin [10, 10] at every desktop width - not the\nrendered geometry, which still follows the measured container.\n\nThe design doc claimed \"no board shrinks and no widget loses a tier\".\nThe 1366 and 1440 frames (422px, 446px) clear the widget threshold; the\n1280 frame (393px) does not. Corrected to draw the metrics/geometry\ndistinction the document already draws two paragraphs earlier.\n\nThe \"do not shift by a pixel\" claim survived in the doc''s Testing\nsection and in the test''s own comment. At 1080p the container is now\n1865, so colWidth is 144.58 rather than the 95.83 the pinned-1280 bug\nproduced. Both now state what the test guarantees: the formula at\nBASE_WIDTH reproduces the previous hardcoded constants.\n\nAlso record the shipped consequence in Risks - ofelia renders\nCompactTier below ~1300px, correct behaviour on a genuinely 393px frame,\nwith recalibration left as a follow-up - and fix three figures: the tier\nparagraph attributed the frame inset to a card padding that does not\nexist (it is a 1px border; the heights are 148px and 298px, not 110 and\n260), the e2e headroom was 11px on the frame rather than 13px on the\ncard, the 1920 card/frame are 608/606, and useContainerWidth is at\nBoard.tsx:28.\n\nComments and prose only; no executable line changed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* build(deploy): add a per-branch rpi environment and deploy scripts\n\nrpi.branch.toml is a disposable stack for trying the checked-out branch on\nreal hardware, with ttl = \"72h\" so a forgotten one reaps itself. It takes the\nbranch through ${BRANCH_NAME}, the only variable rpi 0.26 accepts — hence the\nliteral hostname rather than a derived one.\n\nscripts/deploy-branch.ts reads the branch from git so nobody retypes it, and\nrefuses to deploy without .env.branch, which would otherwise produce a stack\nwhose WebAuthn gate fails with nothing in the deploy log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(deploy): send the branch secrets bundle before the build\n\nEvery branch is a fresh rpi project with an empty secrets store, so the very\nfirst deploy of any branch died after the ~3 minute image build with an opaque\nCompose \"secret file ... does not exist\". deploy:branch now checks\n`rpi secrets ls` and sends the bundle itself when there is none.\n\nAlso strip the `--` that `pnpm run x -- --flag` forwards verbatim: rpi''s parser\nreads a bare `--` as end-of-flags, so the flag after it was rejected as a\npositional.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(browser-automation): read the spliced callback when swc stops inlining it\n\ncheck-bundle extracted the page callback by slicing between `.concat(` and\n`.toString()`, which is the whole function expression only while swc inlines it.\nA second consumer of evidenceFromResponseText — precisely what the public\nbrowser-automation/user-input/cloudflare subpath exists to allow — makes swc emit\na named declaration instead, leaving a bare identifier at the splice site. The\nguard then evaluated that identifier in an empty realm, got a ReferenceError, and\nfailed the image build accusing a perfectly correct bundle of shipping a\nnon-self-contained callback.\n\nResolve the identifier to its declaration, and let the JavaScript parser find\nwhere that declaration ends: counting braces by hand would have to understand\nregex literals, and this body is full of regexes containing quote characters.\nExtraction failures now say so instead of reporting a self-containment defect.\n\nVerified by building all four ways: inlined and named shapes both pass; the\noriginal externals regression and a regex hoisted out of the callback are both\nstill caught.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* fix(ofelia): centre the initial in avatars re-cast to inline-flex\n\nBoth avatars centre their initial with `place-items: center`, but the\nhistory phrase re-casts the box to `inline-flex` so the circle stays in\nthe inline run, and a flex container ignores `justify-items`. The glyph\nwas left-aligned: in an 18px circle it sat 0px from the inline start and\n12.97px from the end, roughly a third of the diameter off centre.\n\nName the inline axis outright so the centring survives whichever display\na caller imposes. `.debt > [data-tone]` re-casts the same way and was hit\nby the same bug.\n\n* fix(ofelia): reserve room for the viewer ring in scrolling columns\n\nThe signed-in viewer''s avatar carries a 3px ring drawn as a spread\nshadow, so it paints outside the border box. Every avatar sits flush\nagainst the inline start of a container that clips: `.historyCol` and\n`.list` scroll (and `overflow-y: auto` clips the inline axis too), and\n`.commentsCol` is `overflow: hidden`. The ring lost its whole left edge —\nmeasured at exactly 3.0px — leaving a flat-sided avatar in the history,\nthe comment list and the composer, in both themes and on mobile.\n\nWiden each clip box outward by the ring width with a padding/negative\nmargin pair, which moves no content, and hold the width in\n`--ofelia-viewer-ring` so the shadow and the reserved room cannot drift.\n\n* fix(ofelia): size the account initial like the duty initial\n\n`MemberAvatar` scaled its initial to half the box while `Avatar` uses\n0.4, and the history phrase renders both at `px={18}` on the same line —\n9px next to 7.2px, which reads as two mismatched type sizes rather than\none set.\n\nShare the ratio through `AVATAR_INITIAL_RATIO`. Taking `Avatar`''s value\nkeeps every pre-existing avatar (the 62px hero, the 26px week strip)\npixel-identical and moves only the account squares this branch added.\n\n* fix(ofelia): raise the standard tier to the height it actually needs\n\nStandardTier lays out to 298px of content, but `tiers.standard` asked for\nonly 200px and the widget card clips instead of scrolling. At the default\nplacement (413x230px) the tier therefore resolved and then cut its own\naction row in half: \"Уборка подтверждена\" was sliced through and \"В долг\"\n/ \"Простить\" never appeared at all.\n\nState the height the tier needs, so anything shorter degrades to\n`compact` rather than rendering truncated, and raise `defaultSize` to\n`{ w: 4, h: 8 }` (~413x310px) so a freshly-placed widget still clears it\n— the same fix shape as the earlier default-placement regression.\n\n* fix(ofelia): let the pinned day header cover the reserved ring strip\n\nThe history column reserves `--ofelia-viewer-ring` of inline-start room\nso the viewer ring is not clipped, and records paint into that strip. The\nsticky day header''s own box stops at the content edge, so the strip stayed\nuncovered: scrolling avatars slid past the pinned header through a 3px\ngap, measured against the column''s border box.\n\nStretch the header back over the strip with a negative inline-start margin\nand return the width as padding, so its background spans the full\nscrollport while nothing inside it moves.\n\n* feat(ofelia): scroll the history to the day picked in the week strip\n\nSelecting a day highlighted it in the strip and changed the panel, but\nleft the history where it was, so the records for that day had to be found\nby hand.\n\n`HistoryList` now keeps a node per duty day and scrolls the matching group\nto the top of its scrollport when the selection changes. Exactly one\nancestor scrolls in each layout — the column on desktop, the dialog body\non mobile — so the native `scrollIntoView` cannot drag two scrollports at\nonce. The first run only records the mounted day: scrolling on mount would\npull the panel above the history off screen in the mobile layout before\nthe reader touched anything.\n\nThe column subscribes to a newly exposed `view.selectedIso` rather than\n`view.selected`, so confirming or forgiving the selected day still does\nnot re-render the history.\n\n* fix(ofelia): scroll only a box the reader can scroll back\n\n`scrollIntoView` moves *every* scroll container in the ancestor chain, and\n`overflow: hidden` boxes are scroll containers too — they just have no\nscrollbar. The widget frame is one: measured at 149px of overflow in the\nmobile layout, `overflow-y: hidden`. Switching a day therefore displaced\nthe whole widget, and with no scrollbar there the reader could not put it\nback.\n\nWalk up to the innermost box that is genuinely user-scrollable\n(`auto`/`scroll` *and* overflowing) and scroll that one by hand, aligning\nthe group with the scrollport top where the sticky header pins. When\nnothing in the chain scrolls there is nothing to reveal, so the layout is\nleft alone rather than nudged.\n\n* fix(ofelia): keep the mobile tab pane from clipping the viewer ring\n\nBounding the tab pane needs `overflow: hidden`, which clips the inline axis\ntoo — `overflow-x` cannot stay `visible` once the other axis is not. So in\nthe mobile layout `.split` becomes a second clipper, outside the columns\nand at the same inline start, and it shaved off the 3px viewer ring that\nthe columns had already reserved room for. Measured at `cutL: 3` on the\ncomment and record avatars; on desktop `.split` is `overflow: visible`, so\nthis only ever showed on narrow screens.\n\nReach outward by the ring width there too, the same padding/negative-margin\npair the columns use.\n\n* docs(cron): rebase the cron spec and plan onto dev\n\ndev landed Node 26, the widget domain/ boundary and Ofelia''s server.ts with\naccount authorship, so those tasks are gone. Authorship for cron records is now\na system arm on the persisted createdBy union rather than a `by: ''system''`\nfield, and the plan drops from 12 tasks to 8.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay back at dev\n\nThe overlay was temporarily aimed at this feature branch (5208c5c5) to test it on\nthe Pi before the `branch` environment existed. rpi.branch.toml now covers that\ncase per branch, so dev goes back to deploying dev — which is also what\nscripts/infra.test.ts asserts, and that test passes again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(widgets): add the cron job contract to widget server definitions\n\n* feat(server): validate widget cron schedules when the registry is built\n\n* feat(server): persist a per-job cron cursor in Valkey\n\n* feat(server): add the widget cron tick scheduler\n\n* fix(server): drain the cron scheduler on stop and pin the no-double-fire invariant\n\ninFlight was replaced on every interval fire instead of chained, so a tick\nthat outlived intervalMs was silently orphaned and stop() could return\nwhile it was still running. Chain onto the existing promise instead.\n\nAlso add a dueOccurrence test pinning that an occurrence already recorded\nin the cursor never re-fires.\n\n* style(server): rewrap the cron registry validation to the formatter''s width\n\n* fix(client): stop an edge cache from serving a stale host federation entry\n\n/remoteEntry.js is the one release file whose URL never changes while its\ncontents do: it names the hashed chunk that calls createInstance. Cached by URL,\nan intermediary keeps serving the previous release''s copy, whose chunk 404s in\nthe new release. The host then never initialises and every loadRemote fails with\n\"#RUNTIME-009 Please call createInstance first\" — a board that renders its shell\nand not one widget.\n\nObserved today on the branch stack: Cloudflare answered /remoteEntry.js with\ncf-cache-status HIT, age 6503, under its default max-age=14400, from a build\nnearly three hours older than the deployed one, while /index.html was DYNAMIC\nand the hashed assets were current. It survived a browser site-data clear, a\ncontainer recreate, a builder-cache prune and a cold rebuild, because none of\nthose touch the edge.\n\nWidget entries already carried no-cache for this exact reason; the host''s own\nentry never did. Give it the same header and pin both halves in the nginx smoke\ntest: the header, and that the chunk the entry names is one this release ships.\n\nThis is not specific to the throwaway branch host — production serves the same\nfile under the same TTL, so any release that changes the board bundle could\nshow an empty board for up to four hours.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(server): start the cron scheduler with the app and expose a test tick\n\n* feat(ofelia): give server-written records a system author\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the harness and tests the required widget identity\n\nThe ofelia authored-ledger work made `identity` a required field of\nWidgetRuntimeProps; the passport-checker widget was written before that\nfield existed. Both landed on dev without a textual conflict, so the\nsemantic conflict only surfaced as four TS2741 errors — all in a dev\nharness and three test call sites, none of them part of the production\nbuild, which is why `pnpm build` kept passing.\n\nFollow the convention the widgets updated in that PR already use: the\nharness takes the live `runtime.identity`, the tests take the\n`makeStaticWidgetIdentity()` double. passport-checker never reads the\nroster, so the empty default keeps every assertion unchanged, and the\nstatic double issues no request — which matters in recovery-flow.test,\nwhere fetch is stubbed with a never-resolving promise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* feat(ofelia): auto-approve unresolved duty days on a nightly cron\n\n* test(e2e): cover the auto-approve cron reaching an open board\n\n* test(server): cover the cron interval path and a retried partial run\n\nClose the three test-coverage gaps from the final whole-branch review:\nexercise makeCronScheduler''s start()/stop() interval path (the only\nproduction entry point, previously untested) with fake timers, cover a\nretry after a partially failed ledger append end to end in the ofelia\ncron suite, and assert append was never called in the widget''s cron\nread-error test to match its manual-path siblings.\n\n* fix(server): fence the generic storage routes off the auth keyspace\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`GET /api/storage?prefix=` passed an unvalidated prefix straight to `scanKeys`, and\n`session:<id>` embeds the session cookie''s own value in the key name, so any\nsigned-in device could enumerate live session tokens and then act as anyone —\nvoiding the server-stamped authorship this release is built on. PUT/DELETE were\nequally unbounded, which also left the new `cron:` cursor client-writable.\n\nRestrict all five generic storage routes to `root:`/`w:i:`/`w:t:`, rejecting a\npartial prefix (including the empty one) so enumeration cannot walk in sideways.\n\nAlongside: re-stamp `ip: ''''` on append as a one-release shim for pre-release\nreaders whose schema requires the field, log every `status >= 500` with its cause\nchain instead of only 500, and cover the dispatch route''s session→viewer wiring,\nwhich had no test at all — replacing it with a literal null left the suite green.\n\n* fix(ofelia): let the nightly auto-close actually discharge a debt\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`getDebtDays` scans forward from its origin, so every day it yields is >= today,\nwhile the cron only ever asks about days 1..7 in the past — `debtDayFor` was\nstructurally unreachable on that path. The auto-close therefore credited the\nrotation duty person instead of the debtor, omitted `onBehalfOf`, and `foldDebt`\nnever decremented: the debt walked forward to the next open day, forever, one\nmis-attributed record per night.\n\nAnchor the scan at `min(target, today)` behind an explicit `allowPastDebtDays`\nthat only `autoApproveDrafts` sets, so the interactive handlers stay anchored at\n`today` and keep matching what the week strip showed the user.\n\nAlongside: keep writing `by`/`author` for one release (pre-release readers require\nthem and their `z.array` fails wholesale on one missing field), drop unparseable\nelements instead of rejecting the whole array, skip the run when the ledger key is\nabsent so unused stacks stop accruing fabricated history, and re-read each day''s\nresolution before appending so a manual close landing mid-run is not overwritten.\n\n* fix(ofelia): surface failures the widget used to swallow\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\nEvery duty action captured its error into an atom nothing read, so a failed tap\nlooked identical to a successful one — and with the nightly cron now closing\nunresolved days, a silently failed «В долг» becomes a system-signed `cleaned`\nrecord at 00:05. A failed comment send additionally discarded the typed text and\nraised an unhandled rejection.\n\nTrack the last failure in write order rather than merging four per-action atoms in\na fixed order (the first non-null shadowed the rest forever), render it in Russian\nrather than leaking `WidgetApiError`''s internal message, and keep the composer''s\ntext on failure.\n\nAlongside: show a retry instead of an endless skeleton when the ledger read fails,\nstop attributing the previous week''s comments to the selected one, keep\nscroll-to-day inside the history column instead of scrolling the whole card, and\nstop the account avatar announcing a name the adjacent text already carries.\n\n* fix(board): migrate stale widget heights once, not on every read\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`tiers.standard.minHeightPx` rose 200→300 and `defaultSize.h` 6→8, but nothing\nmigrated the `h` already persisted, so every placed ofelia card would render\nCompactTier after deploy and lose its week strip and debt chips.\n\nRun the bump once, recorded in a `root:`-scoped marker key a pre-release client\ncannot strip on write-back — clamping on every read instead would have made every\nwidget unshrinkable below its package default, since `minH` sits well under it.\n\nAlongside: preserve unknown keys on the shared board snapshot, so a client that\ndoes not know a field can no longer delete it when it writes the board back.\n\n* fix(client): stop the edge from serving a stale service worker\n\nThis release already gave `/remoteEntry.js` a `no-cache` header after Cloudflare\nwas caught serving a three-hour-old copy of it. `/sw.js` has the identical\nproperty — a stable URL whose contents change every release — and fell through to\nthe catch-all block with no Cache-Control at all, so an installed PWA kept getting\nthe previous release''s worker from the edge for up to four hours and never\ninstalled the new one. That is also what would have made the ofelia record-shape\nincompatibility outlast the deploy by hours rather than a reload.\n\nGive the worker its own location alongside the federation entries, and pin it in\nthe nginx smoke spec the same way the host entry is pinned.\n\nAlongside: raise `proxy_read_timeout` for widget dispatch above the automation\nbudget — the generic `/api/` block inherited nginx''s 60s default, which would have\ncut every long passport check before its own deadline could fire — and re-point\nthe two gate probes at allowlisted keys so they keep isolating the auth and CSRF\ngates instead of tripping the new storage allowlist.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): let the members directory recover from a failed first fetch\n\nThe roster was fetched once from a connect hook that fires only on the 0→1\nsubscriber edge. On the board the identity atoms stay connected for the life of\nthe page, so a transient failure at first paint — routine on the Pi, where the\nservice worker serves the shell while the server container is still starting —\npinned the roster at null for the whole session: no viewer avatar, no viewer ring,\nuntil someone reloaded. An account created later never appeared either.\n\nRetry with backoff on a transport failure, leave a 401 alone, and refresh so a\nlater-created account arrives without a reload. Every await carries its own\nrejection handler: the abort that `wrap` raises on teardown was otherwise an\nunhandled rejection on each disconnect.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add a delete control, and stop leaking recovery sessions\n\nThe board already hands every widget a `requestDelete` callback and clock and\nofelia both use it; passport-checker was the only widget with no way to remove it\nfrom the board. Wire it through the shared `WidgetControls`, in both tiers and in\nthe dev harness.\n\nAlongside, three fixes to the recovery and check paths:\n\n`teardown()` disposed the session but never invalidated an in-flight `start()`, so\nclosing the modal during the ~1s issue request still built the noVNC RFB against a\ndetached node. The recovery slot is a single global, so that orphan answered every\nlater attempt with 409 until its TTL expired.\n\nThe 25s client deadline sat far below the pipeline''s own budget (30s queue + 60s\nexecution, 100s at the board server), so on the Pi a normal check timed out in the\nUI while the invoke resolved later with a real result that nobody read — the\nstatus could never be persisted while the upstream site was slow. Raise it past\nthe server budget, keep a late success instead of dropping it, and ignore a\nstraggler from a superseded attempt so it cannot overwrite a newer result.\n\nThe SSH fallback hardcoded noVNC''s port 6080 after compose made it per-stack, so\nan operator recovering the dev stack tunnelled into production''s live Chromium —\npossibly mid check, with a real passport number on screen. It now uses the port\nthe stack actually published.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* fix(browser-automation): report the stack''s own noVNC host port\n\nThe widget renders an `ssh -L` command so an operator can take over a stuck\nbrowser session, and it reads the port from the rejection''s meta. Nothing ever\nput a port there: `UserInputRequiredError.publicMeta` carried only `sshTarget`,\nso the widget always fell back to 6080 — production''s published port — and an\noperator on dev or branch was told to tunnel into the wrong stack.\n\nThread `NOVNC_HOST_PORT` from the same compose variable the publish line uses\ninto the service config and out through `publicMeta`, so both ends of that\ncontract now agree and each is pinned by a test.\n\nAlso set `AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET` in the dev and branch env templates: it was\nunset there, so the fallback the operator is meant to reach did not render at\nall on exactly the stacks that collide.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(backlog): record the findings this branch deliberately did not fix\n\nThe PR #27 review surfaced nine issues that were judged not worth acting on now:\nsome are contested (one review lens refuted them), some are the release''s own\ndocumented known gaps, and some are pre-existing shapes this branch only made\nvisible. They were about to be lost with the review transcript.\n\nEach entry states what exists today, why it is tolerable, the shape a fix would\ntake, and what would trigger doing the work — matching the two entries already\nhere. The facts in every entry were re-verified against the code rather than\ncopied from the review.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(rpi): move the env configs onto rpi 0.27 groups and ${git.branch}\n\nDeliver secrets through named groups instead of one bundle per environment:\n`prod` for production, `dev` for the passport-checker files the dev stack and\nthe branch stand share, `branch` for the stand''s own .env.branch. Both overlays\nclear [secrets].files, so nothing carries a second copy, and [secrets].env in\neach file is now documented as the local push source it has always been.\n\nResolve the branch stand''s source.branch through ${git.branch} and drop the\nBRANCH_NAME variable everywhere. Since 0.27 the environment key only gets a\nper-branch suffix when the configuration references ${env.slug}, so the stand is\none shared key by design; say so where the comments claimed the opposite.\n\nDelete scripts/deploy-branch.ts. It existed to push .env.branch before a deploy\ncould come up unconfigured, which a missing key bundle does silently. A declared\ngroup that is missing or empty instead fails the deploy at the layer-load step,\nnaming the group and the push that fixes it, so `rpi deploy --env branch` needs\nno wrapper.\n\nLock the layout with infra tests and refresh the docs that still said\n`rpi secrets send` and `--vars BRANCH_NAME=...`.\n\nVerified: rpi config show for base/dev/branch, oxfmt --check, oxlint and\ntsc --noEmit. The new infra assertions were run through an equivalent script —\nroot Vitest cannot start in this checkout, where rolldown fails to resolve any\nconfig entry, including untouched ones.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(rpi): drop the prod secret group and the dead PASSPORT_* variables\n\nProduction would be the only project attaching a `prod` group, and a keyless\npush always writes this file''s env + files into the deploy key''s own bundle,\nwhich is the last layer — so the group could only ever be an identical shadowed\ncopy, and a rotation pushed to it alone would silently never reach production.\nKeep the group mechanism where it earns its place: `dev`, shared by the dev\nstack and the branch stand.\n\nPASSPORT_SERIES/PASSPORT_NUMBER are gone from .env (untracked, so only its\ncomment header records this): nothing in docker-compose.yml reads them, the\nwidget takes those values from the file-backed secrets, and they were riding\ninto every stack''s containers through both groups.\n\nAgent state after this change: groups `dev` (0 keys, 2 files) and `branch`\n(7 keys, 0 files); production''s bundle 0 keys + 2 files; dev''s 7 keys + 0 files,\nwith the passport files finally resolving `<- dev` instead of a stale `<- key`\ncopy that was overriding the group.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: spec the unified widget card controls\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: plan the unified widget card controls\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: require codegen in the plan''s worktree setup\n\n* feat(widget-sdk): make WidgetControls the one widget chrome component\n\n* fix(widget-host): stop hiding card controls where there is no hover\n\n* test(client): assert the touch controls on the element that carries the style\n\n* docs: add the e2e typecheck the workspace gate does not cover\n\n* refactor(clock): take the card chrome from useWidgetChrome\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): take the delete control from useWidgetChrome\n\n* refactor(ofelia): drop OfeliaActionControls for the shared WidgetControls\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): mount the tiny tier chrome once\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): narrow the chrome policy and guard the tiny-tier switch\n\nWhole-branch code review fix wave:\n\n- useWidgetChrome now allows expand+delete only on mode === ''small'' and\n  falls through to close-only otherwise, instead of denying only on\n  ''large''. Equivalent today (WidgetMode is ''small'' | ''large''), but a\n  future third mode now defaults to no card-management chrome rather\n  than silently gaining full expand+delete.\n- TinyTier''s switch gains a `default: view satisfies never` branch.\n  ReactNode already includes undefined, so the prior bare `let body:\n  ReactNode` never tripped TS''s used-before-assignment check on a\n  missing case; the plan''s claim that it did was wrong and is corrected.\n- widget-interactions.spec.ts asserts the desktop half of the\n  hover-visibility rule (opacity 0 -> 1 on the overlay controls\n  container), which mobile-board.spec.ts cannot reach since it forces\n  hasTouch: true.\n- Corrects the spec/plan claim that a standalone dev/ harness shows\n  widget controls: every harness hardcodes mode: ''large'', so\n  useWidgetChrome() returns only onClose there and no controls render.\n\n* feat(widget-host): let a loading card name itself and be deleted\n\nA card whose widget is still loading showed an anonymous shimmer and no\ncontrols, so a component chunk that never resolved left a tile that could\nnot be removed. It now draws the widget''s icon and title — both known\nsynchronously from the codegen catalog before the remote lands — with the\nshared WidgetControls alongside them.\n\nThe controls are placement=\"inline\", not the board card''s usual overlay:\nan overlay is hover-revealed, and hover-hiding the only useful control on\na stuck card is the trap being fixed. Inline already means always-visible,\nso no new visibility rule was needed.\n\nAlso fixes a guard that never fired: the frame''s own onDelete is a\nuseEvent handle and so always truthy, which made the error card''s\n`{onDelete && ...}` unconditional. Both it and the error boundary now read\nthe raw prop.\n\n* fix(e2e): give the auto-approve cron test a ledger to close\n\nThe job returns early on an empty ledger by design — a stack where nobody\never used the widget must not grow a fabricated `cleaned` record a night\n(server.ts, F11). That guard landed in b1fffc0e, six hours after this test\nwas written in ecdd1cd0, and the test was never updated: it resets the\ndatabase, places the widget, touches nothing, and then expects the cron to\nclose a day. The ledger was empty, so the job no-opped and the plaque never\narrived.\n\nSeed one settled earlier day, the way the neighbouring forgive test already\ndoes. 2026-06-15 is a genuine Карина duty day and the entry closes it, so\nthe run skips it and auto-approves 2026-06-16 — the day the card shows.\n\nVerified: pnpm test:e2e:docker now reports 27/27, against 26/27 both on\nthis branch and on origin/dev before the change.\n\n* chore(lint): stop formatting markdown with oxfmt\n\nHand-written tables and wrapped prose in CLAUDE.md and the agent guides read\nbetter than oxfmt''s column padding, which also left the table separator row\nwider than the rows around it. docs/** and design/** were already ignored, so\nthis only extends the same treatment to the root markdown and the skill files.\n\nThe oxlint entry is symmetry: oxlint does not lint markdown today, but the two\nignore lists are maintained together.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* docs(agents): split CLAUDE.md into on-demand guides\n\nCLAUDE.md is read in full at the start of every session, and it had grown to\n~3.4k words. Move the parts that are only needed some of the time next to the\ncode they describe, and leave an index behind:\n\n- docs/agent/{deployment,test-troubleshooting,widget-server,radix-stacked-dialogs}.md,\n  each fronted by a thin .claude/skills wrapper so Claude Code loads it on its\n  own trigger, while Codex and the rest follow the table in CLAUDE.md\n- docs/architecture/overview.md for the descriptive architecture prose\n- the storage-key contract, including the incident that proves it, now lives in\n  the header comment of packages/shared/storage/scope.ts, so it reaches whoever\n  is actually editing the derivation\n\nAlso drop the generic rpi CLI mechanics already covered by the rpi:rpi-cli and\nrpi:rpi-toml skills, and fix what had drifted: pi.toml -> rpi.toml, the\nscopeWithColon normalization lives in host-runtime.ts and not widget-api.ts,\nbrowser-automation is no longer \"the future browser service\", the command list\nmatches package.json, and the hardcoded per-machine skill paths are gone.\n\nCLAUDE.md: 3368 -> 1382 words.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* fix(client): replace the deprecated apple PWA capability meta\n\nChrome flags <meta name=\"apple-mobile-web-app-capable\"> as deprecated and\nasks for the standard mobile-web-app-capable instead. Keeping both would\nkeep the warning, and standalone mode no longer depends on the apple tag:\nthe VitePWA manifest already declares display: standalone, which Safari\nhonours since iOS 16.4.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* fix(passport-checker): load noVNC lazily so the widget can mount\n\n@novnc/novnc@1.7.0 ends core/util/browser.js in a module-scope top-level\nawait: it builds a real VideoDecoder, configures it for 1920x1080\navc1.42401f, decodes a frame and awaits decoder.flush(). That probe\nresolves in milliseconds on desktop Chrome and never settles at all on\nsome devices, and a top-level await that never settles parks every\nimporter in \"evaluating\" forever.\n\nui/PassportChecker.tsx imported model/rfb.ts statically for the prod RFB\nadapter, so the chain was mount-time: import(''./ui/PassportChecker'') never\nresolved, React.lazy never got a payload, and the board rendered this\nwidget''s loading card forever. Nothing was thrown, so no error boundary\nfired and the card was indistinguishable from a slow load.\n\nReach the adapter through model/load-rfb.ts''s dynamic import instead. The\nrecovery model now takes loadRfb and awaits it alongside transport.issue:\nthe store supersedes an unused capability on the next issue() and only\ncounts a live socket as busy, so running them in parallel costs nothing\nand keeps the modal''s time to first frame unchanged. A viewer that fails\nto load lands in the new viewerUnavailable state with a reconnect button\nrather than a silent spinner.\n\nVerified on the built output: avc1.42401f/EncodedVideoChunk now appear\nonly in rfb-*.js, and the widget chunk''s single reference to it is a\ndynamic import(). ui/passport-checker-novnc-graph.test.ts guards the\nchain — it goes red if the static import comes back.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(agent): record the machine and deploy failures that lived only in memory\n\nThe CLAUDE.md split moved detail into docs/agent/* but left ten machine- and\ndeploy-specific failure modes recorded nowhere in the repository. Each of them\nreads as a regression you just caused, and each has cost multiple sessions.\n\ntest-troubleshooting.md gains the worktree symlink failure, the rotten\nnode_modules in the main checkout, pnpm check''s kill-others-on-fail behaviour,\nthe docker e2e run that exits 0 without starting, and the stale vite preview\nthat serves another checkout''s build. It also corrects the Vitest filter: the\ndocumented `--filter client test -- <file>` does not scope, `exec vitest run`\ndoes.\n\ndeployment.md gains the three caches that sit between a build and what runs -\nthe service worker and IndexedDB that survive a redeploy on the shared branch\nhostname, the edge cache on URL-stable files like /remoteEntry.js and /sw.js,\nand the aborted deploy that strands a container on a dead network.\n\n* docs: stop CLAUDE.md repeating itself, and give it a worktree command that works\n\nTesting and Failure modes said nothing the file had not already said. The gate,\nthe PR rules, the commit style and the test locations were each stated twice;\nof the five failure modes, four restated the feature workflow or the global\nrules and the fifth is now in docs/agent/test-troubleshooting.md.\n\nThe worktree command in step 1 has never worked on this machine: AGENTS.md is a\ncommitted symlink Windows cannot materialize, so the documented command fails\noutright. It now carries both required git flags.\n\n* docs(backlog): record three follow-ups the shipped releases left behind\n\nOfelia''s tier thresholds now sit within 10px of their own frames, so any chrome\nadded to the card silently drops it to compact and takes five e2e tests with it.\nThe z.array record schemas mean one new field hangs every still-open client, and\nboth shipped releases papered over it with a compat shim. And an ofelia comment\nfrom an account outside DUTY_ROTATION loses its author on pre-release clients.\n\nAll three were only ever recorded in agent memory, which is not where a\nfollow-up survives.\n\n* docs(context): commit the session review snapshots\n\nThey have been untracked for three sessions running, so each review has had\nnothing to diff against.\n\n* docs: log the PWA update path in the backlog\n\nregisterType: ''autoUpdate'' does reload the page on its own, but the same\nbranch of vite-plugin-pwa''s register.js never calls onNeedRefresh and\nmakes updateServiceWorker() a no-op — so needRefreshAtom, applyUpdate and\nUpdateBanner are dead code, and an already-open client only re-checks once\nan hour. Records what exists, why it is tolerable, and what would trigger\nthe work.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: drop the typescript 7 migration benchmark report\n\nThe migration is done and the numbers have served their purpose. The\ngenerator, scripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts, stays — the report and its\nbenchmarks.json are reproducible from it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(context): add the session-4 startup capture\n\nFollows 3d327e45, which committed the earlier snapshots.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: record the passport-checker loading-skeleton handoff\n\nRoot cause, the fix on feat/widget-controls, what is verified and what is\nnot, plus the device console probes and the list of dead ends not worth\nredoing. Delete it once that branch is released.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: spec the fullscreen close control and back-gesture dismissal\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* docs: plan the fullscreen close control and back-gesture dismissal\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the tiny tier a way out of a restored success\n\nA card narrower than the widget''s 321px `standard` threshold renders\nTinyTier, and its `success` state drew a status chip and no action at all —\nthe only state in that tier without one. That was survivable while the\nwidget always started in `idle`. Once it began restoring the last\nsuccessful check on mount, a narrow card came up in `success` and stayed\nthere: passport-checker deliberately offers no expand affordance, so the\nonly button left on the card was \"Удалить\". Seen on the branch stand at\n314px, where 4 of 12 columns miss the threshold by 7px.\n\nThe chip is now the action rather than gaining one below it, so the state\ncosts no extra height and still fits the smallest tile. It keeps the\nstatus and timestamp it already carried, and its accessible name repeats\nthat text after the action rather than replacing it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* chore(docs): keep only agent and architecture guides in git\n\ndocs/context/ session logs and docs/superpowers/ plans, specs and\nartifacts churn every session and had nothing durable to diff against;\nuntrack them and ignore docs/* except docs/agent/ and docs/architecture/,\nthe canonical checked-in references.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(docs): ignore docs/* except agent and architecture\n\nFollow-up to the previous commit — the .gitignore rule itself was left\nunstaged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* doc: add .playwright-mcp to .gitignore\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): mirror open overlays onto browser history\n\n* chore(docs): drop this branch''s plan and spec from git\n\ndev now keeps only the agent and architecture guides in the repository and\nignores the rest of docs/ (0a17cdaf, 96b2c041). This branch predates that\nrule and still carried its own plan and design spec, which the merge left\ntracked because dev never had those two paths to delete. Untracked here;\nboth files stay on disk, now covered by the ignore rule.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* feat(widget-sdk): add the overlay back-dismiss hook\n\n* test(passport-checker): widen the noVNC graph test''s budget\n\nThe test''s only await is a cold import of the widget''s whole mount graph in\na fresh module registry, which overran the default 5s budget under\n`pnpm check` — five workspace gates running concurrently. It failed there\ntwice and passed every isolated run. Nothing in it waits on behaviour, so\n20s cannot mask a regression: a static noVNC import still fails the\nassertion, not the timer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* test(widget-sdk): add StrictMode and fallback tests for overlay back-dismiss hook\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): coalesce a dropped overlay''s traversal with the push behind it\n\nA history traversal is asynchronous. Measured in real Chromium,\nhistory.go(-1) lands 25-40ms later and a pushState issued in the same task\ndoes not cancel it — jsdom does, which is why the unit tests never caught\nthis. So dropOverlay''s eager go() fired after the following push, rolled the\nbrowser back past the new entry and delivered a spontaneous popstate at the\npre-overlay depth, which reconciled as \"close the overlay that opened 30ms\nago\". Both live call paths hit it: StrictMode''s mount -> cleanup -> mount,\nand any commit that closes one overlay and opens another.\n\ndropOverlay now records the depth history must land on and schedules the\ntraversal on the next task. A pushOverlay arriving first reuses one of the\nowed entries — depth taken from the debt rather than stack.length + 1, so it\ncannot collide with an overlay still waiting above a mid-stack drop — and\nthe flush is cancelled once the debt is spent. A drop with nothing behind it\ntraverses exactly as before.\n\nThe debt is a target depth, not a step count: two drops in one task each\nname the entry they want and the lower wins, where summing their steps\ntraversed past the pre-overlay entry and out of the application. Confirmed\nin Chromium against the module at HEAD, which left the probe page on\nabout:blank after one back press.\n\nThe new tests drive real navigation instead of dispatching a hand-built\nPopStateEvent, since a faked popstate cannot tell one registered entry from\ntwo that happen to carry the same depth.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* chore(widget-sdk): clear the pre-existing lint and type errors in the overlay tests\n\nBoth predate this branch''s overlay work and were failing `pnpm lint` and\n`pnpm typecheck` at HEAD: an unused `e2` binding in overlay-history.test.ts,\nand a `let capturedDismiss` that control-flow analysis narrows to `never`\nbecause it never sees the assignment made inside the component.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): derive a pushed overlay''s depth from history, not the stack\n\n`stack.length + 1` is only correct while depth counts entries one for one. It\nstops doing so in the duplicate-depth mode a mid-stack drop can leave behind,\nand then the number it hands out skips one: from [C(1), E(2), B(2)] the next\npush is stamped 4 on an entry history places directly after the one stamped 2.\nflushOwedTraversal computes `readDepth() - target`, which counts entries, so a\nlater drop of E issues go(-3) where two entries separate it from its target and\ntakes C down with it.\n\n`pushState` inserts its entry immediately after the current one, so the depth is\n`readDepth() + 1` by construction. Identical whenever history and the stack\nagree, which is every ordinary case.\n\nAlso drops the `replaceState` from the coalescing branch — that branch runs only\nwhen the claimed depth equals the current one, so the spread rewrote identical\nstate. Confirmed in Chromium: the four-scenario probe is byte-identical with and\nwithout it. The claim path now writes nothing to history at all, which is what\nthe comments (here and on the hook) now say.\n\nThe two residuals move from the report into the module doc, where the tasks that\nwill wire real overlays to it can see them: two overlays mounted in the same\ncommit as a middle drop, and dropOverlay racing an eager dismissOverlay in one\ntask.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* feat(client): add the app-env registry, palette derivation and OKLCH conversion\n\n* feat(client): close the topmost dialog on the platform back gesture\n\n* feat(client): generate and commit per-environment app icons\n\n* test(client): pin the Escape close to history.back, not just the outcome\n\nwaitFor(() => expandedInstanceId() === null) alone cannot distinguish\n\"routed through requestDismiss/history\" from \"closed onOpenChange(false)\ndirectly\" -- both reach the same null. Spy on history.back and assert it\nwas called synchronously right after the Escape keydown, so a future\nchange that bypasses requestDismiss fails this test instead of staying\ngreen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* feat(client): brand the document title, tokens and icons per environment at build time\n\n* feat(client): show the environment badge in the board header\n\n* build: deliver the rpi environment name into the client image build\n\n* fix(client): serve the environment icons publicly so the login page is branded\n\n* feat(client): close the topmost popover on the platform back gesture\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* test(client): pin the popover Escape close to history.back, not just the outcome\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(client): pin the environment-branding wiring with tests and share the accent constants\n\nGuard the two things nothing in the gate actually exercised: the Header rendering\nEnvBadge, and both Vite configs registering appEnvBranding. Both were verified to\nfail when the guarded code is deleted.\n\nAlso: export ACCENT_L/ACCENT_C from registry.ts so the fixed accent lightness and\nchroma stop being copied across tokens.ts, generate-env-icons.ts and\nicons-lock.test.ts; derive generate-env-icons.ts''s PRODUCTION_FILL from them\ninstead of a fourth hardcoded #645fd1; give the env badge pill flex: none so it\ncan''t be squeezed by BoardSelect; and tighten a handful of comments/test anchors\n(dev-only activation branding limitation, a VITE_APP_ENV override caveat, a hook\ntier name, a looser Dockerfile layer-order assertion, three deployment.md claims).\n\n* feat(clock): give the fullscreen clock a close control\n\n* feat(passport-checker): give the fullscreen widget a close control\n\n* feat(passport-checker): dismiss the recovery modal on the back gesture\n\n* test(passport-checker): assert the async close mechanism, not just its effect\n\nThe three tests asserting a synchronous close on Escape/backdrop pointerdown\nwent stale the moment the recovery modal''s close paths started routing\nthrough history.back(), which is async in jsdom (and ~25-40ms later in real\nChromium). Update them to pin the mechanism (spy on history.back and assert\nit fired) rather than only awaiting the eventual state flip, and to wait for\nour own modal to actually close before checking the underlying Radix dialog\nsurvived, so the survivor assertion cannot pass on a race that hasn''t landed\nyet.\n\n* test: reset overlay-history between tests in the shared widget and client setups\n\nRecoveryModal.tsx''s useOverlayBackDismiss(true, close) now runs on every\nmount, so any test that mounts a Dialog/Popover root or RecoveryModal\ntouches overlay-history.ts''s file-scoped singletons (the entry stack, the\npopstate listener, a pending owed-traversal timer). recovery-flow.test.tsx\ngot its own beforeEach for this; RecoveryModal.test.tsx and\nrecovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx did not, and neither did any client test\nthat mounts a Dialog/Popover (AddWidgetMenu.test.tsx,\nBoardSchemaSelect.test.tsx, etc.) — a gap that let one test''s still-open\noverlay leak state into the next.\n\nReset once per package instead of scattering beforeEach hooks: widget-sdk''s\nshared `./test-setup` (used by every widget package via\ndefineWidgetVitestConfig) and the client package''s own vitest.setup.ts.\nBoth guard the reset to jsdom, since @vitest-environment node files in\nthese packages have no window/history at all.\n\nAdded a proof test per setup file with no reset of its own, so the shared\nhook''s effect is directly observable rather than inferred from a green\nsuite: one test leaks an overlay entry on purpose, the next asserts the\nsingleton started clean.\n\n* test(e2e): cover the fullscreen close control and the back gesture\n\n* fix(client): degrade the env badge to a dot, then hide it, on narrow viewports\n\n.brand has no overflow clipping, so once the header runs out of room it\nslides its content under .actions instead of clipping it. Give the badge\nthe same breakpoint ladder BoardSchemaSelect already uses: at <=640px\n(where the select goes full-width) drop the label text and shrink to a\ndot-only circle; at <=430px (where the select collapses to an icon) hide\nit entirely. The label now lives in its own span so CSS can hide it\nindependently of the dot, while the pill''s own aria-label keeps the\nenvironment announced in the dot-only state.\n\n* fix(client): collapse the theme toggle to one cycling button on narrow viewports\n\nThe three-item ToggleGroup is 102px; below 768px (MOBILE_BREAKPOINT, right\nat the measured collision onset) that alone accounts for a big share of\nthe header overflow. Render both controls and let CSS pick one via a\nmedia query, same approach the rest of the header''s responsiveness uses.\nThe cycle order (light -> dark -> system -> light) moves into the model\nas cycleThemeMode next to themeMode, since the order is domain knowledge,\nnot view glue; the view''s OPTIONS list is now derived from it instead of\nrepeating it. The view-transition DOM interop (--vt-x/--vt-y, startViewTransition)\nis factored into one applyWithViewTransition helper shared by both controls.\n\nThe cycling button''s accessible name (\"Сменить тему\") is deliberately\ngeneric and distinct from the ToggleGroupItem radio names, since jsdom\nrenders both controls at once (media queries don''t evaluate there) and a\ncolliding name would break the existing getByRole(''radio'', ...) tests.\n\nAdds an e2e spec covering the group/button swap at two viewports, the\nonly layer where the media query is real.\n\n* fix(client): let the board select fill the remaining header row, not the whole row\n\n.trigger (or .skeleton, while loading) is BoardSchemaSelect''s own top-level\nelement -- it has no wrapper -- so it''s a direct flex item of .brand\nalongside the logo and EnvBadge. Its <=640px width: 100% therefore resolved\nagainst .brand''s own content box, claiming the entire row for itself as if\nthose siblings weren''t there and pushing their content under .actions.\nflex: 1 1 auto fills only the space actually left after the siblings,\nwhich is what this rule always meant to do.\n\n(.root, the class that width: 100% previously also applied to at this\nbreakpoint, is never applied to any element in BoardSchemaSelect.tsx --\ndead CSS, not the flex item in question. Left untouched, out of scope.)\n\nVerified with a 900->320px real-Chromium sweep: the header now has zero\noverlap at every width down to 320px, closing the residual 545-435px band\nleft after the EnvBadge/ThemeToggle responsive work.\n\n* fix(client): let the board select shrink instead of overflowing the header row\n\nAbove 640px .trigger/.skeleton were width: 220px -- rigid. When the header\nrow ran short (the deployed stands render ''dev'' and ''branch'' in the env\nbadge; ''branch'' is the longest label the dev server never builds locally),\na rigid flex item has nowhere to give, so the surplus went past .actions.\n.triggerText already has overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis, so the\nselect was built to shrink -- nothing let it.\n\nSwitched to width: 220px + flex-shrink: 1 + min-width: 0. A bare\nflex-basis (flex: 0 1 220px) was tried first and doesn''t compose the same\nway: .brand''s own width is auto (content-based), and Chrome''s content-based\nsizing pass for an ancestor like that measures a flex-basis-only child by\nits intrinsic content size, not its flex-basis -- it silently discarded\nthe 220px preference and left the trigger permanently shrunk to ~123px\neven at 1280px with room to spare. An explicit width is what a\ncontent-based ancestor actually measures, so it''s kept alongside\nflex-shrink instead of being replaced by it.\n\nVerified this composes with the two media queries below it, unchanged:\n<=640px (flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0, from d4c7c1af) and <=430px (fixed\n36px icon) -- selectW is exactly 220 at 1280/900 and exactly 36 at every\nwidth <=430, for all three non-production env-badge labels (''local'',\n''dev'', ''branch'').\n\n* docs(widget-runtime): correct overlay-history and MyDevicesDialog comments\n\noverlay-history.ts''s module header claimed every close path routes through\ndismissOverlay and a traversal, but a widget''s fullscreen close control and\nAddWidgetMenu''s close button drop the overlay directly (open set to false,\nunmount) without ever calling dismissOverlay. Rewrite the header to name the\ntwo legitimate shapes -- dismiss and drop -- and clarify that the\n\"only popstate calls close\" invariant is about close specifically, not about\nevery close path routing through history.\n\nDocument two more constraints on callers in dropOverlay''s doc block,\nalongside the two already recorded: a push landing while a flush''s own\nhistory.go is still in flight reads a stale depth (unreachable today, but\nundocumented), and a dismiss cannot be vetoed by its caller once history has\nalready forwarded it to reconcile.\n\nMyDevicesDialog''s belt-and-braces setTimeout comment described a same-tick\nrace with Radix''s deferred pointerDownOutside check. Since the overlay-history\nwork, onOpenChange(false) no longer runs inside the click -- it arrives from\npopstate tens of milliseconds later -- so the described mechanism is stale,\neven though the guard it protects still holds (more strongly, in fact).\nCorrect the explanation without changing the guard.\n\nNo behavior change; comments and doc blocks only.\n\n* docs(agent): preserve the browser history traversal measurement\n\n.superpowers/ is git-ignored and gets deleted once this branch''s work\nfinishes, so the real-Chromium measurement behind overlay-history.ts''s\ndeferred-traversal design (browser-history-probe.md) would otherwise be lost\nentirely, leaving only the numbers already quoted in the module header with\nno reproducible method behind them.\n\nCapture the question, method, measured numbers, and the jsdom/Chromium\ndivergence in docs/agent/browser-history-traversal.md, written as durable\nguidance rather than a session log.\n\nPoint at it from CLAUDE.md. This worktree branched before the CLAUDE.md\nguide-table refactor landed on dev, so there is no existing table to add a\nrow to here; add a new one-row \"Related guides\" table in the same format,\nto be reconciled with dev''s table on the next merge.\n\n* docs: fold the history-traversal guide into the main guide table\n\nThe dev merge brought in the guide table this branch predated, leaving two\ntables side by side. One table, one row.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(client): announce the compact theme button''s state and tighten the header tests\n\nThe compact theme button''s fixed aria-label (\"Сменить тему\") left the\ncurrent mode reaching a screen-reader user only through `title` (not\nreliably announced, least of all by touch screen readers on a <=768px\nviewport) -- a regression against the tri-state group it replaces, which\nannounces the active mode through the radio''s name plus aria-pressed.\nThe accessible name now composes the current mode in: `Тема: ${label}.\nСменить`. It names the state and the action, and can''t drift from\ncycleThemeMode since it describes the current mode, not the next one.\ntitle is kept for the hover affordance. Updated the two vitest queries and\nthe e2e HeaderPage locator that pinned the old fixed name to match on the\nconstant \"Сменить\" suffix instead (regex), since the name now changes\nwith every click.\n\nEnvBadge.test.tsx''s \"label in its own element\" guard couldn''t actually\nfail: getByText matches an element''s direct text-node children and\nreturns the closest such element, so reverting the label back to bare\ntext inside .badge would make getByText(''dev'') resolve to the pill\nitself -- still a <span>, still !== the dot, so both assertions passed on\nold and new markup alike. Rewrote it to compare against the outer pill\n(screen.getByRole(''status'')) instead, the one element getByText can only\nresolve to on the old markup. Verified by temporarily reverting the label\nto bare text: the rewritten test fails on it, passes once restored.\nFolded the now-redundant \"announces via aria-label\" test into the\nexisting \"labels itself for assistive technology\" test, which already\ncovered the same aria-label via a role query.\n\nDeleted BoardSchemaSelect.module.css''s dead .root class entirely (base\nrule and the <=430px block) instead of leaving two more live-looking\nreferences to a class the file''s own comment already called unused.\n\n@media (max-width: 768px) on the compact theme button disagreed by one\nwith the MOBILE_BREAKPOINT constant it''s commented as pairing with\n(grid-metrics.ts treats width < 768 as mobile, i.e. 768 itself is\ndesktop). Changed to max-width: 767px so both agree. Verified first: a\n1280->320px sweep (transitions disabled, 300ms settle) for all three\nnon-production env-badge labels (''local'', ''dev'', ''branch'') shows zero\noverlap at every width including exactly 768px, where the three-item\ngroup is still visible under the new threshold, so 767px doesn''t\nreintroduce the overlap 768px was chosen to avoid.\n\n* test(client): expect one Zakryt control in the fullscreen dialog, not zero\n\nClock now draws its own fullscreen close button (feat(clock): give the\nfullscreen clock a close control), so the \"widget can be expanded without\nduplicate fullscreen or close controls\" e2e test and the FullscreenOverlay\nheader comment -- both inherited unchanged from dev -- were asserting and\ndocumenting the old, no-longer-true premise that Clock draws none. Update\nthe assertion to expect exactly one such button (the widget''s own) and\ncorrect the comment''s example, while keeping the real guarantee both were\nprotecting: no *duplicate* chrome stacked on top of the widget''s.\n\n* chore(docs): drop this branch''s plan and spec from git\n\ndocs/* became local-only except the agent and architecture guides. These two\npredate that change on this branch; the files stay on disk.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* build: add a compose overlay for the local nginx e2e stack\n\ndocker-compose.yml defaults the WebAuthn triple to production\n(board.iiskelo.com, https) while playwright.nginx.config.ts drives\nhttp://localhost:8080. A stack started without overrides fails every passkey\njourney inside navigator.credentials.create(), surfacing only as the generic\nclient-side \"сбой процедуры регистрации\" -- register/options returns 200 and\nno verify call ever follows. README told readers to park the overrides in\n.env, which is unavailable here: rpi.toml uses that same file as its\n[secrets].env bundle, so localhost values would ride into production on the\nnext `rpi secrets push`.\n\nThe overlay carries those three plus ALLOW_TEST_DB_RESET, so\n`pnpm start:docker:nginx-e2e` is a single command that cannot be run half-way.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* fix(e2e): pin the gate logout journey to the logout response and navigation\n\n`page.waitForURL(''/'')` synchronized nothing: the board already IS at ''/'', so\nit resolved in the same tick as the logout click -- measured at 3 ms after it\nand ~20 ms before the model''s own navigation landed. Both halves of the\nresulting race were observed: the session probe reading the pre-logout 200,\nand page.reload() overtaking the navigation, which then re-entered the\nstill-controlling service worker and re-rendered the precached board shell\n(title \"myboard\" instead of \"myboard — активация\").\n\naccount.logout runs POST /api/auth/logout -> purge -> hard navigate, so arming\na waitForResponse and a framenavigated waiter before the click pins the whole\nsequence down.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* fix(server): skip the create-invite entry test when its bundle is missing\n\nThe test guarded only one of its two preconditions. Valkey reachability was\nchecked; the built dist/scripts/create-invite.cjs it executes was not, even\nthough dist/ is gitignored and nothing in `pnpm test` builds it. So the test\nwas green only while nothing listened on 6379, and turned `pnpm check` red\nwith a MODULE_NOT_FOUND the moment any stack was up -- in a file the diff\nnever touched. `pnpm start:docker` publishes 6379 and `pnpm test:e2e:nginx`\nrequires that stack, so running the gate after the e2e suite hit it every time.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* **fix(passport-checker): unify series/number secrets and validate identity via Zod schema**\n\n- Combined series and number secrets into a single `number` field, maintaining format consistency.\n- Introduced strict validation for passport identity using a Zod schema for improved error handling.\n- Adjusted regular expressions to parse series/number from a single input field.\n- Updated browser and test implementations to reflect merged secret logic and schema integration.\n\n* fix(rpi.toml): remove series secrets path from configuration\n\n* fix(docker): remove unused `series` secrets from compose files\n\n* fix: formatting\n\n* fix(passport-checker): align combined secret contracts\n\n* docs: design dual passport checker results\n\n* feat(passport-checker): define dual document results\n\n* feat(passport-checker): check both documents sequentially\n\n* test(passport-checker): cover dual browser submissions\n\n* feat(passport-checker): persist independent document results\n\n* fix(passport-checker): defer partial writes until hydration\n\n* feat(passport-checker): render dual document results\n\n* fix(passport-checker): resolve final review findings\n\n* fix(passport-checker): close final rereview findings\n\n* fix(passport-checker): resolve final release blockers\n\n* fix(passport-checker): support mixed-version PWA rollout\n\n* fix(passport-checker): preserve concurrent document results\n\n* docs: remove passport checker design spec\n\n* fix(passport-checker): use compact layout for short cards\n\n* chore: add dev:docker:browser command\n\n* feat(passport-checker): redesign tiny status card\n\n* feat(passport-checker): redesign tiny, standard and fullscreen per Claude Design (#37)\n\n* docs: replace design/ with the new Claude Design export\n\nFull myboard redesign export superseding the previous one; the\npassport-checker mockup (design/06) now specs distinct tiny/standard/\nfullscreen layouts instead of fullscreen being an enlarged standard.\n\n* feat(passport-checker): redesign the tiny tier per design/06\n\nPer-document icon chips (ID card + passport, each with its own\nsuccess/error badge) replace the single rolled-up status label, so a\npartial result (\"1 из 2\") is visible without opening the widget.\nLayout moves from a header row to a centered column, matching the\nClaude Design mockup; widget chrome switches from inline to the\nshared overlay placement.\n\nStandard and fullscreen are unchanged — fullscreen still needs a\ncheck-history/schedule data-model decision before its mockup (embedded\nrecovery, \"Журнал последних проверок\") can be implemented.\n\n* feat(passport-checker): give fullscreen its own layout per design/06\n\nFullscreen stops being an enlarged Standard card and becomes its own\ntier: both documents render full-detail side by side (StatusBanner in\na bigger frame, no truncation), a static \"what is checked and why\"\npanel explains the widget, and sessionRequired recovery embeds inline\n(noVNC canvas + SSH fallback + countdown) instead of collapsing\nfullscreen to reopen the tile''s portal modal.\n\nThat last part reworks recovery-flow.ts: `recoverySurface` (modal |\ninline) replaces the old fromFullscreen/collapse/restorePending dance,\nsince fullscreen no longer needs to close itself to show recovery —\nthe tile and the fullscreen mount of one instance can be alive at the\nsame time, so PassportChecker.tsx gates the portal modal on `tier !==\n''fullscreen''` in addition to the surface, or both would try to render\nit.\n\nStandard gets one content addition to match: GlobalStatusBanner''s\nsessionRequired banner now explains *why* (\"Автоматика уткнулась в\nкапчу/вход\"), not just that a login is needed.\n\nLeft out of fullscreen on purpose, vs. the mockup: the \"Журнал\nпоследних проверок\" (check history) and \"Расписание\" (schedule)\npanels, and invalidConfig''s \"чего не хватает\" field list — none of\nthat data exists in check-model.ts today (only the latest result per\ndocument is stored, there''s no schedule concept), and fabricating it\nwould be worse than not showing it.\n\ndev/harness.tsx''s `?tier=` now accepts any tier, not just `tiny`, so\nevery layout can be previewed standalone.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqbofeCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAxccQAF2vw1Q/s10jiI2crigSi3BF\nN27BD5x37M9ABszvPZoYmff07nBStS1/dBY9t+bkfWcEttqrQXVtp/sy5IY68vt7\nYNrHuRooh+UYNu/g4m1fOO839k/LXayM2j5SfQN4v2X4aeZWm5ErB7UEXKM1BzaE\nwc4VWSVpbc5Ke86AcXXPbxFsoksu80qVxAQ1JGgnG/AdBvFLtnx26O0EhzE7PWV/\nYc4U2UHnCTeAV9oawHn9JzAn4/ikE8W4U83W3pe8mXc2wz1ep+7uo9qrefy+/44A\nalyrqzIbxGuX29C7x5hfe/TSjJuQwTfdswfHve8kmcngJB+1nCwqnzsndb7ziFWz\nZsJ3tNmvBvyZkzko7SstsLH9c38hY/ixPnTySEHo9K8FYwl7GkW/PaGl1XyP1MkQ\nto/qCwtUKcFI815+N422VZrz+8e+zBphJ3mgoRkLx9rrJ4Em7c3itepRDI+fYat4\nboeNNS+Drlr2/Rxyp2BtnsNlBkz4xDvvH5Crqp+iXFFqQ87b2MgDbjtSp4ldfBBj\nirTzAI3sUQw4aVBbD9MhgVee7oZb6ZPyobr9CKXZ1tYsVfXbGBzJ7m8TYrZJgVhD\ntHg3MITA8Kzqkelqx2z+tWpXnU7C2vr60/Jf4EMgH+I5JE8fqFqMGPsvK/uoEs2F\nTMrXErIGj3xsxk8uceWg\n=dvBi\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-01T23:57:19Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi/myboard/commit/0cfc5f1dbfd9533ac227db0453a6a82971a9072e","node_id":"C_kwDOS9EJdtoAKDBjZmM1ZjFkYmZkOTUzM2FjMjI3ZGIwNDUzYTZhODI5NzFhOTA3MmU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi/myboard/commit/ea12a9cd55976786e9d268e5f8aa9de2d6bfd27f","sha":"ea12a9cd55976786e9d268e5f8aa9de2d6bfd27f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/ea12a9cd55976786e9d268e5f8aa9de2d6bfd27f"}],"sha":"0cfc5f1dbfd9533ac227db0453a6a82971a9072e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/0cfc5f1dbfd9533ac227db0453a6a82971a9072e"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24359879?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi","id":24359879,"login":"khmilevoi","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI0MzU5ODc5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/ea12a9cd55976786e9d268e5f8aa9de2d6bfd27f/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-01T13:52:02Z","email":"Khmilevoi@gmail.com","name":"khmilevoi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-01T13:52:02Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"release: merge dev into main (#36)\n\n* fix(passport-checker): root-cause the recovery-flow Escape-close race\n\nfindByRole(''dialog'') resolves as soon as the dialog''s DOM node commits,\nbut useModalIsolation''s mount effect - which attaches the Escape\nlistener and moves focus in - is a passive effect that can still be\npending a tick later. Firing Escape into that gap dispatched into a\ndocument with no listener yet, so the dialog never closed and the\nfollowing waitFor spun until it timed out; reproduced solo (no\nwhole-suite contention needed) in roughly 1 of 15-40 runs.\n\nEach of the two tests that close the modal now waits for the effect''s\nsynchronous side effect (focus landing inside the dialog) before\ndispatching Escape, which proves the same effect has also attached the\nlistener. Replaces the prior fix round''s blanket file-wide 30s test\ntimeout (and its comment''s now-falsified \"never a hang\" claim) with a\nscoped 10s per-test timeout on only the two tests that actually run\nthis round trip, as a safety margin for ordinary contention rather\nthan a mask for this race.\n\nVerified with 40 solo + 32 self-inflicted-parallel + 8 whole-suite\nreruns after the fix, zero reproductions of the timeout.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): hold focus inside the recovery modal\n\nRadix restores focus from a zero-delay timeout when its dialog unmounts, so\ncollapsing fullscreen for recovery handed focus to the board a tick after the\nmodal mounted. Pull focus back whenever it lands outside the modal.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the unmount-cleanup test teeth and fix a flaky Escape assert\n\nRecoveryModal.test.tsx''s `leaves focus alone once the modal is gone` test\npassed even when the cleanup''s `removeEventListener(''focusin'', ...)` was\ndeleted, or the whole effect cleanup was zeroed out: after unmount the\nportal root is detached, so a leaked focusin listener calling `.focus()` on\na detached descendant is a jsdom/browser no-op and the activeElement assert\nstays green regardless. Pin the actual cleanup contract instead: spy on\nwindow.addEventListener/removeEventListener and assert the exact `focusin`\nlistener useModalIsolation registers on mount is the one removed on unmount,\nalongside the original behavioral assert.\n\nrecovery-flow.test.tsx''s first test asserted\n`dialog.contains(document.activeElement)` synchronously right after\n`findByRole(''dialog'')` resolves on DOM commit, before dispatching Escape.\n`useModalIsolation`''s mount effect (which moves focus in and attaches the\nEscape listener) is passive and can still be pending a tick later, so\nEscape could fire before the listener existed and the test would hang out\nits timeout — the same race Task 4 root-caused and fixed in this file''s\nother two Escape-closing tests by waiting for focus-in-dialog first. Apply\nthe same `await waitFor(...)` wrap here and correct the file-level comment,\nwhich had claimed only two tests reach the Escape-close race when this one\ndoes too.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the modal focusout guard against narrowing\n\nMount the underlying Radix dialog in its own pass, as production does, and\ncover the intra-modal focus move that the proposed guard narrowing would have\nbroken silently. The regression test dispatches the underlying focusout event\ndirectly rather than chaining native .focus() calls: jsdom (like real\nbrowsers) always completes an outer .focus() call on its own target once its\nhandler stack unwinds, so a settled document.activeElement assertion can''t\nobserve a reclaim a nested handler only ever wins transiently — verified\nempirically against the narrowed guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): close a cross-test focus-restore leak in the Radix stack suite\n\nRadix''s FocusScope defers its unmount focus-restore to a real setTimeout(0),\nand testing-library''s auto cleanup() never waits for it, so a prior test''s\ndeferred restore (and an unawaited NoVncCanvas state update from that same\ntest''s async recovery flow) can bleed into the next test — confirmed by\ninstrumentation. A formal trace of the reentrant focus cascade shows the\n''pulls focus back...'' assertion itself can''t actually be flipped by this\n(no yield point exists between its synchronous .focus() call and its\nexpect()), but the leak is real and pre-existing to any suite that\nmounts+unmounts a trapped FocusScope, so close it defensively: flush one\nreal tick after each test''s cleanup so Radix''s deferred work always\nfinishes before the next test starts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): drain Radix''s focus-restore timer from beforeEach, not afterEach\n\ne797d50''s message overstated what that change achieved. It claimed the added\nafterEach ran \"strictly after cleanup()\" and closed the cross-test focus-restore\nleak. It did neither. Vitest resolves sequence.hooks to \"stack\", so same-suite\nafterEach hooks run in REVERSE registration order, and testing-library''s\ncleanup() -- registered first, at setupFiles import time -- therefore runs last.\nThe file-level afterEach ran BEFORE cleanup(), i.e. before the unmount that\nschedules Radix''s setTimeout(0) focus restore, so it drained nothing.\n\nInstrumenting setTimeout and the hook boundaries on this file shows it directly:\nat afterEach entry the modal is still in the DOM and no FocusScope timer is\npending; the timer is scheduled only after the hook returns; the next test''s\nbeforeEach starts with one still queued. Moving the same one-tick flush to\nbeforeEach takes that count to zero before the test body runs. beforeEach is\ncorrect by construction -- it always follows the previous test''s afterEach\nhooks whatever the hook-order mode.\n\nCorrect the scope of the claim too. The restore is focus-neutral in this\nfixture: the previouslyFocusedElement it captured is either document.body,\nwhich jsdom refuses to focus, or an already-detached node, and every observed\nfiring left document.activeElement unchanged. The intermittent failure this was\nchasing did not reproduce at e797d50 in 30 consecutive single-file runs or 6\nwhole-package runs. So this closes a scheduling leak, not an observed failure,\nand the comment now says exactly that instead of claiming otherwise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): link the tier-shared-state follow-up\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): drop the phantom rebuild from the instance store''s connect hook\n\nThe connect hook carried `current ??= { value: make() }` behind a comment\nclaiming it gave a reconnect after disposal a fresh value. Measured against\n@reatom/core@1001.1.0 with the shipped code:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 2\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"],[{\"id\":2},\"a\"]]   built = 2\n\nThe reconnect still returned the DISPOSED value — a dependency-free `computed`\nnever recomputes, so its cached result is what every later read gets. The line\nonly built a second model graph nobody could reach and then handed it to\n`dispose` a second time. Same run with the line removed:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n\nThe comment now states what is true: a stale reconnect reads the cached,\nalready-disposed value, and a fresh value comes only from a new `store()`\nlookup — which is what every real caller does, because it calls `store()`\nduring render and the cleanup has already evicted the stale handle.\n\nWith the phantom build gone, `if (held)` is what prevents the double dispose,\nso a test pins it: replacing it with an unconditional `dispose?.(held?.value,\nkey)` now fails with a second teardown recorded as `[undefined, \"a\"]`.\n\nAlso corrects the module doc''s cost claim for reading atoms inside `make`.\nIt does not \"silently rebuild the whole value\": measured, after such a\ndependency changes the compute function re-runs once, `current ??=`\nshort-circuits it so `make` does not re-run (built stayed 1, the identical\nobject was returned, no subscriber notified), and because the re-run never\nreads the atom again the edge is dropped. The cost is a pointless dependency\nedge, not lost state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): align the tier-shared-state spec with what shipped\n\nThree claims in the design spec described work that was never delivered:\n\n- The instance atom was said to carry `withDisconnectHook`. It carries\n  `withConnectHook`, whose cleanup calls `teardownIfDisconnected()` — which\n  re-checks `isConnected` before disposing and evicts the map entry only while\n  it still points at the handle being torn down.\n- Section 4 \"StrictMode removal\" claimed `<StrictMode>` was dropped from\n  `packages/client/src/app/main.tsx` and replaced by a comment explaining its\n  absence. None of it shipped: `git diff cfff8d2..HEAD -- packages/client/` is\n  empty and the wrapper is still there. Measurement showed the store survives\n  the remount cycle, because the cleanup''s `isConnected` re-check returns\n  without touching a value a live mount still holds. Section 4 is rewritten as\n  \"StrictMode kept, tolerance pinned by test\", stating why the plan''s original\n  reasoning does not apply.\n- The Testing gate, the Documentation list and the Decision Summary repeated\n  the removal. They now say StrictMode stays and that the tolerance is pinned\n  by `describe(''makeWidgetInstanceStore under StrictMode'')`.\n\nThat last claim is measured, not assumed: deleting the `isConnected` re-check\nfrom the cleanup turns two of the three StrictMode tests red, along with the\nnon-StrictMode same-tick-resubscribe test.\n\nThe plan file is left untouched — it is the historical record of what was\nintended.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): narrow unmeasured claims and close two coverage gaps\n\nComment/claim accuracy — all of these stated more than was ever measured:\n\n- `ui/recovery-flow.test.tsx`: the \"roughly 1 in 15-40 runs\" hang frequency\n  was applied to every Escape-closing test in the file. It was measured for\n  exactly one of them, ''does not collapse when recovery opens from the tile''\n  (task-4-report.md, Fix round 3). The others carry the same wait by\n  inspection — identical shape, identical race — with no observed rate. The\n  comment now says so.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"at `beforeEach` entry one (later\n  two) FocusScope timer is pending\" — only the 1 -> 0 transition was ever\n  recorded, so the \"(later two)\" is dropped.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"jsdom (like real browsers) always\n  completes an outer `.focus()` call on its own target\" — true and measured\n  for jsdom, never measured against a real browser. Scoped to jsdom.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: ''keeps focus inside when it moves\n  between two of our own controls'' promised a focus invariant the test does\n  not assert — it asserts that our guard swallows the `focusout` before it\n  reaches `document`. Renamed to say that.\n\nCoverage:\n\n- `model/instance-store.test.ts` (new) covers `dispose: (models) =>\n  models.recoveryModel.teardown()`, the safety net against leaking a live VNC\n  session when the modal outlived the widget. It had no test at all: replacing\n  the callback with a no-op left the whole widget suite green. Verified both\n  new tests fail against that no-op.\n- `ui/PassportChecker.test.tsx`: two assertions used\n  `expect(await screen.findAllBy*(…)).toHaveLength(2)`, which cannot retry —\n  `findAllBy*` resolves on the first snapshot with >= 1 match, so it settles on\n  one mount and never waits for the second. Both are now a re-querying\n  `waitFor`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(backlog): record the two-modal focus ping-pong as a deferred defect\n\nBoth `useModalIsolation` instances register focus containment on `window` in\nthe capture phase, so two mounted modals each see the other''s `focusin` before\nthe root-level `stopPropagation()` can stop it, and each pulls focus into its\nown root — forever.\n\nReproduced in jsdom 29.1.1 with two `useModalIsolation` roots mounted at once:\none modal performs 1 focus pull on mount; two mounted together never settle,\nand a hard test guard at 400 pulls had to stop the run (402 recorded).\n\nDeferred rather than fixed because it is unreachable through the UI: the\npassport checker is the only owner of such a modal, renders at most one\n(non-fullscreen mount only), and its overlay is a full-viewport\n`pointer-events: auto` layer whose `pointerdown` handler closes the modal, so\na second one cannot be opened over the first.\n\nThe entry notes the likely fix — a module-level stack of active modal roots\nwith containment applied only by the topmost.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): correct five unverified claims left by code review\n\nBring wording in the tier-shared-state spec, BACKLOG.md, and a test\ncomment in line with what was actually measured/read: the instance\nstore''s dependency-edge cost (not a rebuilt value), a stale\nuse-modal-isolation.ts line citation (:53 -> :55, two places), the\nroot focusin stopper''s actual bubble phase (not capture), and which\nrecovery-flow tests keep the shared timeout default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay at this branch\n\nPins rpi.dev.toml''s source.branch to feat/passport-checker-widget so the dev\nenvironment runs this PR instead of the dev branch.\n\nREVERT BEFORE MERGING: once this PR lands on dev, source.branch must go back to\n\"dev\" or the dev overlay stays pinned to a branch that no longer moves.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): drop the stale Chromium singleton lock before launch\n\nThe Chromium profile is a named Docker volume, so `SingletonLock` keeps the\n`<hostname>-<pid>` of whichever container wrote it. After a redeploy the new\ncontainer has a new hostname, Chromium reads a lock owned by \"another computer\"\nit cannot probe, and `launchPersistentContext` fails on every task until the\nvolume is touched by hand.\n\nRemove `SingletonLock`, `SingletonCookie`, and `SingletonSocket` before each\nlaunch. `fs.rm` unlinks the symlinks themselves instead of following them, and\n`force` keeps a fresh profile from failing; a removal that does fail is returned\nas a value, logged, and the launch still proceeds. Unconditional removal is safe\nbecause `getPersistentContext` is the only caller of `launch` and the image\nentrypoint runs a single `node dist/index.cjs` - no check for a live Chromium is\nperformed or implied.\n\nAlso asserts that a failed launch keeps its cause, which is what makes the\nPlaywright message reachable from the service log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): log the error behind an internal task failure\n\nInternal failures were logged as `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` only, so the\ntagged error and its cause chain never reached the container log. A Chromium\nlaunch failure therefore showed up as a bare `internal` code with nothing to\ndiagnose. Add the error to the logged fields; `toEnvelopeError` still answers\nthe client with the redacted `internal` code, so only the log gains detail.\n\nNote the trade-off: a handler error whose message carries task data now reaches\nthe container log, which the previous field list deliberately avoided.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): redact handler failures in the internal task log\n\nfa87a47c logged too much. It added `error: outcome` to the internal-failure log\nso a Chromium launch failure would stop being invisible, but that field carries\nwhatever the handler threw. errore''s `toJSON()` serialises `message`, `stack`,\nand the whole `cause` chain, Playwright quotes `page.evaluate` arguments into\nits messages, and the passport widget passes the secret identity as one — so a\nhandler failure could print the passport series and number into the container\nlog. The wrapper''s own `stack` repeats the cause under `Caused by:`, so the\nidentity landed there twice.\n\nThat contradicts the design spec: raw Playwright, network, Zod, and upstream\nresponse details are logged only after redaction, request and response bodies\nare never logged, and logs redact task payloads and secret values\n(docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md\n:350-353, :684).\n\nThe rule now, verified against dispatch.ts:\n\n- `BrowserExecutorError` wraps a failed `executor.acquire(...)`. Acquire runs\n  before the handler and is passed only `(signal, widgetId)` — never the payload\n  or the widget secrets — so its cause chain is Chromium/Playwright launch\n  detail. Still logged in full; this is the stale singleton lock failure\n  fa87a47c existed to surface.\n- Every other internal failure wraps handler output and is reduced to\n  `errorChain`: the names/tags of the error and its `cause` chain and nothing\n  else. No `message`, no `stack`, no foreign properties.\n\nThe log message, the `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` fields, and the\n`code === ''internal''` condition are unchanged.\n\nTests split the single fa87a47c case in two: one pins that an executor failure''s\ncause chain still reaches the log, one asserts a secret-looking cause string is\nabsent from the serialised logged payload. Both were confirmed to fail before\nthe fix and to discriminate after it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add an env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe passport widget''s recovery path (retain the page, issue a single-use\ncapability, stream the live browser over noVNC, let a human solve the\nchallenge, retry) has never been exercised on real hardware, because it only\ntriggers when pasport.org.ua serves a Cloudflare challenge and none has been\nencountered — the smoke test on the Pi returned a normal result. Subproject 8\n(`passport-checker-rpi-integration` in\ndocs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md)\nhas \"embedded noVNC Cloudflare recovery, SSH fallback, and real checker smoke\ntest on the Raspberry Pi\" as an acceptance item and had no way to reach it on\ndemand. PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY is that way.\n\nContract: `1` and nothing else enables it — absent, empty, `0`, `true`, ` 1 `\nare all off (isForcedRecoveryEnabled does exact string equality, no trimming,\nno truthiness coercion). Compose passes it through with a `:-` default so an\nunset variable stays off, and it must never be set in production.\n\nSemantics: at most once per process. The latch lives in the closure\nmakePassportCheckHandler returns, so each handler owns its own and the unit\ntests stay isolated. It fires after a successful goto and after\ncollectNavigationEvidence — the retained page is the real checker page with the\nsite''s own UI loaded, so a human can drive it over noVNC — and returns exactly\nthe retainPageForRecovery() + BrowserSessionRequiredError pair the genuine\nCloudflare branch returns, making the downstream flow byte-identical to a real\nchallenge. The latch is consumed only when the forced path actually fires, so a\nfailed navigation returns its own error untouched and leaves the lever armed:\nan infrastructure hiccup must not burn the single forced run an operator is\nwaiting on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): apply the design-system widget redesign\n\nBring the tile and the recovery modal in line with Паспорт-чекер.dc.html.\n\nBoth tiers now lay out as header -> flexible middle -> action, so the\naction sits on the tile''s bottom edge in every state instead of trailing\nwhatever content happens to be above it. Status banners pick up the\ndesign''s per-state hues, the filled success disc, and the CircleAlert /\nTriangleAlert split that separates a retryable failure from a session\nprompt. The modal gains the access-pill dot, a pulsing LIVE dot, framed\nconnecting/disconnected/expired states, and the shell-style SSH block.\n\nDark theme was effectively missing: the --pc-* hues chained to\n--success-soft / --destructive-soft, which the host token set never\ndefines, and to --success, which is a dark surface green rather than an\nicon green under [data-theme=''dark'']. Every hue is now declared per\ntheme the way ofelia-poop-duty does it. The modal portals to\ndocument.body, outside .widget, so it declares the one hue it needs on\nits own overlay.\n\nThree deliberate departures from the mockup:\n- invalidConfig copy stays server-side (\"Обратитесь к администратору\"),\n  since this widget has no per-instance settings — its config lives in\n  server secrets.\n- the expired frame keeps \"Переподключиться\"; without it an expired\n  capability can only be recovered by closing and reopening the modal.\n- the sessionRequired tiny tile only fills amber, no border: the tile\n  border belongs to the host card, and drawing one from inside the\n  widget would render a second, inset contour.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xwGNctwqXcYi5p4QSSFhA\n\n* docs(browser-automation): design widget user-input detection\n\nMove the \"a human must act\" mechanism out of passport-checker and into the\nautomation platform. The handler gets context.detectUserInput(detector), which\nruns the detector, optionally prepares the page, retains it and returns the\ncanonical error as one indivisible step; retainPageForRecovery leaves the public\ncontext so the halves cannot be separated. Cloudflare becomes a reusable helper\n(probe and predicate together, not just the predicate) that any widget can pass\nin. Also records the removal of the PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY lever.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): correct the user-input spec''s test-seam claims\n\nmakeFakeExecutor fakes BrowserExecutor<FakeContext> with FakeContext = { id,\nsignal }; it never builds a BrowserTaskContext, so it cannot host a recording\ndetectUserInput and needs no change at all. Name the seams that do exist: the\nsix retainPageForRecovery call sites in chromium-executor.test.ts and the three\nBrowserTaskContext literals in diagnostics.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): plan the widget user-input detection refactor\n\nSeven tasks, each green on its own: retire the forced-recovery lever, add the\ndetector and error contracts, move Cloudflare detection into the platform with\nits probe reunited with its predicate, run escalation from the task context,\nrewire the widget, then withdraw retainPageForRecovery from the public context so\nthe retain/escalate pair cannot be taken apart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): make the Cloudflare task a move, not a copy\n\nTask 3 previously left the classifier duplicated between the widget and the\nplatform until Task 5, which any reviewer would rightly flag as verbatim\nduplication of a logic block. The widget now switches to the moved module in the\nsame task, so the logic never exists in two places.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* revert(passport-checker): drop the env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe lever forced one trip through the Cloudflare recovery path per service\nstart so Subproject 8 could exercise noVNC on the Pi on demand. It is being\nretired deliberately ahead of moving user-input detection into the platform;\nthe Pi smoke test needs another answer.\n\nThis reverts the behaviour added in 45c604f1.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(browser-automation): add user-input detector and error contracts\n\nUserInputRequiredError carries the browser_session_required code and sshTarget\nmeta the recovery flow keys off, moved out of the passport-checker widget where\na platform contract had no business living. UserInputProbeError distinguishes\na broken check from a negative one.\n\n* feat(browser-automation): move Cloudflare detection into the platform\n\nThe classifier lived in the passport-checker widget while the DOM probe feeding\nit lived in that widget''s check handler, so the only reusable half was the half\nwithout the probe. Both now sit in browser-automation behind two detector\nfactories: one that probes the live page, one that classifies evidence already\nderived inside the page from a fetch response.\n\nThe widget imports the moved module rather than keeping a copy; it still calls\nthe classifier by hand, which the next commit changes.\n\n* @\ndocs: document the feature branch -> dev -> main workflow\n\nFeature work happens in a dedicated branch inside a ./.worktrees checkout,\nlands in dev through a PR, gets verified on the dev stack, and only then\nreaches main through a dev -> main PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* feat(browser-automation): run user-input detection from the task context\n\ndetectUserInput runs a widget-supplied detector and, on a match, prepares the\npage, retains it and returns UserInputRequiredError as one step, so the two\nhalves cannot drift apart. A detector that fails yields UserInputProbeError and\ndeliberately leaves the page unretained: an undecidable check is not a challenge,\nand pinning a browser page for a recovery nobody will perform is worse.\n\nAUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET moves into the service config, where an unusable value\ndegrades to null rather than failing config loading and killing the service.\n\n* docs(passport-checker): design last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* test(browser-automation): cover the prepare-before-retain order in detectUserInput directly\n\nThe executor-level test asserted hasRetainedPage() from inside the prepare\nmock, but that bookkeeping is only populated at release() time, so it passed\nregardless of call order. Add a direct unit test against makeDetectUserInput\nthat records prepare/retain into one ordered array — genuinely fails if the\norder in detect.ts is inverted (verified by temporarily inverting it).\n\nNo production behavior changes; detect.ts ends identical to before this commit.\n\n* @\ndocs: merge AGENTS.md into CLAUDE.md and symlink it\n\nAGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md had drifted into two partly overlapping rule sets.\nEverything unique to AGENTS.md (project structure, codegen commands, Windows\ntest-runner notes, coding style, UI gotchas, testing, security, failure modes)\nnow lives in CLAUDE.md, and AGENTS.md becomes a symlink so every agent reads\nthe same file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* docs(passport-checker): plan last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* feat(passport-checker): date-aware check timestamp label\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout design\n\nTwo width breakpoints instead of one hardcoded grid: one column below 768,\ntwelve above, with row height and margin scaled by the same factor as the\ncolumn width so the desktop grid zooms instead of stretching on one axis.\n\nThe mobile layout is derived from the desktop one and only persisted once the\nuser drags or resizes, stored as an additive optional `mobileLayout` field so\nno migration of `root:boards` is needed.\n\nAlso records the react-draggable touchstart defect: the whole card is the drag\nhandle today, which cancels page scrolling on touch — harmless on a short\n12-column board, blocking at one column.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): detect required user input through the platform\n\nThe handler no longer collects Cloudflare evidence, classifies it, retains the\npage and builds the escalation error by hand at every site; it passes a detector\nto context.detectUserInput and returns what comes back. browser/challenge.ts and\nthe widget-local BrowserSessionRequiredError are gone — the classifier now lives\nin browser-automation next to the probe that feeds it, and the\nbrowser_session_required contract belongs to the platform that owns the recovery\nflow.\n\n* fix(passport-checker): restore the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nAn automated amend from a concurrent session accidentally stripped this\nline out of 3a12494e. It belongs to the user-input-detection work; this\nrestores it verbatim.\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nRETRYABLE_MESSAGES.user_input_probe vanished twice with no test turning red,\nbecause nothing asserted the actual behaviour: with the entry gone,\nmapCheckError silently falls back to GENERIC_RETRYABLE_MESSAGE and the user\nsees the generic string instead of the specific one. Assert the real output\nof mapCheckError against a hardcoded expected string, not against the table\nitself, so a future deletion or typo of the entry fails this test instead of\ncomparing the map against itself.\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron design spec\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout implementation plan\n\n* feat(board): resolve grid metrics from the container width\n\n* feat(passport-checker): persist the last successful check\n\n* feat(board): derive and reconcile the one-column mobile layout\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron implementation plan\n\nAlso corrects the spec: the jsdom Temporal shims must stay, only the\n--harmony-temporal execArgv becomes redundant on Node 26.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin which detector goes to which detectUserInput call\n\nNothing previously checked that the navigation check gets the page detector\nand the submission check gets the evidence detector -- only the second call''s\nprepare option was asserted. A handler that swapped the two factories would\nleave every test in this file green; only the gated real-browser suite would\nnotice, and it isn''t run in CI. Pull the captured detector off each mock call\nand invoke it directly: the page detector must reach for page.evaluate, the\nevidence detector must ignore its page argument and classify the real\nevidence. Also drops the now-inert hasChallengeForm evidence field from the\nsubmission-escalation test, whose result was always driven by the mock''s\nescalation queue regardless of that field.\n\n* feat(board): add mobile layout materialize, update and reset actions\n\n* refactor(browser-automation): expose only detectUserInput on the task context\n\nretainPageForRecovery was half of a pair a handler had to assemble correctly by\nhand: retaining without escalating strands a browser page, escalating without\nretaining hands the UI a recovery session with nothing behind it. With the retain\nflag reachable only through detectUserInput, neither mistake is expressible.\n\n* test(passport-checker): cover restore-on-mount and cross-placement sync\n\nAdd two widget-level cases pinning storage-backed persistence through\nReact: a fresh mount renders a previously stored result without\ninvoking a check, and a check made in one placement is observed by a\nsecond placement sharing the same storage. Rename the pre-existing\n\"gives separate instance ids separate state\" case to \"gives separate\nstorages separate state\" (it isolates by storage, not instanceId) and\nupdate its comment to point at the new cross-placement case below it.\nPin PASSPORT_LAST_RESULT_KEY''s literal value in check-model.test.ts so\na silent rename cannot slip through green while orphaning deployed\nusers'' stored data under the old key.\n\n* feat(board): switch the grid to resolved metrics and a mobile drag grip\n\n* docs(browser-automation): document how a widget requests manual input\n\n* feat(board): offer a mobile layout reset in the board schema popover\n\n* fix(widgets): address passport-checker persistence review findings\n\n- TinyTier''s success chip now shows checkedAtLabel, so a restored (possibly\n  stale) result reads honestly in the compact tile too, not just standard.\n- Harden the two-placement isolation test: assert placement two stays idle\n  instead of a length-1 findAllByText that can pass mid-race.\n- Clarify why lastResult/transient set order is safe in checkPassport\n  (Reatom notifies after the sync block, not \"order for readability\").\n- Add a test exercising lastResultSchema.safeParse over the exact shape\n  checkPassport writes, since createFakeStorage skips schema validation.\n- Document lastResultSchema as a persistence contract: new fields must be\n  optional/defaulted or every stored value fails validation on next deploy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* fix(passport-checker): remove dead AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET threading\n\nrecoverySshTarget was never read by check.ts''s handler after the\ndetectUserInput refactor moved sshTarget sourcing entirely into the\nplatform config. Delete the widget''s leftover env read and option\nthreading so the README''s \"widgets neither read nor pass it\" claim is\nactually true, and drop the now-meaningless SSH-sanitization test in\nfavor of the equivalent (and broader) coverage in browser-automation''s\nconfig.test.ts.\n\n* docs(ofelia): design the authored ledger and comments\n\nRecords get the authenticated account instead of the per-device \"Я:\"\ntoggle, the write path moves into the widget''s own server.ts, and the\nhistory row is rebuilt: human dates, plain-language phrases, explicit\ndebt effect, visible supersession, no IP.\n\nPulls the Node 26 prerequisite out of the widget-server-cron spec, since\nthe shared domain module it enables is needed here first.\n\nAlso imports the Claude Design workspace under design/ (documents only,\nscreenshots and uploads stripped) and keeps the brief that produced the\nOfelia mock.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs(ofelia): note the cron plan overlap and landing order\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): cover the mobile layout and touch-scroll behaviour end to end\n\n* fix(board): keep the measured grid container mounted\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a 1920 viewport\n\n* docs(ofelia): add the authored ledger implementation plan\n\n15 tasks: Node 26, the pure domain split, the members directory, widget\nidentity, the server-resolved viewer, record schemas, draft construction,\nthe widget server definition, the model rewire, the history and comment\nview models, the account avatar, and the two rebuilt panels.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): wait for the grid relayout to settle before measuring\n\n* fix(browser-automation): bundle workspace imports instead of externalizing them\n\nThe rspack externals predicate was an allow-list whose default was\n\"externalize\". When the user-input refactor introduced the first runtime\nbare-specifier import of a workspace package into the service module graph\n(browser-automation/user-input/cloudflare, from the passport-checker browser\ntask), it was silently externalized: the build stayed green, all tests stayed\ngreen, and the Cloudflare module was simply absent from dist/index.cjs. The\nruntime stage copies only package.json and dist/, so Node resolved the\nspecifier through the exports map to a .ts path that does not exist in the\nimage and the container died on `exec node dist/index.cjs`.\n\nInvert the predicate into a deny-list derived from the package''s own\ndependencies (minus errore, which is ESM-only and must be bundled) plus Node\nbuiltins. An unrecognised bare specifier is now bundled, so the same mistake\nsurfaces as a build-time resolution error rather than a startup crash, and\nadding a real runtime dependency externalizes it with no config edit.\n\nAdd scripts/check-bundle.ts, chained onto `build` so it runs inside the image\nbuild the Dockerfile already performs. It asserts that every require() in the\nbundle is a builtin or a declared dependency, that the scan still matches the\nemitted shape at all, and that the compiled evidenceFromResponseText the widget\nsplices into Chromium still evaluates and runs in a bare vm realm. That last\ncheck is what the BROWSER_IT suite could never prove: it runs under vitest,\nwhere the specifier resolves to .ts through esbuild and the swc path is never\nexercised. Verified: the compiled form is ES5-lowered but self-contained --\ndestructuring became inline property reads, no swc helpers, no hoisted regexes.\n\nAlso:\n- detect.ts: tag the prepare outcome instead of comparing against null, so a\n  prepare that rejects with exactly null is a failure rather than a silent\n  success, matching the detector branch''s `typeof detected !== ''boolean''`.\n- user-input/errors.test.ts: cover toEnvelopeError for UserInputRequiredError\n  with and without an ssh target, pinning with toStrictEqual that `meta` is\n  omitted rather than set to undefined. contracts.test.ts already claimed this\n  coverage lived here.\n- chromium-executor.test.ts: drop an assertion inside a prepare callback that\n  could not fail the test and was vacuous, and rename the case to what it\n  actually verifies; the ordering contract is owned by detect.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* test(board): settle past the grid transition and stop assuming card order\n\n* test(board): correct the stale comment above the breakpoint wait\n\n* chore(node): align every runtime and image on Node 26\n\nnode:26-alpine and node:26-bookworm-slim no longer bundle corepack\n(removed from Node core), so `npm install -g corepack@latest` is added\nahead of every `corepack enable` in the three Dockerfiles and\ndocker-compose.dev.yml. scripts/infra.test.ts''s assertion on the\nbrowser-automation Dockerfile''s base image is updated to match.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(node): unshadow jsdom localStorage and bump the e2e image to Node 26\n\nNode 26 ships its own unflagged global localStorage/sessionStorage (Web\nStorage API), which resolves to undefined without --localstorage-file\nand shadows jsdom''s window.localStorage for bare-identifier reads in\npackages/client''s jsdom test environment (40 failing tests). Disable\nNode''s own globals via execArgv: [''--no-experimental-webstorage''] in\npackages/client/vite.config.ts so jsdom''s storage wins; verified via\nObject.getOwnPropertyDescriptor that the flag actually removes the\nglobal rather than just suppressing the warning. widget-runtime and\nwidget-sdk don''t touch localStorage in tests today, so the flag is not\nadded to their execArgv arrays (narrowest correct remedy).\n\nAlso bumps packages/client/e2e.Dockerfile (used by pnpm test:e2e:docker\nvia docker-compose.e2e.yml) from node:22-bookworm-slim to\nnode:26-bookworm-slim, missed by the original brief''s Files list, with\nthe same corepack@latest install this Node 26 base image now needs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): make the relayout wait actually poll\n\n* refactor(ofelia): extract the pure duty domain out of the Reatom model\n\n* fix(lint): make the widget domain import barrier a real allowlist\n\nThe regex denylist let ''../model/ofelia-duty'' through — domain/ and model/\nare siblings, so a single-''../'' import was both the most likely accidental\nescape and completely unguarded. It also only enumerated known packages, so\nany new npm dependency passed silently.\n\nReplace it with a gitignore-style group allowlist (''**'' minus zod, @shared/*\nand ''./'' siblings), which oxlint evaluates natively. A negative-lookahead\nregex was tried first and rejected: oxlint''s Rust regex engine has no\nlookaround and silently drops such a pattern, disabling the whole barrier.\n\nDomain tests get a second, later override that additionally allows vitest;\neverything else stays blocked there too.\n\n* chore(lint): keep the design mock exports out of lint and format\n\ndesign/ holds vendored Claude Design exports (support.js, *.dc.html)\nthat break the repo''s only gate: oxlint reports 9 errors in\ndesign/support.js, and oxfmt aborts (exit 2) on non-standard markup\nin design/Design System.dc.html before it can check anything else.\n\nAdd design/** to the existing ignorePatterns glob array in both\n.oxlintrc.json and .oxfmtrc.json, the same mechanism already used for\ndocs/**. This is a plain glob matcher (not the Rust regex engine\nbehind no-restricted-imports), so it isn''t subject to that engine''s\nlookaround gap, and it leaves every other rule/override untouched.\n\nFixing the oxfmt abort also uncovered pre-existing, unrelated\nformatting drift in CLAUDE.md, package.json and\nscripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts that the crash had been masking from\nthe format:check summary. Reformatted them (whitespace/ordering only,\nno logic changes) so pnpm format:check is actually green end to end.\n\n* feat(auth): expose the board members directory\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): expose the board identity to widgets\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): stop identity fetch from leaking an unhandled rejection and a discarded error\n\nHandle the AbortError that wrap()''s promise rejects with when the last\nwidget reading identity unmounts mid-fetch, and stop discarding\nBoardMembersError whole: a 401 stays silent (expected on an\nunauthenticated host), everything else is surfaced via console.warn so\nschema drift or a server fault is not silently swallowed.\n\n* feat(widgets): resolve the signed-in viewer for widget server events\n\n* feat(ofelia): carry the authoring account on ledger and comment records\n\n* feat(ofelia): move draft construction and event schemas into the domain\n\n* feat(ofelia): write the ledger and comments from the widget server\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): harden server date parsing and unforgeability coverage\n\n- Wrap Temporal.PlainDate.from in errore.try so a calendar-invalid but\n  regex-valid date (e.g. 2026-02-30) returns an Error instead of\n  throwing through a function typed Error | T.\n- Add regression coverage: forged createdBy/actor in the payload is\n  ignored, the debt handler is exercised, storage-read failures are\n  asserted by identity with no write, and the invalid-date case is\n  asserted as a value.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): split the unforgeability guard into schema and handler halves\n\nThe round-1 forged-payload test went through run(), which parses the\npayload via zod''s default strip mode before the handler ever sees it —\nso it only proved the schema strips forged fields, twice over, and\nwould not catch a handler that starts spreading its raw payload into\nthe draft. Replace it with two schema-level tests (clean + comment\npayloads drop a forged createdBy/actor) and one handler-level test\nthat calls ofeliaServer.handlers.clean directly, bypassing the schema,\nto prove the handler itself never reads those fields even if they\nreach it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* feat(ofelia): drive ledger and comment writes through widget server events\n\nThe client no longer appends to the ledger or the comment key. All four\nday actions collapse into one `invokeDay` that sends `{ date }` to the\nmatching widget-server event, and `send` sends `{ weekStart, text }` —\nwho the actor is, whose debt moves, whether the event applies at all and\nwhich account signed it are now derived and stamped server-side, so a\nclient cannot forge authorship.\n\n`selectedDate()` and `today()` (and `viewWeekStart()`) are read before\nthe first await so they resolve against the widget''s own Reatom context.\n\nThe per-device \"Я: Леша/Карина\" toggle is deleted rather than hidden:\nauthorship comes from the session, so there is nothing left to pick.\n`currentUser` is gone from the duty model, the widget context, the\nfixture and the view-model''s `onSetUser`.\n\nBoth model factories now take `api` and `identity`; `identity` is the\ndirectory the history and comment views read in the follow-up tasks.\nThe view projections drop `ip`/`by`, whose schema fields Tasks 5-8\nremoved, and `authorName` falls back through `createdBy.name`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* style: format the ofelia server test\n\n* feat(ofelia): group history by duty day and mark superseded records\n\n* feat(ofelia): resolve comment authors through the members directory\n\n* fix(ofelia): flush the comment thread''s connect hook via vi.waitFor, not a bare microtask\n\nReview round 1: the timing fix for the comment-thread subscribe/emit race\nassumed a single-microtask flush of Reatom''s connect-hook queue. Match the\nsibling comments-subscription tests in the same file instead — wait for the\nstorage mock''s subscribe call and wrap the whole body in context.start, so\nthe assertion is condition-based rather than scheduling-internals-based.\n\n* feat(ofelia): add the account avatar and its palette\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the history list around duty-day groups\n\n* fix(ofelia): announce the history phrase subject to assistive tech\n\nThe duty circle is the sentence subject in the rebuilt history phrase, and\nAvatar is aria-hidden, so the column announced as bare verbs with no subject.\nPair every circle with a visually hidden name, attribute the debt pill to its\nperson, and separate the chips with real spaces as the mock does.\n\nAlso correct the text-decoration comment to name the real mechanism (atomic\ninline, not descendant cancellation) and cover the three untested branches.\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the comment thread around account authors\n\n* fix(ofelia): restore composer coverage and mute the unknown comment author\n\n* fix(ofelia): break ledger day ties by append order\n\nRouting the writes through the widget server changed where `ts` comes from.\n`/api/storage/:key/append` stamped `Date.now()`, so consecutive appends always\ngot a strictly increasing timestamp. The widget dispatch stamps the app''s\ninjectable `now()`, and the e2e harness pins that clock — so a clean-then-undo\npair on the same day produced two entries with an identical `ts`, and\n`latestOutcomesByDate`''s strict `>` kept the earlier `cleaned` entry as the\nday''s outcome. The `reset` was written but never counted, and the day never\nreopened; `pnpm test:e2e:docker` caught it as a failing \"undo — returns the day\nto pending\".\n\nThe ledger is append-only and iterated in insertion order, so `>=` is the\ncorrect tie-break: on an equal timestamp the later-appended entry is the newer\ndecision. This also hardens production, where two writes can still land in the\nsame millisecond.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: describe server-authored widget records\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): fail widget dispatch when the session account is unreadable\n\nresolveWidgetViewer folded every getAccount failure into `null`, so a Valkey\nfault, a corrupt account record and a deleted account all produced a record\nstamped `createdBy: null` plus a `{ ok: true }` response. The ledger is\nappend-only, so that authorship could never be recovered.\n\nThe three outcomes are now distinct: no session still returns `null`, which is\nwhat the standalone harnesses and the e2e stack (no nginx, so no session cookie)\nrely on to keep writing unattributed records; a failed lookup returns a\nWidgetViewerLookupError, which the dispatch route turns into a fault instead of\na false success.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): order superseded history records by append order\n\n`superseded` sorted on `right.ts - left.ts`, which returns 0 on a tie; the\nstable sort then left tied records in append order, oldest first, contradicting\nthe \"newest first\" contract on HistoryDayGroup. Ties became a supported case\nwhen latestOutcomesByDate started breaking them with `>=`, and the dockerized\ne2e stack freezes the clock, so there every record ties.\n\nThe ledger is append-only, so append order is chronological order: reversing the\nday''s bucket is both correct and cheaper than sorting.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): warn when listAccounts skips an unreadable record\n\nA corrupt or schema-mismatched account record was dropped silently, so the\nroster simply came back short and the symptom read as \"an account vanished\"\nrather than as the data corruption it is. Name the key in a console.warn.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* build: pin corepack to 0.35.0 across images and dev compose\n\n`npm install -g corepack@latest` ran in five image build stages and four dev\ncompose services. There is no CI and the Pi deploy is manual with a 30-minute\nbuild timeout, so a breaking corepack release would only ever surface as a\nfailed deploy. Pin one explicit version everywhere.\n\nVerified: `docker build -f packages/server/Dockerfile` succeeds and the built\nimage reports corepack 0.35.0 with pnpm 10.28.2.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* refactor(ofelia): delete the dead short-date formatter\n\nformatDateShort and MONTHS_SHORT lost their last production consumer when the\ncomment thread moved to its own formatter; only the test still called them. It\nalso formatted through `new Date(ts)` in the local timezone, while every current\nformatter goes through plainDateIn(DUTY_TIME_ZONE, ...).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): drop the tier guards that cannot fail, hoist the env directive\n\nStandardTier and CompactTier each asserted they do not render UserToggle. The\ntoggle only ever lived in RichLayout and the component is now deleted outright,\nso neither assertion could ever fail — they only looked like coverage.\n\nAlso move the `@vitest-environment node` directive in server.test.ts back to\nline 1, where a reader expects it and where model/history-view.test.ts keeps it.\nVitest 4 matches the directive anywhere in the file content, so the file was\nalready running under node; this is readability, not a behaviour fix.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: state the authorship guarantee the code actually provides\n\nCLAUDE.md claimed a server-stamped author \"cannot be forged\". Nothing enforces\nthat: POST /api/storage/:key/append does no session check in Node, and nginx''s\nauth_request only proves a session exists, not whose it is, so any signed-in\nhousehold member can still hand-write a record carrying an arbitrary author.\n\nState what is true instead — the UI can no longer sign a record with someone\nelse''s name, and the generic storage route remains a shared trusted channel\ninside the board. Closing that route is a separate task.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(board): never render the desktop grid below its 1920 baseline\n\nThe desktop zoom is now one-directional: raise the lower clamp on the\ngrid scale from 0.75 to 1, so every container width between the mobile\nbreakpoint and BASE_WIDTH resolves to exactly the historical metrics\n(rowHeight: 30, margin: [10, 10]) and only wider displays scale up.\n\nUntil this branch, useContainerWidth never attached its observer and\npinned the reported width at its 1280 default on every screen. With the\ncontainer measured for real, a 0.75 floor would have shrunk every board\non a 1280/1366/1440 laptop below the size it has always had, dropping an\nh:6 card from 230px to 172.5px and pushing widgets under their own tier\nthresholds. Nothing shrinks now; the 4K aspect drift the scale was added\nfor is still fixed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling record\n\nBring the design doc in line with what shipped: the lower clamp is 1,\nthe 1280 table row shows the baseline metrics, and the paragraph\njustifying the clamp says plainly that it is active across the whole\n768-1920 band rather than a narrow 768-1100 one.\n\nAlso correct the claim that existing 1080p boards \"do not shift by a\npixel\". The formula at 1920 does reproduce the old constants, but the\nmeasured width was previously pinned at 1280 on every screen by the\nuseContainerWidth bug this branch fixes, so wider screens legitimately\nre-flow to use the whole window.\n\nDrop the 1920 viewport pin from ofelia-duty.spec.ts: it only worked\naround the 0.75 clamp, and removing it restores default-viewport\ncoverage. Recompute the footprint arithmetic in the ofelia client test\ncomment, which assumed a container of exactly 1280 rather than the\nviewport minus the board padding and the scrollbar gutter.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a viewport its tier needs\n\nEvery assertion in the duty spec is StandardTier UI, and the widget only\nresolves `standard` at a frame width of 400px or more. Playwright''s\ndefault 1280 viewport is about 7px short: the board measures its own\ncontainer, which is 1225px there once the stable scrollbar gutter and the\nboard''s 20px padding on each side come off, so a w:4 card is ~395px and\nits frame ~393px. `compact` is the correct rendering at that width, so\nthe spec has to declare the width its assertions need.\n\nThe dependency was previously invisible because the board reported a\nhardcoded 1280px container on every screen, which made the card 413px and\nleft the threshold only 13px of headroom against a measurement that was\nwrong by 55px.\n\nCorrect the footprint comment in the widget''s client test to match: the\ntier is measured on the frame inside the card''s 1px border, so a 1280px\nviewport yields ~393x228 and resolves to `compact`, not `standard`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling claims to match what ships\n\nThree statements asserted the opposite of what this branch established,\nall written before the width axis was measured.\n\nThe grid-metrics JSDoc claimed the clamp keeps 1280/1366/1440 boards\n\"the size they have always been\" and widgets \"above their own tier\nthresholds\". Both are false at 1280: the container is 1225 there, so a\nw:4 card is 395px where the old code drew 413px, and ofelia falls under\nits 400px standard threshold into compact. The clamp pins the metrics -\nrowHeight 30 and margin [10, 10] at every desktop width - not the\nrendered geometry, which still follows the measured container.\n\nThe design doc claimed \"no board shrinks and no widget loses a tier\".\nThe 1366 and 1440 frames (422px, 446px) clear the widget threshold; the\n1280 frame (393px) does not. Corrected to draw the metrics/geometry\ndistinction the document already draws two paragraphs earlier.\n\nThe \"do not shift by a pixel\" claim survived in the doc''s Testing\nsection and in the test''s own comment. At 1080p the container is now\n1865, so colWidth is 144.58 rather than the 95.83 the pinned-1280 bug\nproduced. Both now state what the test guarantees: the formula at\nBASE_WIDTH reproduces the previous hardcoded constants.\n\nAlso record the shipped consequence in Risks - ofelia renders\nCompactTier below ~1300px, correct behaviour on a genuinely 393px frame,\nwith recalibration left as a follow-up - and fix three figures: the tier\nparagraph attributed the frame inset to a card padding that does not\nexist (it is a 1px border; the heights are 148px and 298px, not 110 and\n260), the e2e headroom was 11px on the frame rather than 13px on the\ncard, the 1920 card/frame are 608/606, and useContainerWidth is at\nBoard.tsx:28.\n\nComments and prose only; no executable line changed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* build(deploy): add a per-branch rpi environment and deploy scripts\n\nrpi.branch.toml is a disposable stack for trying the checked-out branch on\nreal hardware, with ttl = \"72h\" so a forgotten one reaps itself. It takes the\nbranch through ${BRANCH_NAME}, the only variable rpi 0.26 accepts — hence the\nliteral hostname rather than a derived one.\n\nscripts/deploy-branch.ts reads the branch from git so nobody retypes it, and\nrefuses to deploy without .env.branch, which would otherwise produce a stack\nwhose WebAuthn gate fails with nothing in the deploy log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(deploy): send the branch secrets bundle before the build\n\nEvery branch is a fresh rpi project with an empty secrets store, so the very\nfirst deploy of any branch died after the ~3 minute image build with an opaque\nCompose \"secret file ... does not exist\". deploy:branch now checks\n`rpi secrets ls` and sends the bundle itself when there is none.\n\nAlso strip the `--` that `pnpm run x -- --flag` forwards verbatim: rpi''s parser\nreads a bare `--` as end-of-flags, so the flag after it was rejected as a\npositional.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(browser-automation): read the spliced callback when swc stops inlining it\n\ncheck-bundle extracted the page callback by slicing between `.concat(` and\n`.toString()`, which is the whole function expression only while swc inlines it.\nA second consumer of evidenceFromResponseText — precisely what the public\nbrowser-automation/user-input/cloudflare subpath exists to allow — makes swc emit\na named declaration instead, leaving a bare identifier at the splice site. The\nguard then evaluated that identifier in an empty realm, got a ReferenceError, and\nfailed the image build accusing a perfectly correct bundle of shipping a\nnon-self-contained callback.\n\nResolve the identifier to its declaration, and let the JavaScript parser find\nwhere that declaration ends: counting braces by hand would have to understand\nregex literals, and this body is full of regexes containing quote characters.\nExtraction failures now say so instead of reporting a self-containment defect.\n\nVerified by building all four ways: inlined and named shapes both pass; the\noriginal externals regression and a regex hoisted out of the callback are both\nstill caught.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* fix(ofelia): centre the initial in avatars re-cast to inline-flex\n\nBoth avatars centre their initial with `place-items: center`, but the\nhistory phrase re-casts the box to `inline-flex` so the circle stays in\nthe inline run, and a flex container ignores `justify-items`. The glyph\nwas left-aligned: in an 18px circle it sat 0px from the inline start and\n12.97px from the end, roughly a third of the diameter off centre.\n\nName the inline axis outright so the centring survives whichever display\na caller imposes. `.debt > [data-tone]` re-casts the same way and was hit\nby the same bug.\n\n* fix(ofelia): reserve room for the viewer ring in scrolling columns\n\nThe signed-in viewer''s avatar carries a 3px ring drawn as a spread\nshadow, so it paints outside the border box. Every avatar sits flush\nagainst the inline start of a container that clips: `.historyCol` and\n`.list` scroll (and `overflow-y: auto` clips the inline axis too), and\n`.commentsCol` is `overflow: hidden`. The ring lost its whole left edge —\nmeasured at exactly 3.0px — leaving a flat-sided avatar in the history,\nthe comment list and the composer, in both themes and on mobile.\n\nWiden each clip box outward by the ring width with a padding/negative\nmargin pair, which moves no content, and hold the width in\n`--ofelia-viewer-ring` so the shadow and the reserved room cannot drift.\n\n* fix(ofelia): size the account initial like the duty initial\n\n`MemberAvatar` scaled its initial to half the box while `Avatar` uses\n0.4, and the history phrase renders both at `px={18}` on the same line —\n9px next to 7.2px, which reads as two mismatched type sizes rather than\none set.\n\nShare the ratio through `AVATAR_INITIAL_RATIO`. Taking `Avatar`''s value\nkeeps every pre-existing avatar (the 62px hero, the 26px week strip)\npixel-identical and moves only the account squares this branch added.\n\n* fix(ofelia): raise the standard tier to the height it actually needs\n\nStandardTier lays out to 298px of content, but `tiers.standard` asked for\nonly 200px and the widget card clips instead of scrolling. At the default\nplacement (413x230px) the tier therefore resolved and then cut its own\naction row in half: \"Уборка подтверждена\" was sliced through and \"В долг\"\n/ \"Простить\" never appeared at all.\n\nState the height the tier needs, so anything shorter degrades to\n`compact` rather than rendering truncated, and raise `defaultSize` to\n`{ w: 4, h: 8 }` (~413x310px) so a freshly-placed widget still clears it\n— the same fix shape as the earlier default-placement regression.\n\n* fix(ofelia): let the pinned day header cover the reserved ring strip\n\nThe history column reserves `--ofelia-viewer-ring` of inline-start room\nso the viewer ring is not clipped, and records paint into that strip. The\nsticky day header''s own box stops at the content edge, so the strip stayed\nuncovered: scrolling avatars slid past the pinned header through a 3px\ngap, measured against the column''s border box.\n\nStretch the header back over the strip with a negative inline-start margin\nand return the width as padding, so its background spans the full\nscrollport while nothing inside it moves.\n\n* feat(ofelia): scroll the history to the day picked in the week strip\n\nSelecting a day highlighted it in the strip and changed the panel, but\nleft the history where it was, so the records for that day had to be found\nby hand.\n\n`HistoryList` now keeps a node per duty day and scrolls the matching group\nto the top of its scrollport when the selection changes. Exactly one\nancestor scrolls in each layout — the column on desktop, the dialog body\non mobile — so the native `scrollIntoView` cannot drag two scrollports at\nonce. The first run only records the mounted day: scrolling on mount would\npull the panel above the history off screen in the mobile layout before\nthe reader touched anything.\n\nThe…","tree":{"sha":"7eef907f0b2c70b7a6a4097a8caa69c6c83524fa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/git/trees/7eef907f0b2c70b7a6a4097a8caa69c6c83524fa"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/git/commits/ea12a9cd55976786e9d268e5f8aa9de2d6bfd27f","verification":{"payload":"tree 7eef907f0b2c70b7a6a4097a8caa69c6c83524fa\nparent 56fd7f8fed8ca841fc1b388c832eecb3efe88049\nauthor khmilevoi <Khmilevoi@gmail.com> 1785592322 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785592322 +0200\n\nrelease: merge dev into main (#36)\n\n* fix(passport-checker): root-cause the recovery-flow Escape-close race\n\nfindByRole(''dialog'') resolves as soon as the dialog''s DOM node commits,\nbut useModalIsolation''s mount effect - which attaches the Escape\nlistener and moves focus in - is a passive effect that can still be\npending a tick later. Firing Escape into that gap dispatched into a\ndocument with no listener yet, so the dialog never closed and the\nfollowing waitFor spun until it timed out; reproduced solo (no\nwhole-suite contention needed) in roughly 1 of 15-40 runs.\n\nEach of the two tests that close the modal now waits for the effect''s\nsynchronous side effect (focus landing inside the dialog) before\ndispatching Escape, which proves the same effect has also attached the\nlistener. Replaces the prior fix round''s blanket file-wide 30s test\ntimeout (and its comment''s now-falsified \"never a hang\" claim) with a\nscoped 10s per-test timeout on only the two tests that actually run\nthis round trip, as a safety margin for ordinary contention rather\nthan a mask for this race.\n\nVerified with 40 solo + 32 self-inflicted-parallel + 8 whole-suite\nreruns after the fix, zero reproductions of the timeout.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): hold focus inside the recovery modal\n\nRadix restores focus from a zero-delay timeout when its dialog unmounts, so\ncollapsing fullscreen for recovery handed focus to the board a tick after the\nmodal mounted. Pull focus back whenever it lands outside the modal.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the unmount-cleanup test teeth and fix a flaky Escape assert\n\nRecoveryModal.test.tsx''s `leaves focus alone once the modal is gone` test\npassed even when the cleanup''s `removeEventListener(''focusin'', ...)` was\ndeleted, or the whole effect cleanup was zeroed out: after unmount the\nportal root is detached, so a leaked focusin listener calling `.focus()` on\na detached descendant is a jsdom/browser no-op and the activeElement assert\nstays green regardless. Pin the actual cleanup contract instead: spy on\nwindow.addEventListener/removeEventListener and assert the exact `focusin`\nlistener useModalIsolation registers on mount is the one removed on unmount,\nalongside the original behavioral assert.\n\nrecovery-flow.test.tsx''s first test asserted\n`dialog.contains(document.activeElement)` synchronously right after\n`findByRole(''dialog'')` resolves on DOM commit, before dispatching Escape.\n`useModalIsolation`''s mount effect (which moves focus in and attaches the\nEscape listener) is passive and can still be pending a tick later, so\nEscape could fire before the listener existed and the test would hang out\nits timeout — the same race Task 4 root-caused and fixed in this file''s\nother two Escape-closing tests by waiting for focus-in-dialog first. Apply\nthe same `await waitFor(...)` wrap here and correct the file-level comment,\nwhich had claimed only two tests reach the Escape-close race when this one\ndoes too.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the modal focusout guard against narrowing\n\nMount the underlying Radix dialog in its own pass, as production does, and\ncover the intra-modal focus move that the proposed guard narrowing would have\nbroken silently. The regression test dispatches the underlying focusout event\ndirectly rather than chaining native .focus() calls: jsdom (like real\nbrowsers) always completes an outer .focus() call on its own target once its\nhandler stack unwinds, so a settled document.activeElement assertion can''t\nobserve a reclaim a nested handler only ever wins transiently — verified\nempirically against the narrowed guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): close a cross-test focus-restore leak in the Radix stack suite\n\nRadix''s FocusScope defers its unmount focus-restore to a real setTimeout(0),\nand testing-library''s auto cleanup() never waits for it, so a prior test''s\ndeferred restore (and an unawaited NoVncCanvas state update from that same\ntest''s async recovery flow) can bleed into the next test — confirmed by\ninstrumentation. A formal trace of the reentrant focus cascade shows the\n''pulls focus back...'' assertion itself can''t actually be flipped by this\n(no yield point exists between its synchronous .focus() call and its\nexpect()), but the leak is real and pre-existing to any suite that\nmounts+unmounts a trapped FocusScope, so close it defensively: flush one\nreal tick after each test''s cleanup so Radix''s deferred work always\nfinishes before the next test starts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): drain Radix''s focus-restore timer from beforeEach, not afterEach\n\ne797d50''s message overstated what that change achieved. It claimed the added\nafterEach ran \"strictly after cleanup()\" and closed the cross-test focus-restore\nleak. It did neither. Vitest resolves sequence.hooks to \"stack\", so same-suite\nafterEach hooks run in REVERSE registration order, and testing-library''s\ncleanup() -- registered first, at setupFiles import time -- therefore runs last.\nThe file-level afterEach ran BEFORE cleanup(), i.e. before the unmount that\nschedules Radix''s setTimeout(0) focus restore, so it drained nothing.\n\nInstrumenting setTimeout and the hook boundaries on this file shows it directly:\nat afterEach entry the modal is still in the DOM and no FocusScope timer is\npending; the timer is scheduled only after the hook returns; the next test''s\nbeforeEach starts with one still queued. Moving the same one-tick flush to\nbeforeEach takes that count to zero before the test body runs. beforeEach is\ncorrect by construction -- it always follows the previous test''s afterEach\nhooks whatever the hook-order mode.\n\nCorrect the scope of the claim too. The restore is focus-neutral in this\nfixture: the previouslyFocusedElement it captured is either document.body,\nwhich jsdom refuses to focus, or an already-detached node, and every observed\nfiring left document.activeElement unchanged. The intermittent failure this was\nchasing did not reproduce at e797d50 in 30 consecutive single-file runs or 6\nwhole-package runs. So this closes a scheduling leak, not an observed failure,\nand the comment now says exactly that instead of claiming otherwise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): link the tier-shared-state follow-up\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): drop the phantom rebuild from the instance store''s connect hook\n\nThe connect hook carried `current ??= { value: make() }` behind a comment\nclaiming it gave a reconnect after disposal a fresh value. Measured against\n@reatom/core@1001.1.0 with the shipped code:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 2\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"],[{\"id\":2},\"a\"]]   built = 2\n\nThe reconnect still returned the DISPOSED value — a dependency-free `computed`\nnever recomputes, so its cached result is what every later read gets. The line\nonly built a second model graph nobody could reach and then handed it to\n`dispose` a second time. Same run with the line removed:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n\nThe comment now states what is true: a stale reconnect reads the cached,\nalready-disposed value, and a fresh value comes only from a new `store()`\nlookup — which is what every real caller does, because it calls `store()`\nduring render and the cleanup has already evicted the stale handle.\n\nWith the phantom build gone, `if (held)` is what prevents the double dispose,\nso a test pins it: replacing it with an unconditional `dispose?.(held?.value,\nkey)` now fails with a second teardown recorded as `[undefined, \"a\"]`.\n\nAlso corrects the module doc''s cost claim for reading atoms inside `make`.\nIt does not \"silently rebuild the whole value\": measured, after such a\ndependency changes the compute function re-runs once, `current ??=`\nshort-circuits it so `make` does not re-run (built stayed 1, the identical\nobject was returned, no subscriber notified), and because the re-run never\nreads the atom again the edge is dropped. The cost is a pointless dependency\nedge, not lost state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): align the tier-shared-state spec with what shipped\n\nThree claims in the design spec described work that was never delivered:\n\n- The instance atom was said to carry `withDisconnectHook`. It carries\n  `withConnectHook`, whose cleanup calls `teardownIfDisconnected()` — which\n  re-checks `isConnected` before disposing and evicts the map entry only while\n  it still points at the handle being torn down.\n- Section 4 \"StrictMode removal\" claimed `<StrictMode>` was dropped from\n  `packages/client/src/app/main.tsx` and replaced by a comment explaining its\n  absence. None of it shipped: `git diff cfff8d2..HEAD -- packages/client/` is\n  empty and the wrapper is still there. Measurement showed the store survives\n  the remount cycle, because the cleanup''s `isConnected` re-check returns\n  without touching a value a live mount still holds. Section 4 is rewritten as\n  \"StrictMode kept, tolerance pinned by test\", stating why the plan''s original\n  reasoning does not apply.\n- The Testing gate, the Documentation list and the Decision Summary repeated\n  the removal. They now say StrictMode stays and that the tolerance is pinned\n  by `describe(''makeWidgetInstanceStore under StrictMode'')`.\n\nThat last claim is measured, not assumed: deleting the `isConnected` re-check\nfrom the cleanup turns two of the three StrictMode tests red, along with the\nnon-StrictMode same-tick-resubscribe test.\n\nThe plan file is left untouched — it is the historical record of what was\nintended.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): narrow unmeasured claims and close two coverage gaps\n\nComment/claim accuracy — all of these stated more than was ever measured:\n\n- `ui/recovery-flow.test.tsx`: the \"roughly 1 in 15-40 runs\" hang frequency\n  was applied to every Escape-closing test in the file. It was measured for\n  exactly one of them, ''does not collapse when recovery opens from the tile''\n  (task-4-report.md, Fix round 3). The others carry the same wait by\n  inspection — identical shape, identical race — with no observed rate. The\n  comment now says so.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"at `beforeEach` entry one (later\n  two) FocusScope timer is pending\" — only the 1 -> 0 transition was ever\n  recorded, so the \"(later two)\" is dropped.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"jsdom (like real browsers) always\n  completes an outer `.focus()` call on its own target\" — true and measured\n  for jsdom, never measured against a real browser. Scoped to jsdom.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: ''keeps focus inside when it moves\n  between two of our own controls'' promised a focus invariant the test does\n  not assert — it asserts that our guard swallows the `focusout` before it\n  reaches `document`. Renamed to say that.\n\nCoverage:\n\n- `model/instance-store.test.ts` (new) covers `dispose: (models) =>\n  models.recoveryModel.teardown()`, the safety net against leaking a live VNC\n  session when the modal outlived the widget. It had no test at all: replacing\n  the callback with a no-op left the whole widget suite green. Verified both\n  new tests fail against that no-op.\n- `ui/PassportChecker.test.tsx`: two assertions used\n  `expect(await screen.findAllBy*(…)).toHaveLength(2)`, which cannot retry —\n  `findAllBy*` resolves on the first snapshot with >= 1 match, so it settles on\n  one mount and never waits for the second. Both are now a re-querying\n  `waitFor`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(backlog): record the two-modal focus ping-pong as a deferred defect\n\nBoth `useModalIsolation` instances register focus containment on `window` in\nthe capture phase, so two mounted modals each see the other''s `focusin` before\nthe root-level `stopPropagation()` can stop it, and each pulls focus into its\nown root — forever.\n\nReproduced in jsdom 29.1.1 with two `useModalIsolation` roots mounted at once:\none modal performs 1 focus pull on mount; two mounted together never settle,\nand a hard test guard at 400 pulls had to stop the run (402 recorded).\n\nDeferred rather than fixed because it is unreachable through the UI: the\npassport checker is the only owner of such a modal, renders at most one\n(non-fullscreen mount only), and its overlay is a full-viewport\n`pointer-events: auto` layer whose `pointerdown` handler closes the modal, so\na second one cannot be opened over the first.\n\nThe entry notes the likely fix — a module-level stack of active modal roots\nwith containment applied only by the topmost.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): correct five unverified claims left by code review\n\nBring wording in the tier-shared-state spec, BACKLOG.md, and a test\ncomment in line with what was actually measured/read: the instance\nstore''s dependency-edge cost (not a rebuilt value), a stale\nuse-modal-isolation.ts line citation (:53 -> :55, two places), the\nroot focusin stopper''s actual bubble phase (not capture), and which\nrecovery-flow tests keep the shared timeout default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay at this branch\n\nPins rpi.dev.toml''s source.branch to feat/passport-checker-widget so the dev\nenvironment runs this PR instead of the dev branch.\n\nREVERT BEFORE MERGING: once this PR lands on dev, source.branch must go back to\n\"dev\" or the dev overlay stays pinned to a branch that no longer moves.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): drop the stale Chromium singleton lock before launch\n\nThe Chromium profile is a named Docker volume, so `SingletonLock` keeps the\n`<hostname>-<pid>` of whichever container wrote it. After a redeploy the new\ncontainer has a new hostname, Chromium reads a lock owned by \"another computer\"\nit cannot probe, and `launchPersistentContext` fails on every task until the\nvolume is touched by hand.\n\nRemove `SingletonLock`, `SingletonCookie`, and `SingletonSocket` before each\nlaunch. `fs.rm` unlinks the symlinks themselves instead of following them, and\n`force` keeps a fresh profile from failing; a removal that does fail is returned\nas a value, logged, and the launch still proceeds. Unconditional removal is safe\nbecause `getPersistentContext` is the only caller of `launch` and the image\nentrypoint runs a single `node dist/index.cjs` - no check for a live Chromium is\nperformed or implied.\n\nAlso asserts that a failed launch keeps its cause, which is what makes the\nPlaywright message reachable from the service log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): log the error behind an internal task failure\n\nInternal failures were logged as `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` only, so the\ntagged error and its cause chain never reached the container log. A Chromium\nlaunch failure therefore showed up as a bare `internal` code with nothing to\ndiagnose. Add the error to the logged fields; `toEnvelopeError` still answers\nthe client with the redacted `internal` code, so only the log gains detail.\n\nNote the trade-off: a handler error whose message carries task data now reaches\nthe container log, which the previous field list deliberately avoided.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): redact handler failures in the internal task log\n\nfa87a47c logged too much. It added `error: outcome` to the internal-failure log\nso a Chromium launch failure would stop being invisible, but that field carries\nwhatever the handler threw. errore''s `toJSON()` serialises `message`, `stack`,\nand the whole `cause` chain, Playwright quotes `page.evaluate` arguments into\nits messages, and the passport widget passes the secret identity as one — so a\nhandler failure could print the passport series and number into the container\nlog. The wrapper''s own `stack` repeats the cause under `Caused by:`, so the\nidentity landed there twice.\n\nThat contradicts the design spec: raw Playwright, network, Zod, and upstream\nresponse details are logged only after redaction, request and response bodies\nare never logged, and logs redact task payloads and secret values\n(docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md\n:350-353, :684).\n\nThe rule now, verified against dispatch.ts:\n\n- `BrowserExecutorError` wraps a failed `executor.acquire(...)`. Acquire runs\n  before the handler and is passed only `(signal, widgetId)` — never the payload\n  or the widget secrets — so its cause chain is Chromium/Playwright launch\n  detail. Still logged in full; this is the stale singleton lock failure\n  fa87a47c existed to surface.\n- Every other internal failure wraps handler output and is reduced to\n  `errorChain`: the names/tags of the error and its `cause` chain and nothing\n  else. No `message`, no `stack`, no foreign properties.\n\nThe log message, the `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` fields, and the\n`code === ''internal''` condition are unchanged.\n\nTests split the single fa87a47c case in two: one pins that an executor failure''s\ncause chain still reaches the log, one asserts a secret-looking cause string is\nabsent from the serialised logged payload. Both were confirmed to fail before\nthe fix and to discriminate after it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add an env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe passport widget''s recovery path (retain the page, issue a single-use\ncapability, stream the live browser over noVNC, let a human solve the\nchallenge, retry) has never been exercised on real hardware, because it only\ntriggers when pasport.org.ua serves a Cloudflare challenge and none has been\nencountered — the smoke test on the Pi returned a normal result. Subproject 8\n(`passport-checker-rpi-integration` in\ndocs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md)\nhas \"embedded noVNC Cloudflare recovery, SSH fallback, and real checker smoke\ntest on the Raspberry Pi\" as an acceptance item and had no way to reach it on\ndemand. PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY is that way.\n\nContract: `1` and nothing else enables it — absent, empty, `0`, `true`, ` 1 `\nare all off (isForcedRecoveryEnabled does exact string equality, no trimming,\nno truthiness coercion). Compose passes it through with a `:-` default so an\nunset variable stays off, and it must never be set in production.\n\nSemantics: at most once per process. The latch lives in the closure\nmakePassportCheckHandler returns, so each handler owns its own and the unit\ntests stay isolated. It fires after a successful goto and after\ncollectNavigationEvidence — the retained page is the real checker page with the\nsite''s own UI loaded, so a human can drive it over noVNC — and returns exactly\nthe retainPageForRecovery() + BrowserSessionRequiredError pair the genuine\nCloudflare branch returns, making the downstream flow byte-identical to a real\nchallenge. The latch is consumed only when the forced path actually fires, so a\nfailed navigation returns its own error untouched and leaves the lever armed:\nan infrastructure hiccup must not burn the single forced run an operator is\nwaiting on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): apply the design-system widget redesign\n\nBring the tile and the recovery modal in line with Паспорт-чекер.dc.html.\n\nBoth tiers now lay out as header -> flexible middle -> action, so the\naction sits on the tile''s bottom edge in every state instead of trailing\nwhatever content happens to be above it. Status banners pick up the\ndesign''s per-state hues, the filled success disc, and the CircleAlert /\nTriangleAlert split that separates a retryable failure from a session\nprompt. The modal gains the access-pill dot, a pulsing LIVE dot, framed\nconnecting/disconnected/expired states, and the shell-style SSH block.\n\nDark theme was effectively missing: the --pc-* hues chained to\n--success-soft / --destructive-soft, which the host token set never\ndefines, and to --success, which is a dark surface green rather than an\nicon green under [data-theme=''dark'']. Every hue is now declared per\ntheme the way ofelia-poop-duty does it. The modal portals to\ndocument.body, outside .widget, so it declares the one hue it needs on\nits own overlay.\n\nThree deliberate departures from the mockup:\n- invalidConfig copy stays server-side (\"Обратитесь к администратору\"),\n  since this widget has no per-instance settings — its config lives in\n  server secrets.\n- the expired frame keeps \"Переподключиться\"; without it an expired\n  capability can only be recovered by closing and reopening the modal.\n- the sessionRequired tiny tile only fills amber, no border: the tile\n  border belongs to the host card, and drawing one from inside the\n  widget would render a second, inset contour.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xwGNctwqXcYi5p4QSSFhA\n\n* docs(browser-automation): design widget user-input detection\n\nMove the \"a human must act\" mechanism out of passport-checker and into the\nautomation platform. The handler gets context.detectUserInput(detector), which\nruns the detector, optionally prepares the page, retains it and returns the\ncanonical error as one indivisible step; retainPageForRecovery leaves the public\ncontext so the halves cannot be separated. Cloudflare becomes a reusable helper\n(probe and predicate together, not just the predicate) that any widget can pass\nin. Also records the removal of the PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY lever.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): correct the user-input spec''s test-seam claims\n\nmakeFakeExecutor fakes BrowserExecutor<FakeContext> with FakeContext = { id,\nsignal }; it never builds a BrowserTaskContext, so it cannot host a recording\ndetectUserInput and needs no change at all. Name the seams that do exist: the\nsix retainPageForRecovery call sites in chromium-executor.test.ts and the three\nBrowserTaskContext literals in diagnostics.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): plan the widget user-input detection refactor\n\nSeven tasks, each green on its own: retire the forced-recovery lever, add the\ndetector and error contracts, move Cloudflare detection into the platform with\nits probe reunited with its predicate, run escalation from the task context,\nrewire the widget, then withdraw retainPageForRecovery from the public context so\nthe retain/escalate pair cannot be taken apart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): make the Cloudflare task a move, not a copy\n\nTask 3 previously left the classifier duplicated between the widget and the\nplatform until Task 5, which any reviewer would rightly flag as verbatim\nduplication of a logic block. The widget now switches to the moved module in the\nsame task, so the logic never exists in two places.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* revert(passport-checker): drop the env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe lever forced one trip through the Cloudflare recovery path per service\nstart so Subproject 8 could exercise noVNC on the Pi on demand. It is being\nretired deliberately ahead of moving user-input detection into the platform;\nthe Pi smoke test needs another answer.\n\nThis reverts the behaviour added in 45c604f1.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(browser-automation): add user-input detector and error contracts\n\nUserInputRequiredError carries the browser_session_required code and sshTarget\nmeta the recovery flow keys off, moved out of the passport-checker widget where\na platform contract had no business living. UserInputProbeError distinguishes\na broken check from a negative one.\n\n* feat(browser-automation): move Cloudflare detection into the platform\n\nThe classifier lived in the passport-checker widget while the DOM probe feeding\nit lived in that widget''s check handler, so the only reusable half was the half\nwithout the probe. Both now sit in browser-automation behind two detector\nfactories: one that probes the live page, one that classifies evidence already\nderived inside the page from a fetch response.\n\nThe widget imports the moved module rather than keeping a copy; it still calls\nthe classifier by hand, which the next commit changes.\n\n* @\ndocs: document the feature branch -> dev -> main workflow\n\nFeature work happens in a dedicated branch inside a ./.worktrees checkout,\nlands in dev through a PR, gets verified on the dev stack, and only then\nreaches main through a dev -> main PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* feat(browser-automation): run user-input detection from the task context\n\ndetectUserInput runs a widget-supplied detector and, on a match, prepares the\npage, retains it and returns UserInputRequiredError as one step, so the two\nhalves cannot drift apart. A detector that fails yields UserInputProbeError and\ndeliberately leaves the page unretained: an undecidable check is not a challenge,\nand pinning a browser page for a recovery nobody will perform is worse.\n\nAUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET moves into the service config, where an unusable value\ndegrades to null rather than failing config loading and killing the service.\n\n* docs(passport-checker): design last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* test(browser-automation): cover the prepare-before-retain order in detectUserInput directly\n\nThe executor-level test asserted hasRetainedPage() from inside the prepare\nmock, but that bookkeeping is only populated at release() time, so it passed\nregardless of call order. Add a direct unit test against makeDetectUserInput\nthat records prepare/retain into one ordered array — genuinely fails if the\norder in detect.ts is inverted (verified by temporarily inverting it).\n\nNo production behavior changes; detect.ts ends identical to before this commit.\n\n* @\ndocs: merge AGENTS.md into CLAUDE.md and symlink it\n\nAGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md had drifted into two partly overlapping rule sets.\nEverything unique to AGENTS.md (project structure, codegen commands, Windows\ntest-runner notes, coding style, UI gotchas, testing, security, failure modes)\nnow lives in CLAUDE.md, and AGENTS.md becomes a symlink so every agent reads\nthe same file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* docs(passport-checker): plan last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* feat(passport-checker): date-aware check timestamp label\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout design\n\nTwo width breakpoints instead of one hardcoded grid: one column below 768,\ntwelve above, with row height and margin scaled by the same factor as the\ncolumn width so the desktop grid zooms instead of stretching on one axis.\n\nThe mobile layout is derived from the desktop one and only persisted once the\nuser drags or resizes, stored as an additive optional `mobileLayout` field so\nno migration of `root:boards` is needed.\n\nAlso records the react-draggable touchstart defect: the whole card is the drag\nhandle today, which cancels page scrolling on touch — harmless on a short\n12-column board, blocking at one column.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): detect required user input through the platform\n\nThe handler no longer collects Cloudflare evidence, classifies it, retains the\npage and builds the escalation error by hand at every site; it passes a detector\nto context.detectUserInput and returns what comes back. browser/challenge.ts and\nthe widget-local BrowserSessionRequiredError are gone — the classifier now lives\nin browser-automation next to the probe that feeds it, and the\nbrowser_session_required contract belongs to the platform that owns the recovery\nflow.\n\n* fix(passport-checker): restore the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nAn automated amend from a concurrent session accidentally stripped this\nline out of 3a12494e. It belongs to the user-input-detection work; this\nrestores it verbatim.\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nRETRYABLE_MESSAGES.user_input_probe vanished twice with no test turning red,\nbecause nothing asserted the actual behaviour: with the entry gone,\nmapCheckError silently falls back to GENERIC_RETRYABLE_MESSAGE and the user\nsees the generic string instead of the specific one. Assert the real output\nof mapCheckError against a hardcoded expected string, not against the table\nitself, so a future deletion or typo of the entry fails this test instead of\ncomparing the map against itself.\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron design spec\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout implementation plan\n\n* feat(board): resolve grid metrics from the container width\n\n* feat(passport-checker): persist the last successful check\n\n* feat(board): derive and reconcile the one-column mobile layout\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron implementation plan\n\nAlso corrects the spec: the jsdom Temporal shims must stay, only the\n--harmony-temporal execArgv becomes redundant on Node 26.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin which detector goes to which detectUserInput call\n\nNothing previously checked that the navigation check gets the page detector\nand the submission check gets the evidence detector -- only the second call''s\nprepare option was asserted. A handler that swapped the two factories would\nleave every test in this file green; only the gated real-browser suite would\nnotice, and it isn''t run in CI. Pull the captured detector off each mock call\nand invoke it directly: the page detector must reach for page.evaluate, the\nevidence detector must ignore its page argument and classify the real\nevidence. Also drops the now-inert hasChallengeForm evidence field from the\nsubmission-escalation test, whose result was always driven by the mock''s\nescalation queue regardless of that field.\n\n* feat(board): add mobile layout materialize, update and reset actions\n\n* refactor(browser-automation): expose only detectUserInput on the task context\n\nretainPageForRecovery was half of a pair a handler had to assemble correctly by\nhand: retaining without escalating strands a browser page, escalating without\nretaining hands the UI a recovery session with nothing behind it. With the retain\nflag reachable only through detectUserInput, neither mistake is expressible.\n\n* test(passport-checker): cover restore-on-mount and cross-placement sync\n\nAdd two widget-level cases pinning storage-backed persistence through\nReact: a fresh mount renders a previously stored result without\ninvoking a check, and a check made in one placement is observed by a\nsecond placement sharing the same storage. Rename the pre-existing\n\"gives separate instance ids separate state\" case to \"gives separate\nstorages separate state\" (it isolates by storage, not instanceId) and\nupdate its comment to point at the new cross-placement case below it.\nPin PASSPORT_LAST_RESULT_KEY''s literal value in check-model.test.ts so\na silent rename cannot slip through green while orphaning deployed\nusers'' stored data under the old key.\n\n* feat(board): switch the grid to resolved metrics and a mobile drag grip\n\n* docs(browser-automation): document how a widget requests manual input\n\n* feat(board): offer a mobile layout reset in the board schema popover\n\n* fix(widgets): address passport-checker persistence review findings\n\n- TinyTier''s success chip now shows checkedAtLabel, so a restored (possibly\n  stale) result reads honestly in the compact tile too, not just standard.\n- Harden the two-placement isolation test: assert placement two stays idle\n  instead of a length-1 findAllByText that can pass mid-race.\n- Clarify why lastResult/transient set order is safe in checkPassport\n  (Reatom notifies after the sync block, not \"order for readability\").\n- Add a test exercising lastResultSchema.safeParse over the exact shape\n  checkPassport writes, since createFakeStorage skips schema validation.\n- Document lastResultSchema as a persistence contract: new fields must be\n  optional/defaulted or every stored value fails validation on next deploy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* fix(passport-checker): remove dead AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET threading\n\nrecoverySshTarget was never read by check.ts''s handler after the\ndetectUserInput refactor moved sshTarget sourcing entirely into the\nplatform config. Delete the widget''s leftover env read and option\nthreading so the README''s \"widgets neither read nor pass it\" claim is\nactually true, and drop the now-meaningless SSH-sanitization test in\nfavor of the equivalent (and broader) coverage in browser-automation''s\nconfig.test.ts.\n\n* docs(ofelia): design the authored ledger and comments\n\nRecords get the authenticated account instead of the per-device \"Я:\"\ntoggle, the write path moves into the widget''s own server.ts, and the\nhistory row is rebuilt: human dates, plain-language phrases, explicit\ndebt effect, visible supersession, no IP.\n\nPulls the Node 26 prerequisite out of the widget-server-cron spec, since\nthe shared domain module it enables is needed here first.\n\nAlso imports the Claude Design workspace under design/ (documents only,\nscreenshots and uploads stripped) and keeps the brief that produced the\nOfelia mock.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs(ofelia): note the cron plan overlap and landing order\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): cover the mobile layout and touch-scroll behaviour end to end\n\n* fix(board): keep the measured grid container mounted\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a 1920 viewport\n\n* docs(ofelia): add the authored ledger implementation plan\n\n15 tasks: Node 26, the pure domain split, the members directory, widget\nidentity, the server-resolved viewer, record schemas, draft construction,\nthe widget server definition, the model rewire, the history and comment\nview models, the account avatar, and the two rebuilt panels.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): wait for the grid relayout to settle before measuring\n\n* fix(browser-automation): bundle workspace imports instead of externalizing them\n\nThe rspack externals predicate was an allow-list whose default was\n\"externalize\". When the user-input refactor introduced the first runtime\nbare-specifier import of a workspace package into the service module graph\n(browser-automation/user-input/cloudflare, from the passport-checker browser\ntask), it was silently externalized: the build stayed green, all tests stayed\ngreen, and the Cloudflare module was simply absent from dist/index.cjs. The\nruntime stage copies only package.json and dist/, so Node resolved the\nspecifier through the exports map to a .ts path that does not exist in the\nimage and the container died on `exec node dist/index.cjs`.\n\nInvert the predicate into a deny-list derived from the package''s own\ndependencies (minus errore, which is ESM-only and must be bundled) plus Node\nbuiltins. An unrecognised bare specifier is now bundled, so the same mistake\nsurfaces as a build-time resolution error rather than a startup crash, and\nadding a real runtime dependency externalizes it with no config edit.\n\nAdd scripts/check-bundle.ts, chained onto `build` so it runs inside the image\nbuild the Dockerfile already performs. It asserts that every require() in the\nbundle is a builtin or a declared dependency, that the scan still matches the\nemitted shape at all, and that the compiled evidenceFromResponseText the widget\nsplices into Chromium still evaluates and runs in a bare vm realm. That last\ncheck is what the BROWSER_IT suite could never prove: it runs under vitest,\nwhere the specifier resolves to .ts through esbuild and the swc path is never\nexercised. Verified: the compiled form is ES5-lowered but self-contained --\ndestructuring became inline property reads, no swc helpers, no hoisted regexes.\n\nAlso:\n- detect.ts: tag the prepare outcome instead of comparing against null, so a\n  prepare that rejects with exactly null is a failure rather than a silent\n  success, matching the detector branch''s `typeof detected !== ''boolean''`.\n- user-input/errors.test.ts: cover toEnvelopeError for UserInputRequiredError\n  with and without an ssh target, pinning with toStrictEqual that `meta` is\n  omitted rather than set to undefined. contracts.test.ts already claimed this\n  coverage lived here.\n- chromium-executor.test.ts: drop an assertion inside a prepare callback that\n  could not fail the test and was vacuous, and rename the case to what it\n  actually verifies; the ordering contract is owned by detect.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* test(board): settle past the grid transition and stop assuming card order\n\n* test(board): correct the stale comment above the breakpoint wait\n\n* chore(node): align every runtime and image on Node 26\n\nnode:26-alpine and node:26-bookworm-slim no longer bundle corepack\n(removed from Node core), so `npm install -g corepack@latest` is added\nahead of every `corepack enable` in the three Dockerfiles and\ndocker-compose.dev.yml. scripts/infra.test.ts''s assertion on the\nbrowser-automation Dockerfile''s base image is updated to match.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(node): unshadow jsdom localStorage and bump the e2e image to Node 26\n\nNode 26 ships its own unflagged global localStorage/sessionStorage (Web\nStorage API), which resolves to undefined without --localstorage-file\nand shadows jsdom''s window.localStorage for bare-identifier reads in\npackages/client''s jsdom test environment (40 failing tests). Disable\nNode''s own globals via execArgv: [''--no-experimental-webstorage''] in\npackages/client/vite.config.ts so jsdom''s storage wins; verified via\nObject.getOwnPropertyDescriptor that the flag actually removes the\nglobal rather than just suppressing the warning. widget-runtime and\nwidget-sdk don''t touch localStorage in tests today, so the flag is not\nadded to their execArgv arrays (narrowest correct remedy).\n\nAlso bumps packages/client/e2e.Dockerfile (used by pnpm test:e2e:docker\nvia docker-compose.e2e.yml) from node:22-bookworm-slim to\nnode:26-bookworm-slim, missed by the original brief''s Files list, with\nthe same corepack@latest install this Node 26 base image now needs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): make the relayout wait actually poll\n\n* refactor(ofelia): extract the pure duty domain out of the Reatom model\n\n* fix(lint): make the widget domain import barrier a real allowlist\n\nThe regex denylist let ''../model/ofelia-duty'' through — domain/ and model/\nare siblings, so a single-''../'' import was both the most likely accidental\nescape and completely unguarded. It also only enumerated known packages, so\nany new npm dependency passed silently.\n\nReplace it with a gitignore-style group allowlist (''**'' minus zod, @shared/*\nand ''./'' siblings), which oxlint evaluates natively. A negative-lookahead\nregex was tried first and rejected: oxlint''s Rust regex engine has no\nlookaround and silently drops such a pattern, disabling the whole barrier.\n\nDomain tests get a second, later override that additionally allows vitest;\neverything else stays blocked there too.\n\n* chore(lint): keep the design mock exports out of lint and format\n\ndesign/ holds vendored Claude Design exports (support.js, *.dc.html)\nthat break the repo''s only gate: oxlint reports 9 errors in\ndesign/support.js, and oxfmt aborts (exit 2) on non-standard markup\nin design/Design System.dc.html before it can check anything else.\n\nAdd design/** to the existing ignorePatterns glob array in both\n.oxlintrc.json and .oxfmtrc.json, the same mechanism already used for\ndocs/**. This is a plain glob matcher (not the Rust regex engine\nbehind no-restricted-imports), so it isn''t subject to that engine''s\nlookaround gap, and it leaves every other rule/override untouched.\n\nFixing the oxfmt abort also uncovered pre-existing, unrelated\nformatting drift in CLAUDE.md, package.json and\nscripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts that the crash had been masking from\nthe format:check summary. Reformatted them (whitespace/ordering only,\nno logic changes) so pnpm format:check is actually green end to end.\n\n* feat(auth): expose the board members directory\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): expose the board identity to widgets\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): stop identity fetch from leaking an unhandled rejection and a discarded error\n\nHandle the AbortError that wrap()''s promise rejects with when the last\nwidget reading identity unmounts mid-fetch, and stop discarding\nBoardMembersError whole: a 401 stays silent (expected on an\nunauthenticated host), everything else is surfaced via console.warn so\nschema drift or a server fault is not silently swallowed.\n\n* feat(widgets): resolve the signed-in viewer for widget server events\n\n* feat(ofelia): carry the authoring account on ledger and comment records\n\n* feat(ofelia): move draft construction and event schemas into the domain\n\n* feat(ofelia): write the ledger and comments from the widget server\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): harden server date parsing and unforgeability coverage\n\n- Wrap Temporal.PlainDate.from in errore.try so a calendar-invalid but\n  regex-valid date (e.g. 2026-02-30) returns an Error instead of\n  throwing through a function typed Error | T.\n- Add regression coverage: forged createdBy/actor in the payload is\n  ignored, the debt handler is exercised, storage-read failures are\n  asserted by identity with no write, and the invalid-date case is\n  asserted as a value.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): split the unforgeability guard into schema and handler halves\n\nThe round-1 forged-payload test went through run(), which parses the\npayload via zod''s default strip mode before the handler ever sees it —\nso it only proved the schema strips forged fields, twice over, and\nwould not catch a handler that starts spreading its raw payload into\nthe draft. Replace it with two schema-level tests (clean + comment\npayloads drop a forged createdBy/actor) and one handler-level test\nthat calls ofeliaServer.handlers.clean directly, bypassing the schema,\nto prove the handler itself never reads those fields even if they\nreach it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* feat(ofelia): drive ledger and comment writes through widget server events\n\nThe client no longer appends to the ledger or the comment key. All four\nday actions collapse into one `invokeDay` that sends `{ date }` to the\nmatching widget-server event, and `send` sends `{ weekStart, text }` —\nwho the actor is, whose debt moves, whether the event applies at all and\nwhich account signed it are now derived and stamped server-side, so a\nclient cannot forge authorship.\n\n`selectedDate()` and `today()` (and `viewWeekStart()`) are read before\nthe first await so they resolve against the widget''s own Reatom context.\n\nThe per-device \"Я: Леша/Карина\" toggle is deleted rather than hidden:\nauthorship comes from the session, so there is nothing left to pick.\n`currentUser` is gone from the duty model, the widget context, the\nfixture and the view-model''s `onSetUser`.\n\nBoth model factories now take `api` and `identity`; `identity` is the\ndirectory the history and comment views read in the follow-up tasks.\nThe view projections drop `ip`/`by`, whose schema fields Tasks 5-8\nremoved, and `authorName` falls back through `createdBy.name`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* style: format the ofelia server test\n\n* feat(ofelia): group history by duty day and mark superseded records\n\n* feat(ofelia): resolve comment authors through the members directory\n\n* fix(ofelia): flush the comment thread''s connect hook via vi.waitFor, not a bare microtask\n\nReview round 1: the timing fix for the comment-thread subscribe/emit race\nassumed a single-microtask flush of Reatom''s connect-hook queue. Match the\nsibling comments-subscription tests in the same file instead — wait for the\nstorage mock''s subscribe call and wrap the whole body in context.start, so\nthe assertion is condition-based rather than scheduling-internals-based.\n\n* feat(ofelia): add the account avatar and its palette\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the history list around duty-day groups\n\n* fix(ofelia): announce the history phrase subject to assistive tech\n\nThe duty circle is the sentence subject in the rebuilt history phrase, and\nAvatar is aria-hidden, so the column announced as bare verbs with no subject.\nPair every circle with a visually hidden name, attribute the debt pill to its\nperson, and separate the chips with real spaces as the mock does.\n\nAlso correct the text-decoration comment to name the real mechanism (atomic\ninline, not descendant cancellation) and cover the three untested branches.\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the comment thread around account authors\n\n* fix(ofelia): restore composer coverage and mute the unknown comment author\n\n* fix(ofelia): break ledger day ties by append order\n\nRouting the writes through the widget server changed where `ts` comes from.\n`/api/storage/:key/append` stamped `Date.now()`, so consecutive appends always\ngot a strictly increasing timestamp. The widget dispatch stamps the app''s\ninjectable `now()`, and the e2e harness pins that clock — so a clean-then-undo\npair on the same day produced two entries with an identical `ts`, and\n`latestOutcomesByDate`''s strict `>` kept the earlier `cleaned` entry as the\nday''s outcome. The `reset` was written but never counted, and the day never\nreopened; `pnpm test:e2e:docker` caught it as a failing \"undo — returns the day\nto pending\".\n\nThe ledger is append-only and iterated in insertion order, so `>=` is the\ncorrect tie-break: on an equal timestamp the later-appended entry is the newer\ndecision. This also hardens production, where two writes can still land in the\nsame millisecond.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: describe server-authored widget records\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): fail widget dispatch when the session account is unreadable\n\nresolveWidgetViewer folded every getAccount failure into `null`, so a Valkey\nfault, a corrupt account record and a deleted account all produced a record\nstamped `createdBy: null` plus a `{ ok: true }` response. The ledger is\nappend-only, so that authorship could never be recovered.\n\nThe three outcomes are now distinct: no session still returns `null`, which is\nwhat the standalone harnesses and the e2e stack (no nginx, so no session cookie)\nrely on to keep writing unattributed records; a failed lookup returns a\nWidgetViewerLookupError, which the dispatch route turns into a fault instead of\na false success.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): order superseded history records by append order\n\n`superseded` sorted on `right.ts - left.ts`, which returns 0 on a tie; the\nstable sort then left tied records in append order, oldest first, contradicting\nthe \"newest first\" contract on HistoryDayGroup. Ties became a supported case\nwhen latestOutcomesByDate started breaking them with `>=`, and the dockerized\ne2e stack freezes the clock, so there every record ties.\n\nThe ledger is append-only, so append order is chronological order: reversing the\nday''s bucket is both correct and cheaper than sorting.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): warn when listAccounts skips an unreadable record\n\nA corrupt or schema-mismatched account record was dropped silently, so the\nroster simply came back short and the symptom read as \"an account vanished\"\nrather than as the data corruption it is. Name the key in a console.warn.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* build: pin corepack to 0.35.0 across images and dev compose\n\n`npm install -g corepack@latest` ran in five image build stages and four dev\ncompose services. There is no CI and the Pi deploy is manual with a 30-minute\nbuild timeout, so a breaking corepack release would only ever surface as a\nfailed deploy. Pin one explicit version everywhere.\n\nVerified: `docker build -f packages/server/Dockerfile` succeeds and the built\nimage reports corepack 0.35.0 with pnpm 10.28.2.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* refactor(ofelia): delete the dead short-date formatter\n\nformatDateShort and MONTHS_SHORT lost their last production consumer when the\ncomment thread moved to its own formatter; only the test still called them. It\nalso formatted through `new Date(ts)` in the local timezone, while every current\nformatter goes through plainDateIn(DUTY_TIME_ZONE, ...).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): drop the tier guards that cannot fail, hoist the env directive\n\nStandardTier and CompactTier each asserted they do not render UserToggle. The\ntoggle only ever lived in RichLayout and the component is now deleted outright,\nso neither assertion could ever fail — they only looked like coverage.\n\nAlso move the `@vitest-environment node` directive in server.test.ts back to\nline 1, where a reader expects it and where model/history-view.test.ts keeps it.\nVitest 4 matches the directive anywhere in the file content, so the file was\nalready running under node; this is readability, not a behaviour fix.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: state the authorship guarantee the code actually provides\n\nCLAUDE.md claimed a server-stamped author \"cannot be forged\". Nothing enforces\nthat: POST /api/storage/:key/append does no session check in Node, and nginx''s\nauth_request only proves a session exists, not whose it is, so any signed-in\nhousehold member can still hand-write a record carrying an arbitrary author.\n\nState what is true instead — the UI can no longer sign a record with someone\nelse''s name, and the generic storage route remains a shared trusted channel\ninside the board. Closing that route is a separate task.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(board): never render the desktop grid below its 1920 baseline\n\nThe desktop zoom is now one-directional: raise the lower clamp on the\ngrid scale from 0.75 to 1, so every container width between the mobile\nbreakpoint and BASE_WIDTH resolves to exactly the historical metrics\n(rowHeight: 30, margin: [10, 10]) and only wider displays scale up.\n\nUntil this branch, useContainerWidth never attached its observer and\npinned the reported width at its 1280 default on every screen. With the\ncontainer measured for real, a 0.75 floor would have shrunk every board\non a 1280/1366/1440 laptop below the size it has always had, dropping an\nh:6 card from 230px to 172.5px and pushing widgets under their own tier\nthresholds. Nothing shrinks now; the 4K aspect drift the scale was added\nfor is still fixed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling record\n\nBring the design doc in line with what shipped: the lower clamp is 1,\nthe 1280 table row shows the baseline metrics, and the paragraph\njustifying the clamp says plainly that it is active across the whole\n768-1920 band rather than a narrow 768-1100 one.\n\nAlso correct the claim that existing 1080p boards \"do not shift by a\npixel\". The formula at 1920 does reproduce the old constants, but the\nmeasured width was previously pinned at 1280 on every screen by the\nuseContainerWidth bug this branch fixes, so wider screens legitimately\nre-flow to use the whole window.\n\nDrop the 1920 viewport pin from ofelia-duty.spec.ts: it only worked\naround the 0.75 clamp, and removing it restores default-viewport\ncoverage. Recompute the footprint arithmetic in the ofelia client test\ncomment, which assumed a container of exactly 1280 rather than the\nviewport minus the board padding and the scrollbar gutter.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a viewport its tier needs\n\nEvery assertion in the duty spec is StandardTier UI, and the widget only\nresolves `standard` at a frame width of 400px or more. Playwright''s\ndefault 1280 viewport is about 7px short: the board measures its own\ncontainer, which is 1225px there once the stable scrollbar gutter and the\nboard''s 20px padding on each side come off, so a w:4 card is ~395px and\nits frame ~393px. `compact` is the correct rendering at that width, so\nthe spec has to declare the width its assertions need.\n\nThe dependency was previously invisible because the board reported a\nhardcoded 1280px container on every screen, which made the card 413px and\nleft the threshold only 13px of headroom against a measurement that was\nwrong by 55px.\n\nCorrect the footprint comment in the widget''s client test to match: the\ntier is measured on the frame inside the card''s 1px border, so a 1280px\nviewport yields ~393x228 and resolves to `compact`, not `standard`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling claims to match what ships\n\nThree statements asserted the opposite of what this branch established,\nall written before the width axis was measured.\n\nThe grid-metrics JSDoc claimed the clamp keeps 1280/1366/1440 boards\n\"the size they have always been\" and widgets \"above their own tier\nthresholds\". Both are false at 1280: the container is 1225 there, so a\nw:4 card is 395px where the old code drew 413px, and ofelia falls under\nits 400px standard threshold into compact. The clamp pins the metrics -\nrowHeight 30 and margin [10, 10] at every desktop width - not the\nrendered geometry, which still follows the measured container.\n\nThe design doc claimed \"no board shrinks and no widget loses a tier\".\nThe 1366 and 1440 frames (422px, 446px) clear the widget threshold; the\n1280 frame (393px) does not. Corrected to draw the metrics/geometry\ndistinction the document already draws two paragraphs earlier.\n\nThe \"do not shift by a pixel\" claim survived in the doc''s Testing\nsection and in the test''s own comment. At 1080p the container is now\n1865, so colWidth is 144.58 rather than the 95.83 the pinned-1280 bug\nproduced. Both now state what the test guarantees: the formula at\nBASE_WIDTH reproduces the previous hardcoded constants.\n\nAlso record the shipped consequence in Risks - ofelia renders\nCompactTier below ~1300px, correct behaviour on a genuinely 393px frame,\nwith recalibration left as a follow-up - and fix three figures: the tier\nparagraph attributed the frame inset to a card padding that does not\nexist (it is a 1px border; the heights are 148px and 298px, not 110 and\n260), the e2e headroom was 11px on the frame rather than 13px on the\ncard, the 1920 card/frame are 608/606, and useContainerWidth is at\nBoard.tsx:28.\n\nComments and prose only; no executable line changed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* build(deploy): add a per-branch rpi environment and deploy scripts\n\nrpi.branch.toml is a disposable stack for trying the checked-out branch on\nreal hardware, with ttl = \"72h\" so a forgotten one reaps itself. It takes the\nbranch through ${BRANCH_NAME}, the only variable rpi 0.26 accepts — hence the\nliteral hostname rather than a derived one.\n\nscripts/deploy-branch.ts reads the branch from git so nobody retypes it, and\nrefuses to deploy without .env.branch, which would otherwise produce a stack\nwhose WebAuthn gate fails with nothing in the deploy log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(deploy): send the branch secrets bundle before the build\n\nEvery branch is a fresh rpi project with an empty secrets store, so the very\nfirst deploy of any branch died after the ~3 minute image build with an opaque\nCompose \"secret file ... does not exist\". deploy:branch now checks\n`rpi secrets ls` and sends the bundle itself when there is none.\n\nAlso strip the `--` that `pnpm run x -- --flag` forwards verbatim: rpi''s parser\nreads a bare `--` as end-of-flags, so the flag after it was rejected as a\npositional.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(browser-automation): read the spliced callback when swc stops inlining it\n\ncheck-bundle extracted the page callback by slicing between `.concat(` and\n`.toString()`, which is the whole function expression only while swc inlines it.\nA second consumer of evidenceFromResponseText — precisely what the public\nbrowser-automation/user-input/cloudflare subpath exists to allow — makes swc emit\na named declaration instead, leaving a bare identifier at the splice site. The\nguard then evaluated that identifier in an empty realm, got a ReferenceError, and\nfailed the image build accusing a perfectly correct bundle of shipping a\nnon-self-contained callback.\n\nResolve the identifier to its declaration, and let the JavaScript parser find\nwhere that declaration ends: counting braces by hand would have to understand\nregex literals, and this body is full of regexes containing quote characters.\nExtraction failures now say so instead of reporting a self-containment defect.\n\nVerified by building all four ways: inlined and named shapes both pass; the\noriginal externals regression and a regex hoisted out of the callback are both\nstill caught.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* fix(ofelia): centre the initial in avatars re-cast to inline-flex\n\nBoth avatars centre their initial with `place-items: center`, but the\nhistory phrase re-casts the box to `inline-flex` so the circle stays in\nthe inline run, and a flex container ignores `justify-items`. The glyph\nwas left-aligned: in an 18px circle it sat 0px from the inline start and\n12.97px from the end, roughly a third of the diameter off centre.\n\nName the inline axis outright so the centring survives whichever display\na caller imposes. `.debt > [data-tone]` re-casts the same way and was hit\nby the same bug.\n\n* fix(ofelia): reserve room for the viewer ring in scrolling columns\n\nThe signed-in viewer''s avatar carries a 3px ring drawn as a spread\nshadow, so it paints outside the border box. Every avatar sits flush\nagainst the inline start of a container that clips: `.historyCol` and\n`.list` scroll (and `overflow-y: auto` clips the inline axis too), and\n`.commentsCol` is `overflow: hidden`. The ring lost its whole left edge —\nmeasured at exactly 3.0px — leaving a flat-sided avatar in the history,\nthe comment list and the composer, in both themes and on mobile.\n\nWiden each clip box outward by the ring width with a padding/negative\nmargin pair, which moves no content, and hold the width in\n`--ofelia-viewer-ring` so the shadow and the reserved room cannot drift.\n\n* fix(ofelia): size the account initial like the duty initial\n\n`MemberAvatar` scaled its initial to half the box while `Avatar` uses\n0.4, and the history phrase renders both at `px={18}` on the same line —\n9px next to 7.2px, which reads as two mismatched type sizes rather than\none set.\n\nShare the ratio through `AVATAR_INITIAL_RATIO`. Taking `Avatar`''s value\nkeeps every pre-existing avatar (the 62px hero, the 26px week strip)\npixel-identical and moves only the account squares this branch added.\n\n* fix(ofelia): raise the standard tier to the height it actually needs\n\nStandardTier lays out to 298px of content, but `tiers.standard` asked for\nonly 200px and the widget card clips instead of scrolling. At the default\nplacement (413x230px) the tier therefore resolved and then cut its own\naction row in half: \"Уборка подтверждена\" was sliced through and \"В долг\"\n/ \"Простить\" never appeared at all.\n\nState the height the tier needs, so anything shorter degrades to\n`compact` rather than rendering truncated, and raise `defaultSize` to\n`{ w: 4, h: 8 }` (~413x310px) so a freshly-placed widget still clears it\n— the same fix shape as the earlier default-placement regression.\n\n* fix(ofelia): let the pinned day header cover the reserved ring strip\n\nThe history column reserves `--ofelia-viewer-ring` of inline-start room\nso the viewer ring is not clipped, and records paint into that strip. The\nsticky day header''s own box stops at the content edge, so the strip stayed\nuncovered: scrolling avatars slid past the pinned header through a 3px\ngap, measured against the column''s border box.\n\nStretch the header back over the strip with a negative inline-start margin\nand return the width as padding, so its background spans the full\nscrollport while nothing inside it moves.\n\n* feat(ofelia): scroll the history to the day picked in the week strip\n\nSelecting a day highlighted it in the strip and changed the panel, but\nleft the history where it was, so the records for that day had to be found\nby hand.\n\n`HistoryList` now keeps a node per duty day and scrolls the matching group\nto the top of its scrollport when the selection changes. Exactly one\nancestor scrolls in each layout — the column on desktop, the dialog body\non mobile — so the native `scrollIntoView` cannot drag two scrollports at\nonce. The first run only records the mounted day: scrolling on mount would\npull the panel above the history off screen in the mobile layout before\nthe reader touched anything.\n\nThe column subscribes to a newly exposed `view.selectedIso` rather than\n`view.selected`, so confirming or forgiving the selected day still does\nnot re-render the history.\n\n* fix(ofelia): scroll only a box the reader can scroll back\n\n`scrollIntoView` moves *every* scroll container in the ancestor chain, and\n`overflow: hidden` boxes are scroll containers too — they just have no\nscrollbar. The widget frame is one: measured at 149px of overflow in the\nmobile layout, `overflow-y: hidden`. Switching a day therefore displaced\nthe whole widget, and with no scrollbar there the reader could not put it\nback.\n\nWalk up to the innermost box that is genuinely user-scrollable\n(`auto`/`scroll` *and* overflowing) and scroll that one by hand, aligning\nthe group with the scrollport top where the sticky header pins. When\nnothing in the chain scrolls there is nothing to reveal, so the layout is\nleft alone rather than nudged.\n\n* fix(ofelia): keep the mobile tab pane from clipping the viewer ring\n\nBounding the tab pane needs `overflow: hidden`, which clips the inline axis\ntoo — `overflow-x` cannot stay `visible` once the other axis is not. So in\nthe mobile layout `.split` becomes a second clipper, outside the columns\nand at the same inline start, and it shaved off the 3px viewer ring that\nthe columns had already reserved room for. Measured at `cutL: 3` on the\ncomment and record avatars; on desktop `.split` is `overflow: visible`, so\nthis only ever showed on narrow screens.\n\nReach outward by the ring width there too, the same padding/negative-margin\npair the columns use.\n\n* docs(cron): rebase the cron spec and plan onto dev\n\ndev landed Node 26, the widget domain/ boundary and Ofelia''s server.ts with\naccount authorship, so those tasks are gone. Authorship for cron records is now\na system arm on the persisted createdBy union rather than a `by: ''system''`\nfield, and the plan drops from 12 tasks to 8.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay back at dev\n\nThe overlay was temporarily aimed at this feature branch (5208c5c5) to test it on\nthe Pi before the `branch` environment existed. rpi.branch.toml now covers that\ncase per branch, so dev goes back to deploying dev — which is also what\nscripts/infra.test.ts asserts, and that test passes again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(widgets): add the cron job contract to widget server definitions\n\n* feat(server): validate widget cron schedules when the registry is built\n\n* feat(server): persist a per-job cron cursor in Valkey\n\n* feat(server): add the widget cron tick scheduler\n\n* fix(server): drain the cron scheduler on stop and pin the no-double-fire invariant\n\ninFlight was replaced on every interval fire instead of chained, so a tick\nthat outlived intervalMs was silently orphaned and stop() could return\nwhile it was still running. Chain onto the existing promise instead.\n\nAlso add a dueOccurrence test pinning that an occurrence already recorded\nin the cursor never re-fires.\n\n* style(server): rewrap the cron registry validation to the formatter''s width\n\n* fix(client): stop an edge cache from serving a stale host federation entry\n\n/remoteEntry.js is the one release file whose URL never changes while its\ncontents do: it names the hashed chunk that calls createInstance. Cached by URL,\nan intermediary keeps serving the previous release''s copy, whose chunk 404s in\nthe new release. The host then never initialises and every loadRemote fails with\n\"#RUNTIME-009 Please call createInstance first\" — a board that renders its shell\nand not one widget.\n\nObserved today on the branch stack: Cloudflare answered /remoteEntry.js with\ncf-cache-status HIT, age 6503, under its default max-age=14400, from a build\nnearly three hours older than the deployed one, while /index.html was DYNAMIC\nand the hashed assets were current. It survived a browser site-data clear, a\ncontainer recreate, a builder-cache prune and a cold rebuild, because none of\nthose touch the edge.\n\nWidget entries already carried no-cache for this exact reason; the host''s own\nentry never did. Give it the same header and pin both halves in the nginx smoke\ntest: the header, and that the chunk the entry names is one this release ships.\n\nThis is not specific to the throwaway branch host — production serves the same\nfile under the same TTL, so any release that changes the board bundle could\nshow an empty board for up to four hours.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(server): start the cron scheduler with the app and expose a test tick\n\n* feat(ofelia): give server-written records a system author\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the harness and tests the required widget identity\n\nThe ofelia authored-ledger work made `identity` a required field of\nWidgetRuntimeProps; the passport-checker widget was written before that\nfield existed. Both landed on dev without a textual conflict, so the\nsemantic conflict only surfaced as four TS2741 errors — all in a dev\nharness and three test call sites, none of them part of the production\nbuild, which is why `pnpm build` kept passing.\n\nFollow the convention the widgets updated in that PR already use: the\nharness takes the live `runtime.identity`, the tests take the\n`makeStaticWidgetIdentity()` double. passport-checker never reads the\nroster, so the empty default keeps every assertion unchanged, and the\nstatic double issues no request — which matters in recovery-flow.test,\nwhere fetch is stubbed with a never-resolving promise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* feat(ofelia): auto-approve unresolved duty days on a nightly cron\n\n* test(e2e): cover the auto-approve cron reaching an open board\n\n* test(server): cover the cron interval path and a retried partial run\n\nClose the three test-coverage gaps from the final whole-branch review:\nexercise makeCronScheduler''s start()/stop() interval path (the only\nproduction entry point, previously untested) with fake timers, cover a\nretry after a partially failed ledger append end to end in the ofelia\ncron suite, and assert append was never called in the widget''s cron\nread-error test to match its manual-path siblings.\n\n* fix(server): fence the generic storage routes off the auth keyspace\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`GET /api/storage?prefix=` passed an unvalidated prefix straight to `scanKeys`, and\n`session:<id>` embeds the session cookie''s own value in the key name, so any\nsigned-in device could enumerate live session tokens and then act as anyone —\nvoiding the server-stamped authorship this release is built on. PUT/DELETE were\nequally unbounded, which also left the new `cron:` cursor client-writable.\n\nRestrict all five generic storage routes to `root:`/`w:i:`/`w:t:`, rejecting a\npartial prefix (including the empty one) so enumeration cannot walk in sideways.\n\nAlongside: re-stamp `ip: ''''` on append as a one-release shim for pre-release\nreaders whose schema requires the field, log every `status >= 500` with its cause\nchain instead of only 500, and cover the dispatch route''s session→viewer wiring,\nwhich had no test at all — replacing it with a literal null left the suite green.\n\n* fix(ofelia): let the nightly auto-close actually discharge a debt\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`getDebtDays` scans forward from its origin, so every day it yields is >= today,\nwhile the cron only ever asks about days 1..7 in the past — `debtDayFor` was\nstructurally unreachable on that path. The auto-close therefore credited the\nrotation duty person instead of the debtor, omitted `onBehalfOf`, and `foldDebt`\nnever decremented: the debt walked forward to the next open day, forever, one\nmis-attributed record per night.\n\nAnchor the scan at `min(target, today)` behind an explicit `allowPastDebtDays`\nthat only `autoApproveDrafts` sets, so the interactive handlers stay anchored at\n`today` and keep matching what the week strip showed the user.\n\nAlongside: keep writing `by`/`author` for one release (pre-release readers require\nthem and their `z.array` fails wholesale on one missing field), drop unparseable\nelements instead of rejecting the whole array, skip the run when the ledger key is\nabsent so unused stacks stop accruing fabricated history, and re-read each day''s\nresolution before appending so a manual close landing mid-run is not overwritten.\n\n* fix(ofelia): surface failures the widget used to swallow\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\nEvery duty action captured its error into an atom nothing read, so a failed tap\nlooked identical to a successful one — and with the nightly cron now closing\nunresolved days, a silently failed «В долг» becomes a system-signed `cleaned`\nrecord at 00:05. A failed comment send additionally discarded the typed text and\nraised an unhandled rejection.\n\nTrack the last failure in write order rather than merging four per-action atoms in\na fixed order (the first non-null shadowed the rest forever), render it in Russian\nrather than leaking `WidgetApiError`''s internal message, and keep the composer''s\ntext on failure.\n\nAlongside: show a retry instead of an endless skeleton when the ledger read fails,\nstop attributing the previous week''s comments to the selected one, keep\nscroll-to-day inside the history column instead of scrolling the whole card, and\nstop the account avatar announcing a name the adjacent text already carries.\n\n* fix(board): migrate stale widget heights once, not on every read\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`tiers.standard.minHeightPx` rose 200→300 and `defaultSize.h` 6→8, but nothing\nmigrated the `h` already persisted, so every placed ofelia card would render\nCompactTier after deploy and lose its week strip and debt chips.\n\nRun the bump once, recorded in a `root:`-scoped marker key a pre-release client\ncannot strip on write-back — clamping on every read instead would have made every\nwidget unshrinkable below its package default, since `minH` sits well under it.\n\nAlongside: preserve unknown keys on the shared board snapshot, so a client that\ndoes not know a field can no longer delete it when it writes the board back.\n\n* fix(client): stop the edge from serving a stale service worker\n\nThis release already gave `/remoteEntry.js` a `no-cache` header after Cloudflare\nwas caught serving a three-hour-old copy of it. `/sw.js` has the identical\nproperty — a stable URL whose contents change every release — and fell through to\nthe catch-all block with no Cache-Control at all, so an installed PWA kept getting\nthe previous release''s worker from the edge for up to four hours and never\ninstalled the new one. That is also what would have made the ofelia record-shape\nincompatibility outlast the deploy by hours rather than a reload.\n\nGive the worker its own location alongside the federation entries, and pin it in\nthe nginx smoke spec the same way the host entry is pinned.\n\nAlongside: raise `proxy_read_timeout` for widget dispatch above the automation\nbudget — the generic `/api/` block inherited nginx''s 60s default, which would have\ncut every long passport check before its own deadline could fire — and re-point\nthe two gate probes at allowlisted keys so they keep isolating the auth and CSRF\ngates instead of tripping the new storage allowlist.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): let the members directory recover from a failed first fetch\n\nThe roster was fetched once from a connect hook that fires only on the 0→1\nsubscriber edge. On the board the identity atoms stay connected for the life of\nthe page, so a transient failure at first paint — routine on the Pi, where the\nservice worker serves the shell while the server container is still starting —\npinned the roster at null for the whole session: no viewer avatar, no viewer ring,\nuntil someone reloaded. An account created later never appeared either.\n\nRetry with backoff on a transport failure, leave a 401 alone, and refresh so a\nlater-created account arrives without a reload. Every await carries its own\nrejection handler: the abort that `wrap` raises on teardown was otherwise an\nunhandled rejection on each disconnect.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add a delete control, and stop leaking recovery sessions\n\nThe board already hands every widget a `requestDelete` callback and clock and\nofelia both use it; passport-checker was the only widget with no way to remove it\nfrom the board. Wire it through the shared `WidgetControls`, in both tiers and in\nthe dev harness.\n\nAlongside, three fixes to the recovery and check paths:\n\n`teardown()` disposed the session but never invalidated an in-flight `start()`, so\nclosing the modal during the ~1s issue request still built the noVNC RFB against a\ndetached node. The recovery slot is a single global, so that orphan answered every\nlater attempt with 409 until its TTL expired.\n\nThe 25s client deadline sat far below the pipeline''s own budget (30s queue + 60s\nexecution, 100s at the board server), so on the Pi a normal check timed out in the\nUI while the invoke resolved later with a real result that nobody read — the\nstatus could never be persisted while the upstream site was slow. Raise it past\nthe server budget, keep a late success instead of dropping it, and ignore a\nstraggler from a superseded attempt so it cannot overwrite a newer result.\n\nThe SSH fallback hardcoded noVNC''s port 6080 after compose made it per-stack, so\nan operator recovering the dev stack tunnelled into production''s live Chromium —\npossibly mid check, with a real passport number on screen. It now uses the port\nthe stack actually published.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* fix(browser-automation): report the stack''s own noVNC host port\n\nThe widget renders an `ssh -L` command so an operator can take over a stuck\nbrowser session, and it reads the port from the rejection''s meta. Nothing ever\nput a port there: `UserInputRequiredError.publicMeta` carried only `sshTarget`,\nso the widget always fell back to 6080 — production''s published port — and an\noperator on dev or branch was told to tunnel into the wrong stack.\n\nThread `NOVNC_HOST_PORT` from the same compose variable the publish line uses\ninto the service config and out through `publicMeta`, so both ends of that\ncontract now agree and each is pinned by a test.\n\nAlso set `AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET` in the dev and branch env templates: it was\nunset there, so the fallback the operator is meant to reach did not render at\nall on exactly the stacks that collide.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(backlog): record the findings this branch deliberately did not fix\n\nThe PR #27 review surfaced nine issues that were judged not worth acting on now:\nsome are contested (one review lens refuted them), some are the release''s own\ndocumented known gaps, and some are pre-existing shapes this branch only made\nvisible. They were about to be lost with the review transcript.\n\nEach entry states what exists today, why it is tolerable, the shape a fix would\ntake, and what would trigger doing the work — matching the two entries already\nhere. The facts in every entry were re-verified against the code rather than\ncopied from the review.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(rpi): move the env configs onto rpi 0.27 groups and ${git.branch}\n\nDeliver secrets through named groups instead of one bundle per environment:\n`prod` for production, `dev` for the passport-checker files the dev stack and\nthe branch stand share, `branch` for the stand''s own .env.branch. Both overlays\nclear [secrets].files, so nothing carries a second copy, and [secrets].env in\neach file is now documented as the local push source it has always been.\n\nResolve the branch stand''s source.branch through ${git.branch} and drop the\nBRANCH_NAME variable everywhere. Since 0.27 the environment key only gets a\nper-branch suffix when the configuration references ${env.slug}, so the stand is\none shared key by design; say so where the comments claimed the opposite.\n\nDelete scripts/deploy-branch.ts. It existed to push .env.branch before a deploy\ncould come up unconfigured, which a missing key bundle does silently. A declared\ngroup that is missing or empty instead fails the deploy at the layer-load step,\nnaming the group and the push that fixes it, so `rpi deploy --env branch` needs\nno wrapper.\n\nLock the layout with infra tests and refresh the docs that still said\n`rpi secrets send` and `--vars BRANCH_NAME=...`.\n\nVerified: rpi config show for base/dev/branch, oxfmt --check, oxlint and\ntsc --noEmit. The new infra assertions were run through an equivalent script —\nroot Vitest cannot start in this checkout, where rolldown fails to resolve any\nconfig entry, including untouched ones.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(rpi): drop the prod secret group and the dead PASSPORT_* variables\n\nProduction would be the only project attaching a `prod` group, and a keyless\npush always writes this file''s env + files into the deploy key''s own bundle,\nwhich is the last layer — so the group could only ever be an identical shadowed\ncopy, and a rotation pushed to it alone would silently never reach production.\nKeep the group mechanism where it earns its place: `dev`, shared by the dev\nstack and the branch stand.\n\nPASSPORT_SERIES/PASSPORT_NUMBER are gone from .env (untracked, so only its\ncomment header records this): nothing in docker-compose.yml reads them, the\nwidget takes those values from the file-backed secrets, and they were riding\ninto every stack''s containers through both groups.\n\nAgent state after this change: groups `dev` (0 keys, 2 files) and `branch`\n(7 keys, 0 files); production''s bundle 0 keys + 2 files; dev''s 7 keys + 0 files,\nwith the passport files finally resolving `<- dev` instead of a stale `<- key`\ncopy that was overriding the group.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: spec the unified widget card controls\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: plan the unified widget card controls\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: require codegen in the plan''s worktree setup\n\n* feat(widget-sdk): make WidgetControls the one widget chrome component\n\n* fix(widget-host): stop hiding card controls where there is no hover\n\n* test(client): assert the touch controls on the element that carries the style\n\n* docs: add the e2e typecheck the workspace gate does not cover\n\n* refactor(clock): take the card chrome from useWidgetChrome\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): take the delete control from useWidgetChrome\n\n* refactor(ofelia): drop OfeliaActionControls for the shared WidgetControls\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): mount the tiny tier chrome once\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): narrow the chrome policy and guard the tiny-tier switch\n\nWhole-branch code review fix wave:\n\n- useWidgetChrome now allows expand+delete only on mode === ''small'' and\n  falls through to close-only otherwise, instead of denying only on\n  ''large''. Equivalent today (WidgetMode is ''small'' | ''large''), but a\n  future third mode now defaults to no card-management chrome rather\n  than silently gaining full expand+delete.\n- TinyTier''s switch gains a `default: view satisfies never` branch.\n  ReactNode already includes undefined, so the prior bare `let body:\n  ReactNode` never tripped TS''s used-before-assignment check on a\n  missing case; the plan''s claim that it did was wrong and is corrected.\n- widget-interactions.spec.ts asserts the desktop half of the\n  hover-visibility rule (opacity 0 -> 1 on the overlay controls\n  container), which mobile-board.spec.ts cannot reach since it forces\n  hasTouch: true.\n- Corrects the spec/plan claim that a standalone dev/ harness shows\n  widget controls: every harness hardcodes mode: ''large'', so\n  useWidgetChrome() returns only onClose there and no controls render.\n\n* feat(widget-host): let a loading card name itself and be deleted\n\nA card whose widget is still loading showed an anonymous shimmer and no\ncontrols, so a component chunk that never resolved left a tile that could\nnot be removed. It now draws the widget''s icon and title — both known\nsynchronously from the codegen catalog before the remote lands — with the\nshared WidgetControls alongside them.\n\nThe controls are placement=\"inline\", not the board card''s usual overlay:\nan overlay is hover-revealed, and hover-hiding the only useful control on\na stuck card is the trap being fixed. Inline already means always-visible,\nso no new visibility rule was needed.\n\nAlso fixes a guard that never fired: the frame''s own onDelete is a\nuseEvent handle and so always truthy, which made the error card''s\n`{onDelete && ...}` unconditional. Both it and the error boundary now read\nthe raw prop.\n\n* fix(e2e): give the auto-approve cron test a ledger to close\n\nThe job returns early on an empty ledger by design — a stack where nobody\never used the widget must not grow a fabricated `cleaned` record a night\n(server.ts, F11). That guard landed in b1fffc0e, six hours after this test\nwas written in ecdd1cd0, and the test was never updated: it resets the\ndatabase, places the widget, touches nothing, and then expects the cron to\nclose a day. The ledger was empty, so the job no-opped and the plaque never\narrived.\n\nSeed one settled earlier day, the way the neighbouring forgive test already\ndoes. 2026-06-15 is a genuine Карина duty day and the entry closes it, so\nthe run skips it and auto-approves 2026-06-16 — the day the card shows.\n\nVerified: pnpm test:e2e:docker now reports 27/27, against 26/27 both on\nthis branch and on origin/dev before the change.\n\n* chore(lint): stop formatting markdown with oxfmt\n\nHand-written tables and wrapped prose in CLAUDE.md and the agent guides read\nbetter than oxfmt''s column padding, which also left the table separator row\nwider than the rows around it. docs/** and design/** were already ignored, so\nthis only extends the same treatment to the root markdown and the skill files.\n\nThe oxlint entry is symmetry: oxlint does not lint markdown today, but the two\nignore lists are maintained together.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* docs(agents): split CLAUDE.md into on-demand guides\n\nCLAUDE.md is read in full at the start of every session, and it had grown to\n~3.4k words. Move the parts that are only needed some of the time next to the\ncode they describe, and leave an index behind:\n\n- docs/agent/{deployment,test-troubleshooting,widget-server,radix-stacked-dialogs}.md,\n  each fronted by a thin .claude/skills wrapper so Claude Code loads it on its\n  own trigger, while Codex and the rest follow the table in CLAUDE.md\n- docs/architecture/overview.md for the descriptive architecture prose\n- the storage-key contract, including the incident that proves it, now lives in\n  the header comment of packages/shared/storage/scope.ts, so it reaches whoever\n  is actually editing the derivation\n\nAlso drop the generic rpi CLI mechanics already covered by the rpi:rpi-cli and\nrpi:rpi-toml skills, and fix what had drifted: pi.toml -> rpi.toml, the\nscopeWithColon normalization lives in host-runtime.ts and not widget-api.ts,\nbrowser-automation is no longer \"the future browser service\", the command list\nmatches package.json, and the hardcoded per-machine skill paths are gone.\n\nCLAUDE.md: 3368 -> 1382 words.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* fix(client): replace the deprecated apple PWA capability meta\n\nChrome flags <meta name=\"apple-mobile-web-app-capable\"> as deprecated and\nasks for the standard mobile-web-app-capable instead. Keeping both would\nkeep the warning, and standalone mode no longer depends on the apple tag:\nthe VitePWA manifest already declares display: standalone, which Safari\nhonours since iOS 16.4.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* fix(passport-checker): load noVNC lazily so the widget can mount\n\n@novnc/novnc@1.7.0 ends core/util/browser.js in a module-scope top-level\nawait: it builds a real VideoDecoder, configures it for 1920x1080\navc1.42401f, decodes a frame and awaits decoder.flush(). That probe\nresolves in milliseconds on desktop Chrome and never settles at all on\nsome devices, and a top-level await that never settles parks every\nimporter in \"evaluating\" forever.\n\nui/PassportChecker.tsx imported model/rfb.ts statically for the prod RFB\nadapter, so the chain was mount-time: import(''./ui/PassportChecker'') never\nresolved, React.lazy never got a payload, and the board rendered this\nwidget''s loading card forever. Nothing was thrown, so no error boundary\nfired and the card was indistinguishable from a slow load.\n\nReach the adapter through model/load-rfb.ts''s dynamic import instead. The\nrecovery model now takes loadRfb and awaits it alongside transport.issue:\nthe store supersedes an unused capability on the next issue() and only\ncounts a live socket as busy, so running them in parallel costs nothing\nand keeps the modal''s time to first frame unchanged. A viewer that fails\nto load lands in the new viewerUnavailable state with a reconnect button\nrather than a silent spinner.\n\nVerified on the built output: avc1.42401f/EncodedVideoChunk now appear\nonly in rfb-*.js, and the widget chunk''s single reference to it is a\ndynamic import(). ui/passport-checker-novnc-graph.test.ts guards the\nchain — it goes red if the static import comes back.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(agent): record the machine and deploy failures that lived only in memory\n\nThe CLAUDE.md split moved detail into docs/agent/* but left ten machine- and\ndeploy-specific failure modes recorded nowhere in the repository. Each of them\nreads as a regression you just caused, and each has cost multiple sessions.\n\ntest-troubleshooting.md gains the worktree symlink failure, the rotten\nnode_modules in the main checkout, pnpm check''s kill-others-on-fail behaviour,\nthe docker e2e run that exits 0 without starting, and the stale vite preview\nthat serves another checkout''s build. It also corrects the Vitest filter: the\ndocumented `--filter client test -- <file>` does not scope, `exec vitest run`\ndoes.\n\ndeployment.md gains the three caches that sit between a build and what runs -\nthe service worker and IndexedDB that survive a redeploy on the shared branch\nhostname, the edge cache on URL-stable files like /remoteEntry.js and /sw.js,\nand the aborted deploy that strands a container on a dead network.\n\n* docs: stop CLAUDE.md repeating itself, and give it a worktree command that works\n\nTesting and Failure modes said nothing the file had not already said. The gate,\nthe PR rules, the commit style and the test locations were each stated twice;\nof the five failure modes, four restated the feature workflow or the global\nrules and the fifth is now in docs/agent/test-troubleshooting.md.\n\nThe worktree command in step 1 has never worked on this machine: AGENTS.md is a\ncommitted symlink Windows cannot materialize, so the documented command fails\noutright. It now carries both required git flags.\n\n* docs(backlog): record three follow-ups the shipped releases left behind\n\nOfelia''s tier thresholds now sit within 10px of their own frames, so any chrome\nadded to the card silently drops it to compact and takes five e2e tests with it.\nThe z.array record schemas mean one new field hangs every still-open client, and\nboth shipped releases papered over it with a compat shim. And an ofelia comment\nfrom an account outside DUTY_ROTATION loses its author on pre-release clients.\n\nAll three were only ever recorded in agent memory, which is not where a\nfollow-up survives.\n\n* docs(context): commit the session review snapshots\n\nThey have been untracked for three sessions running, so each review has had\nnothing to diff against.\n\n* docs: log the PWA update path in the backlog\n\nregisterType: ''autoUpdate'' does reload the page on its own, but the same\nbranch of vite-plugin-pwa''s register.js never calls onNeedRefresh and\nmakes updateServiceWorker() a no-op — so needRefreshAtom, applyUpdate and\nUpdateBanner are dead code, and an already-open client only re-checks once\nan hour. Records what exists, why it is tolerable, and what would trigger\nthe work.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: drop the typescript 7 migration benchmark report\n\nThe migration is done and the numbers have served their purpose. The\ngenerator, scripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts, stays — the report and its\nbenchmarks.json are reproducible from it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(context): add the session-4 startup capture\n\nFollows 3d327e45, which committed the earlier snapshots.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: record the passport-checker loading-skeleton handoff\n\nRoot cause, the fix on feat/widget-controls, what is verified and what is\nnot, plus the device console probes and the list of dead ends not worth\nredoing. Delete it once that branch is released.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: spec the fullscreen close control and back-gesture dismissal\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* docs: plan the fullscreen close control and back-gesture dismissal\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the tiny tier a way out of a restored success\n\nA card narrower than the widget''s 321px `standard` threshold renders\nTinyTier, and its `success` state drew a status chip and no action at all —\nthe only state in that tier without one. That was survivable while the\nwidget always started in `idle`. Once it began restoring the last\nsuccessful check on mount, a narrow card came up in `success` and stayed\nthere: passport-checker deliberately offers no expand affordance, so the\nonly button left on the card was \"Удалить\". Seen on the branch stand at\n314px, where 4 of 12 columns miss the threshold by 7px.\n\nThe chip is now the action rather than gaining one below it, so the state\ncosts no extra height and still fits the smallest tile. It keeps the\nstatus and timestamp it already carried, and its accessible name repeats\nthat text after the action rather than replacing it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* chore(docs): keep only agent and architecture guides in git\n\ndocs/context/ session logs and docs/superpowers/ plans, specs and\nartifacts churn every session and had nothing durable to diff against;\nuntrack them and ignore docs/* except docs/agent/ and docs/architecture/,\nthe canonical checked-in references.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(docs): ignore docs/* except agent and architecture\n\nFollow-up to the previous commit — the .gitignore rule itself was left\nunstaged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* doc: add .playwright-mcp to .gitignore\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): mirror open overlays onto browser history\n\n* chore(docs): drop this branch''s plan and spec from git\n\ndev now keeps only the agent and architecture guides in the repository and\nignores the rest of docs/ (0a17cdaf, 96b2c041). This branch predates that\nrule and still carried its own plan and design spec, which the merge left\ntracked because dev never had those two paths to delete. Untracked here;\nboth files stay on disk, now covered by the ignore rule.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* feat(widget-sdk): add the overlay back-dismiss hook\n\n* test(passport-checker): widen the noVNC graph test''s budget\n\nThe test''s only await is a cold import of the widget''s whole mount graph in\na fresh module registry, which overran the default 5s budget under\n`pnpm check` — five workspace gates running concurrently. It failed there\ntwice and passed every isolated run. Nothing in it waits on behaviour, so\n20s cannot mask a regression: a static noVNC import still fails the\nassertion, not the timer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* test(widget-sdk): add StrictMode and fallback tests for overlay back-dismiss hook\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): coalesce a dropped overlay''s traversal with the push behind it\n\nA history traversal is asynchronous. Measured in real Chromium,\nhistory.go(-1) lands 25-40ms later and a pushState issued in the same task\ndoes not cancel it — jsdom does, which is why the unit tests never caught\nthis. So dropOverlay''s eager go() fired after the following push, rolled the\nbrowser back past the new entry and delivered a spontaneous popstate at the\npre-overlay depth, which reconciled as \"close the overlay that opened 30ms\nago\". Both live call paths hit it: StrictMode''s mount -> cleanup -> mount,\nand any commit that closes one overlay and opens another.\n\ndropOverlay now records the depth history must land on and schedules the\ntraversal on the next task. A pushOverlay arriving first reuses one of the\nowed entries — depth taken from the debt rather than stack.length + 1, so it\ncannot collide with an overlay still waiting above a mid-stack drop — and\nthe flush is cancelled once the debt is spent. A drop with nothing behind it\ntraverses exactly as before.\n\nThe debt is a target depth, not a step count: two drops in one task each\nname the entry they want and the lower wins, where summing their steps\ntraversed past the pre-overlay entry and out of the application. Confirmed\nin Chromium against the module at HEAD, which left the probe page on\nabout:blank after one back press.\n\nThe new tests drive real navigation instead of dispatching a hand-built\nPopStateEvent, since a faked popstate cannot tell one registered entry from\ntwo that happen to carry the same depth.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* chore(widget-sdk): clear the pre-existing lint and type errors in the overlay tests\n\nBoth predate this branch''s overlay work and were failing `pnpm lint` and\n`pnpm typecheck` at HEAD: an unused `e2` binding in overlay-history.test.ts,\nand a `let capturedDismiss` that control-flow analysis narrows to `never`\nbecause it never sees the assignment made inside the component.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): derive a pushed overlay''s depth from history, not the stack\n\n`stack.length + 1` is only correct while depth counts entries one for one. It\nstops doing so in the duplicate-depth mode a mid-stack drop can leave behind,\nand then the number it hands out skips one: from [C(1), E(2), B(2)] the next\npush is stamped 4 on an entry history places directly after the one stamped 2.\nflushOwedTraversal computes `readDepth() - target`, which counts entries, so a\nlater drop of E issues go(-3) where two entries separate it from its target and\ntakes C down with it.\n\n`pushState` inserts its entry immediately after the current one, so the depth is\n`readDepth() + 1` by construction. Identical whenever history and the stack\nagree, which is every ordinary case.\n\nAlso drops the `replaceState` from the coalescing branch — that branch runs only\nwhen the claimed depth equals the current one, so the spread rewrote identical\nstate. Confirmed in Chromium: the four-scenario probe is byte-identical with and\nwithout it. The claim path now writes nothing to history at all, which is what\nthe comments (here and on the hook) now say.\n\nThe two residuals move from the report into the module doc, where the tasks that\nwill wire real overlays to it can see them: two overlays mounted in the same\ncommit as a middle drop, and dropOverlay racing an eager dismissOverlay in one\ntask.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* feat(client): add the app-env registry, palette derivation and OKLCH conversion\n\n* feat(client): close the topmost dialog on the platform back gesture\n\n* feat(client): generate and commit per-environment app icons\n\n* test(client): pin the Escape close to history.back, not just the outcome\n\nwaitFor(() => expandedInstanceId() === null) alone cannot distinguish\n\"routed through requestDismiss/history\" from \"closed onOpenChange(false)\ndirectly\" -- both reach the same null. Spy on history.back and assert it\nwas called synchronously right after the Escape keydown, so a future\nchange that bypasses requestDismiss fails this test instead of staying\ngreen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* feat(client): brand the document title, tokens and icons per environment at build time\n\n* feat(client): show the environment badge in the board header\n\n* build: deliver the rpi environment name into the client image build\n\n* fix(client): serve the environment icons publicly so the login page is branded\n\n* feat(client): close the topmost popover on the platform back gesture\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* test(client): pin the popover Escape close to history.back, not just the outcome\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(client): pin the environment-branding wiring with tests and share the accent constants\n\nGuard the two things nothing in the gate actually exercised: the Header rendering\nEnvBadge, and both Vite configs registering appEnvBranding. Both were verified to\nfail when the guarded code is deleted.\n\nAlso: export ACCENT_L/ACCENT_C from registry.ts so the fixed accent lightness and\nchroma stop being copied across tokens.ts, generate-env-icons.ts and\nicons-lock.test.ts; derive generate-env-icons.ts''s PRODUCTION_FILL from them\ninstead of a fourth hardcoded #645fd1; give the env badge pill flex: none so it\ncan''t be squeezed by BoardSelect; and tighten a handful of comments/test anchors\n(dev-only activation branding limitation, a VITE_APP_ENV override caveat, a hook\ntier name, a looser Dockerfile layer-order assertion, three deployment.md claims).\n\n* feat(clock): give the fullscreen clock a close control\n\n* feat(passport-checker): give the fullscreen widget a close control\n\n* feat(passport-checker): dismiss the recovery modal on the back gesture\n\n* test(passport-checker): assert the async close mechanism, not just its effect\n\nThe three tests asserting a synchronous close on Escape/backdrop pointerdown\nwent stale the moment the recovery modal''s close paths started routing\nthrough history.back(), which is async in jsdom (and ~25-40ms later in real\nChromium). Update them to pin the mechanism (spy on history.back and assert\nit fired) rather than only awaiting the eventual state flip, and to wait for\nour own modal to actually close before checking the underlying Radix dialog\nsurvived, so the survivor assertion cannot pass on a race that hasn''t landed\nyet.\n\n* test: reset overlay-history between tests in the shared widget and client setups\n\nRecoveryModal.tsx''s useOverlayBackDismiss(true, close) now runs on every\nmount, so any test that mounts a Dialog/Popover root or RecoveryModal\ntouches overlay-history.ts''s file-scoped singletons (the entry stack, the\npopstate listener, a pending owed-traversal timer). recovery-flow.test.tsx\ngot its own beforeEach for this; RecoveryModal.test.tsx and\nrecovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx did not, and neither did any client test\nthat mounts a Dialog/Popover (AddWidgetMenu.test.tsx,\nBoardSchemaSelect.test.tsx, etc.) — a gap that let one test''s still-open\noverlay leak state into the next.\n\nReset once per package instead of scattering beforeEach hooks: widget-sdk''s\nshared `./test-setup` (used by every widget package via\ndefineWidgetVitestConfig) and the client package''s own vitest.setup.ts.\nBoth guard the reset to jsdom, since @vitest-environment node files in\nthese packages have no window/history at all.\n\nAdded a proof test per setup file with no reset of its own, so the shared\nhook''s effect is directly observable rather than inferred from a green\nsuite: one test leaks an overlay entry on purpose, the next asserts the\nsingleton started clean.\n\n* test(e2e): cover the fullscreen close control and the back gesture\n\n* fix(client): degrade the env badge to a dot, then hide it, on narrow viewports\n\n.brand has no overflow clipping, so once the header runs out of room it\nslides its content under .actions instead of clipping it. Give the badge\nthe same breakpoint ladder BoardSchemaSelect already uses: at <=640px\n(where the select goes full-width) drop the label text and shrink to a\ndot-only circle; at <=430px (where the select collapses to an icon) hide\nit entirely. The label now lives in its own span so CSS can hide it\nindependently of the dot, while the pill''s own aria-label keeps the\nenvironment announced in the dot-only state.\n\n* fix(client): collapse the theme toggle to one cycling button on narrow viewports\n\nThe three-item ToggleGroup is 102px; below 768px (MOBILE_BREAKPOINT, right\nat the measured collision onset) that alone accounts for a big share of\nthe header overflow. Render both controls and let CSS pick one via a\nmedia query, same approach the rest of the header''s responsiveness uses.\nThe cycle order (light -> dark -> system -> light) moves into the model\nas cycleThemeMode next to themeMode, since the order is domain knowledge,\nnot view glue; the view''s OPTIONS list is now derived from it instead of\nrepeating it. The view-transition DOM interop (--vt-x/--vt-y, startViewTransition)\nis factored into one applyWithViewTransition helper shared by both controls.\n\nThe cycling button''s accessible name (\"Сменить тему\") is deliberately\ngeneric and distinct from the ToggleGroupItem radio names, since jsdom\nrenders both controls at once (media queries don''t evaluate there) and a\ncolliding name would break the existing getByRole(''radio'', ...) tests.\n\nAdds an e2e spec covering the group/button swap at two viewports, the\nonly layer where the media query is real.\n\n* fix(client): let the board select fill the remaining header row, not the whole row\n\n.trigger (or .skeleton, while loading) is BoardSchemaSelect''s own top-level\nelement -- it has no wrapper -- so it''s a direct flex item of .brand\nalongside the logo and EnvBadge. Its <=640px width: 100% therefore resolved\nagainst .brand''s own content box, claiming the entire row for itself as if\nthose siblings weren''t there and pushing their content under .actions.\nflex: 1 1 auto fills only the space actually left after the siblings,\nwhich is what this rule always meant to do.\n\n(.root, the class that width: 100% previously also applied to at this\nbreakpoint, is never applied to any element in BoardSchemaSelect.tsx --\ndead CSS, not the flex item in question. Left untouched, out of scope.)\n\nVerified with a 900->320px real-Chromium sweep: the header now has zero\noverlap at every width down to 320px, closing the residual 545-435px band\nleft after the EnvBadge/ThemeToggle responsive work.\n\n* fix(client): let the board select shrink instead of overflowing the header row\n\nAbove 640px .trigger/.skeleton were width: 220px -- rigid. When the header\nrow ran short (the deployed stands render ''dev'' and ''branch'' in the env\nbadge; ''branch'' is the longest label the dev server never builds locally),\na rigid flex item has nowhere to give, so the surplus went past .actions.\n.triggerText already has overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis, so the\nselect was built to shrink -- nothing let it.\n\nSwitched to width: 220px + flex-shrink: 1 + min-width: 0. A bare\nflex-basis (flex: 0 1 220px) was tried first and doesn''t compose the same\nway: .brand''s own width is auto (content-based), and Chrome''s content-based\nsizing pass for an ancestor like that measures a flex-basis-only child by\nits intrinsic content size, not its flex-basis -- it silently discarded\nthe 220px preference and left the trigger permanently shrunk to ~123px\neven at 1280px with room to spare. An explicit width is what a\ncontent-based ancestor actually measures, so it''s kept alongside\nflex-shrink instead of being replaced by it.\n\nVerified this composes with the two media queries below it, unchanged:\n<=640px (flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0, from d4c7c1af) and <=430px (fixed\n36px icon) -- selectW is exactly 220 at 1280/900 and exactly 36 at every\nwidth <=430, for all three non-production env-badge labels (''local'',\n''dev'', ''branch'').\n\n* docs(widget-runtime): correct overlay-history and MyDevicesDialog comments\n\noverlay-history.ts''s module header claimed every close path routes through\ndismissOverlay and a traversal, but a widget''s fullscreen close control and\nAddWidgetMenu''s close button drop the overlay directly (open set to false,\nunmount) without ever calling dismissOverlay. Rewrite the header to name the\ntwo legitimate shapes -- dismiss and drop -- and clarify that the\n\"only popstate calls close\" invariant is about close specifically, not about\nevery close path routing through history.\n\nDocument two more constraints on callers in dropOverlay''s doc block,\nalongside the two already recorded: a push landing while a flush''s own\nhistory.go is still in flight reads a stale depth (unreachable today, but\nundocumented), and a dismiss cannot be vetoed by its caller once history has\nalready forwarded it to reconcile.\n\nMyDevicesDialog''s belt-and-braces setTimeout comment described a same-tick\nrace with Radix''s deferred pointerDownOutside check. Since the overlay-history\nwork, onOpenChange(false) no longer runs inside the click -- it arrives from\npopstate tens of milliseconds later -- so the described mechanism is stale,\neven though the guard it protects still holds (more strongly, in fact).\nCorrect the explanation without changing the guard.\n\nNo behavior change; comments and doc blocks only.\n\n* docs(agent): preserve the browser history traversal measurement\n\n.superpowers/ is git-ignored and gets deleted once this branch''s work\nfinishes, so the real-Chromium measurement behind overlay-history.ts''s\ndeferred-traversal design (browser-history-probe.md) would otherwise be lost\nentirely, leaving only the numbers already quoted in the module header with\nno reproducible method behind them.\n\nCapture the question, method, measured numbers, and the jsdom/Chromium\ndivergence in docs/agent/browser-history-traversal.md, written as durable\nguidance rather than a session log.\n\nPoint at it from CLAUDE.md. This worktree branched before the CLAUDE.md\nguide-table refactor landed on dev, so there is no existing table to add a\nrow to here; add a new one-row \"Related guides\" table in the same format,\nto be reconciled with dev''s table on the next merge.\n\n* docs: fold the history-traversal guide into the main guide table\n\nThe dev merge brought in the guide table this branch predated, leaving two\ntables side by side. One table, one row.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(client): announce the compact theme button''s state and tighten the header tests\n\nThe compact theme button''s fixed aria-label (\"Сменить тему\") left the\ncurrent mode reaching a screen-reader user only through `title` (not\nreliably announced, least of all by touch screen readers on a <=768px\nviewport) -- a regression against the tri-state group it replaces, which\nannounces the active mode through the radio''s name plus aria-pressed.\nThe accessible name now composes the current mode in: `Тема: ${label}.\nСменить`. It names the state and the action, and can''t drift from\ncycleThemeMode since it describes the current mode, not the next one.\ntitle is kept for the hover affordance. Updated the two vitest queries and\nthe e2e HeaderPage locator that pinned the old fixed name to match on the\nconstant \"Сменить\" suffix instead (regex), since the name now changes\nwith every click.\n\nEnvBadge.test.tsx''s \"label in its own element\" guard couldn''t actually\nfail: getByText matches an element''s direct text-node children and\nreturns the closest such element, so reverting the label back to bare\ntext inside .badge would make getByText(''dev'') resolve to the pill\nitself -- still a <span>, still !== the dot, so both assertions passed on\nold and new markup alike. Rewrote it to compare against the outer pill\n(screen.getByRole(''status'')) instead, the one element getByText can only\nresolve to on the old markup. Verified by temporarily reverting the label\nto bare text: the rewritten test fails on it, passes once restored.\nFolded the now-redundant \"announces via aria-label\" test into the\nexisting \"labels itself for assistive technology\" test, which already\ncovered the same aria-label via a role query.\n\nDeleted BoardSchemaSelect.module.css''s dead .root class entirely (base\nrule and the <=430px block) instead of leaving two more live-looking\nreferences to a class the file''s own comment already called unused.\n\n@media (max-width: 768px) on the compact theme button disagreed by one\nwith the MOBILE_BREAKPOINT constant it''s commented as pairing with\n(grid-metrics.ts treats width < 768 as mobile, i.e. 768 itself is\ndesktop). Changed to max-width: 767px so both agree. Verified first: a\n1280->320px sweep (transitions disabled, 300ms settle) for all three\nnon-production env-badge labels (''local'', ''dev'', ''branch'') shows zero\noverlap at every width including exactly 768px, where the three-item\ngroup is still visible under the new threshold, so 767px doesn''t\nreintroduce the overlap 768px was chosen to avoid.\n\n* test(client): expect one Zakryt control in the fullscreen dialog, not zero\n\nClock now draws its own fullscreen close button (feat(clock): give the\nfullscreen clock a close control), so the \"widget can be expanded without\nduplicate fullscreen or close controls\" e2e test and the FullscreenOverlay\nheader comment -- both inherited unchanged from dev -- were asserting and\ndocumenting the old, no-longer-true premise that Clock draws none. Update\nthe assertion to expect exactly one such button (the widget''s own) and\ncorrect the comment''s example, while keeping the real guarantee both were\nprotecting: no *duplicate* chrome stacked on top of the widget''s.\n\n* chore(docs): drop this branch''s plan and spec from git\n\ndocs/* became local-only except the agent and architecture guides. These two\npredate that change on this branch; the files stay on disk.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* build: add a compose overlay for the local nginx e2e stack\n\ndocker-compose.yml defaults the WebAuthn triple to production\n(board.iiskelo.com, https) while playwright.nginx.config.ts drives\nhttp://localhost:8080. A stack started without overrides fails every passkey\njourney inside navigator.credentials.create(), surfacing only as the generic\nclient-side \"сбой процедуры регистрации\" -- register/options returns 200 and\nno verify call ever follows. README told readers to park the overrides in\n.env, which is unavailable here: rpi.toml uses that same file as its\n[secrets].env bundle, so localhost values would ride into production on the\nnext `rpi secrets push`.\n\nThe overlay carries those three plus ALLOW_TEST_DB_RESET, so\n`pnpm start:docker:nginx-e2e` is a single command that cannot be run half-way.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* fix(e2e): pin the gate logout journey to the logout response and navigation\n\n`page.waitForURL(''/'')` synchronized nothing: the board already IS at ''/'', so\nit resolved in the same tick as the logout click -- measured at 3 ms after it\nand ~20 ms before the model''s own navigation landed. Both halves of the\nresulting race were observed: the session probe reading the pre-logout 200,\nand page.reload() overtaking the navigation, which then re-entered the\nstill-controlling service worker and re-rendered the precached board shell\n(title \"myboard\" instead of \"myboard — активация\").\n\naccount.logout runs POST /api/auth/logout -> purge -> hard navigate, so arming\na waitForResponse and a framenavigated waiter before the click pins the whole\nsequence down.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* fix(server): skip the create-invite entry test when its bundle is missing\n\nThe test guarded only one of its two preconditions. Valkey reachability was\nchecked; the built dist/scripts/create-invite.cjs it executes was not, even\nthough dist/ is gitignored and nothing in `pnpm test` builds it. So the test\nwas green only while nothing listened on 6379, and turned `pnpm check` red\nwith a MODULE_NOT_FOUND the moment any stack was up -- in a file the diff\nnever touched. `pnpm start:docker` publishes 6379 and `pnpm test:e2e:nginx`\nrequires that stack, so running the gate after the e2e suite hit it every time.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* **fix(passport-checker): unify series/number secrets and validate identity via Zod schema**\n\n- Combined series and number secrets into a single `number` field, maintaining format consistency.\n- Introduced strict validation for passport identity using a Zod schema for improved error handling.\n- Adjusted regular expressions to parse series/number from a single input field.\n- Updated browser and test implementations to reflect merged secret logic and schema integration.\n\n* fix(rpi.toml): remove series secrets path from configuration\n\n* fix(docker): remove unused `series` secrets from compose files\n\n* fix: formatting\n\n* fix(passport-checker): align combined secret contracts\n\n* docs: design dual passport checker results\n\n* feat(passport-checker): define dual document results\n\n* feat(passport-checker): check both documents sequentially\n\n* test(passport-checker): cover dual browser submissions\n\n* feat(passport-checker): persist independent document results\n\n* fix(passport-checker): defer partial writes until hydration\n\n* feat(passport-checker): render dual document results\n\n* fix(passport-checker): resolve final review findings\n\n* fix(passport-checker): close final rereview findings\n\n* fix(passport-checker): resolve final release blockers\n\n* fix(passport-checker): support mixed-version PWA rollout\n\n* fix(passport-checker): preserve concurrent document results\n\n* docs: remove passport checker design spec\n\n* fix(passport-checker): use compact layout for short cards\n\n* chore: add dev:docker:browser command\n\n* feat(passport-checker): redesign tiny status card\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqbfoCCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAfOUQAEbcZ2Jk35q4mMKf3aa5B/Yd\nYQc+inPvgVcPISo9xa3HOqTwqc5XEhxtsVolTEw1MpSYI8yJPhaScC4J9KA7f2E2\nkjCRn/j85v3lp6htBeOFds4ZCXwVvfApXbCnr/ZrzdN+Ly6EEJzYzt2pYzWaFhg7\nJ3O9nUfzrVsWqujuB1shyl42GCJ0i0FCbbjNlBJGQ+l4jA6t4j6TaafoKVnBdUmd\nCV9fWoxuSdsqfoKXhue9Mn2kISetkDxoC2OG0kMEA/9NTGN/PHl+AxeTgelyIbkZ\nCvUmuK5r+fK8Gu3AM4zdFEkMjUvTPiRyDzeT2uMwZV+4usagyFzXI/6OURCUqYcI\ndz4dn5H0zgtrdJGxMuoaWM5ToAbPGes3dErekdH7Ehv439Qw05G4OWVbmLoKshdw\nebqaiNrHrOrt9xwwpDziKSfboAolY6kgbmG04ODfzJsZZM+yPtu5YrvBpFI6ky2M\nBy/JEnZVwKR02BzIu5gf/X3GZEmTsa29itSR/rtXtPGsYsheKBU2GBQBkmqv5NjW\nllPW/4ijP2mmdkgQD8oUeKlm90AtoIMTwWxHJcZisLgdRGNNSDI9uYLq8D0BWC/h\nC3hZ08z+cvOHzl1Qju4uoYm6UpasCfRRCkRtXcZT3//VN6uXmwerifkcGW5JuIfP\nN/cOmwyw00fO3NO2zl1w\n=776G\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-01T13:52:02Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi/myboard/commit/ea12a9cd55976786e9d268e5f8aa9de2d6bfd27f","node_id":"C_kwDOS9EJdtoAKGVhMTJhOWNkNTU5NzY3ODZlOWQyNjhlNWY4YWE5ZGUyZDZiZmQyN2Y","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi/myboard/commit/56fd7f8fed8ca841fc1b388c832eecb3efe88049","sha":"56fd7f8fed8ca841fc1b388c832eecb3efe88049","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/56fd7f8fed8ca841fc1b388c832eecb3efe88049"}],"sha":"ea12a9cd55976786e9d268e5f8aa9de2d6bfd27f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/ea12a9cd55976786e9d268e5f8aa9de2d6bfd27f"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24359879?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi","id":24359879,"login":"khmilevoi","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI0MzU5ODc5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/56fd7f8fed8ca841fc1b388c832eecb3efe88049/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-30T22:00:09Z","email":"Khmilevoi@gmail.com","name":"khmilevoi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-30T22:00:09Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"release: merge number and series secrets (#34)\n\n* docs(passport-checker): spec the tier-shared state follow-up\n\nCovers the per-instance model store, the StrictMode removal it requires,\nfullscreen collapse/restore around recovery, modal focus containment, and\nwhy the proposed focusout tightening is rejected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): plan the tier-shared state follow-up\n\nSeven tasks: formatting debt, the per-instance store, the lease hook plus\nStrictMode removal, fullscreen collapse/restore, modal focus containment,\nthe focusout-guard regression test, and the doc back-links.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(deploy): add a dev environment overlay on the dev branch\n\nrpi.dev.toml deploys the dev branch to board-dev.iiskelo.com as the\nseparate project myboard--dev, inheriting everything else from rpi.toml.\nThe agent runs compose with -p, so the environment gets its own\ncontainers, network and volumes.\n\nTwo things in docker-compose.yml blocked a second stack on the same Pi\nand are now variable, with the production values as defaults so the prod\ndeploy and the local tooling render byte-for-byte identically:\n\n- The fixed host ports 8080/6379/6080 are bound by the running prod\n  stack; rpi only allocates a port for the ingress service and appends\n  it, so a dev deploy would fail on the binds.\n- RP_ID/RP_NAME/PUBLIC_APP_URL/EXPECTED_ORIGIN were pinned to the\n  production hostname. WebAuthn scopes credentials to RP_ID and verifies\n  assertions against EXPECTED_ORIGIN, so on another hostname the gate\n  rejects every ceremony without failing the deploy.\n\n.env.dev carries the dev values and stays untracked, like .env;\n.env.dev.example documents it. infra.test.ts gains guards for the two\nsilent-failure modes: hostname/RP_ID drift, and dev host ports landing\non a prod bind or inside rpi''s 8000-8999 allocator range.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(widget-sdk): move the instance store into the shared widget helpers\n\nThe mechanism is not passport-specific: it follows from the board mounting a\nplaced widget twice while fullscreen is open. Spec and plan now put a generic\nmakeWidgetInstanceStore factory and useWidgetInstance hook in widget-sdk, with\nthe widget keeping only its typing and disposal policy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(widget-sdk): let reatom connection lifetime own the instance store\n\nMeasured against @reatom/core@1001.1.0: withConnectHook fires on the first\nsubscriber and its cleanup on the last, which is the reference counting the\nstore was hand-rolling. The React lease hook disappears entirely — a reatomMemo\ncomponent that reads the instance atom IS the reference.\n\nTwo findings the measurement pinned down: a computed''s cache survives\ndisconnection, so the disconnect hook must drop the handle or the next mount\nresurrects a disposed value; and StrictMode still has to go, because its remount\ncycle disposes a value while both components are still mounted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widgets): stop the client host build on widgets with no client.ts\n\ndiscoverWidgetIds (widget-build-assets.ts, used only by the client host''s\nstageWidgetBuilds plugin) required every packages/widgets/* directory with\na package.json to have a dist/, the same way scripts/codegen/client.ts\ndiscovers widget candidates. But codegen goes one step further and only\ntreats a widget as a federation remote once it has a client.ts -- exactly\nthe state passport-checker is in right now (browser-task subprojects only,\nits widget UI hasn''t landed on this branch yet). Never having a build\nscript, it never produces a dist/, so every client build broke with\nMissingWidgetBuildError, both locally and on the Pi during\n`rpi deploy --env dev`.\n\nFilter discoverWidgetIds by client.ts too, matching codegen''s criterion.\nAlso set dts: false on the client''s own federation() call (mirroring what\nevery widget''s own config already sets): the host build was unconditionally\nattempting Module Federation''s DTS generation, which -- since widgets are\nconsumed dynamically via loadRemote + the generated catalog, never through\na typed remote import -- had nothing to generate and always failed with a\ncaught-but-alarming \"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading\n''readFile'')\" on every build.\n\nAdd docs/** to both oxfmt''s and oxlint''s ignorePatterns: docs/ carries a\nlot of pre-formatted design/spec markdown and an embedded HTML mockup that\noxfmt doesn''t reflow the same way as their original authoring tools, so a\nroutine `pnpm format` run was rewriting ~18 unrelated files.\n\nAlso drop the leftover PASSPORT_SERIES/PASSPORT_NUMBER lines from\n.env.dev.example: the passport-checker widget reads its series/number from\nplain-value files under the widget package, not from env vars (see\npackages/browser-automation/README.md#provisioning-secrets), so document\nthat instead.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: record the shared widget modal primitive in BACKLOG.md\n\nuseModalIsolation is a hand-rolled modal owned by one widget, and its isolation\nleans on radix-ui internals that can change in a patch release without failing\nanything but our one deliberate stack test. Capture the follow-up, its trigger,\nand the two shapes worth considering instead of leaving it implicit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore(format): apply oxfmt to pre-existing drift\n\nThese 18 files predate this branch and were already failing\npnpm format:check on main. Formatting them here makes the branch\ngate readable.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* style(ofelia-poop-duty): wrap the over-width test declaration\n\nCommit 3eee2f2 on this branch added a timeout option that pushed the `it(...)`\ndeclaration past oxfmt''s print width, so `pnpm format:check` has been red on a\nbranch-authored file. This is the branch''s own drift, not the pre-existing debt\nthe previous commit cleared.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(widget-sdk): add a connection-scoped widget instance store\n\nThe board mounts a placed widget twice while fullscreen is open — once as a\ntile, once in the overlay — so per-mount useMemo state is built twice and lost\non every tier switch. Widgets can now share one value per instanceId, built for\nthe first subscriber and disposed when the last one disconnects.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): make instance-store disconnect race-safe\n\nSwitch the widget instance store from withDisconnectHook to\nwithConnectHook so build and dispose are paired per connect episode,\nmatching the clock-model.ts convention:\n\n- Guard the deferred disconnect cleanup with isConnected(handle) so an\n  unsubscribe immediately followed by a resubscribe of the same key in\n  one synchronous flush (React StrictMode''s double-invoked effects, a\n  same-commit re-key) no longer disposes a value a live mount is still\n  rendering.\n- Only delete the key''s map entry when it still points at the handle\n  running the cleanup, so a stale disconnect can no longer evict a\n  newer, currently-connected handle for the same key.\n- Rebuild the value in the connect hook''s setup too, so a genuine\n  reconnect after disposal gets a fresh value instead of resurrecting\n  a disposed one.\n- Correct the module JSDoc: the value is built lazily on first read\n  (unavoidable, since a component''s initial render reads before its\n  effect subscribes), not \"for the first subscriber\"; only disposal is\n  actually governed by the connection lifetime. Note that a bare read\n  with no subscribing component never triggers dispose.\n\nAdds a regression test reproducing the same-tick unsubscribe/\nresubscribe race directly against the atom''s subscribe API.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): close the orphaned-read leak and stop re-poisoning the instance map\n\nA bare store(key, make)() read outside any subscription built a value that\ndispose never ran for, and left a permanent handles entry: the compute\nfunction now gives an orphaned first build one microtask to be claimed by a\nreal subscriber before tearing it down. The connect hook also stopped\nre-registering its handle in the map on every connect, which was letting a\nstale handle reconnected directly (bypassing store()) repoint future lookups\nat an already-torn-down value; the identity-guarded delete already keeps a\nstale disconnect from evicting a newer handle, so the map only ever reflects\na handle that was actually looked up through the factory.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): drop the timer-based orphan-read teardown in instance-store\n\nThe round-2 fix scheduled a queueMicrotask() to dispose a value that no\nsubscriber had claimed yet, assuming a real mount''s useLayoutEffect\nsubscribe always lands in the same task as its render-phase read. React 19\ntime-slices Suspense retry lanes (every widget renders under lazy +\nSuspense) and resumes via a scheduler macrotask, so that assumption does\nnot hold: the microtask could fire and dispose a value a live mount was\nstill about to read, corrupting a dependency-free computed forever and\nsplitting the map entry across mounts of the same instance.\n\nDisposal is now driven ONLY by Reatom''s real connect/disconnect lifecycle,\nwhich is timing-safe by construction. The tradeoff, documented in the\nmodule doc comment: a bare read with no component ever mounting still\nleaks its built value, since nothing ever subscribes to disconnect it.\nNothing outside this store''s own test currently reads it that way.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): share one model graph across widget mounts\n\nThe board tile and the fullscreen overlay mount the same instance twice, so\nper-mount useMemo models lost every result on a tier switch. Read one shared\ninstance instead, and let only the non-fullscreen mount own the recovery modal.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(widget-sdk): pin the instance store''s StrictMode tolerance\n\nStrictMode''s development-only mount -> unmount -> mount cycle empties the\nsubscriber set, which an earlier draft of this store answered by disposing a\nvalue both mounted components were still rendering. The connect hook''s\nisConnected re-check closes that; this test is what keeps it closed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): collapse fullscreen for recovery and restore it after\n\nOpening recovery from the fullscreen mount now collapses the board''s Radix\ndialog through requestClose, so the modal is never stacked over a trapped\nFocusScope; closing or retrying restores fullscreen through requestFullscreen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): wrap the recovery-open handler in each tier''s own frame\n\nPassportChecker built openRecovery with wrap() once per its own render and\nhanded the same closure to StandardTier/TinyTier, but those tiers re-render\nindependently on their own viewState() changes. The closure stayed bound to\nPassportChecker''s stale render frame, so clicking \"Открыть восстановление\"\nafter an idle->pending->sessionRequired transition (without the parent also\nre-rendering) could silently do nothing -- the root cause of the ~10% flake\nin ui/recovery-flow.test.tsx. PassportChecker now builds a plain (unwrapped)\ncomposition function and each tier wraps it fresh in its own render, mirroring\nthe existing `check` handler pattern.\n\nAlso adds a UI test for the restoreFullscreen wiring''s actual cross-mount\nhandoff: recovery opened from a fullscreen mount, then the tile mount (same\ninstanceId) inherits restorePending and calls its own requestFullscreen on\nclose -- the one assertion that would have caught restoreFullscreen wired to\nthe wrong callback.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): widen recovery-flow.test.tsx''s test budget to stop whole-suite timeouts\n\nThe new fullscreen/tile recovery tests render through Radix''s dialog/focus\nmachinery across two model mounts, which occasionally pushed real wall-clock\ntime past the shared 5s default under whole-suite worker contention (never a\nlogic hang). Set testTimeout to 30000ms for this file only, verified against\nzero reproductions of the line-122 timeout across dozens of whole-suite runs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): root-cause the recovery-flow Escape-close race\n\nfindByRole(''dialog'') resolves as soon as the dialog''s DOM node commits,\nbut useModalIsolation''s mount effect - which attaches the Escape\nlistener and moves focus in - is a passive effect that can still be\npending a tick later. Firing Escape into that gap dispatched into a\ndocument with no listener yet, so the dialog never closed and the\nfollowing waitFor spun until it timed out; reproduced solo (no\nwhole-suite contention needed) in roughly 1 of 15-40 runs.\n\nEach of the two tests that close the modal now waits for the effect''s\nsynchronous side effect (focus landing inside the dialog) before\ndispatching Escape, which proves the same effect has also attached the\nlistener. Replaces the prior fix round''s blanket file-wide 30s test\ntimeout (and its comment''s now-falsified \"never a hang\" claim) with a\nscoped 10s per-test timeout on only the two tests that actually run\nthis round trip, as a safety margin for ordinary contention rather\nthan a mask for this race.\n\nVerified with 40 solo + 32 self-inflicted-parallel + 8 whole-suite\nreruns after the fix, zero reproductions of the timeout.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): hold focus inside the recovery modal\n\nRadix restores focus from a zero-delay timeout when its dialog unmounts, so\ncollapsing fullscreen for recovery handed focus to the board a tick after the\nmodal mounted. Pull focus back whenever it lands outside the modal.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the unmount-cleanup test teeth and fix a flaky Escape assert\n\nRecoveryModal.test.tsx''s `leaves focus alone once the modal is gone` test\npassed even when the cleanup''s `removeEventListener(''focusin'', ...)` was\ndeleted, or the whole effect cleanup was zeroed out: after unmount the\nportal root is detached, so a leaked focusin listener calling `.focus()` on\na detached descendant is a jsdom/browser no-op and the activeElement assert\nstays green regardless. Pin the actual cleanup contract instead: spy on\nwindow.addEventListener/removeEventListener and assert the exact `focusin`\nlistener useModalIsolation registers on mount is the one removed on unmount,\nalongside the original behavioral assert.\n\nrecovery-flow.test.tsx''s first test asserted\n`dialog.contains(document.activeElement)` synchronously right after\n`findByRole(''dialog'')` resolves on DOM commit, before dispatching Escape.\n`useModalIsolation`''s mount effect (which moves focus in and attaches the\nEscape listener) is passive and can still be pending a tick later, so\nEscape could fire before the listener existed and the test would hang out\nits timeout — the same race Task 4 root-caused and fixed in this file''s\nother two Escape-closing tests by waiting for focus-in-dialog first. Apply\nthe same `await waitFor(...)` wrap here and correct the file-level comment,\nwhich had claimed only two tests reach the Escape-close race when this one\ndoes too.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the modal focusout guard against narrowing\n\nMount the underlying Radix dialog in its own pass, as production does, and\ncover the intra-modal focus move that the proposed guard narrowing would have\nbroken silently. The regression test dispatches the underlying focusout event\ndirectly rather than chaining native .focus() calls: jsdom (like real\nbrowsers) always completes an outer .focus() call on its own target once its\nhandler stack unwinds, so a settled document.activeElement assertion can''t\nobserve a reclaim a nested handler only ever wins transiently — verified\nempirically against the narrowed guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): close a cross-test focus-restore leak in the Radix stack suite\n\nRadix''s FocusScope defers its unmount focus-restore to a real setTimeout(0),\nand testing-library''s auto cleanup() never waits for it, so a prior test''s\ndeferred restore (and an unawaited NoVncCanvas state update from that same\ntest''s async recovery flow) can bleed into the next test — confirmed by\ninstrumentation. A formal trace of the reentrant focus cascade shows the\n''pulls focus back...'' assertion itself can''t actually be flipped by this\n(no yield point exists between its synchronous .focus() call and its\nexpect()), but the leak is real and pre-existing to any suite that\nmounts+unmounts a trapped FocusScope, so close it defensively: flush one\nreal tick after each test''s cleanup so Radix''s deferred work always\nfinishes before the next test starts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): drain Radix''s focus-restore timer from beforeEach, not afterEach\n\ne797d50''s message overstated what that change achieved. It claimed the added\nafterEach ran \"strictly after cleanup()\" and closed the cross-test focus-restore\nleak. It did neither. Vitest resolves sequence.hooks to \"stack\", so same-suite\nafterEach hooks run in REVERSE registration order, and testing-library''s\ncleanup() -- registered first, at setupFiles import time -- therefore runs last.\nThe file-level afterEach ran BEFORE cleanup(), i.e. before the unmount that\nschedules Radix''s setTimeout(0) focus restore, so it drained nothing.\n\nInstrumenting setTimeout and the hook boundaries on this file shows it directly:\nat afterEach entry the modal is still in the DOM and no FocusScope timer is\npending; the timer is scheduled only after the hook returns; the next test''s\nbeforeEach starts with one still queued. Moving the same one-tick flush to\nbeforeEach takes that count to zero before the test body runs. beforeEach is\ncorrect by construction -- it always follows the previous test''s afterEach\nhooks whatever the hook-order mode.\n\nCorrect the scope of the claim too. The restore is focus-neutral in this\nfixture: the previouslyFocusedElement it captured is either document.body,\nwhich jsdom refuses to focus, or an already-detached node, and every observed\nfiring left document.activeElement unchanged. The intermittent failure this was\nchasing did not reproduce at e797d50 in 30 consecutive single-file runs or 6\nwhole-package runs. So this closes a scheduling leak, not an observed failure,\nand the comment now says exactly that instead of claiming otherwise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): link the tier-shared-state follow-up\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): drop the phantom rebuild from the instance store''s connect hook\n\nThe connect hook carried `current ??= { value: make() }` behind a comment\nclaiming it gave a reconnect after disposal a fresh value. Measured against\n@reatom/core@1001.1.0 with the shipped code:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 2\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"],[{\"id\":2},\"a\"]]   built = 2\n\nThe reconnect still returned the DISPOSED value — a dependency-free `computed`\nnever recomputes, so its cached result is what every later read gets. The line\nonly built a second model graph nobody could reach and then handed it to\n`dispose` a second time. Same run with the line removed:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n\nThe comment now states what is true: a stale reconnect reads the cached,\nalready-disposed value, and a fresh value comes only from a new `store()`\nlookup — which is what every real caller does, because it calls `store()`\nduring render and the cleanup has already evicted the stale handle.\n\nWith the phantom build gone, `if (held)` is what prevents the double dispose,\nso a test pins it: replacing it with an unconditional `dispose?.(held?.value,\nkey)` now fails with a second teardown recorded as `[undefined, \"a\"]`.\n\nAlso corrects the module doc''s cost claim for reading atoms inside `make`.\nIt does not \"silently rebuild the whole value\": measured, after such a\ndependency changes the compute function re-runs once, `current ??=`\nshort-circuits it so `make` does not re-run (built stayed 1, the identical\nobject was returned, no subscriber notified), and because the re-run never\nreads the atom again the edge is dropped. The cost is a pointless dependency\nedge, not lost state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): align the tier-shared-state spec with what shipped\n\nThree claims in the design spec described work that was never delivered:\n\n- The instance atom was said to carry `withDisconnectHook`. It carries\n  `withConnectHook`, whose cleanup calls `teardownIfDisconnected()` — which\n  re-checks `isConnected` before disposing and evicts the map entry only while\n  it still points at the handle being torn down.\n- Section 4 \"StrictMode removal\" claimed `<StrictMode>` was dropped from\n  `packages/client/src/app/main.tsx` and replaced by a comment explaining its\n  absence. None of it shipped: `git diff cfff8d2..HEAD -- packages/client/` is\n  empty and the wrapper is still there. Measurement showed the store survives\n  the remount cycle, because the cleanup''s `isConnected` re-check returns\n  without touching a value a live mount still holds. Section 4 is rewritten as\n  \"StrictMode kept, tolerance pinned by test\", stating why the plan''s original\n  reasoning does not apply.\n- The Testing gate, the Documentation list and the Decision Summary repeated\n  the removal. They now say StrictMode stays and that the tolerance is pinned\n  by `describe(''makeWidgetInstanceStore under StrictMode'')`.\n\nThat last claim is measured, not assumed: deleting the `isConnected` re-check\nfrom the cleanup turns two of the three StrictMode tests red, along with the\nnon-StrictMode same-tick-resubscribe test.\n\nThe plan file is left untouched — it is the historical record of what was\nintended.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): narrow unmeasured claims and close two coverage gaps\n\nComment/claim accuracy — all of these stated more than was ever measured:\n\n- `ui/recovery-flow.test.tsx`: the \"roughly 1 in 15-40 runs\" hang frequency\n  was applied to every Escape-closing test in the file. It was measured for\n  exactly one of them, ''does not collapse when recovery opens from the tile''\n  (task-4-report.md, Fix round 3). The others carry the same wait by\n  inspection — identical shape, identical race — with no observed rate. The\n  comment now says so.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"at `beforeEach` entry one (later\n  two) FocusScope timer is pending\" — only the 1 -> 0 transition was ever\n  recorded, so the \"(later two)\" is dropped.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"jsdom (like real browsers) always\n  completes an outer `.focus()` call on its own target\" — true and measured\n  for jsdom, never measured against a real browser. Scoped to jsdom.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: ''keeps focus inside when it moves\n  between two of our own controls'' promised a focus invariant the test does\n  not assert — it asserts that our guard swallows the `focusout` before it\n  reaches `document`. Renamed to say that.\n\nCoverage:\n\n- `model/instance-store.test.ts` (new) covers `dispose: (models) =>\n  models.recoveryModel.teardown()`, the safety net against leaking a live VNC\n  session when the modal outlived the widget. It had no test at all: replacing\n  the callback with a no-op left the whole widget suite green. Verified both\n  new tests fail against that no-op.\n- `ui/PassportChecker.test.tsx`: two assertions used\n  `expect(await screen.findAllBy*(…)).toHaveLength(2)`, which cannot retry —\n  `findAllBy*` resolves on the first snapshot with >= 1 match, so it settles on\n  one mount and never waits for the second. Both are now a re-querying\n  `waitFor`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(backlog): record the two-modal focus ping-pong as a deferred defect\n\nBoth `useModalIsolation` instances register focus containment on `window` in\nthe capture phase, so two mounted modals each see the other''s `focusin` before\nthe root-level `stopPropagation()` can stop it, and each pulls focus into its\nown root — forever.\n\nReproduced in jsdom 29.1.1 with two `useModalIsolation` roots mounted at once:\none modal performs 1 focus pull on mount; two mounted together never settle,\nand a hard test guard at 400 pulls had to stop the run (402 recorded).\n\nDeferred rather than fixed because it is unreachable through the UI: the\npassport checker is the only owner of such a modal, renders at most one\n(non-fullscreen mount only), and its overlay is a full-viewport\n`pointer-events: auto` layer whose `pointerdown` handler closes the modal, so\na second one cannot be opened over the first.\n\nThe entry notes the likely fix — a module-level stack of active modal roots\nwith containment applied only by the topmost.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): correct five unverified claims left by code review\n\nBring wording in the tier-shared-state spec, BACKLOG.md, and a test\ncomment in line with what was actually measured/read: the instance\nstore''s dependency-edge cost (not a rebuilt value), a stale\nuse-modal-isolation.ts line citation (:53 -> :55, two places), the\nroot focusin stopper''s actual bubble phase (not capture), and which\nrecovery-flow tests keep the shared timeout default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay at this branch\n\nPins rpi.dev.toml''s source.branch to feat/passport-checker-widget so the dev\nenvironment runs this PR instead of the dev branch.\n\nREVERT BEFORE MERGING: once this PR lands on dev, source.branch must go back to\n\"dev\" or the dev overlay stays pinned to a branch that no longer moves.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): drop the stale Chromium singleton lock before launch\n\nThe Chromium profile is a named Docker volume, so `SingletonLock` keeps the\n`<hostname>-<pid>` of whichever container wrote it. After a redeploy the new\ncontainer has a new hostname, Chromium reads a lock owned by \"another computer\"\nit cannot probe, and `launchPersistentContext` fails on every task until the\nvolume is touched by hand.\n\nRemove `SingletonLock`, `SingletonCookie`, and `SingletonSocket` before each\nlaunch. `fs.rm` unlinks the symlinks themselves instead of following them, and\n`force` keeps a fresh profile from failing; a removal that does fail is returned\nas a value, logged, and the launch still proceeds. Unconditional removal is safe\nbecause `getPersistentContext` is the only caller of `launch` and the image\nentrypoint runs a single `node dist/index.cjs` - no check for a live Chromium is\nperformed or implied.\n\nAlso asserts that a failed launch keeps its cause, which is what makes the\nPlaywright message reachable from the service log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): log the error behind an internal task failure\n\nInternal failures were logged as `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` only, so the\ntagged error and its cause chain never reached the container log. A Chromium\nlaunch failure therefore showed up as a bare `internal` code with nothing to\ndiagnose. Add the error to the logged fields; `toEnvelopeError` still answers\nthe client with the redacted `internal` code, so only the log gains detail.\n\nNote the trade-off: a handler error whose message carries task data now reaches\nthe container log, which the previous field list deliberately avoided.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): redact handler failures in the internal task log\n\nfa87a47c logged too much. It added `error: outcome` to the internal-failure log\nso a Chromium launch failure would stop being invisible, but that field carries\nwhatever the handler threw. errore''s `toJSON()` serialises `message`, `stack`,\nand the whole `cause` chain, Playwright quotes `page.evaluate` arguments into\nits messages, and the passport widget passes the secret identity as one — so a\nhandler failure could print the passport series and number into the container\nlog. The wrapper''s own `stack` repeats the cause under `Caused by:`, so the\nidentity landed there twice.\n\nThat contradicts the design spec: raw Playwright, network, Zod, and upstream\nresponse details are logged only after redaction, request and response bodies\nare never logged, and logs redact task payloads and secret values\n(docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md\n:350-353, :684).\n\nThe rule now, verified against dispatch.ts:\n\n- `BrowserExecutorError` wraps a failed `executor.acquire(...)`. Acquire runs\n  before the handler and is passed only `(signal, widgetId)` — never the payload\n  or the widget secrets — so its cause chain is Chromium/Playwright launch\n  detail. Still logged in full; this is the stale singleton lock failure\n  fa87a47c existed to surface.\n- Every other internal failure wraps handler output and is reduced to\n  `errorChain`: the names/tags of the error and its `cause` chain and nothing\n  else. No `message`, no `stack`, no foreign properties.\n\nThe log message, the `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` fields, and the\n`code === ''internal''` condition are unchanged.\n\nTests split the single fa87a47c case in two: one pins that an executor failure''s\ncause chain still reaches the log, one asserts a secret-looking cause string is\nabsent from the serialised logged payload. Both were confirmed to fail before\nthe fix and to discriminate after it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add an env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe passport widget''s recovery path (retain the page, issue a single-use\ncapability, stream the live browser over noVNC, let a human solve the\nchallenge, retry) has never been exercised on real hardware, because it only\ntriggers when pasport.org.ua serves a Cloudflare challenge and none has been\nencountered — the smoke test on the Pi returned a normal result. Subproject 8\n(`passport-checker-rpi-integration` in\ndocs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md)\nhas \"embedded noVNC Cloudflare recovery, SSH fallback, and real checker smoke\ntest on the Raspberry Pi\" as an acceptance item and had no way to reach it on\ndemand. PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY is that way.\n\nContract: `1` and nothing else enables it — absent, empty, `0`, `true`, ` 1 `\nare all off (isForcedRecoveryEnabled does exact string equality, no trimming,\nno truthiness coercion). Compose passes it through with a `:-` default so an\nunset variable stays off, and it must never be set in production.\n\nSemantics: at most once per process. The latch lives in the closure\nmakePassportCheckHandler returns, so each handler owns its own and the unit\ntests stay isolated. It fires after a successful goto and after\ncollectNavigationEvidence — the retained page is the real checker page with the\nsite''s own UI loaded, so a human can drive it over noVNC — and returns exactly\nthe retainPageForRecovery() + BrowserSessionRequiredError pair the genuine\nCloudflare branch returns, making the downstream flow byte-identical to a real\nchallenge. The latch is consumed only when the forced path actually fires, so a\nfailed navigation returns its own error untouched and leaves the lever armed:\nan infrastructure hiccup must not burn the single forced run an operator is\nwaiting on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): apply the design-system widget redesign\n\nBring the tile and the recovery modal in line with Паспорт-чекер.dc.html.\n\nBoth tiers now lay out as header -> flexible middle -> action, so the\naction sits on the tile''s bottom edge in every state instead of trailing\nwhatever content happens to be above it. Status banners pick up the\ndesign''s per-state hues, the filled success disc, and the CircleAlert /\nTriangleAlert split that separates a retryable failure from a session\nprompt. The modal gains the access-pill dot, a pulsing LIVE dot, framed\nconnecting/disconnected/expired states, and the shell-style SSH block.\n\nDark theme was effectively missing: the --pc-* hues chained to\n--success-soft / --destructive-soft, which the host token set never\ndefines, and to --success, which is a dark surface green rather than an\nicon green under [data-theme=''dark'']. Every hue is now declared per\ntheme the way ofelia-poop-duty does it. The modal portals to\ndocument.body, outside .widget, so it declares the one hue it needs on\nits own overlay.\n\nThree deliberate departures from the mockup:\n- invalidConfig copy stays server-side (\"Обратитесь к администратору\"),\n  since this widget has no per-instance settings — its config lives in\n  server secrets.\n- the expired frame keeps \"Переподключиться\"; without it an expired\n  capability can only be recovered by closing and reopening the modal.\n- the sessionRequired tiny tile only fills amber, no border: the tile\n  border belongs to the host card, and drawing one from inside the\n  widget would render a second, inset contour.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xwGNctwqXcYi5p4QSSFhA\n\n* docs(browser-automation): design widget user-input detection\n\nMove the \"a human must act\" mechanism out of passport-checker and into the\nautomation platform. The handler gets context.detectUserInput(detector), which\nruns the detector, optionally prepares the page, retains it and returns the\ncanonical error as one indivisible step; retainPageForRecovery leaves the public\ncontext so the halves cannot be separated. Cloudflare becomes a reusable helper\n(probe and predicate together, not just the predicate) that any widget can pass\nin. Also records the removal of the PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY lever.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): correct the user-input spec''s test-seam claims\n\nmakeFakeExecutor fakes BrowserExecutor<FakeContext> with FakeContext = { id,\nsignal }; it never builds a BrowserTaskContext, so it cannot host a recording\ndetectUserInput and needs no change at all. Name the seams that do exist: the\nsix retainPageForRecovery call sites in chromium-executor.test.ts and the three\nBrowserTaskContext literals in diagnostics.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): plan the widget user-input detection refactor\n\nSeven tasks, each green on its own: retire the forced-recovery lever, add the\ndetector and error contracts, move Cloudflare detection into the platform with\nits probe reunited with its predicate, run escalation from the task context,\nrewire the widget, then withdraw retainPageForRecovery from the public context so\nthe retain/escalate pair cannot be taken apart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): make the Cloudflare task a move, not a copy\n\nTask 3 previously left the classifier duplicated between the widget and the\nplatform until Task 5, which any reviewer would rightly flag as verbatim\nduplication of a logic block. The widget now switches to the moved module in the\nsame task, so the logic never exists in two places.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* revert(passport-checker): drop the env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe lever forced one trip through the Cloudflare recovery path per service\nstart so Subproject 8 could exercise noVNC on the Pi on demand. It is being\nretired deliberately ahead of moving user-input detection into the platform;\nthe Pi smoke test needs another answer.\n\nThis reverts the behaviour added in 45c604f1.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(browser-automation): add user-input detector and error contracts\n\nUserInputRequiredError carries the browser_session_required code and sshTarget\nmeta the recovery flow keys off, moved out of the passport-checker widget where\na platform contract had no business living. UserInputProbeError distinguishes\na broken check from a negative one.\n\n* feat(browser-automation): move Cloudflare detection into the platform\n\nThe classifier lived in the passport-checker widget while the DOM probe feeding\nit lived in that widget''s check handler, so the only reusable half was the half\nwithout the probe. Both now sit in browser-automation behind two detector\nfactories: one that probes the live page, one that classifies evidence already\nderived inside the page from a fetch response.\n\nThe widget imports the moved module rather than keeping a copy; it still calls\nthe classifier by hand, which the next commit changes.\n\n* @\ndocs: document the feature branch -> dev -> main workflow\n\nFeature work happens in a dedicated branch inside a ./.worktrees checkout,\nlands in dev through a PR, gets verified on the dev stack, and only then\nreaches main through a dev -> main PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* feat(browser-automation): run user-input detection from the task context\n\ndetectUserInput runs a widget-supplied detector and, on a match, prepares the\npage, retains it and returns UserInputRequiredError as one step, so the two\nhalves cannot drift apart. A detector that fails yields UserInputProbeError and\ndeliberately leaves the page unretained: an undecidable check is not a challenge,\nand pinning a browser page for a recovery nobody will perform is worse.\n\nAUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET moves into the service config, where an unusable value\ndegrades to null rather than failing config loading and killing the service.\n\n* docs(passport-checker): design last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* test(browser-automation): cover the prepare-before-retain order in detectUserInput directly\n\nThe executor-level test asserted hasRetainedPage() from inside the prepare\nmock, but that bookkeeping is only populated at release() time, so it passed\nregardless of call order. Add a direct unit test against makeDetectUserInput\nthat records prepare/retain into one ordered array — genuinely fails if the\norder in detect.ts is inverted (verified by temporarily inverting it).\n\nNo production behavior changes; detect.ts ends identical to before this commit.\n\n* @\ndocs: merge AGENTS.md into CLAUDE.md and symlink it\n\nAGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md had drifted into two partly overlapping rule sets.\nEverything unique to AGENTS.md (project structure, codegen commands, Windows\ntest-runner notes, coding style, UI gotchas, testing, security, failure modes)\nnow lives in CLAUDE.md, and AGENTS.md becomes a symlink so every agent reads\nthe same file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* docs(passport-checker): plan last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* feat(passport-checker): date-aware check timestamp label\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout design\n\nTwo width breakpoints instead of one hardcoded grid: one column below 768,\ntwelve above, with row height and margin scaled by the same factor as the\ncolumn width so the desktop grid zooms instead of stretching on one axis.\n\nThe mobile layout is derived from the desktop one and only persisted once the\nuser drags or resizes, stored as an additive optional `mobileLayout` field so\nno migration of `root:boards` is needed.\n\nAlso records the react-draggable touchstart defect: the whole card is the drag\nhandle today, which cancels page scrolling on touch — harmless on a short\n12-column board, blocking at one column.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): detect required user input through the platform\n\nThe handler no longer collects Cloudflare evidence, classifies it, retains the\npage and builds the escalation error by hand at every site; it passes a detector\nto context.detectUserInput and returns what comes back. browser/challenge.ts and\nthe widget-local BrowserSessionRequiredError are gone — the classifier now lives\nin browser-automation next to the probe that feeds it, and the\nbrowser_session_required contract belongs to the platform that owns the recovery\nflow.\n\n* fix(passport-checker): restore the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nAn automated amend from a concurrent session accidentally stripped this\nline out of 3a12494e. It belongs to the user-input-detection work; this\nrestores it verbatim.\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nRETRYABLE_MESSAGES.user_input_probe vanished twice with no test turning red,\nbecause nothing asserted the actual behaviour: with the entry gone,\nmapCheckError silently falls back to GENERIC_RETRYABLE_MESSAGE and the user\nsees the generic string instead of the specific one. Assert the real output\nof mapCheckError against a hardcoded expected string, not against the table\nitself, so a future deletion or typo of the entry fails this test instead of\ncomparing the map against itself.\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron design spec\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout implementation plan\n\n* feat(board): resolve grid metrics from the container width\n\n* feat(passport-checker): persist the last successful check\n\n* feat(board): derive and reconcile the one-column mobile layout\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron implementation plan\n\nAlso corrects the spec: the jsdom Temporal shims must stay, only the\n--harmony-temporal execArgv becomes redundant on Node 26.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin which detector goes to which detectUserInput call\n\nNothing previously checked that the navigation check gets the page detector\nand the submission check gets the evidence detector -- only the second call''s\nprepare option was asserted. A handler that swapped the two factories would\nleave every test in this file green; only the gated real-browser suite would\nnotice, and it isn''t run in CI. Pull the captured detector off each mock call\nand invoke it directly: the page detector must reach for page.evaluate, the\nevidence detector must ignore its page argument and classify the real\nevidence. Also drops the now-inert hasChallengeForm evidence field from the\nsubmission-escalation test, whose result was always driven by the mock''s\nescalation queue regardless of that field.\n\n* feat(board): add mobile layout materialize, update and reset actions\n\n* refactor(browser-automation): expose only detectUserInput on the task context\n\nretainPageForRecovery was half of a pair a handler had to assemble correctly by\nhand: retaining without escalating strands a browser page, escalating without\nretaining hands the UI a recovery session with nothing behind it. With the retain\nflag reachable only through detectUserInput, neither mistake is expressible.\n\n* test(passport-checker): cover restore-on-mount and cross-placement sync\n\nAdd two widget-level cases pinning storage-backed persistence through\nReact: a fresh mount renders a previously stored result without\ninvoking a check, and a check made in one placement is observed by a\nsecond placement sharing the same storage. Rename the pre-existing\n\"gives separate instance ids separate state\" case to \"gives separate\nstorages separate state\" (it isolates by storage, not instanceId) and\nupdate its comment to point at the new cross-placement case below it.\nPin PASSPORT_LAST_RESULT_KEY''s literal value in check-model.test.ts so\na silent rename cannot slip through green while orphaning deployed\nusers'' stored data under the old key.\n\n* feat(board): switch the grid to resolved metrics and a mobile drag grip\n\n* docs(browser-automation): document how a widget requests manual input\n\n* feat(board): offer a mobile layout reset in the board schema popover\n\n* fix(widgets): address passport-checker persistence review findings\n\n- TinyTier''s success chip now shows checkedAtLabel, so a restored (possibly\n  stale) result reads honestly in the compact tile too, not just standard.\n- Harden the two-placement isolation test: assert placement two stays idle\n  instead of a length-1 findAllByText that can pass mid-race.\n- Clarify why lastResult/transient set order is safe in checkPassport\n  (Reatom notifies after the sync block, not \"order for readability\").\n- Add a test exercising lastResultSchema.safeParse over the exact shape\n  checkPassport writes, since createFakeStorage skips schema validation.\n- Document lastResultSchema as a persistence contract: new fields must be\n  optional/defaulted or every stored value fails validation on next deploy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* fix(passport-checker): remove dead AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET threading\n\nrecoverySshTarget was never read by check.ts''s handler after the\ndetectUserInput refactor moved sshTarget sourcing entirely into the\nplatform config. Delete the widget''s leftover env read and option\nthreading so the README''s \"widgets neither read nor pass it\" claim is\nactually true, and drop the now-meaningless SSH-sanitization test in\nfavor of the equivalent (and broader) coverage in browser-automation''s\nconfig.test.ts.\n\n* docs(ofelia): design the authored ledger and comments\n\nRecords get the authenticated account instead of the per-device \"Я:\"\ntoggle, the write path moves into the widget''s own server.ts, and the\nhistory row is rebuilt: human dates, plain-language phrases, explicit\ndebt effect, visible supersession, no IP.\n\nPulls the Node 26 prerequisite out of the widget-server-cron spec, since\nthe shared domain module it enables is needed here first.\n\nAlso imports the Claude Design workspace under design/ (documents only,\nscreenshots and uploads stripped) and keeps the brief that produced the\nOfelia mock.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs(ofelia): note the cron plan overlap and landing order\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): cover the mobile layout and touch-scroll behaviour end to end\n\n* fix(board): keep the measured grid container mounted\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a 1920 viewport\n\n* docs(ofelia): add the authored ledger implementation plan\n\n15 tasks: Node 26, the pure domain split, the members directory, widget\nidentity, the server-resolved viewer, record schemas, draft construction,\nthe widget server definition, the model rewire, the history and comment\nview models, the account avatar, and the two rebuilt panels.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): wait for the grid relayout to settle before measuring\n\n* fix(browser-automation): bundle workspace imports instead of externalizing them\n\nThe rspack externals predicate was an allow-list whose default was\n\"externalize\". When the user-input refactor introduced the first runtime\nbare-specifier import of a workspace package into the service module graph\n(browser-automation/user-input/cloudflare, from the passport-checker browser\ntask), it was silently externalized: the build stayed green, all tests stayed\ngreen, and the Cloudflare module was simply absent from dist/index.cjs. The\nruntime stage copies only package.json and dist/, so Node resolved the\nspecifier through the exports map to a .ts path that does not exist in the\nimage and the container died on `exec node dist/index.cjs`.\n\nInvert the predicate into a deny-list derived from the package''s own\ndependencies (minus errore, which is ESM-only and must be bundled) plus Node\nbuiltins. An unrecognised bare specifier is now bundled, so the same mistake\nsurfaces as a build-time resolution error rather than a startup crash, and\nadding a real runtime dependency externalizes it with no config edit.\n\nAdd scripts/check-bundle.ts, chained onto `build` so it runs inside the image\nbuild the Dockerfile already performs. It asserts that every require() in the\nbundle is a builtin or a declared dependency, that the scan still matches the\nemitted shape at all, and that the compiled evidenceFromResponseText the widget\nsplices into Chromium still evaluates and runs in a bare vm realm. That last\ncheck is what the BROWSER_IT suite could never prove: it runs under vitest,\nwhere the specifier resolves to .ts through esbuild and the swc path is never\nexercised. Verified: the compiled form is ES5-lowered but self-contained --\ndestructuring became inline property reads, no swc helpers, no hoisted regexes.\n\nAlso:\n- detect.ts: tag the prepare outcome instead of comparing against null, so a\n  prepare that rejects with exactly null is a failure rather than a silent\n  success, matching the detector branch''s `typeof detected !== ''boolean''`.\n- user-input/errors.test.ts: cover toEnvelopeError for UserInputRequiredError\n  with and without an ssh target, pinning with toStrictEqual that `meta` is\n  omitted rather than set to undefined. contracts.test.ts already claimed this\n  coverage lived here.\n- chromium-executor.test.ts: drop an assertion inside a prepare callback that\n  could not fail the test and was vacuous, and rename the case to what it\n  actually verifies; the ordering contract is owned by detect.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* test(board): settle past the grid transition and stop assuming card order\n\n* test(board): correct the stale comment above the breakpoint wait\n\n* chore(node): align every runtime and image on Node 26\n\nnode:26-alpine and node:26-bookworm-slim no longer bundle corepack\n(removed from Node core), so `npm install -g corepack@latest` is added\nahead of every `corepack enable` in the three Dockerfiles and\ndocker-compose.dev.yml. scripts/infra.test.ts''s assertion on the\nbrowser-automation Dockerfile''s base image is updated to match.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(node): unshadow jsdom localStorage and bump the e2e image to Node 26\n\nNode 26 ships its own unflagged global localStorage/sessionStorage (Web\nStorage API), which resolves to undefined without --localstorage-file\nand shadows jsdom''s window.localStorage for bare-identifier reads in\npackages/client''s jsdom test environment (40 failing tests). Disable\nNode''s own globals via execArgv: [''--no-experimental-webstorage''] in\npackages/client/vite.config.ts so jsdom''s storage wins; verified via\nObject.getOwnPropertyDescriptor that the flag actually removes the\nglobal rather than just suppressing the warning. widget-runtime and\nwidget-sdk don''t touch localStorage in tests today, so the flag is not\nadded to their execArgv arrays (narrowest correct remedy).\n\nAlso bumps packages/client/e2e.Dockerfile (used by pnpm test:e2e:docker\nvia docker-compose.e2e.yml) from node:22-bookworm-slim to\nnode:26-bookworm-slim, missed by the original brief''s Files list, with\nthe same corepack@latest install this Node 26 base image now needs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): make the relayout wait actually poll\n\n* refactor(ofelia): extract the pure duty domain out of the Reatom model\n\n* fix(lint): make the widget domain import barrier a real allowlist\n\nThe regex denylist let ''../model/ofelia-duty'' through — domain/ and model/\nare siblings, so a single-''../'' import was both the most likely accidental\nescape and completely unguarded. It also only enumerated known packages, so\nany new npm dependency passed silently.\n\nReplace it with a gitignore-style group allowlist (''**'' minus zod, @shared/*\nand ''./'' siblings), which oxlint evaluates natively. A negative-lookahead\nregex was tried first and rejected: oxlint''s Rust regex engine has no\nlookaround and silently drops such a pattern, disabling the whole barrier.\n\nDomain tests get a second, later override that additionally allows vitest;\neverything else stays blocked there too.\n\n* chore(lint): keep the design mock exports out of lint and format\n\ndesign/ holds vendored Claude Design exports (support.js, *.dc.html)\nthat break the repo''s only gate: oxlint reports 9 errors in\ndesign/support.js, and oxfmt aborts (exit 2) on non-standard markup\nin design/Design System.dc.html before it can check anything else.\n\nAdd design/** to the existing ignorePatterns glob array in both\n.oxlintrc.json and .oxfmtrc.json, the same mechanism already used for\ndocs/**. This is a plain glob matcher (not the Rust regex engine\nbehind no-restricted-imports), so it isn''t subject to that engine''s\nlookaround gap, and it leaves every other rule/override untouched.\n\nFixing the oxfmt abort also uncovered pre-existing, unrelated\nformatting drift in CLAUDE.md, package.json and\nscripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts that the crash had been masking from\nthe format:check summary. Reformatted them (whitespace/ordering only,\nno logic changes) so pnpm format:check is actually green end to end.\n\n* feat(auth): expose the board members directory\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): expose the board identity to widgets\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): stop identity fetch from leaking an unhandled rejection and a discarded error\n\nHandle the AbortError that wrap()''s promise rejects with when the last\nwidget reading identity unmounts mid-fetch, and stop discarding\nBoardMembersError whole: a 401 stays silent (expected on an\nunauthenticated host), everything else is surfaced via console.warn so\nschema drift or a server fault is not silently swallowed.\n\n* feat(widgets): resolve the signed-in viewer for widget server events\n\n* feat(ofelia): carry the authoring account on ledger and comment records\n\n* feat(ofelia): move draft construction and event schemas into the domain\n\n* feat(ofelia): write the ledger and comments from the widget server\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): harden server date parsing and unforgeability coverage\n\n- Wrap Temporal.PlainDate.from in errore.try so a calendar-invalid but\n  regex-valid date (e.g. 2026-02-30) returns an Error instead of\n  throwing through a function typed Error | T.\n- Add regression coverage: forged createdBy/actor in the payload is\n  ignored, the debt handler is exercised, storage-read failures are\n  asserted by identity with no write, and the invalid-date case is\n  asserted as a value.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): split the unforgeability guard into schema and handler halves\n\nThe round-1 forged-payload test went through run(), which parses the\npayload via zod''s default strip mode before the handler ever sees it —\nso it only proved the schema strips forged fields, twice over, and\nwould not catch a handler that starts spreading its raw payload into\nthe draft. Replace it with two schema-level tests (clean + comment\npayloads drop a forged createdBy/actor) and one handler-level test\nthat calls ofeliaServer.handlers.clean directly, bypassing the schema,\nto prove the handler itself never reads those fields even if they\nreach it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* feat(ofelia): drive ledger and comment writes through widget server events\n\nThe client no longer appends to the ledger or the comment key. All four\nday actions collapse into one `invokeDay` that sends `{ date }` to the\nmatching widget-server event, and `send` sends `{ weekStart, text }` —\nwho the actor is, whose debt moves, whether the event applies at all and\nwhich account signed it are now derived and stamped server-side, so a\nclient cannot forge authorship.\n\n`selectedDate()` and `today()` (and `viewWeekStart()`) are read before\nthe first await so they resolve against the widget''s own Reatom context.\n\nThe per-device \"Я: Леша/Карина\" toggle is deleted rather than hidden:\nauthorship comes from the session, so there is nothing left to pick.\n`currentUser` is gone from the duty model, the widget context, the\nfixture and the view-model''s `onSetUser`.\n\nBoth model factories now take `api` and `identity`; `identity` is the\ndirectory the history and comment views read in the follow-up tasks.\nThe view projections drop `ip`/`by`, whose schema fields Tasks 5-8\nremoved, and `authorName` falls back through `createdBy.name`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* style: format the ofelia server test\n\n* feat(ofelia): group history by duty day and mark superseded records\n\n* feat(ofelia): resolve comment authors through the members directory\n\n* fix(ofelia): flush the comment thread''s connect hook via vi.waitFor, not a bare microtask\n\nReview round 1: the timing fix for the comment-thread subscribe/emit race\nassumed a single-microtask flush of Reatom''s connect-hook queue. Match the\nsibling comments-subscription tests in the same file instead — wait for the\nstorage mock''s subscribe call and wrap the whole body in context.start, so\nthe assertion is condition-based rather than scheduling-internals-based.\n\n* feat(ofelia): add the account avatar and its palette\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the history list around duty-day groups\n\n* fix(ofelia): announce the history phrase subject to assistive tech\n\nThe duty circle is the sentence subject in the rebuilt history phrase, and\nAvatar is aria-hidden, so the column announced as bare verbs with no subject.\nPair every circle with a visually hidden name, attribute the debt pill to its\nperson, and separate the chips with real spaces as the mock does.\n\nAlso correct the text-decoration comment to name the real mechanism (atomic\ninline, not descendant cancellation) and cover the three untested branches.\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the comment thread around account authors\n\n* fix(ofelia): restore composer coverage and mute the unknown comment author\n\n* fix(ofelia): break ledger day ties by append order\n\nRouting the writes through the widget server changed where `ts` comes from.\n`/api/storage/:key/append` stamped `Date.now()`, so consecutive appends always\ngot a strictly increasing timestamp. The widget dispatch stamps the app''s\ninjectable `now()`, and the e2e harness pins that clock — so a clean-then-undo\npair on the same day produced two entries with an identical `ts`, and\n`latestOutcomesByDate`''s strict `>` kept the earlier `cleaned` entry as the\nday''s outcome. The `reset` was written but never counted, and the day never\nreopened; `pnpm test:e2e:docker` caught it as a failing \"undo — returns the day\nto pending\".\n\nThe ledger is append-only and iterated in insertion order, so `>=` is the\ncorrect tie-break: on an equal timestamp the later-appended entry is the newer\ndecision. This also hardens production, where two writes can still land in the\nsame millisecond.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: describe server-authored widget records\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): fail widget dispatch when the session account is unreadable\n\nresolveWidgetViewer folded every getAccount failure into `null`, so a Valkey\nfault, a corrupt account record and a deleted account all produced a record\nstamped `createdBy: null` plus a `{ ok: true }` response. The ledger is\nappend-only, so that authorship could never be recovered.\n\nThe three outcomes are now distinct: no session still returns `null`, which is\nwhat the standalone harnesses and the e2e stack (no nginx, so no session cookie)\nrely on to keep writing unattributed records; a failed lookup returns a\nWidgetViewerLookupError, which the dispatch route turns into a fault instead of\na false success.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): order superseded history records by append order\n\n`superseded` sorted on `right.ts - left.ts`, which returns 0 on a tie; the\nstable sort then left tied records in append order, oldest first, contradicting\nthe \"newest first\" contract on HistoryDayGroup. Ties became a supported case\nwhen latestOutcomesByDate started breaking them with `>=`, and the dockerized\ne2e stack freezes the clock, so there every record ties.\n\nThe ledger is append-only, so append order is chronological order: reversing the\nday''s bucket is both correct and cheaper than sorting.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): warn when listAccounts skips an unreadable record\n\nA corrupt or schema-mismatched account record was dropped silently, so the\nroster simply came back short and the symptom read as \"an account vanished\"\nrather than as the data corruption it is. Name the key in a console.warn.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* build: pin corepack to 0.35.0 across images and dev compose\n\n`npm install -g corepack@latest` ran in five image build stages and four dev\ncompose services. There is no CI and the Pi deploy is manual with a 30-minute\nbuild timeout, so a breaking corepack release would only ever surface as a\nfailed deploy. Pin one explicit version everywhere.\n\nVerified: `docker build -f packages/server/Dockerfile` succeeds and the built\nimage reports corepack 0.35.0 with pnpm 10.28.2.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* refactor(ofelia): delete the dead short-date formatter\n\nformatDateShort and MONTHS_SHORT lost their last production consumer when the\ncomment thread moved to its own formatter; only the test still called them. It\nalso formatted through `new Date(ts)` in the local timezone, while every current\nformatter goes through plainDateIn(DUTY_TIME_ZONE, ...).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): drop the tier guards that cannot fail, hoist the env directive\n\nStandardTier and CompactTier each asserted they do not render UserToggle. The\ntoggle only ever lived in RichLayout and the component is now deleted outright,\nso neither assertion could ever fail — they only looked like coverage.\n\nAlso move the `@vitest-environment node` directive in server.test…","tree":{"sha":"e4dc8b4d7ae3f858fcd609ee1662de61bdd55749","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/git/trees/e4dc8b4d7ae3f858fcd609ee1662de61bdd55749"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/git/commits/56fd7f8fed8ca841fc1b388c832eecb3efe88049","verification":{"payload":"tree e4dc8b4d7ae3f858fcd609ee1662de61bdd55749\nparent 7d06bcae7f4543a188e8beaeb0c809d39f9c1080\nauthor khmilevoi <Khmilevoi@gmail.com> 1785448809 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785448809 +0200\n\nrelease: merge number and series secrets (#34)\n\n* docs(passport-checker): spec the tier-shared state follow-up\n\nCovers the per-instance model store, the StrictMode removal it requires,\nfullscreen collapse/restore around recovery, modal focus containment, and\nwhy the proposed focusout tightening is rejected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): plan the tier-shared state follow-up\n\nSeven tasks: formatting debt, the per-instance store, the lease hook plus\nStrictMode removal, fullscreen collapse/restore, modal focus containment,\nthe focusout-guard regression test, and the doc back-links.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(deploy): add a dev environment overlay on the dev branch\n\nrpi.dev.toml deploys the dev branch to board-dev.iiskelo.com as the\nseparate project myboard--dev, inheriting everything else from rpi.toml.\nThe agent runs compose with -p, so the environment gets its own\ncontainers, network and volumes.\n\nTwo things in docker-compose.yml blocked a second stack on the same Pi\nand are now variable, with the production values as defaults so the prod\ndeploy and the local tooling render byte-for-byte identically:\n\n- The fixed host ports 8080/6379/6080 are bound by the running prod\n  stack; rpi only allocates a port for the ingress service and appends\n  it, so a dev deploy would fail on the binds.\n- RP_ID/RP_NAME/PUBLIC_APP_URL/EXPECTED_ORIGIN were pinned to the\n  production hostname. WebAuthn scopes credentials to RP_ID and verifies\n  assertions against EXPECTED_ORIGIN, so on another hostname the gate\n  rejects every ceremony without failing the deploy.\n\n.env.dev carries the dev values and stays untracked, like .env;\n.env.dev.example documents it. infra.test.ts gains guards for the two\nsilent-failure modes: hostname/RP_ID drift, and dev host ports landing\non a prod bind or inside rpi''s 8000-8999 allocator range.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(widget-sdk): move the instance store into the shared widget helpers\n\nThe mechanism is not passport-specific: it follows from the board mounting a\nplaced widget twice while fullscreen is open. Spec and plan now put a generic\nmakeWidgetInstanceStore factory and useWidgetInstance hook in widget-sdk, with\nthe widget keeping only its typing and disposal policy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(widget-sdk): let reatom connection lifetime own the instance store\n\nMeasured against @reatom/core@1001.1.0: withConnectHook fires on the first\nsubscriber and its cleanup on the last, which is the reference counting the\nstore was hand-rolling. The React lease hook disappears entirely — a reatomMemo\ncomponent that reads the instance atom IS the reference.\n\nTwo findings the measurement pinned down: a computed''s cache survives\ndisconnection, so the disconnect hook must drop the handle or the next mount\nresurrects a disposed value; and StrictMode still has to go, because its remount\ncycle disposes a value while both components are still mounted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widgets): stop the client host build on widgets with no client.ts\n\ndiscoverWidgetIds (widget-build-assets.ts, used only by the client host''s\nstageWidgetBuilds plugin) required every packages/widgets/* directory with\na package.json to have a dist/, the same way scripts/codegen/client.ts\ndiscovers widget candidates. But codegen goes one step further and only\ntreats a widget as a federation remote once it has a client.ts -- exactly\nthe state passport-checker is in right now (browser-task subprojects only,\nits widget UI hasn''t landed on this branch yet). Never having a build\nscript, it never produces a dist/, so every client build broke with\nMissingWidgetBuildError, both locally and on the Pi during\n`rpi deploy --env dev`.\n\nFilter discoverWidgetIds by client.ts too, matching codegen''s criterion.\nAlso set dts: false on the client''s own federation() call (mirroring what\nevery widget''s own config already sets): the host build was unconditionally\nattempting Module Federation''s DTS generation, which -- since widgets are\nconsumed dynamically via loadRemote + the generated catalog, never through\na typed remote import -- had nothing to generate and always failed with a\ncaught-but-alarming \"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading\n''readFile'')\" on every build.\n\nAdd docs/** to both oxfmt''s and oxlint''s ignorePatterns: docs/ carries a\nlot of pre-formatted design/spec markdown and an embedded HTML mockup that\noxfmt doesn''t reflow the same way as their original authoring tools, so a\nroutine `pnpm format` run was rewriting ~18 unrelated files.\n\nAlso drop the leftover PASSPORT_SERIES/PASSPORT_NUMBER lines from\n.env.dev.example: the passport-checker widget reads its series/number from\nplain-value files under the widget package, not from env vars (see\npackages/browser-automation/README.md#provisioning-secrets), so document\nthat instead.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: record the shared widget modal primitive in BACKLOG.md\n\nuseModalIsolation is a hand-rolled modal owned by one widget, and its isolation\nleans on radix-ui internals that can change in a patch release without failing\nanything but our one deliberate stack test. Capture the follow-up, its trigger,\nand the two shapes worth considering instead of leaving it implicit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore(format): apply oxfmt to pre-existing drift\n\nThese 18 files predate this branch and were already failing\npnpm format:check on main. Formatting them here makes the branch\ngate readable.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* style(ofelia-poop-duty): wrap the over-width test declaration\n\nCommit 3eee2f2 on this branch added a timeout option that pushed the `it(...)`\ndeclaration past oxfmt''s print width, so `pnpm format:check` has been red on a\nbranch-authored file. This is the branch''s own drift, not the pre-existing debt\nthe previous commit cleared.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(widget-sdk): add a connection-scoped widget instance store\n\nThe board mounts a placed widget twice while fullscreen is open — once as a\ntile, once in the overlay — so per-mount useMemo state is built twice and lost\non every tier switch. Widgets can now share one value per instanceId, built for\nthe first subscriber and disposed when the last one disconnects.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): make instance-store disconnect race-safe\n\nSwitch the widget instance store from withDisconnectHook to\nwithConnectHook so build and dispose are paired per connect episode,\nmatching the clock-model.ts convention:\n\n- Guard the deferred disconnect cleanup with isConnected(handle) so an\n  unsubscribe immediately followed by a resubscribe of the same key in\n  one synchronous flush (React StrictMode''s double-invoked effects, a\n  same-commit re-key) no longer disposes a value a live mount is still\n  rendering.\n- Only delete the key''s map entry when it still points at the handle\n  running the cleanup, so a stale disconnect can no longer evict a\n  newer, currently-connected handle for the same key.\n- Rebuild the value in the connect hook''s setup too, so a genuine\n  reconnect after disposal gets a fresh value instead of resurrecting\n  a disposed one.\n- Correct the module JSDoc: the value is built lazily on first read\n  (unavoidable, since a component''s initial render reads before its\n  effect subscribes), not \"for the first subscriber\"; only disposal is\n  actually governed by the connection lifetime. Note that a bare read\n  with no subscribing component never triggers dispose.\n\nAdds a regression test reproducing the same-tick unsubscribe/\nresubscribe race directly against the atom''s subscribe API.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): close the orphaned-read leak and stop re-poisoning the instance map\n\nA bare store(key, make)() read outside any subscription built a value that\ndispose never ran for, and left a permanent handles entry: the compute\nfunction now gives an orphaned first build one microtask to be claimed by a\nreal subscriber before tearing it down. The connect hook also stopped\nre-registering its handle in the map on every connect, which was letting a\nstale handle reconnected directly (bypassing store()) repoint future lookups\nat an already-torn-down value; the identity-guarded delete already keeps a\nstale disconnect from evicting a newer handle, so the map only ever reflects\na handle that was actually looked up through the factory.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): drop the timer-based orphan-read teardown in instance-store\n\nThe round-2 fix scheduled a queueMicrotask() to dispose a value that no\nsubscriber had claimed yet, assuming a real mount''s useLayoutEffect\nsubscribe always lands in the same task as its render-phase read. React 19\ntime-slices Suspense retry lanes (every widget renders under lazy +\nSuspense) and resumes via a scheduler macrotask, so that assumption does\nnot hold: the microtask could fire and dispose a value a live mount was\nstill about to read, corrupting a dependency-free computed forever and\nsplitting the map entry across mounts of the same instance.\n\nDisposal is now driven ONLY by Reatom''s real connect/disconnect lifecycle,\nwhich is timing-safe by construction. The tradeoff, documented in the\nmodule doc comment: a bare read with no component ever mounting still\nleaks its built value, since nothing ever subscribes to disconnect it.\nNothing outside this store''s own test currently reads it that way.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): share one model graph across widget mounts\n\nThe board tile and the fullscreen overlay mount the same instance twice, so\nper-mount useMemo models lost every result on a tier switch. Read one shared\ninstance instead, and let only the non-fullscreen mount own the recovery modal.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(widget-sdk): pin the instance store''s StrictMode tolerance\n\nStrictMode''s development-only mount -> unmount -> mount cycle empties the\nsubscriber set, which an earlier draft of this store answered by disposing a\nvalue both mounted components were still rendering. The connect hook''s\nisConnected re-check closes that; this test is what keeps it closed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): collapse fullscreen for recovery and restore it after\n\nOpening recovery from the fullscreen mount now collapses the board''s Radix\ndialog through requestClose, so the modal is never stacked over a trapped\nFocusScope; closing or retrying restores fullscreen through requestFullscreen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): wrap the recovery-open handler in each tier''s own frame\n\nPassportChecker built openRecovery with wrap() once per its own render and\nhanded the same closure to StandardTier/TinyTier, but those tiers re-render\nindependently on their own viewState() changes. The closure stayed bound to\nPassportChecker''s stale render frame, so clicking \"Открыть восстановление\"\nafter an idle->pending->sessionRequired transition (without the parent also\nre-rendering) could silently do nothing -- the root cause of the ~10% flake\nin ui/recovery-flow.test.tsx. PassportChecker now builds a plain (unwrapped)\ncomposition function and each tier wraps it fresh in its own render, mirroring\nthe existing `check` handler pattern.\n\nAlso adds a UI test for the restoreFullscreen wiring''s actual cross-mount\nhandoff: recovery opened from a fullscreen mount, then the tile mount (same\ninstanceId) inherits restorePending and calls its own requestFullscreen on\nclose -- the one assertion that would have caught restoreFullscreen wired to\nthe wrong callback.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): widen recovery-flow.test.tsx''s test budget to stop whole-suite timeouts\n\nThe new fullscreen/tile recovery tests render through Radix''s dialog/focus\nmachinery across two model mounts, which occasionally pushed real wall-clock\ntime past the shared 5s default under whole-suite worker contention (never a\nlogic hang). Set testTimeout to 30000ms for this file only, verified against\nzero reproductions of the line-122 timeout across dozens of whole-suite runs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): root-cause the recovery-flow Escape-close race\n\nfindByRole(''dialog'') resolves as soon as the dialog''s DOM node commits,\nbut useModalIsolation''s mount effect - which attaches the Escape\nlistener and moves focus in - is a passive effect that can still be\npending a tick later. Firing Escape into that gap dispatched into a\ndocument with no listener yet, so the dialog never closed and the\nfollowing waitFor spun until it timed out; reproduced solo (no\nwhole-suite contention needed) in roughly 1 of 15-40 runs.\n\nEach of the two tests that close the modal now waits for the effect''s\nsynchronous side effect (focus landing inside the dialog) before\ndispatching Escape, which proves the same effect has also attached the\nlistener. Replaces the prior fix round''s blanket file-wide 30s test\ntimeout (and its comment''s now-falsified \"never a hang\" claim) with a\nscoped 10s per-test timeout on only the two tests that actually run\nthis round trip, as a safety margin for ordinary contention rather\nthan a mask for this race.\n\nVerified with 40 solo + 32 self-inflicted-parallel + 8 whole-suite\nreruns after the fix, zero reproductions of the timeout.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): hold focus inside the recovery modal\n\nRadix restores focus from a zero-delay timeout when its dialog unmounts, so\ncollapsing fullscreen for recovery handed focus to the board a tick after the\nmodal mounted. Pull focus back whenever it lands outside the modal.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the unmount-cleanup test teeth and fix a flaky Escape assert\n\nRecoveryModal.test.tsx''s `leaves focus alone once the modal is gone` test\npassed even when the cleanup''s `removeEventListener(''focusin'', ...)` was\ndeleted, or the whole effect cleanup was zeroed out: after unmount the\nportal root is detached, so a leaked focusin listener calling `.focus()` on\na detached descendant is a jsdom/browser no-op and the activeElement assert\nstays green regardless. Pin the actual cleanup contract instead: spy on\nwindow.addEventListener/removeEventListener and assert the exact `focusin`\nlistener useModalIsolation registers on mount is the one removed on unmount,\nalongside the original behavioral assert.\n\nrecovery-flow.test.tsx''s first test asserted\n`dialog.contains(document.activeElement)` synchronously right after\n`findByRole(''dialog'')` resolves on DOM commit, before dispatching Escape.\n`useModalIsolation`''s mount effect (which moves focus in and attaches the\nEscape listener) is passive and can still be pending a tick later, so\nEscape could fire before the listener existed and the test would hang out\nits timeout — the same race Task 4 root-caused and fixed in this file''s\nother two Escape-closing tests by waiting for focus-in-dialog first. Apply\nthe same `await waitFor(...)` wrap here and correct the file-level comment,\nwhich had claimed only two tests reach the Escape-close race when this one\ndoes too.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the modal focusout guard against narrowing\n\nMount the underlying Radix dialog in its own pass, as production does, and\ncover the intra-modal focus move that the proposed guard narrowing would have\nbroken silently. The regression test dispatches the underlying focusout event\ndirectly rather than chaining native .focus() calls: jsdom (like real\nbrowsers) always completes an outer .focus() call on its own target once its\nhandler stack unwinds, so a settled document.activeElement assertion can''t\nobserve a reclaim a nested handler only ever wins transiently — verified\nempirically against the narrowed guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): close a cross-test focus-restore leak in the Radix stack suite\n\nRadix''s FocusScope defers its unmount focus-restore to a real setTimeout(0),\nand testing-library''s auto cleanup() never waits for it, so a prior test''s\ndeferred restore (and an unawaited NoVncCanvas state update from that same\ntest''s async recovery flow) can bleed into the next test — confirmed by\ninstrumentation. A formal trace of the reentrant focus cascade shows the\n''pulls focus back...'' assertion itself can''t actually be flipped by this\n(no yield point exists between its synchronous .focus() call and its\nexpect()), but the leak is real and pre-existing to any suite that\nmounts+unmounts a trapped FocusScope, so close it defensively: flush one\nreal tick after each test''s cleanup so Radix''s deferred work always\nfinishes before the next test starts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): drain Radix''s focus-restore timer from beforeEach, not afterEach\n\ne797d50''s message overstated what that change achieved. It claimed the added\nafterEach ran \"strictly after cleanup()\" and closed the cross-test focus-restore\nleak. It did neither. Vitest resolves sequence.hooks to \"stack\", so same-suite\nafterEach hooks run in REVERSE registration order, and testing-library''s\ncleanup() -- registered first, at setupFiles import time -- therefore runs last.\nThe file-level afterEach ran BEFORE cleanup(), i.e. before the unmount that\nschedules Radix''s setTimeout(0) focus restore, so it drained nothing.\n\nInstrumenting setTimeout and the hook boundaries on this file shows it directly:\nat afterEach entry the modal is still in the DOM and no FocusScope timer is\npending; the timer is scheduled only after the hook returns; the next test''s\nbeforeEach starts with one still queued. Moving the same one-tick flush to\nbeforeEach takes that count to zero before the test body runs. beforeEach is\ncorrect by construction -- it always follows the previous test''s afterEach\nhooks whatever the hook-order mode.\n\nCorrect the scope of the claim too. The restore is focus-neutral in this\nfixture: the previouslyFocusedElement it captured is either document.body,\nwhich jsdom refuses to focus, or an already-detached node, and every observed\nfiring left document.activeElement unchanged. The intermittent failure this was\nchasing did not reproduce at e797d50 in 30 consecutive single-file runs or 6\nwhole-package runs. So this closes a scheduling leak, not an observed failure,\nand the comment now says exactly that instead of claiming otherwise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): link the tier-shared-state follow-up\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): drop the phantom rebuild from the instance store''s connect hook\n\nThe connect hook carried `current ??= { value: make() }` behind a comment\nclaiming it gave a reconnect after disposal a fresh value. Measured against\n@reatom/core@1001.1.0 with the shipped code:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 2\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"],[{\"id\":2},\"a\"]]   built = 2\n\nThe reconnect still returned the DISPOSED value — a dependency-free `computed`\nnever recomputes, so its cached result is what every later read gets. The line\nonly built a second model graph nobody could reach and then handed it to\n`dispose` a second time. Same run with the line removed:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n\nThe comment now states what is true: a stale reconnect reads the cached,\nalready-disposed value, and a fresh value comes only from a new `store()`\nlookup — which is what every real caller does, because it calls `store()`\nduring render and the cleanup has already evicted the stale handle.\n\nWith the phantom build gone, `if (held)` is what prevents the double dispose,\nso a test pins it: replacing it with an unconditional `dispose?.(held?.value,\nkey)` now fails with a second teardown recorded as `[undefined, \"a\"]`.\n\nAlso corrects the module doc''s cost claim for reading atoms inside `make`.\nIt does not \"silently rebuild the whole value\": measured, after such a\ndependency changes the compute function re-runs once, `current ??=`\nshort-circuits it so `make` does not re-run (built stayed 1, the identical\nobject was returned, no subscriber notified), and because the re-run never\nreads the atom again the edge is dropped. The cost is a pointless dependency\nedge, not lost state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): align the tier-shared-state spec with what shipped\n\nThree claims in the design spec described work that was never delivered:\n\n- The instance atom was said to carry `withDisconnectHook`. It carries\n  `withConnectHook`, whose cleanup calls `teardownIfDisconnected()` — which\n  re-checks `isConnected` before disposing and evicts the map entry only while\n  it still points at the handle being torn down.\n- Section 4 \"StrictMode removal\" claimed `<StrictMode>` was dropped from\n  `packages/client/src/app/main.tsx` and replaced by a comment explaining its\n  absence. None of it shipped: `git diff cfff8d2..HEAD -- packages/client/` is\n  empty and the wrapper is still there. Measurement showed the store survives\n  the remount cycle, because the cleanup''s `isConnected` re-check returns\n  without touching a value a live mount still holds. Section 4 is rewritten as\n  \"StrictMode kept, tolerance pinned by test\", stating why the plan''s original\n  reasoning does not apply.\n- The Testing gate, the Documentation list and the Decision Summary repeated\n  the removal. They now say StrictMode stays and that the tolerance is pinned\n  by `describe(''makeWidgetInstanceStore under StrictMode'')`.\n\nThat last claim is measured, not assumed: deleting the `isConnected` re-check\nfrom the cleanup turns two of the three StrictMode tests red, along with the\nnon-StrictMode same-tick-resubscribe test.\n\nThe plan file is left untouched — it is the historical record of what was\nintended.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): narrow unmeasured claims and close two coverage gaps\n\nComment/claim accuracy — all of these stated more than was ever measured:\n\n- `ui/recovery-flow.test.tsx`: the \"roughly 1 in 15-40 runs\" hang frequency\n  was applied to every Escape-closing test in the file. It was measured for\n  exactly one of them, ''does not collapse when recovery opens from the tile''\n  (task-4-report.md, Fix round 3). The others carry the same wait by\n  inspection — identical shape, identical race — with no observed rate. The\n  comment now says so.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"at `beforeEach` entry one (later\n  two) FocusScope timer is pending\" — only the 1 -> 0 transition was ever\n  recorded, so the \"(later two)\" is dropped.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"jsdom (like real browsers) always\n  completes an outer `.focus()` call on its own target\" — true and measured\n  for jsdom, never measured against a real browser. Scoped to jsdom.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: ''keeps focus inside when it moves\n  between two of our own controls'' promised a focus invariant the test does\n  not assert — it asserts that our guard swallows the `focusout` before it\n  reaches `document`. Renamed to say that.\n\nCoverage:\n\n- `model/instance-store.test.ts` (new) covers `dispose: (models) =>\n  models.recoveryModel.teardown()`, the safety net against leaking a live VNC\n  session when the modal outlived the widget. It had no test at all: replacing\n  the callback with a no-op left the whole widget suite green. Verified both\n  new tests fail against that no-op.\n- `ui/PassportChecker.test.tsx`: two assertions used\n  `expect(await screen.findAllBy*(…)).toHaveLength(2)`, which cannot retry —\n  `findAllBy*` resolves on the first snapshot with >= 1 match, so it settles on\n  one mount and never waits for the second. Both are now a re-querying\n  `waitFor`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(backlog): record the two-modal focus ping-pong as a deferred defect\n\nBoth `useModalIsolation` instances register focus containment on `window` in\nthe capture phase, so two mounted modals each see the other''s `focusin` before\nthe root-level `stopPropagation()` can stop it, and each pulls focus into its\nown root — forever.\n\nReproduced in jsdom 29.1.1 with two `useModalIsolation` roots mounted at once:\none modal performs 1 focus pull on mount; two mounted together never settle,\nand a hard test guard at 400 pulls had to stop the run (402 recorded).\n\nDeferred rather than fixed because it is unreachable through the UI: the\npassport checker is the only owner of such a modal, renders at most one\n(non-fullscreen mount only), and its overlay is a full-viewport\n`pointer-events: auto` layer whose `pointerdown` handler closes the modal, so\na second one cannot be opened over the first.\n\nThe entry notes the likely fix — a module-level stack of active modal roots\nwith containment applied only by the topmost.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): correct five unverified claims left by code review\n\nBring wording in the tier-shared-state spec, BACKLOG.md, and a test\ncomment in line with what was actually measured/read: the instance\nstore''s dependency-edge cost (not a rebuilt value), a stale\nuse-modal-isolation.ts line citation (:53 -> :55, two places), the\nroot focusin stopper''s actual bubble phase (not capture), and which\nrecovery-flow tests keep the shared timeout default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay at this branch\n\nPins rpi.dev.toml''s source.branch to feat/passport-checker-widget so the dev\nenvironment runs this PR instead of the dev branch.\n\nREVERT BEFORE MERGING: once this PR lands on dev, source.branch must go back to\n\"dev\" or the dev overlay stays pinned to a branch that no longer moves.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): drop the stale Chromium singleton lock before launch\n\nThe Chromium profile is a named Docker volume, so `SingletonLock` keeps the\n`<hostname>-<pid>` of whichever container wrote it. After a redeploy the new\ncontainer has a new hostname, Chromium reads a lock owned by \"another computer\"\nit cannot probe, and `launchPersistentContext` fails on every task until the\nvolume is touched by hand.\n\nRemove `SingletonLock`, `SingletonCookie`, and `SingletonSocket` before each\nlaunch. `fs.rm` unlinks the symlinks themselves instead of following them, and\n`force` keeps a fresh profile from failing; a removal that does fail is returned\nas a value, logged, and the launch still proceeds. Unconditional removal is safe\nbecause `getPersistentContext` is the only caller of `launch` and the image\nentrypoint runs a single `node dist/index.cjs` - no check for a live Chromium is\nperformed or implied.\n\nAlso asserts that a failed launch keeps its cause, which is what makes the\nPlaywright message reachable from the service log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): log the error behind an internal task failure\n\nInternal failures were logged as `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` only, so the\ntagged error and its cause chain never reached the container log. A Chromium\nlaunch failure therefore showed up as a bare `internal` code with nothing to\ndiagnose. Add the error to the logged fields; `toEnvelopeError` still answers\nthe client with the redacted `internal` code, so only the log gains detail.\n\nNote the trade-off: a handler error whose message carries task data now reaches\nthe container log, which the previous field list deliberately avoided.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): redact handler failures in the internal task log\n\nfa87a47c logged too much. It added `error: outcome` to the internal-failure log\nso a Chromium launch failure would stop being invisible, but that field carries\nwhatever the handler threw. errore''s `toJSON()` serialises `message`, `stack`,\nand the whole `cause` chain, Playwright quotes `page.evaluate` arguments into\nits messages, and the passport widget passes the secret identity as one — so a\nhandler failure could print the passport series and number into the container\nlog. The wrapper''s own `stack` repeats the cause under `Caused by:`, so the\nidentity landed there twice.\n\nThat contradicts the design spec: raw Playwright, network, Zod, and upstream\nresponse details are logged only after redaction, request and response bodies\nare never logged, and logs redact task payloads and secret values\n(docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md\n:350-353, :684).\n\nThe rule now, verified against dispatch.ts:\n\n- `BrowserExecutorError` wraps a failed `executor.acquire(...)`. Acquire runs\n  before the handler and is passed only `(signal, widgetId)` — never the payload\n  or the widget secrets — so its cause chain is Chromium/Playwright launch\n  detail. Still logged in full; this is the stale singleton lock failure\n  fa87a47c existed to surface.\n- Every other internal failure wraps handler output and is reduced to\n  `errorChain`: the names/tags of the error and its `cause` chain and nothing\n  else. No `message`, no `stack`, no foreign properties.\n\nThe log message, the `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` fields, and the\n`code === ''internal''` condition are unchanged.\n\nTests split the single fa87a47c case in two: one pins that an executor failure''s\ncause chain still reaches the log, one asserts a secret-looking cause string is\nabsent from the serialised logged payload. Both were confirmed to fail before\nthe fix and to discriminate after it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add an env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe passport widget''s recovery path (retain the page, issue a single-use\ncapability, stream the live browser over noVNC, let a human solve the\nchallenge, retry) has never been exercised on real hardware, because it only\ntriggers when pasport.org.ua serves a Cloudflare challenge and none has been\nencountered — the smoke test on the Pi returned a normal result. Subproject 8\n(`passport-checker-rpi-integration` in\ndocs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md)\nhas \"embedded noVNC Cloudflare recovery, SSH fallback, and real checker smoke\ntest on the Raspberry Pi\" as an acceptance item and had no way to reach it on\ndemand. PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY is that way.\n\nContract: `1` and nothing else enables it — absent, empty, `0`, `true`, ` 1 `\nare all off (isForcedRecoveryEnabled does exact string equality, no trimming,\nno truthiness coercion). Compose passes it through with a `:-` default so an\nunset variable stays off, and it must never be set in production.\n\nSemantics: at most once per process. The latch lives in the closure\nmakePassportCheckHandler returns, so each handler owns its own and the unit\ntests stay isolated. It fires after a successful goto and after\ncollectNavigationEvidence — the retained page is the real checker page with the\nsite''s own UI loaded, so a human can drive it over noVNC — and returns exactly\nthe retainPageForRecovery() + BrowserSessionRequiredError pair the genuine\nCloudflare branch returns, making the downstream flow byte-identical to a real\nchallenge. The latch is consumed only when the forced path actually fires, so a\nfailed navigation returns its own error untouched and leaves the lever armed:\nan infrastructure hiccup must not burn the single forced run an operator is\nwaiting on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): apply the design-system widget redesign\n\nBring the tile and the recovery modal in line with Паспорт-чекер.dc.html.\n\nBoth tiers now lay out as header -> flexible middle -> action, so the\naction sits on the tile''s bottom edge in every state instead of trailing\nwhatever content happens to be above it. Status banners pick up the\ndesign''s per-state hues, the filled success disc, and the CircleAlert /\nTriangleAlert split that separates a retryable failure from a session\nprompt. The modal gains the access-pill dot, a pulsing LIVE dot, framed\nconnecting/disconnected/expired states, and the shell-style SSH block.\n\nDark theme was effectively missing: the --pc-* hues chained to\n--success-soft / --destructive-soft, which the host token set never\ndefines, and to --success, which is a dark surface green rather than an\nicon green under [data-theme=''dark'']. Every hue is now declared per\ntheme the way ofelia-poop-duty does it. The modal portals to\ndocument.body, outside .widget, so it declares the one hue it needs on\nits own overlay.\n\nThree deliberate departures from the mockup:\n- invalidConfig copy stays server-side (\"Обратитесь к администратору\"),\n  since this widget has no per-instance settings — its config lives in\n  server secrets.\n- the expired frame keeps \"Переподключиться\"; without it an expired\n  capability can only be recovered by closing and reopening the modal.\n- the sessionRequired tiny tile only fills amber, no border: the tile\n  border belongs to the host card, and drawing one from inside the\n  widget would render a second, inset contour.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xwGNctwqXcYi5p4QSSFhA\n\n* docs(browser-automation): design widget user-input detection\n\nMove the \"a human must act\" mechanism out of passport-checker and into the\nautomation platform. The handler gets context.detectUserInput(detector), which\nruns the detector, optionally prepares the page, retains it and returns the\ncanonical error as one indivisible step; retainPageForRecovery leaves the public\ncontext so the halves cannot be separated. Cloudflare becomes a reusable helper\n(probe and predicate together, not just the predicate) that any widget can pass\nin. Also records the removal of the PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY lever.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): correct the user-input spec''s test-seam claims\n\nmakeFakeExecutor fakes BrowserExecutor<FakeContext> with FakeContext = { id,\nsignal }; it never builds a BrowserTaskContext, so it cannot host a recording\ndetectUserInput and needs no change at all. Name the seams that do exist: the\nsix retainPageForRecovery call sites in chromium-executor.test.ts and the three\nBrowserTaskContext literals in diagnostics.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): plan the widget user-input detection refactor\n\nSeven tasks, each green on its own: retire the forced-recovery lever, add the\ndetector and error contracts, move Cloudflare detection into the platform with\nits probe reunited with its predicate, run escalation from the task context,\nrewire the widget, then withdraw retainPageForRecovery from the public context so\nthe retain/escalate pair cannot be taken apart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): make the Cloudflare task a move, not a copy\n\nTask 3 previously left the classifier duplicated between the widget and the\nplatform until Task 5, which any reviewer would rightly flag as verbatim\nduplication of a logic block. The widget now switches to the moved module in the\nsame task, so the logic never exists in two places.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* revert(passport-checker): drop the env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe lever forced one trip through the Cloudflare recovery path per service\nstart so Subproject 8 could exercise noVNC on the Pi on demand. It is being\nretired deliberately ahead of moving user-input detection into the platform;\nthe Pi smoke test needs another answer.\n\nThis reverts the behaviour added in 45c604f1.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(browser-automation): add user-input detector and error contracts\n\nUserInputRequiredError carries the browser_session_required code and sshTarget\nmeta the recovery flow keys off, moved out of the passport-checker widget where\na platform contract had no business living. UserInputProbeError distinguishes\na broken check from a negative one.\n\n* feat(browser-automation): move Cloudflare detection into the platform\n\nThe classifier lived in the passport-checker widget while the DOM probe feeding\nit lived in that widget''s check handler, so the only reusable half was the half\nwithout the probe. Both now sit in browser-automation behind two detector\nfactories: one that probes the live page, one that classifies evidence already\nderived inside the page from a fetch response.\n\nThe widget imports the moved module rather than keeping a copy; it still calls\nthe classifier by hand, which the next commit changes.\n\n* @\ndocs: document the feature branch -> dev -> main workflow\n\nFeature work happens in a dedicated branch inside a ./.worktrees checkout,\nlands in dev through a PR, gets verified on the dev stack, and only then\nreaches main through a dev -> main PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* feat(browser-automation): run user-input detection from the task context\n\ndetectUserInput runs a widget-supplied detector and, on a match, prepares the\npage, retains it and returns UserInputRequiredError as one step, so the two\nhalves cannot drift apart. A detector that fails yields UserInputProbeError and\ndeliberately leaves the page unretained: an undecidable check is not a challenge,\nand pinning a browser page for a recovery nobody will perform is worse.\n\nAUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET moves into the service config, where an unusable value\ndegrades to null rather than failing config loading and killing the service.\n\n* docs(passport-checker): design last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* test(browser-automation): cover the prepare-before-retain order in detectUserInput directly\n\nThe executor-level test asserted hasRetainedPage() from inside the prepare\nmock, but that bookkeeping is only populated at release() time, so it passed\nregardless of call order. Add a direct unit test against makeDetectUserInput\nthat records prepare/retain into one ordered array — genuinely fails if the\norder in detect.ts is inverted (verified by temporarily inverting it).\n\nNo production behavior changes; detect.ts ends identical to before this commit.\n\n* @\ndocs: merge AGENTS.md into CLAUDE.md and symlink it\n\nAGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md had drifted into two partly overlapping rule sets.\nEverything unique to AGENTS.md (project structure, codegen commands, Windows\ntest-runner notes, coding style, UI gotchas, testing, security, failure modes)\nnow lives in CLAUDE.md, and AGENTS.md becomes a symlink so every agent reads\nthe same file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* docs(passport-checker): plan last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* feat(passport-checker): date-aware check timestamp label\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout design\n\nTwo width breakpoints instead of one hardcoded grid: one column below 768,\ntwelve above, with row height and margin scaled by the same factor as the\ncolumn width so the desktop grid zooms instead of stretching on one axis.\n\nThe mobile layout is derived from the desktop one and only persisted once the\nuser drags or resizes, stored as an additive optional `mobileLayout` field so\nno migration of `root:boards` is needed.\n\nAlso records the react-draggable touchstart defect: the whole card is the drag\nhandle today, which cancels page scrolling on touch — harmless on a short\n12-column board, blocking at one column.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): detect required user input through the platform\n\nThe handler no longer collects Cloudflare evidence, classifies it, retains the\npage and builds the escalation error by hand at every site; it passes a detector\nto context.detectUserInput and returns what comes back. browser/challenge.ts and\nthe widget-local BrowserSessionRequiredError are gone — the classifier now lives\nin browser-automation next to the probe that feeds it, and the\nbrowser_session_required contract belongs to the platform that owns the recovery\nflow.\n\n* fix(passport-checker): restore the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nAn automated amend from a concurrent session accidentally stripped this\nline out of 3a12494e. It belongs to the user-input-detection work; this\nrestores it verbatim.\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nRETRYABLE_MESSAGES.user_input_probe vanished twice with no test turning red,\nbecause nothing asserted the actual behaviour: with the entry gone,\nmapCheckError silently falls back to GENERIC_RETRYABLE_MESSAGE and the user\nsees the generic string instead of the specific one. Assert the real output\nof mapCheckError against a hardcoded expected string, not against the table\nitself, so a future deletion or typo of the entry fails this test instead of\ncomparing the map against itself.\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron design spec\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout implementation plan\n\n* feat(board): resolve grid metrics from the container width\n\n* feat(passport-checker): persist the last successful check\n\n* feat(board): derive and reconcile the one-column mobile layout\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron implementation plan\n\nAlso corrects the spec: the jsdom Temporal shims must stay, only the\n--harmony-temporal execArgv becomes redundant on Node 26.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin which detector goes to which detectUserInput call\n\nNothing previously checked that the navigation check gets the page detector\nand the submission check gets the evidence detector -- only the second call''s\nprepare option was asserted. A handler that swapped the two factories would\nleave every test in this file green; only the gated real-browser suite would\nnotice, and it isn''t run in CI. Pull the captured detector off each mock call\nand invoke it directly: the page detector must reach for page.evaluate, the\nevidence detector must ignore its page argument and classify the real\nevidence. Also drops the now-inert hasChallengeForm evidence field from the\nsubmission-escalation test, whose result was always driven by the mock''s\nescalation queue regardless of that field.\n\n* feat(board): add mobile layout materialize, update and reset actions\n\n* refactor(browser-automation): expose only detectUserInput on the task context\n\nretainPageForRecovery was half of a pair a handler had to assemble correctly by\nhand: retaining without escalating strands a browser page, escalating without\nretaining hands the UI a recovery session with nothing behind it. With the retain\nflag reachable only through detectUserInput, neither mistake is expressible.\n\n* test(passport-checker): cover restore-on-mount and cross-placement sync\n\nAdd two widget-level cases pinning storage-backed persistence through\nReact: a fresh mount renders a previously stored result without\ninvoking a check, and a check made in one placement is observed by a\nsecond placement sharing the same storage. Rename the pre-existing\n\"gives separate instance ids separate state\" case to \"gives separate\nstorages separate state\" (it isolates by storage, not instanceId) and\nupdate its comment to point at the new cross-placement case below it.\nPin PASSPORT_LAST_RESULT_KEY''s literal value in check-model.test.ts so\na silent rename cannot slip through green while orphaning deployed\nusers'' stored data under the old key.\n\n* feat(board): switch the grid to resolved metrics and a mobile drag grip\n\n* docs(browser-automation): document how a widget requests manual input\n\n* feat(board): offer a mobile layout reset in the board schema popover\n\n* fix(widgets): address passport-checker persistence review findings\n\n- TinyTier''s success chip now shows checkedAtLabel, so a restored (possibly\n  stale) result reads honestly in the compact tile too, not just standard.\n- Harden the two-placement isolation test: assert placement two stays idle\n  instead of a length-1 findAllByText that can pass mid-race.\n- Clarify why lastResult/transient set order is safe in checkPassport\n  (Reatom notifies after the sync block, not \"order for readability\").\n- Add a test exercising lastResultSchema.safeParse over the exact shape\n  checkPassport writes, since createFakeStorage skips schema validation.\n- Document lastResultSchema as a persistence contract: new fields must be\n  optional/defaulted or every stored value fails validation on next deploy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* fix(passport-checker): remove dead AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET threading\n\nrecoverySshTarget was never read by check.ts''s handler after the\ndetectUserInput refactor moved sshTarget sourcing entirely into the\nplatform config. Delete the widget''s leftover env read and option\nthreading so the README''s \"widgets neither read nor pass it\" claim is\nactually true, and drop the now-meaningless SSH-sanitization test in\nfavor of the equivalent (and broader) coverage in browser-automation''s\nconfig.test.ts.\n\n* docs(ofelia): design the authored ledger and comments\n\nRecords get the authenticated account instead of the per-device \"Я:\"\ntoggle, the write path moves into the widget''s own server.ts, and the\nhistory row is rebuilt: human dates, plain-language phrases, explicit\ndebt effect, visible supersession, no IP.\n\nPulls the Node 26 prerequisite out of the widget-server-cron spec, since\nthe shared domain module it enables is needed here first.\n\nAlso imports the Claude Design workspace under design/ (documents only,\nscreenshots and uploads stripped) and keeps the brief that produced the\nOfelia mock.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs(ofelia): note the cron plan overlap and landing order\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): cover the mobile layout and touch-scroll behaviour end to end\n\n* fix(board): keep the measured grid container mounted\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a 1920 viewport\n\n* docs(ofelia): add the authored ledger implementation plan\n\n15 tasks: Node 26, the pure domain split, the members directory, widget\nidentity, the server-resolved viewer, record schemas, draft construction,\nthe widget server definition, the model rewire, the history and comment\nview models, the account avatar, and the two rebuilt panels.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): wait for the grid relayout to settle before measuring\n\n* fix(browser-automation): bundle workspace imports instead of externalizing them\n\nThe rspack externals predicate was an allow-list whose default was\n\"externalize\". When the user-input refactor introduced the first runtime\nbare-specifier import of a workspace package into the service module graph\n(browser-automation/user-input/cloudflare, from the passport-checker browser\ntask), it was silently externalized: the build stayed green, all tests stayed\ngreen, and the Cloudflare module was simply absent from dist/index.cjs. The\nruntime stage copies only package.json and dist/, so Node resolved the\nspecifier through the exports map to a .ts path that does not exist in the\nimage and the container died on `exec node dist/index.cjs`.\n\nInvert the predicate into a deny-list derived from the package''s own\ndependencies (minus errore, which is ESM-only and must be bundled) plus Node\nbuiltins. An unrecognised bare specifier is now bundled, so the same mistake\nsurfaces as a build-time resolution error rather than a startup crash, and\nadding a real runtime dependency externalizes it with no config edit.\n\nAdd scripts/check-bundle.ts, chained onto `build` so it runs inside the image\nbuild the Dockerfile already performs. It asserts that every require() in the\nbundle is a builtin or a declared dependency, that the scan still matches the\nemitted shape at all, and that the compiled evidenceFromResponseText the widget\nsplices into Chromium still evaluates and runs in a bare vm realm. That last\ncheck is what the BROWSER_IT suite could never prove: it runs under vitest,\nwhere the specifier resolves to .ts through esbuild and the swc path is never\nexercised. Verified: the compiled form is ES5-lowered but self-contained --\ndestructuring became inline property reads, no swc helpers, no hoisted regexes.\n\nAlso:\n- detect.ts: tag the prepare outcome instead of comparing against null, so a\n  prepare that rejects with exactly null is a failure rather than a silent\n  success, matching the detector branch''s `typeof detected !== ''boolean''`.\n- user-input/errors.test.ts: cover toEnvelopeError for UserInputRequiredError\n  with and without an ssh target, pinning with toStrictEqual that `meta` is\n  omitted rather than set to undefined. contracts.test.ts already claimed this\n  coverage lived here.\n- chromium-executor.test.ts: drop an assertion inside a prepare callback that\n  could not fail the test and was vacuous, and rename the case to what it\n  actually verifies; the ordering contract is owned by detect.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* test(board): settle past the grid transition and stop assuming card order\n\n* test(board): correct the stale comment above the breakpoint wait\n\n* chore(node): align every runtime and image on Node 26\n\nnode:26-alpine and node:26-bookworm-slim no longer bundle corepack\n(removed from Node core), so `npm install -g corepack@latest` is added\nahead of every `corepack enable` in the three Dockerfiles and\ndocker-compose.dev.yml. scripts/infra.test.ts''s assertion on the\nbrowser-automation Dockerfile''s base image is updated to match.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(node): unshadow jsdom localStorage and bump the e2e image to Node 26\n\nNode 26 ships its own unflagged global localStorage/sessionStorage (Web\nStorage API), which resolves to undefined without --localstorage-file\nand shadows jsdom''s window.localStorage for bare-identifier reads in\npackages/client''s jsdom test environment (40 failing tests). Disable\nNode''s own globals via execArgv: [''--no-experimental-webstorage''] in\npackages/client/vite.config.ts so jsdom''s storage wins; verified via\nObject.getOwnPropertyDescriptor that the flag actually removes the\nglobal rather than just suppressing the warning. widget-runtime and\nwidget-sdk don''t touch localStorage in tests today, so the flag is not\nadded to their execArgv arrays (narrowest correct remedy).\n\nAlso bumps packages/client/e2e.Dockerfile (used by pnpm test:e2e:docker\nvia docker-compose.e2e.yml) from node:22-bookworm-slim to\nnode:26-bookworm-slim, missed by the original brief''s Files list, with\nthe same corepack@latest install this Node 26 base image now needs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): make the relayout wait actually poll\n\n* refactor(ofelia): extract the pure duty domain out of the Reatom model\n\n* fix(lint): make the widget domain import barrier a real allowlist\n\nThe regex denylist let ''../model/ofelia-duty'' through — domain/ and model/\nare siblings, so a single-''../'' import was both the most likely accidental\nescape and completely unguarded. It also only enumerated known packages, so\nany new npm dependency passed silently.\n\nReplace it with a gitignore-style group allowlist (''**'' minus zod, @shared/*\nand ''./'' siblings), which oxlint evaluates natively. A negative-lookahead\nregex was tried first and rejected: oxlint''s Rust regex engine has no\nlookaround and silently drops such a pattern, disabling the whole barrier.\n\nDomain tests get a second, later override that additionally allows vitest;\neverything else stays blocked there too.\n\n* chore(lint): keep the design mock exports out of lint and format\n\ndesign/ holds vendored Claude Design exports (support.js, *.dc.html)\nthat break the repo''s only gate: oxlint reports 9 errors in\ndesign/support.js, and oxfmt aborts (exit 2) on non-standard markup\nin design/Design System.dc.html before it can check anything else.\n\nAdd design/** to the existing ignorePatterns glob array in both\n.oxlintrc.json and .oxfmtrc.json, the same mechanism already used for\ndocs/**. This is a plain glob matcher (not the Rust regex engine\nbehind no-restricted-imports), so it isn''t subject to that engine''s\nlookaround gap, and it leaves every other rule/override untouched.\n\nFixing the oxfmt abort also uncovered pre-existing, unrelated\nformatting drift in CLAUDE.md, package.json and\nscripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts that the crash had been masking from\nthe format:check summary. Reformatted them (whitespace/ordering only,\nno logic changes) so pnpm format:check is actually green end to end.\n\n* feat(auth): expose the board members directory\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): expose the board identity to widgets\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): stop identity fetch from leaking an unhandled rejection and a discarded error\n\nHandle the AbortError that wrap()''s promise rejects with when the last\nwidget reading identity unmounts mid-fetch, and stop discarding\nBoardMembersError whole: a 401 stays silent (expected on an\nunauthenticated host), everything else is surfaced via console.warn so\nschema drift or a server fault is not silently swallowed.\n\n* feat(widgets): resolve the signed-in viewer for widget server events\n\n* feat(ofelia): carry the authoring account on ledger and comment records\n\n* feat(ofelia): move draft construction and event schemas into the domain\n\n* feat(ofelia): write the ledger and comments from the widget server\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): harden server date parsing and unforgeability coverage\n\n- Wrap Temporal.PlainDate.from in errore.try so a calendar-invalid but\n  regex-valid date (e.g. 2026-02-30) returns an Error instead of\n  throwing through a function typed Error | T.\n- Add regression coverage: forged createdBy/actor in the payload is\n  ignored, the debt handler is exercised, storage-read failures are\n  asserted by identity with no write, and the invalid-date case is\n  asserted as a value.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): split the unforgeability guard into schema and handler halves\n\nThe round-1 forged-payload test went through run(), which parses the\npayload via zod''s default strip mode before the handler ever sees it —\nso it only proved the schema strips forged fields, twice over, and\nwould not catch a handler that starts spreading its raw payload into\nthe draft. Replace it with two schema-level tests (clean + comment\npayloads drop a forged createdBy/actor) and one handler-level test\nthat calls ofeliaServer.handlers.clean directly, bypassing the schema,\nto prove the handler itself never reads those fields even if they\nreach it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* feat(ofelia): drive ledger and comment writes through widget server events\n\nThe client no longer appends to the ledger or the comment key. All four\nday actions collapse into one `invokeDay` that sends `{ date }` to the\nmatching widget-server event, and `send` sends `{ weekStart, text }` —\nwho the actor is, whose debt moves, whether the event applies at all and\nwhich account signed it are now derived and stamped server-side, so a\nclient cannot forge authorship.\n\n`selectedDate()` and `today()` (and `viewWeekStart()`) are read before\nthe first await so they resolve against the widget''s own Reatom context.\n\nThe per-device \"Я: Леша/Карина\" toggle is deleted rather than hidden:\nauthorship comes from the session, so there is nothing left to pick.\n`currentUser` is gone from the duty model, the widget context, the\nfixture and the view-model''s `onSetUser`.\n\nBoth model factories now take `api` and `identity`; `identity` is the\ndirectory the history and comment views read in the follow-up tasks.\nThe view projections drop `ip`/`by`, whose schema fields Tasks 5-8\nremoved, and `authorName` falls back through `createdBy.name`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* style: format the ofelia server test\n\n* feat(ofelia): group history by duty day and mark superseded records\n\n* feat(ofelia): resolve comment authors through the members directory\n\n* fix(ofelia): flush the comment thread''s connect hook via vi.waitFor, not a bare microtask\n\nReview round 1: the timing fix for the comment-thread subscribe/emit race\nassumed a single-microtask flush of Reatom''s connect-hook queue. Match the\nsibling comments-subscription tests in the same file instead — wait for the\nstorage mock''s subscribe call and wrap the whole body in context.start, so\nthe assertion is condition-based rather than scheduling-internals-based.\n\n* feat(ofelia): add the account avatar and its palette\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the history list around duty-day groups\n\n* fix(ofelia): announce the history phrase subject to assistive tech\n\nThe duty circle is the sentence subject in the rebuilt history phrase, and\nAvatar is aria-hidden, so the column announced as bare verbs with no subject.\nPair every circle with a visually hidden name, attribute the debt pill to its\nperson, and separate the chips with real spaces as the mock does.\n\nAlso correct the text-decoration comment to name the real mechanism (atomic\ninline, not descendant cancellation) and cover the three untested branches.\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the comment thread around account authors\n\n* fix(ofelia): restore composer coverage and mute the unknown comment author\n\n* fix(ofelia): break ledger day ties by append order\n\nRouting the writes through the widget server changed where `ts` comes from.\n`/api/storage/:key/append` stamped `Date.now()`, so consecutive appends always\ngot a strictly increasing timestamp. The widget dispatch stamps the app''s\ninjectable `now()`, and the e2e harness pins that clock — so a clean-then-undo\npair on the same day produced two entries with an identical `ts`, and\n`latestOutcomesByDate`''s strict `>` kept the earlier `cleaned` entry as the\nday''s outcome. The `reset` was written but never counted, and the day never\nreopened; `pnpm test:e2e:docker` caught it as a failing \"undo — returns the day\nto pending\".\n\nThe ledger is append-only and iterated in insertion order, so `>=` is the\ncorrect tie-break: on an equal timestamp the later-appended entry is the newer\ndecision. This also hardens production, where two writes can still land in the\nsame millisecond.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: describe server-authored widget records\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): fail widget dispatch when the session account is unreadable\n\nresolveWidgetViewer folded every getAccount failure into `null`, so a Valkey\nfault, a corrupt account record and a deleted account all produced a record\nstamped `createdBy: null` plus a `{ ok: true }` response. The ledger is\nappend-only, so that authorship could never be recovered.\n\nThe three outcomes are now distinct: no session still returns `null`, which is\nwhat the standalone harnesses and the e2e stack (no nginx, so no session cookie)\nrely on to keep writing unattributed records; a failed lookup returns a\nWidgetViewerLookupError, which the dispatch route turns into a fault instead of\na false success.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): order superseded history records by append order\n\n`superseded` sorted on `right.ts - left.ts`, which returns 0 on a tie; the\nstable sort then left tied records in append order, oldest first, contradicting\nthe \"newest first\" contract on HistoryDayGroup. Ties became a supported case\nwhen latestOutcomesByDate started breaking them with `>=`, and the dockerized\ne2e stack freezes the clock, so there every record ties.\n\nThe ledger is append-only, so append order is chronological order: reversing the\nday''s bucket is both correct and cheaper than sorting.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): warn when listAccounts skips an unreadable record\n\nA corrupt or schema-mismatched account record was dropped silently, so the\nroster simply came back short and the symptom read as \"an account vanished\"\nrather than as the data corruption it is. Name the key in a console.warn.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* build: pin corepack to 0.35.0 across images and dev compose\n\n`npm install -g corepack@latest` ran in five image build stages and four dev\ncompose services. There is no CI and the Pi deploy is manual with a 30-minute\nbuild timeout, so a breaking corepack release would only ever surface as a\nfailed deploy. Pin one explicit version everywhere.\n\nVerified: `docker build -f packages/server/Dockerfile` succeeds and the built\nimage reports corepack 0.35.0 with pnpm 10.28.2.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* refactor(ofelia): delete the dead short-date formatter\n\nformatDateShort and MONTHS_SHORT lost their last production consumer when the\ncomment thread moved to its own formatter; only the test still called them. It\nalso formatted through `new Date(ts)` in the local timezone, while every current\nformatter goes through plainDateIn(DUTY_TIME_ZONE, ...).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): drop the tier guards that cannot fail, hoist the env directive\n\nStandardTier and CompactTier each asserted they do not render UserToggle. The\ntoggle only ever lived in RichLayout and the component is now deleted outright,\nso neither assertion could ever fail — they only looked like coverage.\n\nAlso move the `@vitest-environment node` directive in server.test.ts back to\nline 1, where a reader expects it and where model/history-view.test.ts keeps it.\nVitest 4 matches the directive anywhere in the file content, so the file was\nalready running under node; this is readability, not a behaviour fix.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: state the authorship guarantee the code actually provides\n\nCLAUDE.md claimed a server-stamped author \"cannot be forged\". Nothing enforces\nthat: POST /api/storage/:key/append does no session check in Node, and nginx''s\nauth_request only proves a session exists, not whose it is, so any signed-in\nhousehold member can still hand-write a record carrying an arbitrary author.\n\nState what is true instead — the UI can no longer sign a record with someone\nelse''s name, and the generic storage route remains a shared trusted channel\ninside the board. Closing that route is a separate task.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(board): never render the desktop grid below its 1920 baseline\n\nThe desktop zoom is now one-directional: raise the lower clamp on the\ngrid scale from 0.75 to 1, so every container width between the mobile\nbreakpoint and BASE_WIDTH resolves to exactly the historical metrics\n(rowHeight: 30, margin: [10, 10]) and only wider displays scale up.\n\nUntil this branch, useContainerWidth never attached its observer and\npinned the reported width at its 1280 default on every screen. With the\ncontainer measured for real, a 0.75 floor would have shrunk every board\non a 1280/1366/1440 laptop below the size it has always had, dropping an\nh:6 card from 230px to 172.5px and pushing widgets under their own tier\nthresholds. Nothing shrinks now; the 4K aspect drift the scale was added\nfor is still fixed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling record\n\nBring the design doc in line with what shipped: the lower clamp is 1,\nthe 1280 table row shows the baseline metrics, and the paragraph\njustifying the clamp says plainly that it is active across the whole\n768-1920 band rather than a narrow 768-1100 one.\n\nAlso correct the claim that existing 1080p boards \"do not shift by a\npixel\". The formula at 1920 does reproduce the old constants, but the\nmeasured width was previously pinned at 1280 on every screen by the\nuseContainerWidth bug this branch fixes, so wider screens legitimately\nre-flow to use the whole window.\n\nDrop the 1920 viewport pin from ofelia-duty.spec.ts: it only worked\naround the 0.75 clamp, and removing it restores default-viewport\ncoverage. Recompute the footprint arithmetic in the ofelia client test\ncomment, which assumed a container of exactly 1280 rather than the\nviewport minus the board padding and the scrollbar gutter.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a viewport its tier needs\n\nEvery assertion in the duty spec is StandardTier UI, and the widget only\nresolves `standard` at a frame width of 400px or more. Playwright''s\ndefault 1280 viewport is about 7px short: the board measures its own\ncontainer, which is 1225px there once the stable scrollbar gutter and the\nboard''s 20px padding on each side come off, so a w:4 card is ~395px and\nits frame ~393px. `compact` is the correct rendering at that width, so\nthe spec has to declare the width its assertions need.\n\nThe dependency was previously invisible because the board reported a\nhardcoded 1280px container on every screen, which made the card 413px and\nleft the threshold only 13px of headroom against a measurement that was\nwrong by 55px.\n\nCorrect the footprint comment in the widget''s client test to match: the\ntier is measured on the frame inside the card''s 1px border, so a 1280px\nviewport yields ~393x228 and resolves to `compact`, not `standard`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling claims to match what ships\n\nThree statements asserted the opposite of what this branch established,\nall written before the width axis was measured.\n\nThe grid-metrics JSDoc claimed the clamp keeps 1280/1366/1440 boards\n\"the size they have always been\" and widgets \"above their own tier\nthresholds\". Both are false at 1280: the container is 1225 there, so a\nw:4 card is 395px where the old code drew 413px, and ofelia falls under\nits 400px standard threshold into compact. The clamp pins the metrics -\nrowHeight 30 and margin [10, 10] at every desktop width - not the\nrendered geometry, which still follows the measured container.\n\nThe design doc claimed \"no board shrinks and no widget loses a tier\".\nThe 1366 and 1440 frames (422px, 446px) clear the widget threshold; the\n1280 frame (393px) does not. Corrected to draw the metrics/geometry\ndistinction the document already draws two paragraphs earlier.\n\nThe \"do not shift by a pixel\" claim survived in the doc''s Testing\nsection and in the test''s own comment. At 1080p the container is now\n1865, so colWidth is 144.58 rather than the 95.83 the pinned-1280 bug\nproduced. Both now state what the test guarantees: the formula at\nBASE_WIDTH reproduces the previous hardcoded constants.\n\nAlso record the shipped consequence in Risks - ofelia renders\nCompactTier below ~1300px, correct behaviour on a genuinely 393px frame,\nwith recalibration left as a follow-up - and fix three figures: the tier\nparagraph attributed the frame inset to a card padding that does not\nexist (it is a 1px border; the heights are 148px and 298px, not 110 and\n260), the e2e headroom was 11px on the frame rather than 13px on the\ncard, the 1920 card/frame are 608/606, and useContainerWidth is at\nBoard.tsx:28.\n\nComments and prose only; no executable line changed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* build(deploy): add a per-branch rpi environment and deploy scripts\n\nrpi.branch.toml is a disposable stack for trying the checked-out branch on\nreal hardware, with ttl = \"72h\" so a forgotten one reaps itself. It takes the\nbranch through ${BRANCH_NAME}, the only variable rpi 0.26 accepts — hence the\nliteral hostname rather than a derived one.\n\nscripts/deploy-branch.ts reads the branch from git so nobody retypes it, and\nrefuses to deploy without .env.branch, which would otherwise produce a stack\nwhose WebAuthn gate fails with nothing in the deploy log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(deploy): send the branch secrets bundle before the build\n\nEvery branch is a fresh rpi project with an empty secrets store, so the very\nfirst deploy of any branch died after the ~3 minute image build with an opaque\nCompose \"secret file ... does not exist\". deploy:branch now checks\n`rpi secrets ls` and sends the bundle itself when there is none.\n\nAlso strip the `--` that `pnpm run x -- --flag` forwards verbatim: rpi''s parser\nreads a bare `--` as end-of-flags, so the flag after it was rejected as a\npositional.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(browser-automation): read the spliced callback when swc stops inlining it\n\ncheck-bundle extracted the page callback by slicing between `.concat(` and\n`.toString()`, which is the whole function expression only while swc inlines it.\nA second consumer of evidenceFromResponseText — precisely what the public\nbrowser-automation/user-input/cloudflare subpath exists to allow — makes swc emit\na named declaration instead, leaving a bare identifier at the splice site. The\nguard then evaluated that identifier in an empty realm, got a ReferenceError, and\nfailed the image build accusing a perfectly correct bundle of shipping a\nnon-self-contained callback.\n\nResolve the identifier to its declaration, and let the JavaScript parser find\nwhere that declaration ends: counting braces by hand would have to understand\nregex literals, and this body is full of regexes containing quote characters.\nExtraction failures now say so instead of reporting a self-containment defect.\n\nVerified by building all four ways: inlined and named shapes both pass; the\noriginal externals regression and a regex hoisted out of the callback are both\nstill caught.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* fix(ofelia): centre the initial in avatars re-cast to inline-flex\n\nBoth avatars centre their initial with `place-items: center`, but the\nhistory phrase re-casts the box to `inline-flex` so the circle stays in\nthe inline run, and a flex container ignores `justify-items`. The glyph\nwas left-aligned: in an 18px circle it sat 0px from the inline start and\n12.97px from the end, roughly a third of the diameter off centre.\n\nName the inline axis outright so the centring survives whichever display\na caller imposes. `.debt > [data-tone]` re-casts the same way and was hit\nby the same bug.\n\n* fix(ofelia): reserve room for the viewer ring in scrolling columns\n\nThe signed-in viewer''s avatar carries a 3px ring drawn as a spread\nshadow, so it paints outside the border box. Every avatar sits flush\nagainst the inline start of a container that clips: `.historyCol` and\n`.list` scroll (and `overflow-y: auto` clips the inline axis too), and\n`.commentsCol` is `overflow: hidden`. The ring lost its whole left edge —\nmeasured at exactly 3.0px — leaving a flat-sided avatar in the history,\nthe comment list and the composer, in both themes and on mobile.\n\nWiden each clip box outward by the ring width with a padding/negative\nmargin pair, which moves no content, and hold the width in\n`--ofelia-viewer-ring` so the shadow and the reserved room cannot drift.\n\n* fix(ofelia): size the account initial like the duty initial\n\n`MemberAvatar` scaled its initial to half the box while `Avatar` uses\n0.4, and the history phrase renders both at `px={18}` on the same line —\n9px next to 7.2px, which reads as two mismatched type sizes rather than\none set.\n\nShare the ratio through `AVATAR_INITIAL_RATIO`. Taking `Avatar`''s value\nkeeps every pre-existing avatar (the 62px hero, the 26px week strip)\npixel-identical and moves only the account squares this branch added.\n\n* fix(ofelia): raise the standard tier to the height it actually needs\n\nStandardTier lays out to 298px of content, but `tiers.standard` asked for\nonly 200px and the widget card clips instead of scrolling. At the default\nplacement (413x230px) the tier therefore resolved and then cut its own\naction row in half: \"Уборка подтверждена\" was sliced through and \"В долг\"\n/ \"Простить\" never appeared at all.\n\nState the height the tier needs, so anything shorter degrades to\n`compact` rather than rendering truncated, and raise `defaultSize` to\n`{ w: 4, h: 8 }` (~413x310px) so a freshly-placed widget still clears it\n— the same fix shape as the earlier default-placement regression.\n\n* fix(ofelia): let the pinned day header cover the reserved ring strip\n\nThe history column reserves `--ofelia-viewer-ring` of inline-start room\nso the viewer ring is not clipped, and records paint into that strip. The\nsticky day header''s own box stops at the content edge, so the strip stayed\nuncovered: scrolling avatars slid past the pinned header through a 3px\ngap, measured against the column''s border box.\n\nStretch the header back over the strip with a negative inline-start margin\nand return the width as padding, so its background spans the full\nscrollport while nothing inside it moves.\n\n* feat(ofelia): scroll the history to the day picked in the week strip\n\nSelecting a day highlighted it in the strip and changed the panel, but\nleft the history where it was, so the records for that day had to be found\nby hand.\n\n`HistoryList` now keeps a node per duty day and scrolls the matching group\nto the top of its scrollport when the selection changes. Exactly one\nancestor scrolls in each layout — the column on desktop, the dialog body\non mobile — so the native `scrollIntoView` cannot drag two scrollports at\nonce. The first run only records the mounted day: scrolling on mount would\npull the panel above the history off screen in the mobile layout before\nthe reader touched anything.\n\nThe column subscribes to a newly exposed `view.selectedIso` rather than\n`view.selected`, so confirming or forgiving the selected day still does\nnot re-render the history.\n\n* fix(ofelia): scroll only a box the reader can scroll back\n\n`scrollIntoView` moves *every* scroll container in the ancestor chain, and\n`overflow: hidden` boxes are scroll containers too — they just have no\nscrollbar. The widget frame is one: measured at 149px of overflow in the\nmobile layout, `overflow-y: hidden`. Switching a day therefore displaced\nthe whole widget, and with no scrollbar there the reader could not put it\nback.\n\nWalk up to the innermost box that is genuinely user-scrollable\n(`auto`/`scroll` *and* overflowing) and scroll that one by hand, aligning\nthe group with the scrollport top where the sticky header pins. When\nnothing in the chain scrolls there is nothing to reveal, so the layout is\nleft alone rather than nudged.\n\n* fix(ofelia): keep the mobile tab pane from clipping the viewer ring\n\nBounding the tab pane needs `overflow: hidden`, which clips the inline axis\ntoo — `overflow-x` cannot stay `visible` once the other axis is not. So in\nthe mobile layout `.split` becomes a second clipper, outside the columns\nand at the same inline start, and it shaved off the 3px viewer ring that\nthe columns had already reserved room for. Measured at `cutL: 3` on the\ncomment and record avatars; on desktop `.split` is `overflow: visible`, so\nthis only ever showed on narrow screens.\n\nReach outward by the ring width there too, the same padding/negative-margin\npair the columns use.\n\n* docs(cron): rebase the cron spec and plan onto dev\n\ndev landed Node 26, the widget domain/ boundary and Ofelia''s server.ts with\naccount authorship, so those tasks are gone. Authorship for cron records is now\na system arm on the persisted createdBy union rather than a `by: ''system''`\nfield, and the plan drops from 12 tasks to 8.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay back at dev\n\nThe overlay was temporarily aimed at this feature branch (5208c5c5) to test it on\nthe Pi before the `branch` environment existed. rpi.branch.toml now covers that\ncase per branch, so dev goes back to deploying dev — which is also what\nscripts/infra.test.ts asserts, and that test passes again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(widgets): add the cron job contract to widget server definitions\n\n* feat(server): validate widget cron schedules when the registry is built\n\n* feat(server): persist a per-job cron cursor in Valkey\n\n* feat(server): add the widget cron tick scheduler\n\n* fix(server): drain the cron scheduler on stop and pin the no-double-fire invariant\n\ninFlight was replaced on every interval fire instead of chained, so a tick\nthat outlived intervalMs was silently orphaned and stop() could return\nwhile it was still running. Chain onto the existing promise instead.\n\nAlso add a dueOccurrence test pinning that an occurrence already recorded\nin the cursor never re-fires.\n\n* style(server): rewrap the cron registry validation to the formatter''s width\n\n* fix(client): stop an edge cache from serving a stale host federation entry\n\n/remoteEntry.js is the one release file whose URL never changes while its\ncontents do: it names the hashed chunk that calls createInstance. Cached by URL,\nan intermediary keeps serving the previous release''s copy, whose chunk 404s in\nthe new release. The host then never initialises and every loadRemote fails with\n\"#RUNTIME-009 Please call createInstance first\" — a board that renders its shell\nand not one widget.\n\nObserved today on the branch stack: Cloudflare answered /remoteEntry.js with\ncf-cache-status HIT, age 6503, under its default max-age=14400, from a build\nnearly three hours older than the deployed one, while /index.html was DYNAMIC\nand the hashed assets were current. It survived a browser site-data clear, a\ncontainer recreate, a builder-cache prune and a cold rebuild, because none of\nthose touch the edge.\n\nWidget entries already carried no-cache for this exact reason; the host''s own\nentry never did. Give it the same header and pin both halves in the nginx smoke\ntest: the header, and that the chunk the entry names is one this release ships.\n\nThis is not specific to the throwaway branch host — production serves the same\nfile under the same TTL, so any release that changes the board bundle could\nshow an empty board for up to four hours.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(server): start the cron scheduler with the app and expose a test tick\n\n* feat(ofelia): give server-written records a system author\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the harness and tests the required widget identity\n\nThe ofelia authored-ledger work made `identity` a required field of\nWidgetRuntimeProps; the passport-checker widget was written before that\nfield existed. Both landed on dev without a textual conflict, so the\nsemantic conflict only surfaced as four TS2741 errors — all in a dev\nharness and three test call sites, none of them part of the production\nbuild, which is why `pnpm build` kept passing.\n\nFollow the convention the widgets updated in that PR already use: the\nharness takes the live `runtime.identity`, the tests take the\n`makeStaticWidgetIdentity()` double. passport-checker never reads the\nroster, so the empty default keeps every assertion unchanged, and the\nstatic double issues no request — which matters in recovery-flow.test,\nwhere fetch is stubbed with a never-resolving promise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* feat(ofelia): auto-approve unresolved duty days on a nightly cron\n\n* test(e2e): cover the auto-approve cron reaching an open board\n\n* test(server): cover the cron interval path and a retried partial run\n\nClose the three test-coverage gaps from the final whole-branch review:\nexercise makeCronScheduler''s start()/stop() interval path (the only\nproduction entry point, previously untested) with fake timers, cover a\nretry after a partially failed ledger append end to end in the ofelia\ncron suite, and assert append was never called in the widget''s cron\nread-error test to match its manual-path siblings.\n\n* fix(server): fence the generic storage routes off the auth keyspace\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`GET /api/storage?prefix=` passed an unvalidated prefix straight to `scanKeys`, and\n`session:<id>` embeds the session cookie''s own value in the key name, so any\nsigned-in device could enumerate live session tokens and then act as anyone —\nvoiding the server-stamped authorship this release is built on. PUT/DELETE were\nequally unbounded, which also left the new `cron:` cursor client-writable.\n\nRestrict all five generic storage routes to `root:`/`w:i:`/`w:t:`, rejecting a\npartial prefix (including the empty one) so enumeration cannot walk in sideways.\n\nAlongside: re-stamp `ip: ''''` on append as a one-release shim for pre-release\nreaders whose schema requires the field, log every `status >= 500` with its cause\nchain instead of only 500, and cover the dispatch route''s session→viewer wiring,\nwhich had no test at all — replacing it with a literal null left the suite green.\n\n* fix(ofelia): let the nightly auto-close actually discharge a debt\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`getDebtDays` scans forward from its origin, so every day it yields is >= today,\nwhile the cron only ever asks about days 1..7 in the past — `debtDayFor` was\nstructurally unreachable on that path. The auto-close therefore credited the\nrotation duty person instead of the debtor, omitted `onBehalfOf`, and `foldDebt`\nnever decremented: the debt walked forward to the next open day, forever, one\nmis-attributed record per night.\n\nAnchor the scan at `min(target, today)` behind an explicit `allowPastDebtDays`\nthat only `autoApproveDrafts` sets, so the interactive handlers stay anchored at\n`today` and keep matching what the week strip showed the user.\n\nAlongside: keep writing `by`/`author` for one release (pre-release readers require\nthem and their `z.array` fails wholesale on one missing field), drop unparseable\nelements instead of rejecting the whole array, skip the run when the ledger key is\nabsent so unused stacks stop accruing fabricated history, and re-read each day''s\nresolution before appending so a manual close landing mid-run is not overwritten.\n\n* fix(ofelia): surface failures the widget used to swallow\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\nEvery duty action captured its error into an atom nothing read, so a failed tap\nlooked identical to a successful one — and with the nightly cron now closing\nunresolved days, a silently failed «В долг» becomes a system-signed `cleaned`\nrecord at 00:05. A failed comment send additionally discarded the typed text and\nraised an unhandled rejection.\n\nTrack the last failure in write order rather than merging four per-action atoms in\na fixed order (the first non-null shadowed the rest forever), render it in Russian\nrather than leaking `WidgetApiError`''s internal message, and keep the composer''s\ntext on failure.\n\nAlongside: show a retry instead of an endless skeleton when the ledger read fails,\nstop attributing the previous week''s comments to the selected one, keep\nscroll-to-day inside the history column instead of scrolling the whole card, and\nstop the account avatar announcing a name the adjacent text already carries.\n\n* fix(board): migrate stale widget heights once, not on every read\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`tiers.standard.minHeightPx` rose 200→300 and `defaultSize.h` 6→8, but nothing\nmigrated the `h` already persisted, so every placed ofelia card would render\nCompactTier after deploy and lose its week strip and debt chips.\n\nRun the bump once, recorded in a `root:`-scoped marker key a pre-release client\ncannot strip on write-back — clamping on every read instead would have made every\nwidget unshrinkable below its package default, since `minH` sits well under it.\n\nAlongside: preserve unknown keys on the shared board snapshot, so a client that\ndoes not know a field can no longer delete it when it writes the board back.\n\n* fix(client): stop the edge from serving a stale service worker\n\nThis release already gave `/remoteEntry.js` a `no-cache` header after Cloudflare\nwas caught serving a three-hour-old copy of it. `/sw.js` has the identical\nproperty — a stable URL whose contents change every release — and fell through to\nthe catch-all block with no Cache-Control at all, so an installed PWA kept getting\nthe previous release''s worker from the edge for up to four hours and never\ninstalled the new one. That is also what would have made the ofelia record-shape\nincompatibility outlast the deploy by hours rather than a reload.\n\nGive the worker its own location alongside the federation entries, and pin it in\nthe nginx smoke spec the same way the host entry is pinned.\n\nAlongside: raise `proxy_read_timeout` for widget dispatch above the automation\nbudget — the generic `/api/` block inherited nginx''s 60s default, which would have\ncut every long passport check before its own deadline could fire — and re-point\nthe two gate probes at allowlisted keys so they keep isolating the auth and CSRF\ngates instead of tripping the new storage allowlist.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): let the members directory recover from a failed first fetch\n\nThe roster was fetched once from a connect hook that fires only on the 0→1\nsubscriber edge. On the board the identity atoms stay connected for the life of\nthe page, so a transient failure at first paint — routine on the Pi, where the\nservice worker serves the shell while the server container is still starting —\npinned the roster at null for the whole session: no viewer avatar, no viewer ring,\nuntil someone reloaded. An account created later never appeared either.\n\nRetry with backoff on a transport failure, leave a 401 alone, and refresh so a\nlater-created account arrives without a reload. Every await carries its own\nrejection handler: the abort that `wrap` raises on teardown was otherwise an\nunhandled rejection on each disconnect.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add a delete control, and stop leaking recovery sessions\n\nThe board already hands every widget a `requestDelete` callback and clock and\nofelia both use it; passport-checker was the only widget with no way to remove it\nfrom the board. Wire it through the shared `WidgetControls`, in both tiers and in\nthe dev harness.\n\nAlongside, three fixes to the recovery and check paths:\n\n`teardown()` disposed the session but never invalidated an in-flight `start()`, so\nclosing the modal during the ~1s issue request still built the noVNC RFB against a\ndetached node. The recovery slot is a single global, so that orphan answered every\nlater attempt with 409 until its TTL expired.\n\nThe 25s client deadline sat far below the pipeline''s own budget (30s queue + 60s\nexecution, 100s at the board server), so on the Pi a normal check timed out in the\nUI while the invoke resolved later with a real result that nobody read — the\nstatus could never be persisted while the upstream site was slow. Raise it past\nthe server budget, keep a late success instead of dropping it, and ignore a\nstraggler from a superseded attempt so it cannot overwrite a newer result.\n\nThe SSH fallback hardcoded noVNC''s port 6080 after compose made it per-stack, so\nan operator recovering the dev stack tunnelled into production''s live Chromium —\npossibly mid check, with a real passport number on screen. It now uses the port\nthe stack actually published.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* fix(browser-automation): report the stack''s own noVNC host port\n\nThe widget renders an `ssh -L` command so an operator can take over a stuck\nbrowser session, and it reads the port from the rejection''s meta. Nothing ever\nput a port there: `UserInputRequiredError.publicMeta` carried only `sshTarget`,\nso the widget always fell back to 6080 — production''s published port — and an\noperator on dev or branch was told to tunnel into the wrong stack.\n\nThread `NOVNC_HOST_PORT` from the same compose variable the publish line uses\ninto the service config and out through `publicMeta`, so both ends of that\ncontract now agree and each is pinned by a test.\n\nAlso set `AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET` in the dev and branch env templates: it was\nunset there, so the fallback the operator is meant to reach did not render at\nall on exactly the stacks that collide.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(backlog): record the findings this branch deliberately did not fix\n\nThe PR #27 review surfaced nine issues that were judged not worth acting on now:\nsome are contested (one review lens refuted them), some are the release''s own\ndocumented known gaps, and some are pre-existing shapes this branch only made\nvisible. They were about to be lost with the review transcript.\n\nEach entry states what exists today, why it is tolerable, the shape a fix would\ntake, and what would trigger doing the work — matching the two entries already\nhere. The facts in every entry were re-verified against the code rather than\ncopied from the review.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(rpi): move the env configs onto rpi 0.27 groups and ${git.branch}\n\nDeliver secrets through named groups instead of one bundle per environment:\n`prod` for production, `dev` for the passport-checker files the dev stack and\nthe branch stand share, `branch` for the stand''s own .env.branch. Both overlays\nclear [secrets].files, so nothing carries a second copy, and [secrets].env in\neach file is now documented as the local push source it has always been.\n\nResolve the branch stand''s source.branch through ${git.branch} and drop the\nBRANCH_NAME variable everywhere. Since 0.27 the environment key only gets a\nper-branch suffix when the configuration references ${env.slug}, so the stand is\none shared key by design; say so where the comments claimed the opposite.\n\nDelete scripts/deploy-branch.ts. It existed to push .env.branch before a deploy\ncould come up unconfigured, which a missing key bundle does silently. A declared\ngroup that is missing or empty instead fails the deploy at the layer-load step,\nnaming the group and the push that fixes it, so `rpi deploy --env branch` needs\nno wrapper.\n\nLock the layout with infra tests and refresh the docs that still said\n`rpi secrets send` and `--vars BRANCH_NAME=...`.\n\nVerified: rpi config show for base/dev/branch, oxfmt --check, oxlint and\ntsc --noEmit. The new infra assertions were run through an equivalent script —\nroot Vitest cannot start in this checkout, where rolldown fails to resolve any\nconfig entry, including untouched ones.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(rpi): drop the prod secret group and the dead PASSPORT_* variables\n\nProduction would be the only project attaching a `prod` group, and a keyless\npush always writes this file''s env + files into the deploy key''s own bundle,\nwhich is the last layer — so the group could only ever be an identical shadowed\ncopy, and a rotation pushed to it alone would silently never reach production.\nKeep the group mechanism where it earns its place: `dev`, shared by the dev\nstack and the branch stand.\n\nPASSPORT_SERIES/PASSPORT_NUMBER are gone from .env (untracked, so only its\ncomment header records this): nothing in docker-compose.yml reads them, the\nwidget takes those values from the file-backed secrets, and they were riding\ninto every stack''s containers through both groups.\n\nAgent state after this change: groups `dev` (0 keys, 2 files) and `branch`\n(7 keys, 0 files); production''s bundle 0 keys + 2 files; dev''s 7 keys + 0 files,\nwith the passport files finally resolving `<- dev` instead of a stale `<- key`\ncopy that was overriding the group.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: spec the unified widget card controls\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: plan the unified widget card controls\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: require codegen in the plan''s worktree setup\n\n* feat(widget-sdk): make WidgetControls the one widget chrome component\n\n* fix(widget-host): stop hiding card controls where there is no hover\n\n* test(client): assert the touch controls on the element that carries the style\n\n* docs: add the e2e typecheck the workspace gate does not cover\n\n* refactor(clock): take the card chrome from useWidgetChrome\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): take the delete control from useWidgetChrome\n\n* refactor(ofelia): drop OfeliaActionControls for the shared WidgetControls\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): mount the tiny tier chrome once\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): narrow the chrome policy and guard the tiny-tier switch\n\nWhole-branch code review fix wave:\n\n- useWidgetChrome now allows expand+delete only on mode === ''small'' and\n  falls through to close-only otherwise, instead of denying only on\n  ''large''. Equivalent today (WidgetMode is ''small'' | ''large''), but a\n  future third mode now defaults to no card-management chrome rather\n  than silently gaining full expand+delete.\n- TinyTier''s switch gains a `default: view satisfies never` branch.\n  ReactNode already includes undefined, so the prior bare `let body:\n  ReactNode` never tripped TS''s used-before-assignment check on a\n  missing case; the plan''s claim that it did was wrong and is corrected.\n- widget-interactions.spec.ts asserts the desktop half of the\n  hover-visibility rule (opacity 0 -> 1 on the overlay controls\n  container), which mobile-board.spec.ts cannot reach since it forces\n  hasTouch: true.\n- Corrects the spec/plan claim that a standalone dev/ harness shows\n  widget controls: every harness hardcodes mode: ''large'', so\n  useWidgetChrome() returns only onClose there and no controls render.\n\n* feat(widget-host): let a loading card name itself and be deleted\n\nA card whose widget is still loading showed an anonymous shimmer and no\ncontrols, so a component chunk that never resolved left a tile that could\nnot be removed. It now draws the widget''s icon and title — both known\nsynchronously from the codegen catalog before the remote lands — with the\nshared WidgetControls alongside them.\n\nThe controls are placement=\"inline\", not the board card''s usual overlay:\nan overlay is hover-revealed, and hover-hiding the only useful control on\na stuck card is the trap being fixed. Inline already means always-visible,\nso no new visibility rule was needed.\n\nAlso fixes a guard that never fired: the frame''s own onDelete is a\nuseEvent handle and so always truthy, which made the error card''s\n`{onDelete && ...}` unconditional. Both it and the error boundary now read\nthe raw prop.\n\n* fix(e2e): give the auto-approve cron test a ledger to close\n\nThe job returns early on an empty ledger by design — a stack where nobody\never used the widget must not grow a fabricated `cleaned` record a night\n(server.ts, F11). That guard landed in b1fffc0e, six hours after this test\nwas written in ecdd1cd0, and the test was never updated: it resets the\ndatabase, places the widget, touches nothing, and then expects the cron to\nclose a day. The ledger was empty, so the job no-opped and the plaque never\narrived.\n\nSeed one settled earlier day, the way the neighbouring forgive test already\ndoes. 2026-06-15 is a genuine Карина duty day and the entry closes it, so\nthe run skips it and auto-approves 2026-06-16 — the day the card shows.\n\nVerified: pnpm test:e2e:docker now reports 27/27, against 26/27 both on\nthis branch and on origin/dev before the change.\n\n* chore(lint): stop formatting markdown with oxfmt\n\nHand-written tables and wrapped prose in CLAUDE.md and the agent guides read\nbetter than oxfmt''s column padding, which also left the table separator row\nwider than the rows around it. docs/** and design/** were already ignored, so\nthis only extends the same treatment to the root markdown and the skill files.\n\nThe oxlint entry is symmetry: oxlint does not lint markdown today, but the two\nignore lists are maintained together.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* docs(agents): split CLAUDE.md into on-demand guides\n\nCLAUDE.md is read in full at the start of every session, and it had grown to\n~3.4k words. Move the parts that are only needed some of the time next to the\ncode they describe, and leave an index behind:\n\n- docs/agent/{deployment,test-troubleshooting,widget-server,radix-stacked-dialogs}.md,\n  each fronted by a thin .claude/skills wrapper so Claude Code loads it on its\n  own trigger, while Codex and the rest follow the table in CLAUDE.md\n- docs/architecture/overview.md for the descriptive architecture prose\n- the storage-key contract, including the incident that proves it, now lives in\n  the header comment of packages/shared/storage/scope.ts, so it reaches whoever\n  is actually editing the derivation\n\nAlso drop the generic rpi CLI mechanics already covered by the rpi:rpi-cli and\nrpi:rpi-toml skills, and fix what had drifted: pi.toml -> rpi.toml, the\nscopeWithColon normalization lives in host-runtime.ts and not widget-api.ts,\nbrowser-automation is no longer \"the future browser service\", the command list\nmatches package.json, and the hardcoded per-machine skill paths are gone.\n\nCLAUDE.md: 3368 -> 1382 words.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* fix(client): replace the deprecated apple PWA capability meta\n\nChrome flags <meta name=\"apple-mobile-web-app-capable\"> as deprecated and\nasks for the standard mobile-web-app-capable instead. Keeping both would\nkeep the warning, and standalone mode no longer depends on the apple tag:\nthe VitePWA manifest already declares display: standalone, which Safari\nhonours since iOS 16.4.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* fix(passport-checker): load noVNC lazily so the widget can mount\n\n@novnc/novnc@1.7.0 ends core/util/browser.js in a module-scope top-level\nawait: it builds a real VideoDecoder, configures it for 1920x1080\navc1.42401f, decodes a frame and awaits decoder.flush(). That probe\nresolves in milliseconds on desktop Chrome and never settles at all on\nsome devices, and a top-level await that never settles parks every\nimporter in \"evaluating\" forever.\n\nui/PassportChecker.tsx imported model/rfb.ts statically for the prod RFB\nadapter, so the chain was mount-time: import(''./ui/PassportChecker'') never\nresolved, React.lazy never got a payload, and the board rendered this\nwidget''s loading card forever. Nothing was thrown, so no error boundary\nfired and the card was indistinguishable from a slow load.\n\nReach the adapter through model/load-rfb.ts''s dynamic import instead. The\nrecovery model now takes loadRfb and awaits it alongside transport.issue:\nthe store supersedes an unused capability on the next issue() and only\ncounts a live socket as busy, so running them in parallel costs nothing\nand keeps the modal''s time to first frame unchanged. A viewer that fails\nto load lands in the new viewerUnavailable state with a reconnect button\nrather than a silent spinner.\n\nVerified on the built output: avc1.42401f/EncodedVideoChunk now appear\nonly in rfb-*.js, and the widget chunk''s single reference to it is a\ndynamic import(). ui/passport-checker-novnc-graph.test.ts guards the\nchain — it goes red if the static import comes back.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(agent): record the machine and deploy failures that lived only in memory\n\nThe CLAUDE.md split moved detail into docs/agent/* but left ten machine- and\ndeploy-specific failure modes recorded nowhere in the repository. Each of them\nreads as a regression you just caused, and each has cost multiple sessions.\n\ntest-troubleshooting.md gains the worktree symlink failure, the rotten\nnode_modules in the main checkout, pnpm check''s kill-others-on-fail behaviour,\nthe docker e2e run that exits 0 without starting, and the stale vite preview\nthat serves another checkout''s build. It also corrects the Vitest filter: the\ndocumented `--filter client test -- <file>` does not scope, `exec vitest run`\ndoes.\n\ndeployment.md gains the three caches that sit between a build and what runs -\nthe service worker and IndexedDB that survive a redeploy on the shared branch\nhostname, the edge cache on URL-stable files like /remoteEntry.js and /sw.js,\nand the aborted deploy that strands a container on a dead network.\n\n* docs: stop CLAUDE.md repeating itself, and give it a worktree command that works\n\nTesting and Failure modes said nothing the file had not already said. The gate,\nthe PR rules, the commit style and the test locations were each stated twice;\nof the five failure modes, four restated the feature workflow or the global\nrules and the fifth is now in docs/agent/test-troubleshooting.md.\n\nThe worktree command in step 1 has never worked on this machine: AGENTS.md is a\ncommitted symlink Windows cannot materialize, so the documented command fails\noutright. It now carries both required git flags.\n\n* docs(backlog): record three follow-ups the shipped releases left behind\n\nOfelia''s tier thresholds now sit within 10px of their own frames, so any chrome\nadded to the card silently drops it to compact and takes five e2e tests with it.\nThe z.array record schemas mean one new field hangs every still-open client, and\nboth shipped releases papered over it with a compat shim. And an ofelia comment\nfrom an account outside DUTY_ROTATION loses its author on pre-release clients.\n\nAll three were only ever recorded in agent memory, which is not where a\nfollow-up survives.\n\n* docs(context): commit the session review snapshots\n\nThey have been untracked for three sessions running, so each review has had\nnothing to diff against.\n\n* docs: log the PWA update path in the backlog\n\nregisterType: ''autoUpdate'' does reload the page on its own, but the same\nbranch of vite-plugin-pwa''s register.js never calls onNeedRefresh and\nmakes updateServiceWorker() a no-op — so needRefreshAtom, applyUpdate and\nUpdateBanner are dead code, and an already-open client only re-checks once\nan hour. Records what exists, why it is tolerable, and what would trigger\nthe work.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: drop the typescript 7 migration benchmark report\n\nThe migration is done and the numbers have served their purpose. The\ngenerator, scripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts, stays — the report and its\nbenchmarks.json are reproducible from it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(context): add the session-4 startup capture\n\nFollows 3d327e45, which committed the earlier snapshots.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: record the passport-checker loading-skeleton handoff\n\nRoot cause, the fix on feat/widget-controls, what is verified and what is\nnot, plus the device console probes and the list of dead ends not worth\nredoing. Delete it once that branch is released.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: spec the fullscreen close control and back-gesture dismissal\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* docs: plan the fullscreen close control and back-gesture dismissal\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the tiny tier a way out of a restored success\n\nA card narrower than the widget''s 321px `standard` threshold renders\nTinyTier, and its `success` state drew a status chip and no action at all —\nthe only state in that tier without one. That was survivable while the\nwidget always started in `idle`. Once it began restoring the last\nsuccessful check on mount, a narrow card came up in `success` and stayed\nthere: passport-checker deliberately offers no expand affordance, so the\nonly button left on the card was \"Удалить\". Seen on the branch stand at\n314px, where 4 of 12 columns miss the threshold by 7px.\n\nThe chip is now the action rather than gaining one below it, so the state\ncosts no extra height and still fits the smallest tile. It keeps the\nstatus and timestamp it already carried, and its accessible name repeats\nthat text after the action rather than replacing it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* chore(docs): keep only agent and architecture guides in git\n\ndocs/context/ session logs and docs/superpowers/ plans, specs and\nartifacts churn every session and had nothing durable to diff against;\nuntrack them and ignore docs/* except docs/agent/ and docs/architecture/,\nthe canonical checked-in references.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(docs): ignore docs/* except agent and architecture\n\nFollow-up to the previous commit — the .gitignore rule itself was left\nunstaged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* doc: add .playwright-mcp to .gitignore\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): mirror open overlays onto browser history\n\n* chore(docs): drop this branch''s plan and spec from git\n\ndev now keeps only the agent and architecture guides in the repository and\nignores the rest of docs/ (0a17cdaf, 96b2c041). This branch predates that\nrule and still carried its own plan and design spec, which the merge left\ntracked because dev never had those two paths to delete. Untracked here;\nboth files stay on disk, now covered by the ignore rule.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* feat(widget-sdk): add the overlay back-dismiss hook\n\n* test(passport-checker): widen the noVNC graph test''s budget\n\nThe test''s only await is a cold import of the widget''s whole mount graph in\na fresh module registry, which overran the default 5s budget under\n`pnpm check` — five workspace gates running concurrently. It failed there\ntwice and passed every isolated run. Nothing in it waits on behaviour, so\n20s cannot mask a regression: a static noVNC import still fails the\nassertion, not the timer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* test(widget-sdk): add StrictMode and fallback tests for overlay back-dismiss hook\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): coalesce a dropped overlay''s traversal with the push behind it\n\nA history traversal is asynchronous. Measured in real Chromium,\nhistory.go(-1) lands 25-40ms later and a pushState issued in the same task\ndoes not cancel it — jsdom does, which is why the unit tests never caught\nthis. So dropOverlay''s eager go() fired after the following push, rolled the\nbrowser back past the new entry and delivered a spontaneous popstate at the\npre-overlay depth, which reconciled as \"close the overlay that opened 30ms\nago\". Both live call paths hit it: StrictMode''s mount -> cleanup -> mount,\nand any commit that closes one overlay and opens another.\n\ndropOverlay now records the depth history must land on and schedules the\ntraversal on the next task. A pushOverlay arriving first reuses one of the\nowed entries — depth taken from the debt rather than stack.length + 1, so it\ncannot collide with an overlay still waiting above a mid-stack drop — and\nthe flush is cancelled once the debt is spent. A drop with nothing behind it\ntraverses exactly as before.\n\nThe debt is a target depth, not a step count: two drops in one task each\nname the entry they want and the lower wins, where summing their steps\ntraversed past the pre-overlay entry and out of the application. Confirmed\nin Chromium against the module at HEAD, which left the probe page on\nabout:blank after one back press.\n\nThe new tests drive real navigation instead of dispatching a hand-built\nPopStateEvent, since a faked popstate cannot tell one registered entry from\ntwo that happen to carry the same depth.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* chore(widget-sdk): clear the pre-existing lint and type errors in the overlay tests\n\nBoth predate this branch''s overlay work and were failing `pnpm lint` and\n`pnpm typecheck` at HEAD: an unused `e2` binding in overlay-history.test.ts,\nand a `let capturedDismiss` that control-flow analysis narrows to `never`\nbecause it never sees the assignment made inside the component.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): derive a pushed overlay''s depth from history, not the stack\n\n`stack.length + 1` is only correct while depth counts entries one for one. It\nstops doing so in the duplicate-depth mode a mid-stack drop can leave behind,\nand then the number it hands out skips one: from [C(1), E(2), B(2)] the next\npush is stamped 4 on an entry history places directly after the one stamped 2.\nflushOwedTraversal computes `readDepth() - target`, which counts entries, so a\nlater drop of E issues go(-3) where two entries separate it from its target and\ntakes C down with it.\n\n`pushState` inserts its entry immediately after the current one, so the depth is\n`readDepth() + 1` by construction. Identical whenever history and the stack\nagree, which is every ordinary case.\n\nAlso drops the `replaceState` from the coalescing branch — that branch runs only\nwhen the claimed depth equals the current one, so the spread rewrote identical\nstate. Confirmed in Chromium: the four-scenario probe is byte-identical with and\nwithout it. The claim path now writes nothing to history at all, which is what\nthe comments (here and on the hook) now say.\n\nThe two residuals move from the report into the module doc, where the tasks that\nwill wire real overlays to it can see them: two overlays mounted in the same\ncommit as a middle drop, and dropOverlay racing an eager dismissOverlay in one\ntask.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* feat(client): add the app-env registry, palette derivation and OKLCH conversion\n\n* feat(client): close the topmost dialog on the platform back gesture\n\n* feat(client): generate and commit per-environment app icons\n\n* test(client): pin the Escape close to history.back, not just the outcome\n\nwaitFor(() => expandedInstanceId() === null) alone cannot distinguish\n\"routed through requestDismiss/history\" from \"closed onOpenChange(false)\ndirectly\" -- both reach the same null. Spy on history.back and assert it\nwas called synchronously right after the Escape keydown, so a future\nchange that bypasses requestDismiss fails this test instead of staying\ngreen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* feat(client): brand the document title, tokens and icons per environment at build time\n\n* feat(client): show the environment badge in the board header\n\n* build: deliver the rpi environment name into the client image build\n\n* fix(client): serve the environment icons publicly so the login page is branded\n\n* feat(client): close the topmost popover on the platform back gesture\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* test(client): pin the popover Escape close to history.back, not just the outcome\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(client): pin the environment-branding wiring with tests and share the accent constants\n\nGuard the two things nothing in the gate actually exercised: the Header rendering\nEnvBadge, and both Vite configs registering appEnvBranding. Both were verified to\nfail when the guarded code is deleted.\n\nAlso: export ACCENT_L/ACCENT_C from registry.ts so the fixed accent lightness and\nchroma stop being copied across tokens.ts, generate-env-icons.ts and\nicons-lock.test.ts; derive generate-env-icons.ts''s PRODUCTION_FILL from them\ninstead of a fourth hardcoded #645fd1; give the env badge pill flex: none so it\ncan''t be squeezed by BoardSelect; and tighten a handful of comments/test anchors\n(dev-only activation branding limitation, a VITE_APP_ENV override caveat, a hook\ntier name, a looser Dockerfile layer-order assertion, three deployment.md claims).\n\n* feat(clock): give the fullscreen clock a close control\n\n* feat(passport-checker): give the fullscreen widget a close control\n\n* feat(passport-checker): dismiss the recovery modal on the back gesture\n\n* test(passport-checker): assert the async close mechanism, not just its effect\n\nThe three tests asserting a synchronous close on Escape/backdrop pointerdown\nwent stale the moment the recovery modal''s close paths started routing\nthrough history.back(), which is async in jsdom (and ~25-40ms later in real\nChromium). Update them to pin the mechanism (spy on history.back and assert\nit fired) rather than only awaiting the eventual state flip, and to wait for\nour own modal to actually close before checking the underlying Radix dialog\nsurvived, so the survivor assertion cannot pass on a race that hasn''t landed\nyet.\n\n* test: reset overlay-history between tests in the shared widget and client setups\n\nRecoveryModal.tsx''s useOverlayBackDismiss(true, close) now runs on every\nmount, so any test that mounts a Dialog/Popover root or RecoveryModal\ntouches overlay-history.ts''s file-scoped singletons (the entry stack, the\npopstate listener, a pending owed-traversal timer). recovery-flow.test.tsx\ngot its own beforeEach for this; RecoveryModal.test.tsx and\nrecovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx did not, and neither did any client test\nthat mounts a Dialog/Popover (AddWidgetMenu.test.tsx,\nBoardSchemaSelect.test.tsx, etc.) — a gap that let one test''s still-open\noverlay leak state into the next.\n\nReset once per package instead of scattering beforeEach hooks: widget-sdk''s\nshared `./test-setup` (used by every widget package via\ndefineWidgetVitestConfig) and the client package''s own vitest.setup.ts.\nBoth guard the reset to jsdom, since @vitest-environment node files in\nthese packages have no window/history at all.\n\nAdded a proof test per setup file with no reset of its own, so the shared\nhook''s effect is directly observable rather than inferred from a green\nsuite: one test leaks an overlay entry on purpose, the next asserts the\nsingleton started clean.\n\n* test(e2e): cover the fullscreen close control and the back gesture\n\n* fix(client): degrade the env badge to a dot, then hide it, on narrow viewports\n\n.brand has no overflow clipping, so once the header runs out of room it\nslides its content under .actions instead of clipping it. Give the badge\nthe same breakpoint ladder BoardSchemaSelect already uses: at <=640px\n(where the select goes full-width) drop the label text and shrink to a\ndot-only circle; at <=430px (where the select collapses to an icon) hide\nit entirely. The label now lives in its own span so CSS can hide it\nindependently of the dot, while the pill''s own aria-label keeps the\nenvironment announced in the dot-only state.\n\n* fix(client): collapse the theme toggle to one cycling button on narrow viewports\n\nThe three-item ToggleGroup is 102px; below 768px (MOBILE_BREAKPOINT, right\nat the measured collision onset) that alone accounts for a big share of\nthe header overflow. Render both controls and let CSS pick one via a\nmedia query, same approach the rest of the header''s responsiveness uses.\nThe cycle order (light -> dark -> system -> light) moves into the model\nas cycleThemeMode next to themeMode, since the order is domain knowledge,\nnot view glue; the view''s OPTIONS list is now derived from it instead of\nrepeating it. The view-transition DOM interop (--vt-x/--vt-y, startViewTransition)\nis factored into one applyWithViewTransition helper shared by both controls.\n\nThe cycling button''s accessible name (\"Сменить тему\") is deliberately\ngeneric and distinct from the ToggleGroupItem radio names, since jsdom\nrenders both controls at once (media queries don''t evaluate there) and a\ncolliding name would break the existing getByRole(''radio'', ...) tests.\n\nAdds an e2e spec covering the group/button swap at two viewports, the\nonly layer where the media query is real.\n\n* fix(client): let the board select fill the remaining header row, not the whole row\n\n.trigger (or .skeleton, while loading) is BoardSchemaSelect''s own top-level\nelement -- it has no wrapper -- so it''s a direct flex item of .brand\nalongside the logo and EnvBadge. Its <=640px width: 100% therefore resolved\nagainst .brand''s own content box, claiming the entire row for itself as if\nthose siblings weren''t there and pushing their content under .actions.\nflex: 1 1 auto fills only the space actually left after the siblings,\nwhich is what this rule always meant to do.\n\n(.root, the class that width: 100% previously also applied to at this\nbreakpoint, is never applied to any element in BoardSchemaSelect.tsx --\ndead CSS, not the flex item in question. Left untouched, out of scope.)\n\nVerified with a 900->320px real-Chromium sweep: the header now has zero\noverlap at every width down to 320px, closing the residual 545-435px band\nleft after the EnvBadge/ThemeToggle responsive work.\n\n* fix(client): let the board select shrink instead of overflowing the header row\n\nAbove 640px .trigger/.skeleton were width: 220px -- rigid. When the header\nrow ran short (the deployed stands render ''dev'' and ''branch'' in the env\nbadge; ''branch'' is the longest label the dev server never builds locally),\na rigid flex item has nowhere to give, so the surplus went past .actions.\n.triggerText already has overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis, so the\nselect was built to shrink -- nothing let it.\n\nSwitched to width: 220px + flex-shrink: 1 + min-width: 0. A bare\nflex-basis (flex: 0 1 220px) was tried first and doesn''t compose the same\nway: .brand''s own width is auto (content-based), and Chrome''s content-based\nsizing pass for an ancestor like that measures a flex-basis-only child by\nits intrinsic content size, not its flex-basis -- it silently discarded\nthe 220px preference and left the trigger permanently shrunk to ~123px\neven at 1280px with room to spare. An explicit width is what a\ncontent-based ancestor actually measures, so it''s kept alongside\nflex-shrink instead of being replaced by it.\n\nVerified this composes with the two media queries below it, unchanged:\n<=640px (flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0, from d4c7c1af) and <=430px (fixed\n36px icon) -- selectW is exactly 220 at 1280/900 and exactly 36 at every\nwidth <=430, for all three non-production env-badge labels (''local'',\n''dev'', ''branch'').\n\n* docs(widget-runtime): correct overlay-history and MyDevicesDialog comments\n\noverlay-history.ts''s module header claimed every close path routes through\ndismissOverlay and a traversal, but a widget''s fullscreen close control and\nAddWidgetMenu''s close button drop the overlay directly (open set to false,\nunmount) without ever calling dismissOverlay. Rewrite the header to name the\ntwo legitimate shapes -- dismiss and drop -- and clarify that the\n\"only popstate calls close\" invariant is about close specifically, not about\nevery close path routing through history.\n\nDocument two more constraints on callers in dropOverlay''s doc block,\nalongside the two already recorded: a push landing while a flush''s own\nhistory.go is still in flight reads a stale depth (unreachable today, but\nundocumented), and a dismiss cannot be vetoed by its caller once history has\nalready forwarded it to reconcile.\n\nMyDevicesDialog''s belt-and-braces setTimeout comment described a same-tick\nrace with Radix''s deferred pointerDownOutside check. Since the overlay-history\nwork, onOpenChange(false) no longer runs inside the click -- it arrives from\npopstate tens of milliseconds later -- so the described mechanism is stale,\neven though the guard it protects still holds (more strongly, in fact).\nCorrect the explanation without changing the guard.\n\nNo behavior change; comments and doc blocks only.\n\n* docs(agent): preserve the browser history traversal measurement\n\n.superpowers/ is git-ignored and gets deleted once this branch''s work\nfinishes, so the real-Chromium measurement behind overlay-history.ts''s\ndeferred-traversal design (browser-history-probe.md) would otherwise be lost\nentirely, leaving only the numbers already quoted in the module header with\nno reproducible method behind them.\n\nCapture the question, method, measured numbers, and the jsdom/Chromium\ndivergence in docs/agent/browser-history-traversal.md, written as durable\nguidance rather than a session log.\n\nPoint at it from CLAUDE.md. This worktree branched before the CLAUDE.md\nguide-table refactor landed on dev, so there is no existing table to add a\nrow to here; add a new one-row \"Related guides\" table in the same format,\nto be reconciled with dev''s table on the next merge.\n\n* docs: fold the history-traversal guide into the main guide table\n\nThe dev merge brought in the guide table this branch predated, leaving two\ntables side by side. One table, one row.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(client): announce the compact theme button''s state and tighten the header tests\n\nThe compact theme button''s fixed aria-label (\"Сменить тему\") left the\ncurrent mode reaching a screen-reader user only through `title` (not\nreliably announced, least of all by touch screen readers on a <=768px\nviewport) -- a regression against the tri-state group it replaces, which\nannounces the active mode through the radio''s name plus aria-pressed.\nThe accessible name now composes the current mode in: `Тема: ${label}.\nСменить`. It names the state and the action, and can''t drift from\ncycleThemeMode since it describes the current mode, not the next one.\ntitle is kept for the hover affordance. Updated the two vitest queries and\nthe e2e HeaderPage locator that pinned the old fixed name to match on the\nconstant \"Сменить\" suffix instead (regex), since the name now changes\nwith every click.\n\nEnvBadge.test.tsx''s \"label in its own element\" guard couldn''t actually\nfail: getByText matches an element''s direct text-node children and\nreturns the closest such element, so reverting the label back to bare\ntext inside .badge would make getByText(''dev'') resolve to the pill\nitself -- still a <span>, still !== the dot, so both assertions passed on\nold and new markup alike. Rewrote it to compare against the outer pill\n(screen.getByRole(''status'')) instead, the one element getByText can only\nresolve to on the old markup. Verified by temporarily reverting the label\nto bare text: the rewritten test fails on it, passes once restored.\nFolded the now-redundant \"announces via aria-label\" test into the\nexisting \"labels itself for assistive technology\" test, which already\ncovered the same aria-label via a role query.\n\nDeleted BoardSchemaSelect.module.css''s dead .root class entirely (base\nrule and the <=430px block) instead of leaving two more live-looking\nreferences to a class the file''s own comment already called unused.\n\n@media (max-width: 768px) on the compact theme button disagreed by one\nwith the MOBILE_BREAKPOINT constant it''s commented as pairing with\n(grid-metrics.ts treats width < 768 as mobile, i.e. 768 itself is\ndesktop). Changed to max-width: 767px so both agree. Verified first: a\n1280->320px sweep (transitions disabled, 300ms settle) for all three\nnon-production env-badge labels (''local'', ''dev'', ''branch'') shows zero\noverlap at every width including exactly 768px, where the three-item\ngroup is still visible under the new threshold, so 767px doesn''t\nreintroduce the overlap 768px was chosen to avoid.\n\n* test(client): expect one Zakryt control in the fullscreen dialog, not zero\n\nClock now draws its own fullscreen close button (feat(clock): give the\nfullscreen clock a close control), so the \"widget can be expanded without\nduplicate fullscreen or close controls\" e2e test and the FullscreenOverlay\nheader comment -- both inherited unchanged from dev -- were asserting and\ndocumenting the old, no-longer-true premise that Clock draws none. Update\nthe assertion to expect exactly one such button (the widget''s own) and\ncorrect the comment''s example, while keeping the real guarantee both were\nprotecting: no *duplicate* chrome stacked on top of the widget''s.\n\n* chore(docs): drop this branch''s plan and spec from git\n\ndocs/* became local-only except the agent and architecture guides. These two\npredate that change on this branch; the files stay on disk.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* build: add a compose overlay for the local nginx e2e stack\n\ndocker-compose.yml defaults the WebAuthn triple to production\n(board.iiskelo.com, https) while playwright.nginx.config.ts drives\nhttp://localhost:8080. A stack started without overrides fails every passkey\njourney inside navigator.credentials.create(), surfacing only as the generic\nclient-side \"сбой процедуры регистрации\" -- register/options returns 200 and\nno verify call ever follows. README told readers to park the overrides in\n.env, which is unavailable here: rpi.toml uses that same file as its\n[secrets].env bundle, so localhost values would ride into production on the\nnext `rpi secrets push`.\n\nThe overlay carries those three plus ALLOW_TEST_DB_RESET, so\n`pnpm start:docker:nginx-e2e` is a single command that cannot be run half-way.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* fix(e2e): pin the gate logout journey to the logout response and navigation\n\n`page.waitForURL(''/'')` synchronized nothing: the board already IS at ''/'', so\nit resolved in the same tick as the logout click -- measured at 3 ms after it\nand ~20 ms before the model''s own navigation landed. Both halves of the\nresulting race were observed: the session probe reading the pre-logout 200,\nand page.reload() overtaking the navigation, which then re-entered the\nstill-controlling service worker and re-rendered the precached board shell\n(title \"myboard\" instead of \"myboard — активация\").\n\naccount.logout runs POST /api/auth/logout -> purge -> hard navigate, so arming\na waitForResponse and a framenavigated waiter before the click pins the whole\nsequence down.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* fix(server): skip the create-invite entry test when its bundle is missing\n\nThe test guarded only one of its two preconditions. Valkey reachability was\nchecked; the built dist/scripts/create-invite.cjs it executes was not, even\nthough dist/ is gitignored and nothing in `pnpm test` builds it. So the test\nwas green only while nothing listened on 6379, and turned `pnpm check` red\nwith a MODULE_NOT_FOUND the moment any stack was up -- in a file the diff\nnever touched. `pnpm start:docker` publishes 6379 and `pnpm test:e2e:nginx`\nrequires that stack, so running the gate after the e2e suite hit it every time.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* **fix(passport-checker): unify series/number secrets and validate identity via Zod schema**\n\n- Combined series and number secrets into a single `number` field, maintaining format consistency.\n- Introduced strict validation for passport identity using a Zod schema for improved error handling.\n- Adjusted regular expressions to parse series/number from a single input field.\n- Updated browser and test implementations to reflect merged secret logic and schema integration.\n\n* fix(rpi.toml): remove series secrets path from configuration\n\n* fix(docker): remove unused `series` secrets from compose files\n\n* fix: formatting\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqa8lpCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAfYkQAJ3p5Qdg31InEscg8+Eg6sfP\njpLbM36mcconXQodlk7hDFPOUcC7NyijP2yJ+a1xEknrbfTdcaBIo0kzNFLkgiOW\nKhqDgJjmBKmn1/NCNK9tP6Zsh23D4YRHdCGNPKAKtnKp39s0eqRN9iWuLMaHyBUh\nGtGxvs5qxFggfU/aSR3cJVSs/oWiAg5K62eG8mBaCmqiZy2qr0VtXp7AlscNyQZl\nxrzLBc8YTzUcmKBPY7A7ay+0CY5hIAmZQ4reB/+5ftzzklDSm38laheWR48S4w88\nBYjrmJEJv/JiNQOt1bboxKfxnOBWAV/WqtqIMkBib6f6Biq4z51T6zIPCWBKvBbe\no3U03I02cFfGxW7Mmn/8EDBBikNjzJ83RDr8Z2fzAw1vsMPeqj7VLLSk+6Rkgeol\nRp8SHtWnql3tFGnFR4PdgoouapzM4Y5HTy6SBnzSQ/GFUa8iAes48iH3NEwOv0dA\nr+JqEaPUy9FnS3ZCl8n3bPsK6FsRNGBO9WgNWBlnfTjSLf+l0nMldY1QeISt03Cw\nw3fp4e6WwZ+DAHa0MBl1zhVVo83XKb7VTVXFbaQwGGY0IZKMZqZpu38GeYB4QwrY\ng+4r5tyVneuDa0cZucrA2inRTR7fkj2kdIduLycjwrMlTuE1pQuBK7bxEETUtibq\nged1Aw+r48dMiWs4h5k1\n=2Czp\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-07-30T22:00:10Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi/myboard/commit/56fd7f8fed8ca841fc1b388c832eecb3efe88049","node_id":"C_kwDOS9EJdtoAKDU2ZmQ3ZjhmZWQ4Y2E4NDFmYzFiMzg4YzgzMmVlY2IzZWZlODgwNDk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi/myboard/commit/7d06bcae7f4543a188e8beaeb0c809d39f9c1080","sha":"7d06bcae7f4543a188e8beaeb0c809d39f9c1080","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/7d06bcae7f4543a188e8beaeb0c809d39f9c1080"}],"sha":"56fd7f8fed8ca841fc1b388c832eecb3efe88049","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/56fd7f8fed8ca841fc1b388c832eecb3efe88049"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24359879?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/khmilevoi","id":24359879,"login":"khmilevoi","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI0MzU5ODc5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/khmilevoi","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/commits/7d06bcae7f4543a188e8beaeb0c809d39f9c1080/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-28T11:26:15Z","email":"Khmilevoi@gmail.com","name":"khmilevoi"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-28T11:26:15Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"release: authored ofelia ledger, passport-checker SP7, responsive board, widget server cron (#27)\n\n* style(docs): format the passport widget plan and spec with oxfmt\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GFDdxaQ8MLEM6eoyUxPm3f\n\n* test(client): include the passport widget icon in the registry expectation\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GFDdxaQ8MLEM6eoyUxPm3f\n\n* test(ofelia-poop-duty): give the polyfill-import test a load-tolerant timeout\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GFDdxaQ8MLEM6eoyUxPm3f\n\n* fix(passport-checker): make the recovery modal survive a modal radix layer underneath\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GFDdxaQ8MLEM6eoyUxPm3f\n\n* chore: fix mf dts\n\n* docs(passport-checker): spec the tier-shared state follow-up\n\nCovers the per-instance model store, the StrictMode removal it requires,\nfullscreen collapse/restore around recovery, modal focus containment, and\nwhy the proposed focusout tightening is rejected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): plan the tier-shared state follow-up\n\nSeven tasks: formatting debt, the per-instance store, the lease hook plus\nStrictMode removal, fullscreen collapse/restore, modal focus containment,\nthe focusout-guard regression test, and the doc back-links.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(deploy): add a dev environment overlay on the dev branch\n\nrpi.dev.toml deploys the dev branch to board-dev.iiskelo.com as the\nseparate project myboard--dev, inheriting everything else from rpi.toml.\nThe agent runs compose with -p, so the environment gets its own\ncontainers, network and volumes.\n\nTwo things in docker-compose.yml blocked a second stack on the same Pi\nand are now variable, with the production values as defaults so the prod\ndeploy and the local tooling render byte-for-byte identically:\n\n- The fixed host ports 8080/6379/6080 are bound by the running prod\n  stack; rpi only allocates a port for the ingress service and appends\n  it, so a dev deploy would fail on the binds.\n- RP_ID/RP_NAME/PUBLIC_APP_URL/EXPECTED_ORIGIN were pinned to the\n  production hostname. WebAuthn scopes credentials to RP_ID and verifies\n  assertions against EXPECTED_ORIGIN, so on another hostname the gate\n  rejects every ceremony without failing the deploy.\n\n.env.dev carries the dev values and stays untracked, like .env;\n.env.dev.example documents it. infra.test.ts gains guards for the two\nsilent-failure modes: hostname/RP_ID drift, and dev host ports landing\non a prod bind or inside rpi''s 8000-8999 allocator range.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(widget-sdk): move the instance store into the shared widget helpers\n\nThe mechanism is not passport-specific: it follows from the board mounting a\nplaced widget twice while fullscreen is open. Spec and plan now put a generic\nmakeWidgetInstanceStore factory and useWidgetInstance hook in widget-sdk, with\nthe widget keeping only its typing and disposal policy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(widget-sdk): let reatom connection lifetime own the instance store\n\nMeasured against @reatom/core@1001.1.0: withConnectHook fires on the first\nsubscriber and its cleanup on the last, which is the reference counting the\nstore was hand-rolling. The React lease hook disappears entirely — a reatomMemo\ncomponent that reads the instance atom IS the reference.\n\nTwo findings the measurement pinned down: a computed''s cache survives\ndisconnection, so the disconnect hook must drop the handle or the next mount\nresurrects a disposed value; and StrictMode still has to go, because its remount\ncycle disposes a value while both components are still mounted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widgets): stop the client host build on widgets with no client.ts\n\ndiscoverWidgetIds (widget-build-assets.ts, used only by the client host''s\nstageWidgetBuilds plugin) required every packages/widgets/* directory with\na package.json to have a dist/, the same way scripts/codegen/client.ts\ndiscovers widget candidates. But codegen goes one step further and only\ntreats a widget as a federation remote once it has a client.ts -- exactly\nthe state passport-checker is in right now (browser-task subprojects only,\nits widget UI hasn''t landed on this branch yet). Never having a build\nscript, it never produces a dist/, so every client build broke with\nMissingWidgetBuildError, both locally and on the Pi during\n`rpi deploy --env dev`.\n\nFilter discoverWidgetIds by client.ts too, matching codegen''s criterion.\nAlso set dts: false on the client''s own federation() call (mirroring what\nevery widget''s own config already sets): the host build was unconditionally\nattempting Module Federation''s DTS generation, which -- since widgets are\nconsumed dynamically via loadRemote + the generated catalog, never through\na typed remote import -- had nothing to generate and always failed with a\ncaught-but-alarming \"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading\n''readFile'')\" on every build.\n\nAdd docs/** to both oxfmt''s and oxlint''s ignorePatterns: docs/ carries a\nlot of pre-formatted design/spec markdown and an embedded HTML mockup that\noxfmt doesn''t reflow the same way as their original authoring tools, so a\nroutine `pnpm format` run was rewriting ~18 unrelated files.\n\nAlso drop the leftover PASSPORT_SERIES/PASSPORT_NUMBER lines from\n.env.dev.example: the passport-checker widget reads its series/number from\nplain-value files under the widget package, not from env vars (see\npackages/browser-automation/README.md#provisioning-secrets), so document\nthat instead.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: record the shared widget modal primitive in BACKLOG.md\n\nuseModalIsolation is a hand-rolled modal owned by one widget, and its isolation\nleans on radix-ui internals that can change in a patch release without failing\nanything but our one deliberate stack test. Capture the follow-up, its trigger,\nand the two shapes worth considering instead of leaving it implicit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore(format): apply oxfmt to pre-existing drift\n\nThese 18 files predate this branch and were already failing\npnpm format:check on main. Formatting them here makes the branch\ngate readable.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* style(ofelia-poop-duty): wrap the over-width test declaration\n\nCommit 3eee2f2 on this branch added a timeout option that pushed the `it(...)`\ndeclaration past oxfmt''s print width, so `pnpm format:check` has been red on a\nbranch-authored file. This is the branch''s own drift, not the pre-existing debt\nthe previous commit cleared.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(widget-sdk): add a connection-scoped widget instance store\n\nThe board mounts a placed widget twice while fullscreen is open — once as a\ntile, once in the overlay — so per-mount useMemo state is built twice and lost\non every tier switch. Widgets can now share one value per instanceId, built for\nthe first subscriber and disposed when the last one disconnects.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): make instance-store disconnect race-safe\n\nSwitch the widget instance store from withDisconnectHook to\nwithConnectHook so build and dispose are paired per connect episode,\nmatching the clock-model.ts convention:\n\n- Guard the deferred disconnect cleanup with isConnected(handle) so an\n  unsubscribe immediately followed by a resubscribe of the same key in\n  one synchronous flush (React StrictMode''s double-invoked effects, a\n  same-commit re-key) no longer disposes a value a live mount is still\n  rendering.\n- Only delete the key''s map entry when it still points at the handle\n  running the cleanup, so a stale disconnect can no longer evict a\n  newer, currently-connected handle for the same key.\n- Rebuild the value in the connect hook''s setup too, so a genuine\n  reconnect after disposal gets a fresh value instead of resurrecting\n  a disposed one.\n- Correct the module JSDoc: the value is built lazily on first read\n  (unavoidable, since a component''s initial render reads before its\n  effect subscribes), not \"for the first subscriber\"; only disposal is\n  actually governed by the connection lifetime. Note that a bare read\n  with no subscribing component never triggers dispose.\n\nAdds a regression test reproducing the same-tick unsubscribe/\nresubscribe race directly against the atom''s subscribe API.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): close the orphaned-read leak and stop re-poisoning the instance map\n\nA bare store(key, make)() read outside any subscription built a value that\ndispose never ran for, and left a permanent handles entry: the compute\nfunction now gives an orphaned first build one microtask to be claimed by a\nreal subscriber before tearing it down. The connect hook also stopped\nre-registering its handle in the map on every connect, which was letting a\nstale handle reconnected directly (bypassing store()) repoint future lookups\nat an already-torn-down value; the identity-guarded delete already keeps a\nstale disconnect from evicting a newer handle, so the map only ever reflects\na handle that was actually looked up through the factory.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): drop the timer-based orphan-read teardown in instance-store\n\nThe round-2 fix scheduled a queueMicrotask() to dispose a value that no\nsubscriber had claimed yet, assuming a real mount''s useLayoutEffect\nsubscribe always lands in the same task as its render-phase read. React 19\ntime-slices Suspense retry lanes (every widget renders under lazy +\nSuspense) and resumes via a scheduler macrotask, so that assumption does\nnot hold: the microtask could fire and dispose a value a live mount was\nstill about to read, corrupting a dependency-free computed forever and\nsplitting the map entry across mounts of the same instance.\n\nDisposal is now driven ONLY by Reatom''s real connect/disconnect lifecycle,\nwhich is timing-safe by construction. The tradeoff, documented in the\nmodule doc comment: a bare read with no component ever mounting still\nleaks its built value, since nothing ever subscribes to disconnect it.\nNothing outside this store''s own test currently reads it that way.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): share one model graph across widget mounts\n\nThe board tile and the fullscreen overlay mount the same instance twice, so\nper-mount useMemo models lost every result on a tier switch. Read one shared\ninstance instead, and let only the non-fullscreen mount own the recovery modal.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(widget-sdk): pin the instance store''s StrictMode tolerance\n\nStrictMode''s development-only mount -> unmount -> mount cycle empties the\nsubscriber set, which an earlier draft of this store answered by disposing a\nvalue both mounted components were still rendering. The connect hook''s\nisConnected re-check closes that; this test is what keeps it closed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): collapse fullscreen for recovery and restore it after\n\nOpening recovery from the fullscreen mount now collapses the board''s Radix\ndialog through requestClose, so the modal is never stacked over a trapped\nFocusScope; closing or retrying restores fullscreen through requestFullscreen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): wrap the recovery-open handler in each tier''s own frame\n\nPassportChecker built openRecovery with wrap() once per its own render and\nhanded the same closure to StandardTier/TinyTier, but those tiers re-render\nindependently on their own viewState() changes. The closure stayed bound to\nPassportChecker''s stale render frame, so clicking \"Открыть восстановление\"\nafter an idle->pending->sessionRequired transition (without the parent also\nre-rendering) could silently do nothing -- the root cause of the ~10% flake\nin ui/recovery-flow.test.tsx. PassportChecker now builds a plain (unwrapped)\ncomposition function and each tier wraps it fresh in its own render, mirroring\nthe existing `check` handler pattern.\n\nAlso adds a UI test for the restoreFullscreen wiring''s actual cross-mount\nhandoff: recovery opened from a fullscreen mount, then the tile mount (same\ninstanceId) inherits restorePending and calls its own requestFullscreen on\nclose -- the one assertion that would have caught restoreFullscreen wired to\nthe wrong callback.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): widen recovery-flow.test.tsx''s test budget to stop whole-suite timeouts\n\nThe new fullscreen/tile recovery tests render through Radix''s dialog/focus\nmachinery across two model mounts, which occasionally pushed real wall-clock\ntime past the shared 5s default under whole-suite worker contention (never a\nlogic hang). Set testTimeout to 30000ms for this file only, verified against\nzero reproductions of the line-122 timeout across dozens of whole-suite runs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): root-cause the recovery-flow Escape-close race\n\nfindByRole(''dialog'') resolves as soon as the dialog''s DOM node commits,\nbut useModalIsolation''s mount effect - which attaches the Escape\nlistener and moves focus in - is a passive effect that can still be\npending a tick later. Firing Escape into that gap dispatched into a\ndocument with no listener yet, so the dialog never closed and the\nfollowing waitFor spun until it timed out; reproduced solo (no\nwhole-suite contention needed) in roughly 1 of 15-40 runs.\n\nEach of the two tests that close the modal now waits for the effect''s\nsynchronous side effect (focus landing inside the dialog) before\ndispatching Escape, which proves the same effect has also attached the\nlistener. Replaces the prior fix round''s blanket file-wide 30s test\ntimeout (and its comment''s now-falsified \"never a hang\" claim) with a\nscoped 10s per-test timeout on only the two tests that actually run\nthis round trip, as a safety margin for ordinary contention rather\nthan a mask for this race.\n\nVerified with 40 solo + 32 self-inflicted-parallel + 8 whole-suite\nreruns after the fix, zero reproductions of the timeout.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): hold focus inside the recovery modal\n\nRadix restores focus from a zero-delay timeout when its dialog unmounts, so\ncollapsing fullscreen for recovery handed focus to the board a tick after the\nmodal mounted. Pull focus back whenever it lands outside the modal.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the unmount-cleanup test teeth and fix a flaky Escape assert\n\nRecoveryModal.test.tsx''s `leaves focus alone once the modal is gone` test\npassed even when the cleanup''s `removeEventListener(''focusin'', ...)` was\ndeleted, or the whole effect cleanup was zeroed out: after unmount the\nportal root is detached, so a leaked focusin listener calling `.focus()` on\na detached descendant is a jsdom/browser no-op and the activeElement assert\nstays green regardless. Pin the actual cleanup contract instead: spy on\nwindow.addEventListener/removeEventListener and assert the exact `focusin`\nlistener useModalIsolation registers on mount is the one removed on unmount,\nalongside the original behavioral assert.\n\nrecovery-flow.test.tsx''s first test asserted\n`dialog.contains(document.activeElement)` synchronously right after\n`findByRole(''dialog'')` resolves on DOM commit, before dispatching Escape.\n`useModalIsolation`''s mount effect (which moves focus in and attaches the\nEscape listener) is passive and can still be pending a tick later, so\nEscape could fire before the listener existed and the test would hang out\nits timeout — the same race Task 4 root-caused and fixed in this file''s\nother two Escape-closing tests by waiting for focus-in-dialog first. Apply\nthe same `await waitFor(...)` wrap here and correct the file-level comment,\nwhich had claimed only two tests reach the Escape-close race when this one\ndoes too.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the modal focusout guard against narrowing\n\nMount the underlying Radix dialog in its own pass, as production does, and\ncover the intra-modal focus move that the proposed guard narrowing would have\nbroken silently. The regression test dispatches the underlying focusout event\ndirectly rather than chaining native .focus() calls: jsdom (like real\nbrowsers) always completes an outer .focus() call on its own target once its\nhandler stack unwinds, so a settled document.activeElement assertion can''t\nobserve a reclaim a nested handler only ever wins transiently — verified\nempirically against the narrowed guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): close a cross-test focus-restore leak in the Radix stack suite\n\nRadix''s FocusScope defers its unmount focus-restore to a real setTimeout(0),\nand testing-library''s auto cleanup() never waits for it, so a prior test''s\ndeferred restore (and an unawaited NoVncCanvas state update from that same\ntest''s async recovery flow) can bleed into the next test — confirmed by\ninstrumentation. A formal trace of the reentrant focus cascade shows the\n''pulls focus back...'' assertion itself can''t actually be flipped by this\n(no yield point exists between its synchronous .focus() call and its\nexpect()), but the leak is real and pre-existing to any suite that\nmounts+unmounts a trapped FocusScope, so close it defensively: flush one\nreal tick after each test''s cleanup so Radix''s deferred work always\nfinishes before the next test starts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): drain Radix''s focus-restore timer from beforeEach, not afterEach\n\ne797d50''s message overstated what that change achieved. It claimed the added\nafterEach ran \"strictly after cleanup()\" and closed the cross-test focus-restore\nleak. It did neither. Vitest resolves sequence.hooks to \"stack\", so same-suite\nafterEach hooks run in REVERSE registration order, and testing-library''s\ncleanup() -- registered first, at setupFiles import time -- therefore runs last.\nThe file-level afterEach ran BEFORE cleanup(), i.e. before the unmount that\nschedules Radix''s setTimeout(0) focus restore, so it drained nothing.\n\nInstrumenting setTimeout and the hook boundaries on this file shows it directly:\nat afterEach entry the modal is still in the DOM and no FocusScope timer is\npending; the timer is scheduled only after the hook returns; the next test''s\nbeforeEach starts with one still queued. Moving the same one-tick flush to\nbeforeEach takes that count to zero before the test body runs. beforeEach is\ncorrect by construction -- it always follows the previous test''s afterEach\nhooks whatever the hook-order mode.\n\nCorrect the scope of the claim too. The restore is focus-neutral in this\nfixture: the previouslyFocusedElement it captured is either document.body,\nwhich jsdom refuses to focus, or an already-detached node, and every observed\nfiring left document.activeElement unchanged. The intermittent failure this was\nchasing did not reproduce at e797d50 in 30 consecutive single-file runs or 6\nwhole-package runs. So this closes a scheduling leak, not an observed failure,\nand the comment now says exactly that instead of claiming otherwise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): link the tier-shared-state follow-up\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): drop the phantom rebuild from the instance store''s connect hook\n\nThe connect hook carried `current ??= { value: make() }` behind a comment\nclaiming it gave a reconnect after disposal a fresh value. Measured against\n@reatom/core@1001.1.0 with the shipped code:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 2\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"],[{\"id\":2},\"a\"]]   built = 2\n\nThe reconnect still returned the DISPOSED value — a dependency-free `computed`\nnever recomputes, so its cached result is what every later read gets. The line\nonly built a second model graph nobody could reach and then handed it to\n`dispose` a second time. Same run with the line removed:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n\nThe comment now states what is true: a stale reconnect reads the cached,\nalready-disposed value, and a fresh value comes only from a new `store()`\nlookup — which is what every real caller does, because it calls `store()`\nduring render and the cleanup has already evicted the stale handle.\n\nWith the phantom build gone, `if (held)` is what prevents the double dispose,\nso a test pins it: replacing it with an unconditional `dispose?.(held?.value,\nkey)` now fails with a second teardown recorded as `[undefined, \"a\"]`.\n\nAlso corrects the module doc''s cost claim for reading atoms inside `make`.\nIt does not \"silently rebuild the whole value\": measured, after such a\ndependency changes the compute function re-runs once, `current ??=`\nshort-circuits it so `make` does not re-run (built stayed 1, the identical\nobject was returned, no subscriber notified), and because the re-run never\nreads the atom again the edge is dropped. The cost is a pointless dependency\nedge, not lost state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): align the tier-shared-state spec with what shipped\n\nThree claims in the design spec described work that was never delivered:\n\n- The instance atom was said to carry `withDisconnectHook`. It carries\n  `withConnectHook`, whose cleanup calls `teardownIfDisconnected()` — which\n  re-checks `isConnected` before disposing and evicts the map entry only while\n  it still points at the handle being torn down.\n- Section 4 \"StrictMode removal\" claimed `<StrictMode>` was dropped from\n  `packages/client/src/app/main.tsx` and replaced by a comment explaining its\n  absence. None of it shipped: `git diff cfff8d2..HEAD -- packages/client/` is\n  empty and the wrapper is still there. Measurement showed the store survives\n  the remount cycle, because the cleanup''s `isConnected` re-check returns\n  without touching a value a live mount still holds. Section 4 is rewritten as\n  \"StrictMode kept, tolerance pinned by test\", stating why the plan''s original\n  reasoning does not apply.\n- The Testing gate, the Documentation list and the Decision Summary repeated\n  the removal. They now say StrictMode stays and that the tolerance is pinned\n  by `describe(''makeWidgetInstanceStore under StrictMode'')`.\n\nThat last claim is measured, not assumed: deleting the `isConnected` re-check\nfrom the cleanup turns two of the three StrictMode tests red, along with the\nnon-StrictMode same-tick-resubscribe test.\n\nThe plan file is left untouched — it is the historical record of what was\nintended.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): narrow unmeasured claims and close two coverage gaps\n\nComment/claim accuracy — all of these stated more than was ever measured:\n\n- `ui/recovery-flow.test.tsx`: the \"roughly 1 in 15-40 runs\" hang frequency\n  was applied to every Escape-closing test in the file. It was measured for\n  exactly one of them, ''does not collapse when recovery opens from the tile''\n  (task-4-report.md, Fix round 3). The others carry the same wait by\n  inspection — identical shape, identical race — with no observed rate. The\n  comment now says so.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"at `beforeEach` entry one (later\n  two) FocusScope timer is pending\" — only the 1 -> 0 transition was ever\n  recorded, so the \"(later two)\" is dropped.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"jsdom (like real browsers) always\n  completes an outer `.focus()` call on its own target\" — true and measured\n  for jsdom, never measured against a real browser. Scoped to jsdom.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: ''keeps focus inside when it moves\n  between two of our own controls'' promised a focus invariant the test does\n  not assert — it asserts that our guard swallows the `focusout` before it\n  reaches `document`. Renamed to say that.\n\nCoverage:\n\n- `model/instance-store.test.ts` (new) covers `dispose: (models) =>\n  models.recoveryModel.teardown()`, the safety net against leaking a live VNC\n  session when the modal outlived the widget. It had no test at all: replacing\n  the callback with a no-op left the whole widget suite green. Verified both\n  new tests fail against that no-op.\n- `ui/PassportChecker.test.tsx`: two assertions used\n  `expect(await screen.findAllBy*(…)).toHaveLength(2)`, which cannot retry —\n  `findAllBy*` resolves on the first snapshot with >= 1 match, so it settles on\n  one mount and never waits for the second. Both are now a re-querying\n  `waitFor`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(backlog): record the two-modal focus ping-pong as a deferred defect\n\nBoth `useModalIsolation` instances register focus containment on `window` in\nthe capture phase, so two mounted modals each see the other''s `focusin` before\nthe root-level `stopPropagation()` can stop it, and each pulls focus into its\nown root — forever.\n\nReproduced in jsdom 29.1.1 with two `useModalIsolation` roots mounted at once:\none modal performs 1 focus pull on mount; two mounted together never settle,\nand a hard test guard at 400 pulls had to stop the run (402 recorded).\n\nDeferred rather than fixed because it is unreachable through the UI: the\npassport checker is the only owner of such a modal, renders at most one\n(non-fullscreen mount only), and its overlay is a full-viewport\n`pointer-events: auto` layer whose `pointerdown` handler closes the modal, so\na second one cannot be opened over the first.\n\nThe entry notes the likely fix — a module-level stack of active modal roots\nwith containment applied only by the topmost.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): correct five unverified claims left by code review\n\nBring wording in the tier-shared-state spec, BACKLOG.md, and a test\ncomment in line with what was actually measured/read: the instance\nstore''s dependency-edge cost (not a rebuilt value), a stale\nuse-modal-isolation.ts line citation (:53 -> :55, two places), the\nroot focusin stopper''s actual bubble phase (not capture), and which\nrecovery-flow tests keep the shared timeout default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay at this branch\n\nPins rpi.dev.toml''s source.branch to feat/passport-checker-widget so the dev\nenvironment runs this PR instead of the dev branch.\n\nREVERT BEFORE MERGING: once this PR lands on dev, source.branch must go back to\n\"dev\" or the dev overlay stays pinned to a branch that no longer moves.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): drop the stale Chromium singleton lock before launch\n\nThe Chromium profile is a named Docker volume, so `SingletonLock` keeps the\n`<hostname>-<pid>` of whichever container wrote it. After a redeploy the new\ncontainer has a new hostname, Chromium reads a lock owned by \"another computer\"\nit cannot probe, and `launchPersistentContext` fails on every task until the\nvolume is touched by hand.\n\nRemove `SingletonLock`, `SingletonCookie`, and `SingletonSocket` before each\nlaunch. `fs.rm` unlinks the symlinks themselves instead of following them, and\n`force` keeps a fresh profile from failing; a removal that does fail is returned\nas a value, logged, and the launch still proceeds. Unconditional removal is safe\nbecause `getPersistentContext` is the only caller of `launch` and the image\nentrypoint runs a single `node dist/index.cjs` - no check for a live Chromium is\nperformed or implied.\n\nAlso asserts that a failed launch keeps its cause, which is what makes the\nPlaywright message reachable from the service log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): log the error behind an internal task failure\n\nInternal failures were logged as `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` only, so the\ntagged error and its cause chain never reached the container log. A Chromium\nlaunch failure therefore showed up as a bare `internal` code with nothing to\ndiagnose. Add the error to the logged fields; `toEnvelopeError` still answers\nthe client with the redacted `internal` code, so only the log gains detail.\n\nNote the trade-off: a handler error whose message carries task data now reaches\nthe container log, which the previous field list deliberately avoided.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): redact handler failures in the internal task log\n\nfa87a47c logged too much. It added `error: outcome` to the internal-failure log\nso a Chromium launch failure would stop being invisible, but that field carries\nwhatever the handler threw. errore''s `toJSON()` serialises `message`, `stack`,\nand the whole `cause` chain, Playwright quotes `page.evaluate` arguments into\nits messages, and the passport widget passes the secret identity as one — so a\nhandler failure could print the passport series and number into the container\nlog. The wrapper''s own `stack` repeats the cause under `Caused by:`, so the\nidentity landed there twice.\n\nThat contradicts the design spec: raw Playwright, network, Zod, and upstream\nresponse details are logged only after redaction, request and response bodies\nare never logged, and logs redact task payloads and secret values\n(docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md\n:350-353, :684).\n\nThe rule now, verified against dispatch.ts:\n\n- `BrowserExecutorError` wraps a failed `executor.acquire(...)`. Acquire runs\n  before the handler and is passed only `(signal, widgetId)` — never the payload\n  or the widget secrets — so its cause chain is Chromium/Playwright launch\n  detail. Still logged in full; this is the stale singleton lock failure\n  fa87a47c existed to surface.\n- Every other internal failure wraps handler output and is reduced to\n  `errorChain`: the names/tags of the error and its `cause` chain and nothing\n  else. No `message`, no `stack`, no foreign properties.\n\nThe log message, the `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` fields, and the\n`code === ''internal''` condition are unchanged.\n\nTests split the single fa87a47c case in two: one pins that an executor failure''s\ncause chain still reaches the log, one asserts a secret-looking cause string is\nabsent from the serialised logged payload. Both were confirmed to fail before\nthe fix and to discriminate after it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add an env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe passport widget''s recovery path (retain the page, issue a single-use\ncapability, stream the live browser over noVNC, let a human solve the\nchallenge, retry) has never been exercised on real hardware, because it only\ntriggers when pasport.org.ua serves a Cloudflare challenge and none has been\nencountered — the smoke test on the Pi returned a normal result. Subproject 8\n(`passport-checker-rpi-integration` in\ndocs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md)\nhas \"embedded noVNC Cloudflare recovery, SSH fallback, and real checker smoke\ntest on the Raspberry Pi\" as an acceptance item and had no way to reach it on\ndemand. PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY is that way.\n\nContract: `1` and nothing else enables it — absent, empty, `0`, `true`, ` 1 `\nare all off (isForcedRecoveryEnabled does exact string equality, no trimming,\nno truthiness coercion). Compose passes it through with a `:-` default so an\nunset variable stays off, and it must never be set in production.\n\nSemantics: at most once per process. The latch lives in the closure\nmakePassportCheckHandler returns, so each handler owns its own and the unit\ntests stay isolated. It fires after a successful goto and after\ncollectNavigationEvidence — the retained page is the real checker page with the\nsite''s own UI loaded, so a human can drive it over noVNC — and returns exactly\nthe retainPageForRecovery() + BrowserSessionRequiredError pair the genuine\nCloudflare branch returns, making the downstream flow byte-identical to a real\nchallenge. The latch is consumed only when the forced path actually fires, so a\nfailed navigation returns its own error untouched and leaves the lever armed:\nan infrastructure hiccup must not burn the single forced run an operator is\nwaiting on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): apply the design-system widget redesign\n\nBring the tile and the recovery modal in line with Паспорт-чекер.dc.html.\n\nBoth tiers now lay out as header -> flexible middle -> action, so the\naction sits on the tile''s bottom edge in every state instead of trailing\nwhatever content happens to be above it. Status banners pick up the\ndesign''s per-state hues, the filled success disc, and the CircleAlert /\nTriangleAlert split that separates a retryable failure from a session\nprompt. The modal gains the access-pill dot, a pulsing LIVE dot, framed\nconnecting/disconnected/expired states, and the shell-style SSH block.\n\nDark theme was effectively missing: the --pc-* hues chained to\n--success-soft / --destructive-soft, which the host token set never\ndefines, and to --success, which is a dark surface green rather than an\nicon green under [data-theme=''dark'']. Every hue is now declared per\ntheme the way ofelia-poop-duty does it. The modal portals to\ndocument.body, outside .widget, so it declares the one hue it needs on\nits own overlay.\n\nThree deliberate departures from the mockup:\n- invalidConfig copy stays server-side (\"Обратитесь к администратору\"),\n  since this widget has no per-instance settings — its config lives in\n  server secrets.\n- the expired frame keeps \"Переподключиться\"; without it an expired\n  capability can only be recovered by closing and reopening the modal.\n- the sessionRequired tiny tile only fills amber, no border: the tile\n  border belongs to the host card, and drawing one from inside the\n  widget would render a second, inset contour.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xwGNctwqXcYi5p4QSSFhA\n\n* docs(browser-automation): design widget user-input detection\n\nMove the \"a human must act\" mechanism out of passport-checker and into the\nautomation platform. The handler gets context.detectUserInput(detector), which\nruns the detector, optionally prepares the page, retains it and returns the\ncanonical error as one indivisible step; retainPageForRecovery leaves the public\ncontext so the halves cannot be separated. Cloudflare becomes a reusable helper\n(probe and predicate together, not just the predicate) that any widget can pass\nin. Also records the removal of the PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY lever.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): correct the user-input spec''s test-seam claims\n\nmakeFakeExecutor fakes BrowserExecutor<FakeContext> with FakeContext = { id,\nsignal }; it never builds a BrowserTaskContext, so it cannot host a recording\ndetectUserInput and needs no change at all. Name the seams that do exist: the\nsix retainPageForRecovery call sites in chromium-executor.test.ts and the three\nBrowserTaskContext literals in diagnostics.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): plan the widget user-input detection refactor\n\nSeven tasks, each green on its own: retire the forced-recovery lever, add the\ndetector and error contracts, move Cloudflare detection into the platform with\nits probe reunited with its predicate, run escalation from the task context,\nrewire the widget, then withdraw retainPageForRecovery from the public context so\nthe retain/escalate pair cannot be taken apart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): make the Cloudflare task a move, not a copy\n\nTask 3 previously left the classifier duplicated between the widget and the\nplatform until Task 5, which any reviewer would rightly flag as verbatim\nduplication of a logic block. The widget now switches to the moved module in the\nsame task, so the logic never exists in two places.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* revert(passport-checker): drop the env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe lever forced one trip through the Cloudflare recovery path per service\nstart so Subproject 8 could exercise noVNC on the Pi on demand. It is being\nretired deliberately ahead of moving user-input detection into the platform;\nthe Pi smoke test needs another answer.\n\nThis reverts the behaviour added in 45c604f1.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(browser-automation): add user-input detector and error contracts\n\nUserInputRequiredError carries the browser_session_required code and sshTarget\nmeta the recovery flow keys off, moved out of the passport-checker widget where\na platform contract had no business living. UserInputProbeError distinguishes\na broken check from a negative one.\n\n* feat(browser-automation): move Cloudflare detection into the platform\n\nThe classifier lived in the passport-checker widget while the DOM probe feeding\nit lived in that widget''s check handler, so the only reusable half was the half\nwithout the probe. Both now sit in browser-automation behind two detector\nfactories: one that probes the live page, one that classifies evidence already\nderived inside the page from a fetch response.\n\nThe widget imports the moved module rather than keeping a copy; it still calls\nthe classifier by hand, which the next commit changes.\n\n* @\ndocs: document the feature branch -> dev -> main workflow\n\nFeature work happens in a dedicated branch inside a ./.worktrees checkout,\nlands in dev through a PR, gets verified on the dev stack, and only then\nreaches main through a dev -> main PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* feat(browser-automation): run user-input detection from the task context\n\ndetectUserInput runs a widget-supplied detector and, on a match, prepares the\npage, retains it and returns UserInputRequiredError as one step, so the two\nhalves cannot drift apart. A detector that fails yields UserInputProbeError and\ndeliberately leaves the page unretained: an undecidable check is not a challenge,\nand pinning a browser page for a recovery nobody will perform is worse.\n\nAUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET moves into the service config, where an unusable value\ndegrades to null rather than failing config loading and killing the service.\n\n* docs(passport-checker): design last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* test(browser-automation): cover the prepare-before-retain order in detectUserInput directly\n\nThe executor-level test asserted hasRetainedPage() from inside the prepare\nmock, but that bookkeeping is only populated at release() time, so it passed\nregardless of call order. Add a direct unit test against makeDetectUserInput\nthat records prepare/retain into one ordered array — genuinely fails if the\norder in detect.ts is inverted (verified by temporarily inverting it).\n\nNo production behavior changes; detect.ts ends identical to before this commit.\n\n* @\ndocs: merge AGENTS.md into CLAUDE.md and symlink it\n\nAGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md had drifted into two partly overlapping rule sets.\nEverything unique to AGENTS.md (project structure, codegen commands, Windows\ntest-runner notes, coding style, UI gotchas, testing, security, failure modes)\nnow lives in CLAUDE.md, and AGENTS.md becomes a symlink so every agent reads\nthe same file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* docs(passport-checker): plan last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* feat(passport-checker): date-aware check timestamp label\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout design\n\nTwo width breakpoints instead of one hardcoded grid: one column below 768,\ntwelve above, with row height and margin scaled by the same factor as the\ncolumn width so the desktop grid zooms instead of stretching on one axis.\n\nThe mobile layout is derived from the desktop one and only persisted once the\nuser drags or resizes, stored as an additive optional `mobileLayout` field so\nno migration of `root:boards` is needed.\n\nAlso records the react-draggable touchstart defect: the whole card is the drag\nhandle today, which cancels page scrolling on touch — harmless on a short\n12-column board, blocking at one column.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): detect required user input through the platform\n\nThe handler no longer collects Cloudflare evidence, classifies it, retains the\npage and builds the escalation error by hand at every site; it passes a detector\nto context.detectUserInput and returns what comes back. browser/challenge.ts and\nthe widget-local BrowserSessionRequiredError are gone — the classifier now lives\nin browser-automation next to the probe that feeds it, and the\nbrowser_session_required contract belongs to the platform that owns the recovery\nflow.\n\n* fix(passport-checker): restore the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nAn automated amend from a concurrent session accidentally stripped this\nline out of 3a12494e. It belongs to the user-input-detection work; this\nrestores it verbatim.\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nRETRYABLE_MESSAGES.user_input_probe vanished twice with no test turning red,\nbecause nothing asserted the actual behaviour: with the entry gone,\nmapCheckError silently falls back to GENERIC_RETRYABLE_MESSAGE and the user\nsees the generic string instead of the specific one. Assert the real output\nof mapCheckError against a hardcoded expected string, not against the table\nitself, so a future deletion or typo of the entry fails this test instead of\ncomparing the map against itself.\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron design spec\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout implementation plan\n\n* feat(board): resolve grid metrics from the container width\n\n* feat(passport-checker): persist the last successful check\n\n* feat(board): derive and reconcile the one-column mobile layout\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron implementation plan\n\nAlso corrects the spec: the jsdom Temporal shims must stay, only the\n--harmony-temporal execArgv becomes redundant on Node 26.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin which detector goes to which detectUserInput call\n\nNothing previously checked that the navigation check gets the page detector\nand the submission check gets the evidence detector -- only the second call''s\nprepare option was asserted. A handler that swapped the two factories would\nleave every test in this file green; only the gated real-browser suite would\nnotice, and it isn''t run in CI. Pull the captured detector off each mock call\nand invoke it directly: the page detector must reach for page.evaluate, the\nevidence detector must ignore its page argument and classify the real\nevidence. Also drops the now-inert hasChallengeForm evidence field from the\nsubmission-escalation test, whose result was always driven by the mock''s\nescalation queue regardless of that field.\n\n* feat(board): add mobile layout materialize, update and reset actions\n\n* refactor(browser-automation): expose only detectUserInput on the task context\n\nretainPageForRecovery was half of a pair a handler had to assemble correctly by\nhand: retaining without escalating strands a browser page, escalating without\nretaining hands the UI a recovery session with nothing behind it. With the retain\nflag reachable only through detectUserInput, neither mistake is expressible.\n\n* test(passport-checker): cover restore-on-mount and cross-placement sync\n\nAdd two widget-level cases pinning storage-backed persistence through\nReact: a fresh mount renders a previously stored result without\ninvoking a check, and a check made in one placement is observed by a\nsecond placement sharing the same storage. Rename the pre-existing\n\"gives separate instance ids separate state\" case to \"gives separate\nstorages separate state\" (it isolates by storage, not instanceId) and\nupdate its comment to point at the new cross-placement case below it.\nPin PASSPORT_LAST_RESULT_KEY''s literal value in check-model.test.ts so\na silent rename cannot slip through green while orphaning deployed\nusers'' stored data under the old key.\n\n* feat(board): switch the grid to resolved metrics and a mobile drag grip\n\n* docs(browser-automation): document how a widget requests manual input\n\n* feat(board): offer a mobile layout reset in the board schema popover\n\n* fix(widgets): address passport-checker persistence review findings\n\n- TinyTier''s success chip now shows checkedAtLabel, so a restored (possibly\n  stale) result reads honestly in the compact tile too, not just standard.\n- Harden the two-placement isolation test: assert placement two stays idle\n  instead of a length-1 findAllByText that can pass mid-race.\n- Clarify why lastResult/transient set order is safe in checkPassport\n  (Reatom notifies after the sync block, not \"order for readability\").\n- Add a test exercising lastResultSchema.safeParse over the exact shape\n  checkPassport writes, since createFakeStorage skips schema validation.\n- Document lastResultSchema as a persistence contract: new fields must be\n  optional/defaulted or every stored value fails validation on next deploy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* fix(passport-checker): remove dead AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET threading\n\nrecoverySshTarget was never read by check.ts''s handler after the\ndetectUserInput refactor moved sshTarget sourcing entirely into the\nplatform config. Delete the widget''s leftover env read and option\nthreading so the README''s \"widgets neither read nor pass it\" claim is\nactually true, and drop the now-meaningless SSH-sanitization test in\nfavor of the equivalent (and broader) coverage in browser-automation''s\nconfig.test.ts.\n\n* docs(ofelia): design the authored ledger and comments\n\nRecords get the authenticated account instead of the per-device \"Я:\"\ntoggle, the write path moves into the widget''s own server.ts, and the\nhistory row is rebuilt: human dates, plain-language phrases, explicit\ndebt effect, visible supersession, no IP.\n\nPulls the Node 26 prerequisite out of the widget-server-cron spec, since\nthe shared domain module it enables is needed here first.\n\nAlso imports the Claude Design workspace under design/ (documents only,\nscreenshots and uploads stripped) and keeps the brief that produced the\nOfelia mock.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs(ofelia): note the cron plan overlap and landing order\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): cover the mobile layout and touch-scroll behaviour end to end\n\n* fix(board): keep the measured grid container mounted\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a 1920 viewport\n\n* docs(ofelia): add the authored ledger implementation plan\n\n15 tasks: Node 26, the pure domain split, the members directory, widget\nidentity, the server-resolved viewer, record schemas, draft construction,\nthe widget server definition, the model rewire, the history and comment\nview models, the account avatar, and the two rebuilt panels.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): wait for the grid relayout to settle before measuring\n\n* fix(browser-automation): bundle workspace imports instead of externalizing them\n\nThe rspack externals predicate was an allow-list whose default was\n\"externalize\". When the user-input refactor introduced the first runtime\nbare-specifier import of a workspace package into the service module graph\n(browser-automation/user-input/cloudflare, from the passport-checker browser\ntask), it was silently externalized: the build stayed green, all tests stayed\ngreen, and the Cloudflare module was simply absent from dist/index.cjs. The\nruntime stage copies only package.json and dist/, so Node resolved the\nspecifier through the exports map to a .ts path that does not exist in the\nimage and the container died on `exec node dist/index.cjs`.\n\nInvert the predicate into a deny-list derived from the package''s own\ndependencies (minus errore, which is ESM-only and must be bundled) plus Node\nbuiltins. An unrecognised bare specifier is now bundled, so the same mistake\nsurfaces as a build-time resolution error rather than a startup crash, and\nadding a real runtime dependency externalizes it with no config edit.\n\nAdd scripts/check-bundle.ts, chained onto `build` so it runs inside the image\nbuild the Dockerfile already performs. It asserts that every require() in the\nbundle is a builtin or a declared dependency, that the scan still matches the\nemitted shape at all, and that the compiled evidenceFromResponseText the widget\nsplices into Chromium still evaluates and runs in a bare vm realm. That last\ncheck is what the BROWSER_IT suite could never prove: it runs under vitest,\nwhere the specifier resolves to .ts through esbuild and the swc path is never\nexercised. Verified: the compiled form is ES5-lowered but self-contained --\ndestructuring became inline property reads, no swc helpers, no hoisted regexes.\n\nAlso:\n- detect.ts: tag the prepare outcome instead of comparing against null, so a\n  prepare that rejects with exactly null is a failure rather than a silent\n  success, matching the detector branch''s `typeof detected !== ''boolean''`.\n- user-input/errors.test.ts: cover toEnvelopeError for UserInputRequiredError\n  with and without an ssh target, pinning with toStrictEqual that `meta` is\n  omitted rather than set to undefined. contracts.test.ts already claimed this\n  coverage lived here.\n- chromium-executor.test.ts: drop an assertion inside a prepare callback that\n  could not fail the test and was vacuous, and rename the case to what it\n  actually verifies; the ordering contract is owned by detect.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* test(board): settle past the grid transition and stop assuming card order\n\n* test(board): correct the stale comment above the breakpoint wait\n\n* chore(node): align every runtime and image on Node 26\n\nnode:26-alpine and node:26-bookworm-slim no longer bundle corepack\n(removed from Node core), so `npm install -g corepack@latest` is added\nahead of every `corepack enable` in the three Dockerfiles and\ndocker-compose.dev.yml. scripts/infra.test.ts''s assertion on the\nbrowser-automation Dockerfile''s base image is updated to match.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(node): unshadow jsdom localStorage and bump the e2e image to Node 26\n\nNode 26 ships its own unflagged global localStorage/sessionStorage (Web\nStorage API), which resolves to undefined without --localstorage-file\nand shadows jsdom''s window.localStorage for bare-identifier reads in\npackages/client''s jsdom test environment (40 failing tests). Disable\nNode''s own globals via execArgv: [''--no-experimental-webstorage''] in\npackages/client/vite.config.ts so jsdom''s storage wins; verified via\nObject.getOwnPropertyDescriptor that the flag actually removes the\nglobal rather than just suppressing the warning. widget-runtime and\nwidget-sdk don''t touch localStorage in tests today, so the flag is not\nadded to their execArgv arrays (narrowest correct remedy).\n\nAlso bumps packages/client/e2e.Dockerfile (used by pnpm test:e2e:docker\nvia docker-compose.e2e.yml) from node:22-bookworm-slim to\nnode:26-bookworm-slim, missed by the original brief''s Files list, with\nthe same corepack@latest install this Node 26 base image now needs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): make the relayout wait actually poll\n\n* refactor(ofelia): extract the pure duty domain out of the Reatom model\n\n* fix(lint): make the widget domain import barrier a real allowlist\n\nThe regex denylist let ''../model/ofelia-duty'' through — domain/ and model/\nare siblings, so a single-''../'' import was both the most likely accidental\nescape and completely unguarded. It also only enumerated known packages, so\nany new npm dependency passed silently.\n\nReplace it with a gitignore-style group allowlist (''**'' minus zod, @shared/*\nand ''./'' siblings), which oxlint evaluates natively. A negative-lookahead\nregex was tried first and rejected: oxlint''s Rust regex engine has no\nlookaround and silently drops such a pattern, disabling the whole barrier.\n\nDomain tests get a second, later override that additionally allows vitest;\neverything else stays blocked there too.\n\n* chore(lint): keep the design mock exports out of lint and format\n\ndesign/ holds vendored Claude Design exports (support.js, *.dc.html)\nthat break the repo''s only gate: oxlint reports 9 errors in\ndesign/support.js, and oxfmt aborts (exit 2) on non-standard markup\nin design/Design System.dc.html before it can check anything else.\n\nAdd design/** to the existing ignorePatterns glob array in both\n.oxlintrc.json and .oxfmtrc.json, the same mechanism already used for\ndocs/**. This is a plain glob matcher (not the Rust regex engine\nbehind no-restricted-imports), so it isn''t subject to that engine''s\nlookaround gap, and it leaves every other rule/override untouched.\n\nFixing the oxfmt abort also uncovered pre-existing, unrelated\nformatting drift in CLAUDE.md, package.json and\nscripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts that the crash had been masking from\nthe format:check summary. Reformatted them (whitespace/ordering only,\nno logic changes) so pnpm format:check is actually green end to end.\n\n* feat(auth): expose the board members directory\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): expose the board identity to widgets\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): stop identity fetch from leaking an unhandled rejection and a discarded error\n\nHandle the AbortError that wrap()''s promise rejects with when the last\nwidget reading identity unmounts mid-fetch, and stop discarding\nBoardMembersError whole: a 401 stays silent (expected on an\nunauthenticated host), everything else is surfaced via console.warn so\nschema drift or a server fault is not silently swallowed.\n\n* feat(widgets): resolve the signed-in viewer for widget server events\n\n* feat(ofelia): carry the authoring account on ledger and comment records\n\n* feat(ofelia): move draft construction and event schemas into the domain\n\n* feat(ofelia): write the ledger and comments from the widget server\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): harden server date parsing and unforgeability coverage\n\n- Wrap Temporal.PlainDate.from in errore.try so a calendar-invalid but\n  regex-valid date (e.g. 2026-02-30) returns an Error instead of\n  throwing through a function typed Error | T.\n- Add regression coverage: forged createdBy/actor in the payload is\n  ignored, the debt handler is exercised, storage-read failures are\n  asserted by identity with no write, and the invalid-date case is\n  asserted as a value.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): split the unforgeability guard into schema and handler halves\n\nThe round-1 forged-payload test went through run(), which parses the\npayload via zod''s default strip mode before the handler ever sees it —\nso it only proved the schema strips forged fields, twice over, and\nwould not catch a handler that starts spreading its raw payload into\nthe draft. Replace it with two schema-level tests (clean + comment\npayloads drop a forged createdBy/actor) and one handler-level test\nthat calls ofeliaServer.handlers.clean directly, bypassing the schema,\nto prove the handler itself never reads those fields even if they\nreach it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* feat(ofelia): drive ledger and comment writes through widget server events\n\nThe client no longer appends to the ledger or the comment key. All four\nday actions collapse into one `invokeDay` that sends `{ date }` to the\nmatching widget-server event, and `send` sends `{ weekStart, text }` —\nwho the actor is, whose debt moves, whether the event applies at all and\nwhich account signed it are now derived and stamped server-side, so a\nclient cannot forge authorship.\n\n`selectedDate()` and `today()` (and `viewWeekStart()`) are read before\nthe first await so they resolve against the widget''s own Reatom context.\n\nThe per-device \"Я: Леша/Карина\" toggle is deleted rather than hidden:\nauthorship comes from the session, so there is nothing left to pick.\n`currentUser` is gone from the duty model, the widget context, the\nfixture and the view-model''s `onSetUser`.\n\nBoth model factories now take `api` and `identity`; `identity` is the\ndirectory the history and comment views read in the follow-up tasks.\nThe view projections drop `ip`/`by`, whose schema fields Tasks 5-8\nremoved, and `authorName` falls back through `createdBy.name`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* style: format the ofelia server test\n\n* feat(ofelia): group history by duty day and mark superseded records\n\n* feat(ofelia): resolve comment authors through the members directory\n\n* fix(ofelia): flush the comment thread''s connect hook via vi.waitFor, not a bare microtask\n\nReview round 1: the timing fix for the comment-thread subscribe/emit race\nassumed a single-microtask flush of Reatom''s connect-hook queue. Match the\nsibling comments-subscription tests in the same file instead — wait for the\nstorage mock''s subscribe call and wrap the whole body in context.start, so\nthe assertion is condition-based rather than scheduling-internals-based.\n\n* feat(ofelia): add the account avatar and its palette\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the history list around duty-day groups\n\n* fix(ofelia): announce the history phrase subject to assistive tech\n\nThe duty circle is the sentence subject in the rebuilt history phrase, and\nAvatar is aria-hidden, so the column announced as bare verbs with no subject.\nPair every circle with a visually hidden name, attribute the debt pill to its\nperson, and separate the chips with real spaces as the mock does.\n\nAlso correct the text-decoration comment to name the real mechanism (atomic\ninline, not descendant cancellation) and cover the three untested branches.\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the comment thread around account authors\n\n* fix(ofelia): restore composer coverage and mute the unknown comment author\n\n* fix(ofelia): break ledger day ties by append order\n\nRouting the writes through the widget server changed where `ts` comes from.\n`/api/storage/:key/append` stamped `Date.now()`, so consecutive appends always\ngot a strictly increasing timestamp. The widget dispatch stamps the app''s\ninjectable `now()`, and the e2e harness pins that clock — so a clean-then-undo\npair on the same day produced two entries with an identical `ts`, and\n`latestOutcomesByDate`''s strict `>` kept the earlier `cleaned` entry as the\nday''s outcome. The `reset` was written but never counted, and the day never\nreopened; `pnpm test:e2e:docker` caught it as a failing \"undo — returns the day\nto pending\".\n\nThe ledger is append-only and iterated in insertion order, so `>=` is the\ncorrect tie-break: on an equal timestamp the later-appended entry is the newer\ndecision. This also hardens production, where two writes can still land in the\nsame millisecond.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: describe server-authored widget records\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): fail widget dispatch when the session account is unreadable\n\nresolveWidgetViewer folded every getAccount failure into `null`, so a Valkey\nfault, a corrupt account record and a deleted account all produced a record\nstamped `createdBy: null` plus a `{ ok: true }` response. The ledger is\nappend-only, so that authorship could never be recovered.\n\nThe three outcomes are now distinct: no session still returns `null`, which is\nwhat the standalone harnesses and the e2e stack (no nginx, so no session cookie)\nrely on to keep writing unattributed records; a failed lookup returns a\nWidgetViewerLookupError, which the dispatch route turns into a fault instead of\na false success.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): order superseded history records by append order\n\n`superseded` sorted on `right.ts - left.ts`, which returns 0 on a tie; the\nstable sort then left tied records in append order, oldest first, contradicting\nthe \"newest first\" contract on HistoryDayGroup. Ties became a supported case\nwhen latestOutcomesByDate started breaking them with `>=`, and the dockerized\ne2e stack freezes the clock, so there every record ties.\n\nThe ledger is append-only, so append order is chronological order: reversing the\nday''s bucket is both correct and cheaper than sorting.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): warn when listAccounts skips an unreadable record\n\nA corrupt or schema-mismatched account record was dropped silently, so the\nroster simply came back short and the symptom read as \"an account vanished\"\nrather than as the data corruption it is. Name the key in a console.warn.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* build: pin corepack to 0.35.0 across images and dev compose\n\n`npm install -g corepack@latest` ran in five image build stages and four dev\ncompose services. There is no CI and the Pi deploy is manual with a 30-minute\nbuild timeout, so a breaking corepack release would only ever surface as a\nfailed deploy. Pin one explicit version everywhere.\n\nVerified: `docker build -f packages/server/Dockerfile` succeeds and the built\nimage reports corepack 0.35.0 with pnpm 10.28.2.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/sessi…","tree":{"sha":"37029399fb18059d4abcbdbca9bae02a3fe457ea","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/git/trees/37029399fb18059d4abcbdbca9bae02a3fe457ea"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmilevoi/myboard/git/commits/7d06bcae7f4543a188e8beaeb0c809d39f9c1080","verification":{"payload":"tree 37029399fb18059d4abcbdbca9bae02a3fe457ea\nparent b8b2be610ada3d3b9c03ba49919dcc69bb75130a\nauthor khmilevoi <Khmilevoi@gmail.com> 1785237975 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1785237975 +0200\n\nrelease: authored ofelia ledger, passport-checker SP7, responsive board, widget server cron (#27)\n\n* style(docs): format the passport widget plan and spec with oxfmt\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GFDdxaQ8MLEM6eoyUxPm3f\n\n* test(client): include the passport widget icon in the registry expectation\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GFDdxaQ8MLEM6eoyUxPm3f\n\n* test(ofelia-poop-duty): give the polyfill-import test a load-tolerant timeout\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GFDdxaQ8MLEM6eoyUxPm3f\n\n* fix(passport-checker): make the recovery modal survive a modal radix layer underneath\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GFDdxaQ8MLEM6eoyUxPm3f\n\n* chore: fix mf dts\n\n* docs(passport-checker): spec the tier-shared state follow-up\n\nCovers the per-instance model store, the StrictMode removal it requires,\nfullscreen collapse/restore around recovery, modal focus containment, and\nwhy the proposed focusout tightening is rejected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): plan the tier-shared state follow-up\n\nSeven tasks: formatting debt, the per-instance store, the lease hook plus\nStrictMode removal, fullscreen collapse/restore, modal focus containment,\nthe focusout-guard regression test, and the doc back-links.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(deploy): add a dev environment overlay on the dev branch\n\nrpi.dev.toml deploys the dev branch to board-dev.iiskelo.com as the\nseparate project myboard--dev, inheriting everything else from rpi.toml.\nThe agent runs compose with -p, so the environment gets its own\ncontainers, network and volumes.\n\nTwo things in docker-compose.yml blocked a second stack on the same Pi\nand are now variable, with the production values as defaults so the prod\ndeploy and the local tooling render byte-for-byte identically:\n\n- The fixed host ports 8080/6379/6080 are bound by the running prod\n  stack; rpi only allocates a port for the ingress service and appends\n  it, so a dev deploy would fail on the binds.\n- RP_ID/RP_NAME/PUBLIC_APP_URL/EXPECTED_ORIGIN were pinned to the\n  production hostname. WebAuthn scopes credentials to RP_ID and verifies\n  assertions against EXPECTED_ORIGIN, so on another hostname the gate\n  rejects every ceremony without failing the deploy.\n\n.env.dev carries the dev values and stays untracked, like .env;\n.env.dev.example documents it. infra.test.ts gains guards for the two\nsilent-failure modes: hostname/RP_ID drift, and dev host ports landing\non a prod bind or inside rpi''s 8000-8999 allocator range.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(widget-sdk): move the instance store into the shared widget helpers\n\nThe mechanism is not passport-specific: it follows from the board mounting a\nplaced widget twice while fullscreen is open. Spec and plan now put a generic\nmakeWidgetInstanceStore factory and useWidgetInstance hook in widget-sdk, with\nthe widget keeping only its typing and disposal policy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(widget-sdk): let reatom connection lifetime own the instance store\n\nMeasured against @reatom/core@1001.1.0: withConnectHook fires on the first\nsubscriber and its cleanup on the last, which is the reference counting the\nstore was hand-rolling. The React lease hook disappears entirely — a reatomMemo\ncomponent that reads the instance atom IS the reference.\n\nTwo findings the measurement pinned down: a computed''s cache survives\ndisconnection, so the disconnect hook must drop the handle or the next mount\nresurrects a disposed value; and StrictMode still has to go, because its remount\ncycle disposes a value while both components are still mounted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widgets): stop the client host build on widgets with no client.ts\n\ndiscoverWidgetIds (widget-build-assets.ts, used only by the client host''s\nstageWidgetBuilds plugin) required every packages/widgets/* directory with\na package.json to have a dist/, the same way scripts/codegen/client.ts\ndiscovers widget candidates. But codegen goes one step further and only\ntreats a widget as a federation remote once it has a client.ts -- exactly\nthe state passport-checker is in right now (browser-task subprojects only,\nits widget UI hasn''t landed on this branch yet). Never having a build\nscript, it never produces a dist/, so every client build broke with\nMissingWidgetBuildError, both locally and on the Pi during\n`rpi deploy --env dev`.\n\nFilter discoverWidgetIds by client.ts too, matching codegen''s criterion.\nAlso set dts: false on the client''s own federation() call (mirroring what\nevery widget''s own config already sets): the host build was unconditionally\nattempting Module Federation''s DTS generation, which -- since widgets are\nconsumed dynamically via loadRemote + the generated catalog, never through\na typed remote import -- had nothing to generate and always failed with a\ncaught-but-alarming \"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading\n''readFile'')\" on every build.\n\nAdd docs/** to both oxfmt''s and oxlint''s ignorePatterns: docs/ carries a\nlot of pre-formatted design/spec markdown and an embedded HTML mockup that\noxfmt doesn''t reflow the same way as their original authoring tools, so a\nroutine `pnpm format` run was rewriting ~18 unrelated files.\n\nAlso drop the leftover PASSPORT_SERIES/PASSPORT_NUMBER lines from\n.env.dev.example: the passport-checker widget reads its series/number from\nplain-value files under the widget package, not from env vars (see\npackages/browser-automation/README.md#provisioning-secrets), so document\nthat instead.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: record the shared widget modal primitive in BACKLOG.md\n\nuseModalIsolation is a hand-rolled modal owned by one widget, and its isolation\nleans on radix-ui internals that can change in a patch release without failing\nanything but our one deliberate stack test. Capture the follow-up, its trigger,\nand the two shapes worth considering instead of leaving it implicit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* chore(format): apply oxfmt to pre-existing drift\n\nThese 18 files predate this branch and were already failing\npnpm format:check on main. Formatting them here makes the branch\ngate readable.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* style(ofelia-poop-duty): wrap the over-width test declaration\n\nCommit 3eee2f2 on this branch added a timeout option that pushed the `it(...)`\ndeclaration past oxfmt''s print width, so `pnpm format:check` has been red on a\nbranch-authored file. This is the branch''s own drift, not the pre-existing debt\nthe previous commit cleared.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(widget-sdk): add a connection-scoped widget instance store\n\nThe board mounts a placed widget twice while fullscreen is open — once as a\ntile, once in the overlay — so per-mount useMemo state is built twice and lost\non every tier switch. Widgets can now share one value per instanceId, built for\nthe first subscriber and disposed when the last one disconnects.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): make instance-store disconnect race-safe\n\nSwitch the widget instance store from withDisconnectHook to\nwithConnectHook so build and dispose are paired per connect episode,\nmatching the clock-model.ts convention:\n\n- Guard the deferred disconnect cleanup with isConnected(handle) so an\n  unsubscribe immediately followed by a resubscribe of the same key in\n  one synchronous flush (React StrictMode''s double-invoked effects, a\n  same-commit re-key) no longer disposes a value a live mount is still\n  rendering.\n- Only delete the key''s map entry when it still points at the handle\n  running the cleanup, so a stale disconnect can no longer evict a\n  newer, currently-connected handle for the same key.\n- Rebuild the value in the connect hook''s setup too, so a genuine\n  reconnect after disposal gets a fresh value instead of resurrecting\n  a disposed one.\n- Correct the module JSDoc: the value is built lazily on first read\n  (unavoidable, since a component''s initial render reads before its\n  effect subscribes), not \"for the first subscriber\"; only disposal is\n  actually governed by the connection lifetime. Note that a bare read\n  with no subscribing component never triggers dispose.\n\nAdds a regression test reproducing the same-tick unsubscribe/\nresubscribe race directly against the atom''s subscribe API.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): close the orphaned-read leak and stop re-poisoning the instance map\n\nA bare store(key, make)() read outside any subscription built a value that\ndispose never ran for, and left a permanent handles entry: the compute\nfunction now gives an orphaned first build one microtask to be claimed by a\nreal subscriber before tearing it down. The connect hook also stopped\nre-registering its handle in the map on every connect, which was letting a\nstale handle reconnected directly (bypassing store()) repoint future lookups\nat an already-torn-down value; the identity-guarded delete already keeps a\nstale disconnect from evicting a newer handle, so the map only ever reflects\na handle that was actually looked up through the factory.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): drop the timer-based orphan-read teardown in instance-store\n\nThe round-2 fix scheduled a queueMicrotask() to dispose a value that no\nsubscriber had claimed yet, assuming a real mount''s useLayoutEffect\nsubscribe always lands in the same task as its render-phase read. React 19\ntime-slices Suspense retry lanes (every widget renders under lazy +\nSuspense) and resumes via a scheduler macrotask, so that assumption does\nnot hold: the microtask could fire and dispose a value a live mount was\nstill about to read, corrupting a dependency-free computed forever and\nsplitting the map entry across mounts of the same instance.\n\nDisposal is now driven ONLY by Reatom''s real connect/disconnect lifecycle,\nwhich is timing-safe by construction. The tradeoff, documented in the\nmodule doc comment: a bare read with no component ever mounting still\nleaks its built value, since nothing ever subscribes to disconnect it.\nNothing outside this store''s own test currently reads it that way.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): share one model graph across widget mounts\n\nThe board tile and the fullscreen overlay mount the same instance twice, so\nper-mount useMemo models lost every result on a tier switch. Read one shared\ninstance instead, and let only the non-fullscreen mount own the recovery modal.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(widget-sdk): pin the instance store''s StrictMode tolerance\n\nStrictMode''s development-only mount -> unmount -> mount cycle empties the\nsubscriber set, which an earlier draft of this store answered by disposing a\nvalue both mounted components were still rendering. The connect hook''s\nisConnected re-check closes that; this test is what keeps it closed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): collapse fullscreen for recovery and restore it after\n\nOpening recovery from the fullscreen mount now collapses the board''s Radix\ndialog through requestClose, so the modal is never stacked over a trapped\nFocusScope; closing or retrying restores fullscreen through requestFullscreen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): wrap the recovery-open handler in each tier''s own frame\n\nPassportChecker built openRecovery with wrap() once per its own render and\nhanded the same closure to StandardTier/TinyTier, but those tiers re-render\nindependently on their own viewState() changes. The closure stayed bound to\nPassportChecker''s stale render frame, so clicking \"Открыть восстановление\"\nafter an idle->pending->sessionRequired transition (without the parent also\nre-rendering) could silently do nothing -- the root cause of the ~10% flake\nin ui/recovery-flow.test.tsx. PassportChecker now builds a plain (unwrapped)\ncomposition function and each tier wraps it fresh in its own render, mirroring\nthe existing `check` handler pattern.\n\nAlso adds a UI test for the restoreFullscreen wiring''s actual cross-mount\nhandoff: recovery opened from a fullscreen mount, then the tile mount (same\ninstanceId) inherits restorePending and calls its own requestFullscreen on\nclose -- the one assertion that would have caught restoreFullscreen wired to\nthe wrong callback.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): widen recovery-flow.test.tsx''s test budget to stop whole-suite timeouts\n\nThe new fullscreen/tile recovery tests render through Radix''s dialog/focus\nmachinery across two model mounts, which occasionally pushed real wall-clock\ntime past the shared 5s default under whole-suite worker contention (never a\nlogic hang). Set testTimeout to 30000ms for this file only, verified against\nzero reproductions of the line-122 timeout across dozens of whole-suite runs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): root-cause the recovery-flow Escape-close race\n\nfindByRole(''dialog'') resolves as soon as the dialog''s DOM node commits,\nbut useModalIsolation''s mount effect - which attaches the Escape\nlistener and moves focus in - is a passive effect that can still be\npending a tick later. Firing Escape into that gap dispatched into a\ndocument with no listener yet, so the dialog never closed and the\nfollowing waitFor spun until it timed out; reproduced solo (no\nwhole-suite contention needed) in roughly 1 of 15-40 runs.\n\nEach of the two tests that close the modal now waits for the effect''s\nsynchronous side effect (focus landing inside the dialog) before\ndispatching Escape, which proves the same effect has also attached the\nlistener. Replaces the prior fix round''s blanket file-wide 30s test\ntimeout (and its comment''s now-falsified \"never a hang\" claim) with a\nscoped 10s per-test timeout on only the two tests that actually run\nthis round trip, as a safety margin for ordinary contention rather\nthan a mask for this race.\n\nVerified with 40 solo + 32 self-inflicted-parallel + 8 whole-suite\nreruns after the fix, zero reproductions of the timeout.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): hold focus inside the recovery modal\n\nRadix restores focus from a zero-delay timeout when its dialog unmounts, so\ncollapsing fullscreen for recovery handed focus to the board a tick after the\nmodal mounted. Pull focus back whenever it lands outside the modal.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the unmount-cleanup test teeth and fix a flaky Escape assert\n\nRecoveryModal.test.tsx''s `leaves focus alone once the modal is gone` test\npassed even when the cleanup''s `removeEventListener(''focusin'', ...)` was\ndeleted, or the whole effect cleanup was zeroed out: after unmount the\nportal root is detached, so a leaked focusin listener calling `.focus()` on\na detached descendant is a jsdom/browser no-op and the activeElement assert\nstays green regardless. Pin the actual cleanup contract instead: spy on\nwindow.addEventListener/removeEventListener and assert the exact `focusin`\nlistener useModalIsolation registers on mount is the one removed on unmount,\nalongside the original behavioral assert.\n\nrecovery-flow.test.tsx''s first test asserted\n`dialog.contains(document.activeElement)` synchronously right after\n`findByRole(''dialog'')` resolves on DOM commit, before dispatching Escape.\n`useModalIsolation`''s mount effect (which moves focus in and attaches the\nEscape listener) is passive and can still be pending a tick later, so\nEscape could fire before the listener existed and the test would hang out\nits timeout — the same race Task 4 root-caused and fixed in this file''s\nother two Escape-closing tests by waiting for focus-in-dialog first. Apply\nthe same `await waitFor(...)` wrap here and correct the file-level comment,\nwhich had claimed only two tests reach the Escape-close race when this one\ndoes too.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the modal focusout guard against narrowing\n\nMount the underlying Radix dialog in its own pass, as production does, and\ncover the intra-modal focus move that the proposed guard narrowing would have\nbroken silently. The regression test dispatches the underlying focusout event\ndirectly rather than chaining native .focus() calls: jsdom (like real\nbrowsers) always completes an outer .focus() call on its own target once its\nhandler stack unwinds, so a settled document.activeElement assertion can''t\nobserve a reclaim a nested handler only ever wins transiently — verified\nempirically against the narrowed guard.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): close a cross-test focus-restore leak in the Radix stack suite\n\nRadix''s FocusScope defers its unmount focus-restore to a real setTimeout(0),\nand testing-library''s auto cleanup() never waits for it, so a prior test''s\ndeferred restore (and an unawaited NoVncCanvas state update from that same\ntest''s async recovery flow) can bleed into the next test — confirmed by\ninstrumentation. A formal trace of the reentrant focus cascade shows the\n''pulls focus back...'' assertion itself can''t actually be flipped by this\n(no yield point exists between its synchronous .focus() call and its\nexpect()), but the leak is real and pre-existing to any suite that\nmounts+unmounts a trapped FocusScope, so close it defensively: flush one\nreal tick after each test''s cleanup so Radix''s deferred work always\nfinishes before the next test starts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): drain Radix''s focus-restore timer from beforeEach, not afterEach\n\ne797d50''s message overstated what that change achieved. It claimed the added\nafterEach ran \"strictly after cleanup()\" and closed the cross-test focus-restore\nleak. It did neither. Vitest resolves sequence.hooks to \"stack\", so same-suite\nafterEach hooks run in REVERSE registration order, and testing-library''s\ncleanup() -- registered first, at setupFiles import time -- therefore runs last.\nThe file-level afterEach ran BEFORE cleanup(), i.e. before the unmount that\nschedules Radix''s setTimeout(0) focus restore, so it drained nothing.\n\nInstrumenting setTimeout and the hook boundaries on this file shows it directly:\nat afterEach entry the modal is still in the DOM and no FocusScope timer is\npending; the timer is scheduled only after the hook returns; the next test''s\nbeforeEach starts with one still queued. Moving the same one-tick flush to\nbeforeEach takes that count to zero before the test body runs. beforeEach is\ncorrect by construction -- it always follows the previous test''s afterEach\nhooks whatever the hook-order mode.\n\nCorrect the scope of the claim too. The restore is focus-neutral in this\nfixture: the previouslyFocusedElement it captured is either document.body,\nwhich jsdom refuses to focus, or an already-detached node, and every observed\nfiring left document.activeElement unchanged. The intermittent failure this was\nchasing did not reproduce at e797d50 in 30 consecutive single-file runs or 6\nwhole-package runs. So this closes a scheduling leak, not an observed failure,\nand the comment now says exactly that instead of claiming otherwise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): link the tier-shared-state follow-up\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): drop the phantom rebuild from the instance store''s connect hook\n\nThe connect hook carried `current ??= { value: make() }` behind a comment\nclaiming it gave a reconnect after disposal a fresh value. Measured against\n@reatom/core@1001.1.0 with the shipped code:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 2\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"],[{\"id\":2},\"a\"]]   built = 2\n\nThe reconnect still returned the DISPOSED value — a dependency-free `computed`\nnever recomputes, so its cached result is what every later read gets. The line\nonly built a second model graph nobody could reach and then handed it to\n`dispose` a second time. Same run with the line removed:\n\n    after first subscribe:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after unsubscribe:        disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n    after STALE reconnect:    read = {\"id\":1}   built = 1\n    after second unsubscribe: disposed = [[{\"id\":1},\"a\"]]   built = 1\n\nThe comment now states what is true: a stale reconnect reads the cached,\nalready-disposed value, and a fresh value comes only from a new `store()`\nlookup — which is what every real caller does, because it calls `store()`\nduring render and the cleanup has already evicted the stale handle.\n\nWith the phantom build gone, `if (held)` is what prevents the double dispose,\nso a test pins it: replacing it with an unconditional `dispose?.(held?.value,\nkey)` now fails with a second teardown recorded as `[undefined, \"a\"]`.\n\nAlso corrects the module doc''s cost claim for reading atoms inside `make`.\nIt does not \"silently rebuild the whole value\": measured, after such a\ndependency changes the compute function re-runs once, `current ??=`\nshort-circuits it so `make` does not re-run (built stayed 1, the identical\nobject was returned, no subscriber notified), and because the re-run never\nreads the atom again the edge is dropped. The cost is a pointless dependency\nedge, not lost state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): align the tier-shared-state spec with what shipped\n\nThree claims in the design spec described work that was never delivered:\n\n- The instance atom was said to carry `withDisconnectHook`. It carries\n  `withConnectHook`, whose cleanup calls `teardownIfDisconnected()` — which\n  re-checks `isConnected` before disposing and evicts the map entry only while\n  it still points at the handle being torn down.\n- Section 4 \"StrictMode removal\" claimed `<StrictMode>` was dropped from\n  `packages/client/src/app/main.tsx` and replaced by a comment explaining its\n  absence. None of it shipped: `git diff cfff8d2..HEAD -- packages/client/` is\n  empty and the wrapper is still there. Measurement showed the store survives\n  the remount cycle, because the cleanup''s `isConnected` re-check returns\n  without touching a value a live mount still holds. Section 4 is rewritten as\n  \"StrictMode kept, tolerance pinned by test\", stating why the plan''s original\n  reasoning does not apply.\n- The Testing gate, the Documentation list and the Decision Summary repeated\n  the removal. They now say StrictMode stays and that the tolerance is pinned\n  by `describe(''makeWidgetInstanceStore under StrictMode'')`.\n\nThat last claim is measured, not assumed: deleting the `isConnected` re-check\nfrom the cleanup turns two of the three StrictMode tests red, along with the\nnon-StrictMode same-tick-resubscribe test.\n\nThe plan file is left untouched — it is the historical record of what was\nintended.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* test(passport-checker): narrow unmeasured claims and close two coverage gaps\n\nComment/claim accuracy — all of these stated more than was ever measured:\n\n- `ui/recovery-flow.test.tsx`: the \"roughly 1 in 15-40 runs\" hang frequency\n  was applied to every Escape-closing test in the file. It was measured for\n  exactly one of them, ''does not collapse when recovery opens from the tile''\n  (task-4-report.md, Fix round 3). The others carry the same wait by\n  inspection — identical shape, identical race — with no observed rate. The\n  comment now says so.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"at `beforeEach` entry one (later\n  two) FocusScope timer is pending\" — only the 1 -> 0 transition was ever\n  recorded, so the \"(later two)\" is dropped.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: \"jsdom (like real browsers) always\n  completes an outer `.focus()` call on its own target\" — true and measured\n  for jsdom, never measured against a real browser. Scoped to jsdom.\n- `ui/recovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx`: ''keeps focus inside when it moves\n  between two of our own controls'' promised a focus invariant the test does\n  not assert — it asserts that our guard swallows the `focusout` before it\n  reaches `document`. Renamed to say that.\n\nCoverage:\n\n- `model/instance-store.test.ts` (new) covers `dispose: (models) =>\n  models.recoveryModel.teardown()`, the safety net against leaking a live VNC\n  session when the modal outlived the widget. It had no test at all: replacing\n  the callback with a no-op left the whole widget suite green. Verified both\n  new tests fail against that no-op.\n- `ui/PassportChecker.test.tsx`: two assertions used\n  `expect(await screen.findAllBy*(…)).toHaveLength(2)`, which cannot retry —\n  `findAllBy*` resolves on the first snapshot with >= 1 match, so it settles on\n  one mount and never waits for the second. Both are now a re-querying\n  `waitFor`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(backlog): record the two-modal focus ping-pong as a deferred defect\n\nBoth `useModalIsolation` instances register focus containment on `window` in\nthe capture phase, so two mounted modals each see the other''s `focusin` before\nthe root-level `stopPropagation()` can stop it, and each pulls focus into its\nown root — forever.\n\nReproduced in jsdom 29.1.1 with two `useModalIsolation` roots mounted at once:\none modal performs 1 focus pull on mount; two mounted together never settle,\nand a hard test guard at 400 pulls had to stop the run (402 recorded).\n\nDeferred rather than fixed because it is unreachable through the UI: the\npassport checker is the only owner of such a modal, renders at most one\n(non-fullscreen mount only), and its overlay is a full-viewport\n`pointer-events: auto` layer whose `pointerdown` handler closes the modal, so\na second one cannot be opened over the first.\n\nThe entry notes the likely fix — a module-level stack of active modal roots\nwith containment applied only by the topmost.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(passport-checker): correct five unverified claims left by code review\n\nBring wording in the tier-shared-state spec, BACKLOG.md, and a test\ncomment in line with what was actually measured/read: the instance\nstore''s dependency-edge cost (not a rebuilt value), a stale\nuse-modal-isolation.ts line citation (:53 -> :55, two places), the\nroot focusin stopper''s actual bubble phase (not capture), and which\nrecovery-flow tests keep the shared timeout default.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay at this branch\n\nPins rpi.dev.toml''s source.branch to feat/passport-checker-widget so the dev\nenvironment runs this PR instead of the dev branch.\n\nREVERT BEFORE MERGING: once this PR lands on dev, source.branch must go back to\n\"dev\" or the dev overlay stays pinned to a branch that no longer moves.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): drop the stale Chromium singleton lock before launch\n\nThe Chromium profile is a named Docker volume, so `SingletonLock` keeps the\n`<hostname>-<pid>` of whichever container wrote it. After a redeploy the new\ncontainer has a new hostname, Chromium reads a lock owned by \"another computer\"\nit cannot probe, and `launchPersistentContext` fails on every task until the\nvolume is touched by hand.\n\nRemove `SingletonLock`, `SingletonCookie`, and `SingletonSocket` before each\nlaunch. `fs.rm` unlinks the symlinks themselves instead of following them, and\n`force` keeps a fresh profile from failing; a removal that does fail is returned\nas a value, logged, and the launch still proceeds. Unconditional removal is safe\nbecause `getPersistentContext` is the only caller of `launch` and the image\nentrypoint runs a single `node dist/index.cjs` - no check for a live Chromium is\nperformed or implied.\n\nAlso asserts that a failed launch keeps its cause, which is what makes the\nPlaywright message reachable from the service log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): log the error behind an internal task failure\n\nInternal failures were logged as `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` only, so the\ntagged error and its cause chain never reached the container log. A Chromium\nlaunch failure therefore showed up as a bare `internal` code with nothing to\ndiagnose. Add the error to the logged fields; `toEnvelopeError` still answers\nthe client with the redacted `internal` code, so only the log gains detail.\n\nNote the trade-off: a handler error whose message carries task data now reaches\nthe container log, which the previous field list deliberately avoided.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(browser-automation): redact handler failures in the internal task log\n\nfa87a47c logged too much. It added `error: outcome` to the internal-failure log\nso a Chromium launch failure would stop being invisible, but that field carries\nwhatever the handler threw. errore''s `toJSON()` serialises `message`, `stack`,\nand the whole `cause` chain, Playwright quotes `page.evaluate` arguments into\nits messages, and the passport widget passes the secret identity as one — so a\nhandler failure could print the passport series and number into the container\nlog. The wrapper''s own `stack` repeats the cause under `Caused by:`, so the\nidentity landed there twice.\n\nThat contradicts the design spec: raw Playwright, network, Zod, and upstream\nresponse details are logged only after redaction, request and response bodies\nare never logged, and logs redact task payloads and secret values\n(docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md\n:350-353, :684).\n\nThe rule now, verified against dispatch.ts:\n\n- `BrowserExecutorError` wraps a failed `executor.acquire(...)`. Acquire runs\n  before the handler and is passed only `(signal, widgetId)` — never the payload\n  or the widget secrets — so its cause chain is Chromium/Playwright launch\n  detail. Still logged in full; this is the stale singleton lock failure\n  fa87a47c existed to surface.\n- Every other internal failure wraps handler output and is reduced to\n  `errorChain`: the names/tags of the error and its `cause` chain and nothing\n  else. No `message`, no `stack`, no foreign properties.\n\nThe log message, the `{ widgetId, taskId, code }` fields, and the\n`code === ''internal''` condition are unchanged.\n\nTests split the single fa87a47c case in two: one pins that an executor failure''s\ncause chain still reaches the log, one asserts a secret-looking cause string is\nabsent from the serialised logged payload. Both were confirmed to fail before\nthe fix and to discriminate after it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add an env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe passport widget''s recovery path (retain the page, issue a single-use\ncapability, stream the live browser over noVNC, let a human solve the\nchallenge, retry) has never been exercised on real hardware, because it only\ntriggers when pasport.org.ua serves a Cloudflare challenge and none has been\nencountered — the smoke test on the Pi returned a normal result. Subproject 8\n(`passport-checker-rpi-integration` in\ndocs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-passport-checker-browser-automation-design.md)\nhas \"embedded noVNC Cloudflare recovery, SSH fallback, and real checker smoke\ntest on the Raspberry Pi\" as an acceptance item and had no way to reach it on\ndemand. PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY is that way.\n\nContract: `1` and nothing else enables it — absent, empty, `0`, `true`, ` 1 `\nare all off (isForcedRecoveryEnabled does exact string equality, no trimming,\nno truthiness coercion). Compose passes it through with a `:-` default so an\nunset variable stays off, and it must never be set in production.\n\nSemantics: at most once per process. The latch lives in the closure\nmakePassportCheckHandler returns, so each handler owns its own and the unit\ntests stay isolated. It fires after a successful goto and after\ncollectNavigationEvidence — the retained page is the real checker page with the\nsite''s own UI loaded, so a human can drive it over noVNC — and returns exactly\nthe retainPageForRecovery() + BrowserSessionRequiredError pair the genuine\nCloudflare branch returns, making the downstream flow byte-identical to a real\nchallenge. The latch is consumed only when the forced path actually fires, so a\nfailed navigation returns its own error untouched and leaves the lever armed:\nan infrastructure hiccup must not burn the single forced run an operator is\nwaiting on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(passport-checker): apply the design-system widget redesign\n\nBring the tile and the recovery modal in line with Паспорт-чекер.dc.html.\n\nBoth tiers now lay out as header -> flexible middle -> action, so the\naction sits on the tile''s bottom edge in every state instead of trailing\nwhatever content happens to be above it. Status banners pick up the\ndesign''s per-state hues, the filled success disc, and the CircleAlert /\nTriangleAlert split that separates a retryable failure from a session\nprompt. The modal gains the access-pill dot, a pulsing LIVE dot, framed\nconnecting/disconnected/expired states, and the shell-style SSH block.\n\nDark theme was effectively missing: the --pc-* hues chained to\n--success-soft / --destructive-soft, which the host token set never\ndefines, and to --success, which is a dark surface green rather than an\nicon green under [data-theme=''dark'']. Every hue is now declared per\ntheme the way ofelia-poop-duty does it. The modal portals to\ndocument.body, outside .widget, so it declares the one hue it needs on\nits own overlay.\n\nThree deliberate departures from the mockup:\n- invalidConfig copy stays server-side (\"Обратитесь к администратору\"),\n  since this widget has no per-instance settings — its config lives in\n  server secrets.\n- the expired frame keeps \"Переподключиться\"; without it an expired\n  capability can only be recovered by closing and reopening the modal.\n- the sessionRequired tiny tile only fills amber, no border: the tile\n  border belongs to the host card, and drawing one from inside the\n  widget would render a second, inset contour.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014xwGNctwqXcYi5p4QSSFhA\n\n* docs(browser-automation): design widget user-input detection\n\nMove the \"a human must act\" mechanism out of passport-checker and into the\nautomation platform. The handler gets context.detectUserInput(detector), which\nruns the detector, optionally prepares the page, retains it and returns the\ncanonical error as one indivisible step; retainPageForRecovery leaves the public\ncontext so the halves cannot be separated. Cloudflare becomes a reusable helper\n(probe and predicate together, not just the predicate) that any widget can pass\nin. Also records the removal of the PASSPORT_FORCE_RECOVERY lever.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): correct the user-input spec''s test-seam claims\n\nmakeFakeExecutor fakes BrowserExecutor<FakeContext> with FakeContext = { id,\nsignal }; it never builds a BrowserTaskContext, so it cannot host a recording\ndetectUserInput and needs no change at all. Name the seams that do exist: the\nsix retainPageForRecovery call sites in chromium-executor.test.ts and the three\nBrowserTaskContext literals in diagnostics.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): plan the widget user-input detection refactor\n\nSeven tasks, each green on its own: retire the forced-recovery lever, add the\ndetector and error contracts, move Cloudflare detection into the platform with\nits probe reunited with its predicate, run escalation from the task context,\nrewire the widget, then withdraw retainPageForRecovery from the public context so\nthe retain/escalate pair cannot be taken apart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* docs(browser-automation): make the Cloudflare task a move, not a copy\n\nTask 3 previously left the classifier duplicated between the widget and the\nplatform until Task 5, which any reviewer would rightly flag as verbatim\nduplication of a logic block. The widget now switches to the moved module in the\nsame task, so the logic never exists in two places.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* revert(passport-checker): drop the env-gated forced recovery lever\n\nThe lever forced one trip through the Cloudflare recovery path per service\nstart so Subproject 8 could exercise noVNC on the Pi on demand. It is being\nretired deliberately ahead of moving user-input detection into the platform;\nthe Pi smoke test needs another answer.\n\nThis reverts the behaviour added in 45c604f1.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(browser-automation): add user-input detector and error contracts\n\nUserInputRequiredError carries the browser_session_required code and sshTarget\nmeta the recovery flow keys off, moved out of the passport-checker widget where\na platform contract had no business living. UserInputProbeError distinguishes\na broken check from a negative one.\n\n* feat(browser-automation): move Cloudflare detection into the platform\n\nThe classifier lived in the passport-checker widget while the DOM probe feeding\nit lived in that widget''s check handler, so the only reusable half was the half\nwithout the probe. Both now sit in browser-automation behind two detector\nfactories: one that probes the live page, one that classifies evidence already\nderived inside the page from a fetch response.\n\nThe widget imports the moved module rather than keeping a copy; it still calls\nthe classifier by hand, which the next commit changes.\n\n* @\ndocs: document the feature branch -> dev -> main workflow\n\nFeature work happens in a dedicated branch inside a ./.worktrees checkout,\nlands in dev through a PR, gets verified on the dev stack, and only then\nreaches main through a dev -> main PR.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* feat(browser-automation): run user-input detection from the task context\n\ndetectUserInput runs a widget-supplied detector and, on a match, prepares the\npage, retains it and returns UserInputRequiredError as one step, so the two\nhalves cannot drift apart. A detector that fails yields UserInputProbeError and\ndeliberately leaves the page unretained: an undecidable check is not a challenge,\nand pinning a browser page for a recovery nobody will perform is worse.\n\nAUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET moves into the service config, where an unusable value\ndegrades to null rather than failing config loading and killing the service.\n\n* docs(passport-checker): design last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* test(browser-automation): cover the prepare-before-retain order in detectUserInput directly\n\nThe executor-level test asserted hasRetainedPage() from inside the prepare\nmock, but that bookkeeping is only populated at release() time, so it passed\nregardless of call order. Add a direct unit test against makeDetectUserInput\nthat records prepare/retain into one ordered array — genuinely fails if the\norder in detect.ts is inverted (verified by temporarily inverting it).\n\nNo production behavior changes; detect.ts ends identical to before this commit.\n\n* @\ndocs: merge AGENTS.md into CLAUDE.md and symlink it\n\nAGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md had drifted into two partly overlapping rule sets.\nEverything unique to AGENTS.md (project structure, codegen commands, Windows\ntest-runner notes, coding style, UI gotchas, testing, security, failure modes)\nnow lives in CLAUDE.md, and AGENTS.md becomes a symlink so every agent reads\nthe same file.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UiWozKdReG1rgTfvtj9mo3\n@\n\n* docs(passport-checker): plan last-result persistence\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* feat(passport-checker): date-aware check timestamp label\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout design\n\nTwo width breakpoints instead of one hardcoded grid: one column below 768,\ntwelve above, with row height and margin scaled by the same factor as the\ncolumn width so the desktop grid zooms instead of stretching on one axis.\n\nThe mobile layout is derived from the desktop one and only persisted once the\nuser drags or resizes, stored as an additive optional `mobileLayout` field so\nno migration of `root:boards` is needed.\n\nAlso records the react-draggable touchstart defect: the whole card is the drag\nhandle today, which cancels page scrolling on touch — harmless on a short\n12-column board, blocking at one column.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): detect required user input through the platform\n\nThe handler no longer collects Cloudflare evidence, classifies it, retains the\npage and builds the escalation error by hand at every site; it passes a detector\nto context.detectUserInput and returns what comes back. browser/challenge.ts and\nthe widget-local BrowserSessionRequiredError are gone — the classifier now lives\nin browser-automation next to the probe that feeds it, and the\nbrowser_session_required contract belongs to the platform that owns the recovery\nflow.\n\n* fix(passport-checker): restore the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nAn automated amend from a concurrent session accidentally stripped this\nline out of 3a12494e. It belongs to the user-input-detection work; this\nrestores it verbatim.\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin the user_input_probe retryable message\n\nRETRYABLE_MESSAGES.user_input_probe vanished twice with no test turning red,\nbecause nothing asserted the actual behaviour: with the entry gone,\nmapCheckError silently falls back to GENERIC_RETRYABLE_MESSAGE and the user\nsees the generic string instead of the specific one. Assert the real output\nof mapCheckError against a hardcoded expected string, not against the table\nitself, so a future deletion or typo of the entry fails this test instead of\ncomparing the map against itself.\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron design spec\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(board): add the responsive board layout implementation plan\n\n* feat(board): resolve grid metrics from the container width\n\n* feat(passport-checker): persist the last successful check\n\n* feat(board): derive and reconcile the one-column mobile layout\n\n* docs(cron): add the widget server cron implementation plan\n\nAlso corrects the spec: the jsdom Temporal shims must stay, only the\n--harmony-temporal execArgv becomes redundant on Node 26.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* test(passport-checker): pin which detector goes to which detectUserInput call\n\nNothing previously checked that the navigation check gets the page detector\nand the submission check gets the evidence detector -- only the second call''s\nprepare option was asserted. A handler that swapped the two factories would\nleave every test in this file green; only the gated real-browser suite would\nnotice, and it isn''t run in CI. Pull the captured detector off each mock call\nand invoke it directly: the page detector must reach for page.evaluate, the\nevidence detector must ignore its page argument and classify the real\nevidence. Also drops the now-inert hasChallengeForm evidence field from the\nsubmission-escalation test, whose result was always driven by the mock''s\nescalation queue regardless of that field.\n\n* feat(board): add mobile layout materialize, update and reset actions\n\n* refactor(browser-automation): expose only detectUserInput on the task context\n\nretainPageForRecovery was half of a pair a handler had to assemble correctly by\nhand: retaining without escalating strands a browser page, escalating without\nretaining hands the UI a recovery session with nothing behind it. With the retain\nflag reachable only through detectUserInput, neither mistake is expressible.\n\n* test(passport-checker): cover restore-on-mount and cross-placement sync\n\nAdd two widget-level cases pinning storage-backed persistence through\nReact: a fresh mount renders a previously stored result without\ninvoking a check, and a check made in one placement is observed by a\nsecond placement sharing the same storage. Rename the pre-existing\n\"gives separate instance ids separate state\" case to \"gives separate\nstorages separate state\" (it isolates by storage, not instanceId) and\nupdate its comment to point at the new cross-placement case below it.\nPin PASSPORT_LAST_RESULT_KEY''s literal value in check-model.test.ts so\na silent rename cannot slip through green while orphaning deployed\nusers'' stored data under the old key.\n\n* feat(board): switch the grid to resolved metrics and a mobile drag grip\n\n* docs(browser-automation): document how a widget requests manual input\n\n* feat(board): offer a mobile layout reset in the board schema popover\n\n* fix(widgets): address passport-checker persistence review findings\n\n- TinyTier''s success chip now shows checkedAtLabel, so a restored (possibly\n  stale) result reads honestly in the compact tile too, not just standard.\n- Harden the two-placement isolation test: assert placement two stays idle\n  instead of a length-1 findAllByText that can pass mid-race.\n- Clarify why lastResult/transient set order is safe in checkPassport\n  (Reatom notifies after the sync block, not \"order for readability\").\n- Add a test exercising lastResultSchema.safeParse over the exact shape\n  checkPassport writes, since createFakeStorage skips schema validation.\n- Document lastResultSchema as a persistence contract: new fields must be\n  optional/defaulted or every stored value fails validation on next deploy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9hNiTzztGEbh2B37bWC3y\n\n* fix(passport-checker): remove dead AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET threading\n\nrecoverySshTarget was never read by check.ts''s handler after the\ndetectUserInput refactor moved sshTarget sourcing entirely into the\nplatform config. Delete the widget''s leftover env read and option\nthreading so the README''s \"widgets neither read nor pass it\" claim is\nactually true, and drop the now-meaningless SSH-sanitization test in\nfavor of the equivalent (and broader) coverage in browser-automation''s\nconfig.test.ts.\n\n* docs(ofelia): design the authored ledger and comments\n\nRecords get the authenticated account instead of the per-device \"Я:\"\ntoggle, the write path moves into the widget''s own server.ts, and the\nhistory row is rebuilt: human dates, plain-language phrases, explicit\ndebt effect, visible supersession, no IP.\n\nPulls the Node 26 prerequisite out of the widget-server-cron spec, since\nthe shared domain module it enables is needed here first.\n\nAlso imports the Claude Design workspace under design/ (documents only,\nscreenshots and uploads stripped) and keeps the brief that produced the\nOfelia mock.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs(ofelia): note the cron plan overlap and landing order\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): cover the mobile layout and touch-scroll behaviour end to end\n\n* fix(board): keep the measured grid container mounted\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a 1920 viewport\n\n* docs(ofelia): add the authored ledger implementation plan\n\n15 tasks: Node 26, the pure domain split, the members directory, widget\nidentity, the server-resolved viewer, record schemas, draft construction,\nthe widget server definition, the model rewire, the history and comment\nview models, the account avatar, and the two rebuilt panels.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): wait for the grid relayout to settle before measuring\n\n* fix(browser-automation): bundle workspace imports instead of externalizing them\n\nThe rspack externals predicate was an allow-list whose default was\n\"externalize\". When the user-input refactor introduced the first runtime\nbare-specifier import of a workspace package into the service module graph\n(browser-automation/user-input/cloudflare, from the passport-checker browser\ntask), it was silently externalized: the build stayed green, all tests stayed\ngreen, and the Cloudflare module was simply absent from dist/index.cjs. The\nruntime stage copies only package.json and dist/, so Node resolved the\nspecifier through the exports map to a .ts path that does not exist in the\nimage and the container died on `exec node dist/index.cjs`.\n\nInvert the predicate into a deny-list derived from the package''s own\ndependencies (minus errore, which is ESM-only and must be bundled) plus Node\nbuiltins. An unrecognised bare specifier is now bundled, so the same mistake\nsurfaces as a build-time resolution error rather than a startup crash, and\nadding a real runtime dependency externalizes it with no config edit.\n\nAdd scripts/check-bundle.ts, chained onto `build` so it runs inside the image\nbuild the Dockerfile already performs. It asserts that every require() in the\nbundle is a builtin or a declared dependency, that the scan still matches the\nemitted shape at all, and that the compiled evidenceFromResponseText the widget\nsplices into Chromium still evaluates and runs in a bare vm realm. That last\ncheck is what the BROWSER_IT suite could never prove: it runs under vitest,\nwhere the specifier resolves to .ts through esbuild and the swc path is never\nexercised. Verified: the compiled form is ES5-lowered but self-contained --\ndestructuring became inline property reads, no swc helpers, no hoisted regexes.\n\nAlso:\n- detect.ts: tag the prepare outcome instead of comparing against null, so a\n  prepare that rejects with exactly null is a failure rather than a silent\n  success, matching the detector branch''s `typeof detected !== ''boolean''`.\n- user-input/errors.test.ts: cover toEnvelopeError for UserInputRequiredError\n  with and without an ssh target, pinning with toStrictEqual that `meta` is\n  omitted rather than set to undefined. contracts.test.ts already claimed this\n  coverage lived here.\n- chromium-executor.test.ts: drop an assertion inside a prepare callback that\n  could not fail the test and was vacuous, and rename the case to what it\n  actually verifies; the ordering contract is owned by detect.test.ts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* test(board): settle past the grid transition and stop assuming card order\n\n* test(board): correct the stale comment above the breakpoint wait\n\n* chore(node): align every runtime and image on Node 26\n\nnode:26-alpine and node:26-bookworm-slim no longer bundle corepack\n(removed from Node core), so `npm install -g corepack@latest` is added\nahead of every `corepack enable` in the three Dockerfiles and\ndocker-compose.dev.yml. scripts/infra.test.ts''s assertion on the\nbrowser-automation Dockerfile''s base image is updated to match.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(node): unshadow jsdom localStorage and bump the e2e image to Node 26\n\nNode 26 ships its own unflagged global localStorage/sessionStorage (Web\nStorage API), which resolves to undefined without --localstorage-file\nand shadows jsdom''s window.localStorage for bare-identifier reads in\npackages/client''s jsdom test environment (40 failing tests). Disable\nNode''s own globals via execArgv: [''--no-experimental-webstorage''] in\npackages/client/vite.config.ts so jsdom''s storage wins; verified via\nObject.getOwnPropertyDescriptor that the flag actually removes the\nglobal rather than just suppressing the warning. widget-runtime and\nwidget-sdk don''t touch localStorage in tests today, so the flag is not\nadded to their execArgv arrays (narrowest correct remedy).\n\nAlso bumps packages/client/e2e.Dockerfile (used by pnpm test:e2e:docker\nvia docker-compose.e2e.yml) from node:22-bookworm-slim to\nnode:26-bookworm-slim, missed by the original brief''s Files list, with\nthe same corepack@latest install this Node 26 base image now needs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(board): make the relayout wait actually poll\n\n* refactor(ofelia): extract the pure duty domain out of the Reatom model\n\n* fix(lint): make the widget domain import barrier a real allowlist\n\nThe regex denylist let ''../model/ofelia-duty'' through — domain/ and model/\nare siblings, so a single-''../'' import was both the most likely accidental\nescape and completely unguarded. It also only enumerated known packages, so\nany new npm dependency passed silently.\n\nReplace it with a gitignore-style group allowlist (''**'' minus zod, @shared/*\nand ''./'' siblings), which oxlint evaluates natively. A negative-lookahead\nregex was tried first and rejected: oxlint''s Rust regex engine has no\nlookaround and silently drops such a pattern, disabling the whole barrier.\n\nDomain tests get a second, later override that additionally allows vitest;\neverything else stays blocked there too.\n\n* chore(lint): keep the design mock exports out of lint and format\n\ndesign/ holds vendored Claude Design exports (support.js, *.dc.html)\nthat break the repo''s only gate: oxlint reports 9 errors in\ndesign/support.js, and oxfmt aborts (exit 2) on non-standard markup\nin design/Design System.dc.html before it can check anything else.\n\nAdd design/** to the existing ignorePatterns glob array in both\n.oxlintrc.json and .oxfmtrc.json, the same mechanism already used for\ndocs/**. This is a plain glob matcher (not the Rust regex engine\nbehind no-restricted-imports), so it isn''t subject to that engine''s\nlookaround gap, and it leaves every other rule/override untouched.\n\nFixing the oxfmt abort also uncovered pre-existing, unrelated\nformatting drift in CLAUDE.md, package.json and\nscripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts that the crash had been masking from\nthe format:check summary. Reformatted them (whitespace/ordering only,\nno logic changes) so pnpm format:check is actually green end to end.\n\n* feat(auth): expose the board members directory\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): expose the board identity to widgets\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): stop identity fetch from leaking an unhandled rejection and a discarded error\n\nHandle the AbortError that wrap()''s promise rejects with when the last\nwidget reading identity unmounts mid-fetch, and stop discarding\nBoardMembersError whole: a 401 stays silent (expected on an\nunauthenticated host), everything else is surfaced via console.warn so\nschema drift or a server fault is not silently swallowed.\n\n* feat(widgets): resolve the signed-in viewer for widget server events\n\n* feat(ofelia): carry the authoring account on ledger and comment records\n\n* feat(ofelia): move draft construction and event schemas into the domain\n\n* feat(ofelia): write the ledger and comments from the widget server\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): harden server date parsing and unforgeability coverage\n\n- Wrap Temporal.PlainDate.from in errore.try so a calendar-invalid but\n  regex-valid date (e.g. 2026-02-30) returns an Error instead of\n  throwing through a function typed Error | T.\n- Add regression coverage: forged createdBy/actor in the payload is\n  ignored, the debt handler is exercised, storage-read failures are\n  asserted by identity with no write, and the invalid-date case is\n  asserted as a value.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): split the unforgeability guard into schema and handler halves\n\nThe round-1 forged-payload test went through run(), which parses the\npayload via zod''s default strip mode before the handler ever sees it —\nso it only proved the schema strips forged fields, twice over, and\nwould not catch a handler that starts spreading its raw payload into\nthe draft. Replace it with two schema-level tests (clean + comment\npayloads drop a forged createdBy/actor) and one handler-level test\nthat calls ofeliaServer.handlers.clean directly, bypassing the schema,\nto prove the handler itself never reads those fields even if they\nreach it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* feat(ofelia): drive ledger and comment writes through widget server events\n\nThe client no longer appends to the ledger or the comment key. All four\nday actions collapse into one `invokeDay` that sends `{ date }` to the\nmatching widget-server event, and `send` sends `{ weekStart, text }` —\nwho the actor is, whose debt moves, whether the event applies at all and\nwhich account signed it are now derived and stamped server-side, so a\nclient cannot forge authorship.\n\n`selectedDate()` and `today()` (and `viewWeekStart()`) are read before\nthe first await so they resolve against the widget''s own Reatom context.\n\nThe per-device \"Я: Леша/Карина\" toggle is deleted rather than hidden:\nauthorship comes from the session, so there is nothing left to pick.\n`currentUser` is gone from the duty model, the widget context, the\nfixture and the view-model''s `onSetUser`.\n\nBoth model factories now take `api` and `identity`; `identity` is the\ndirectory the history and comment views read in the follow-up tasks.\nThe view projections drop `ip`/`by`, whose schema fields Tasks 5-8\nremoved, and `authorName` falls back through `createdBy.name`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* style: format the ofelia server test\n\n* feat(ofelia): group history by duty day and mark superseded records\n\n* feat(ofelia): resolve comment authors through the members directory\n\n* fix(ofelia): flush the comment thread''s connect hook via vi.waitFor, not a bare microtask\n\nReview round 1: the timing fix for the comment-thread subscribe/emit race\nassumed a single-microtask flush of Reatom''s connect-hook queue. Match the\nsibling comments-subscription tests in the same file instead — wait for the\nstorage mock''s subscribe call and wrap the whole body in context.start, so\nthe assertion is condition-based rather than scheduling-internals-based.\n\n* feat(ofelia): add the account avatar and its palette\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the history list around duty-day groups\n\n* fix(ofelia): announce the history phrase subject to assistive tech\n\nThe duty circle is the sentence subject in the rebuilt history phrase, and\nAvatar is aria-hidden, so the column announced as bare verbs with no subject.\nPair every circle with a visually hidden name, attribute the debt pill to its\nperson, and separate the chips with real spaces as the mock does.\n\nAlso correct the text-decoration comment to name the real mechanism (atomic\ninline, not descendant cancellation) and cover the three untested branches.\n\n* feat(ofelia): rebuild the comment thread around account authors\n\n* fix(ofelia): restore composer coverage and mute the unknown comment author\n\n* fix(ofelia): break ledger day ties by append order\n\nRouting the writes through the widget server changed where `ts` comes from.\n`/api/storage/:key/append` stamped `Date.now()`, so consecutive appends always\ngot a strictly increasing timestamp. The widget dispatch stamps the app''s\ninjectable `now()`, and the e2e harness pins that clock — so a clean-then-undo\npair on the same day produced two entries with an identical `ts`, and\n`latestOutcomesByDate`''s strict `>` kept the earlier `cleaned` entry as the\nday''s outcome. The `reset` was written but never counted, and the day never\nreopened; `pnpm test:e2e:docker` caught it as a failing \"undo — returns the day\nto pending\".\n\nThe ledger is append-only and iterated in insertion order, so `>=` is the\ncorrect tie-break: on an equal timestamp the later-appended entry is the newer\ndecision. This also hardens production, where two writes can still land in the\nsame millisecond.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: describe server-authored widget records\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): fail widget dispatch when the session account is unreadable\n\nresolveWidgetViewer folded every getAccount failure into `null`, so a Valkey\nfault, a corrupt account record and a deleted account all produced a record\nstamped `createdBy: null` plus a `{ ok: true }` response. The ledger is\nappend-only, so that authorship could never be recovered.\n\nThe three outcomes are now distinct: no session still returns `null`, which is\nwhat the standalone harnesses and the e2e stack (no nginx, so no session cookie)\nrely on to keep writing unattributed records; a failed lookup returns a\nWidgetViewerLookupError, which the dispatch route turns into a fault instead of\na false success.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(ofelia): order superseded history records by append order\n\n`superseded` sorted on `right.ts - left.ts`, which returns 0 on a tie; the\nstable sort then left tied records in append order, oldest first, contradicting\nthe \"newest first\" contract on HistoryDayGroup. Ties became a supported case\nwhen latestOutcomesByDate started breaking them with `>=`, and the dockerized\ne2e stack freezes the clock, so there every record ties.\n\nThe ledger is append-only, so append order is chronological order: reversing the\nday''s bucket is both correct and cheaper than sorting.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(server): warn when listAccounts skips an unreadable record\n\nA corrupt or schema-mismatched account record was dropped silently, so the\nroster simply came back short and the symptom read as \"an account vanished\"\nrather than as the data corruption it is. Name the key in a console.warn.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* build: pin corepack to 0.35.0 across images and dev compose\n\n`npm install -g corepack@latest` ran in five image build stages and four dev\ncompose services. There is no CI and the Pi deploy is manual with a 30-minute\nbuild timeout, so a breaking corepack release would only ever surface as a\nfailed deploy. Pin one explicit version everywhere.\n\nVerified: `docker build -f packages/server/Dockerfile` succeeds and the built\nimage reports corepack 0.35.0 with pnpm 10.28.2.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* refactor(ofelia): delete the dead short-date formatter\n\nformatDateShort and MONTHS_SHORT lost their last production consumer when the\ncomment thread moved to its own formatter; only the test still called them. It\nalso formatted through `new Date(ts)` in the local timezone, while every current\nformatter goes through plainDateIn(DUTY_TIME_ZONE, ...).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* test(ofelia): drop the tier guards that cannot fail, hoist the env directive\n\nStandardTier and CompactTier each asserted they do not render UserToggle. The\ntoggle only ever lived in RichLayout and the component is now deleted outright,\nso neither assertion could ever fail — they only looked like coverage.\n\nAlso move the `@vitest-environment node` directive in server.test.ts back to\nline 1, where a reader expects it and where model/history-view.test.ts keeps it.\nVitest 4 matches the directive anywhere in the file content, so the file was\nalready running under node; this is readability, not a behaviour fix.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* docs: state the authorship guarantee the code actually provides\n\nCLAUDE.md claimed a server-stamped author \"cannot be forged\". Nothing enforces\nthat: POST /api/storage/:key/append does no session check in Node, and nginx''s\nauth_request only proves a session exists, not whose it is, so any signed-in\nhousehold member can still hand-write a record carrying an arbitrary author.\n\nState what is true instead — the UI can no longer sign a record with someone\nelse''s name, and the generic storage route remains a shared trusted channel\ninside the board. Closing that route is a separate task.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(board): never render the desktop grid below its 1920 baseline\n\nThe desktop zoom is now one-directional: raise the lower clamp on the\ngrid scale from 0.75 to 1, so every container width between the mobile\nbreakpoint and BASE_WIDTH resolves to exactly the historical metrics\n(rowHeight: 30, margin: [10, 10]) and only wider displays scale up.\n\nUntil this branch, useContainerWidth never attached its observer and\npinned the reported width at its 1280 default on every screen. With the\ncontainer measured for real, a 0.75 floor would have shrunk every board\non a 1280/1366/1440 laptop below the size it has always had, dropping an\nh:6 card from 230px to 172.5px and pushing widgets under their own tier\nthresholds. Nothing shrinks now; the 4K aspect drift the scale was added\nfor is still fixed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling record\n\nBring the design doc in line with what shipped: the lower clamp is 1,\nthe 1280 table row shows the baseline metrics, and the paragraph\njustifying the clamp says plainly that it is active across the whole\n768-1920 band rather than a narrow 768-1100 one.\n\nAlso correct the claim that existing 1080p boards \"do not shift by a\npixel\". The formula at 1920 does reproduce the old constants, but the\nmeasured width was previously pinned at 1280 on every screen by the\nuseContainerWidth bug this branch fixes, so wider screens legitimately\nre-flow to use the whole window.\n\nDrop the 1920 viewport pin from ofelia-duty.spec.ts: it only worked\naround the 0.75 clamp, and removing it restores default-viewport\ncoverage. Recompute the footprint arithmetic in the ofelia client test\ncomment, which assumed a container of exactly 1280 rather than the\nviewport minus the board padding and the scrollbar gutter.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* test(ofelia): pin the duty spec to a viewport its tier needs\n\nEvery assertion in the duty spec is StandardTier UI, and the widget only\nresolves `standard` at a frame width of 400px or more. Playwright''s\ndefault 1280 viewport is about 7px short: the board measures its own\ncontainer, which is 1225px there once the stable scrollbar gutter and the\nboard''s 20px padding on each side come off, so a w:4 card is ~395px and\nits frame ~393px. `compact` is the correct rendering at that width, so\nthe spec has to declare the width its assertions need.\n\nThe dependency was previously invisible because the board reported a\nhardcoded 1280px container on every screen, which made the card 413px and\nleft the threshold only 13px of headroom against a measurement that was\nwrong by 55px.\n\nCorrect the footprint comment in the widget''s client test to match: the\ntier is measured on the frame inside the card''s 1px border, so a 1280px\nviewport yields ~393x228 and resolves to `compact`, not `standard`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* docs(board): correct the grid scaling claims to match what ships\n\nThree statements asserted the opposite of what this branch established,\nall written before the width axis was measured.\n\nThe grid-metrics JSDoc claimed the clamp keeps 1280/1366/1440 boards\n\"the size they have always been\" and widgets \"above their own tier\nthresholds\". Both are false at 1280: the container is 1225 there, so a\nw:4 card is 395px where the old code drew 413px, and ofelia falls under\nits 400px standard threshold into compact. The clamp pins the metrics -\nrowHeight 30 and margin [10, 10] at every desktop width - not the\nrendered geometry, which still follows the measured container.\n\nThe design doc claimed \"no board shrinks and no widget loses a tier\".\nThe 1366 and 1440 frames (422px, 446px) clear the widget threshold; the\n1280 frame (393px) does not. Corrected to draw the metrics/geometry\ndistinction the document already draws two paragraphs earlier.\n\nThe \"do not shift by a pixel\" claim survived in the doc''s Testing\nsection and in the test''s own comment. At 1080p the container is now\n1865, so colWidth is 144.58 rather than the 95.83 the pinned-1280 bug\nproduced. Both now state what the test guarantees: the formula at\nBASE_WIDTH reproduces the previous hardcoded constants.\n\nAlso record the shipped consequence in Risks - ofelia renders\nCompactTier below ~1300px, correct behaviour on a genuinely 393px frame,\nwith recalibration left as a follow-up - and fix three figures: the tier\nparagraph attributed the frame inset to a card padding that does not\nexist (it is a 1px border; the heights are 148px and 298px, not 110 and\n260), the e2e headroom was 11px on the frame rather than 13px on the\ncard, the 1920 card/frame are 608/606, and useContainerWidth is at\nBoard.tsx:28.\n\nComments and prose only; no executable line changed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* build(deploy): add a per-branch rpi environment and deploy scripts\n\nrpi.branch.toml is a disposable stack for trying the checked-out branch on\nreal hardware, with ttl = \"72h\" so a forgotten one reaps itself. It takes the\nbranch through ${BRANCH_NAME}, the only variable rpi 0.26 accepts — hence the\nliteral hostname rather than a derived one.\n\nscripts/deploy-branch.ts reads the branch from git so nobody retypes it, and\nrefuses to deploy without .env.branch, which would otherwise produce a stack\nwhose WebAuthn gate fails with nothing in the deploy log.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(deploy): send the branch secrets bundle before the build\n\nEvery branch is a fresh rpi project with an empty secrets store, so the very\nfirst deploy of any branch died after the ~3 minute image build with an opaque\nCompose \"secret file ... does not exist\". deploy:branch now checks\n`rpi secrets ls` and sends the bundle itself when there is none.\n\nAlso strip the `--` that `pnpm run x -- --flag` forwards verbatim: rpi''s parser\nreads a bare `--` as end-of-flags, so the flag after it was rejected as a\npositional.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013iX5NML6xkP1SsmY47LuzR\n\n* fix(browser-automation): read the spliced callback when swc stops inlining it\n\ncheck-bundle extracted the page callback by slicing between `.concat(` and\n`.toString()`, which is the whole function expression only while swc inlines it.\nA second consumer of evidenceFromResponseText — precisely what the public\nbrowser-automation/user-input/cloudflare subpath exists to allow — makes swc emit\na named declaration instead, leaving a bare identifier at the splice site. The\nguard then evaluated that identifier in an empty realm, got a ReferenceError, and\nfailed the image build accusing a perfectly correct bundle of shipping a\nnon-self-contained callback.\n\nResolve the identifier to its declaration, and let the JavaScript parser find\nwhere that declaration ends: counting braces by hand would have to understand\nregex literals, and this body is full of regexes containing quote characters.\nExtraction failures now say so instead of reporting a self-containment defect.\n\nVerified by building all four ways: inlined and named shapes both pass; the\noriginal externals regression and a regex hoisted out of the callback are both\nstill caught.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* fix(ofelia): centre the initial in avatars re-cast to inline-flex\n\nBoth avatars centre their initial with `place-items: center`, but the\nhistory phrase re-casts the box to `inline-flex` so the circle stays in\nthe inline run, and a flex container ignores `justify-items`. The glyph\nwas left-aligned: in an 18px circle it sat 0px from the inline start and\n12.97px from the end, roughly a third of the diameter off centre.\n\nName the inline axis outright so the centring survives whichever display\na caller imposes. `.debt > [data-tone]` re-casts the same way and was hit\nby the same bug.\n\n* fix(ofelia): reserve room for the viewer ring in scrolling columns\n\nThe signed-in viewer''s avatar carries a 3px ring drawn as a spread\nshadow, so it paints outside the border box. Every avatar sits flush\nagainst the inline start of a container that clips: `.historyCol` and\n`.list` scroll (and `overflow-y: auto` clips the inline axis too), and\n`.commentsCol` is `overflow: hidden`. The ring lost its whole left edge —\nmeasured at exactly 3.0px — leaving a flat-sided avatar in the history,\nthe comment list and the composer, in both themes and on mobile.\n\nWiden each clip box outward by the ring width with a padding/negative\nmargin pair, which moves no content, and hold the width in\n`--ofelia-viewer-ring` so the shadow and the reserved room cannot drift.\n\n* fix(ofelia): size the account initial like the duty initial\n\n`MemberAvatar` scaled its initial to half the box while `Avatar` uses\n0.4, and the history phrase renders both at `px={18}` on the same line —\n9px next to 7.2px, which reads as two mismatched type sizes rather than\none set.\n\nShare the ratio through `AVATAR_INITIAL_RATIO`. Taking `Avatar`''s value\nkeeps every pre-existing avatar (the 62px hero, the 26px week strip)\npixel-identical and moves only the account squares this branch added.\n\n* fix(ofelia): raise the standard tier to the height it actually needs\n\nStandardTier lays out to 298px of content, but `tiers.standard` asked for\nonly 200px and the widget card clips instead of scrolling. At the default\nplacement (413x230px) the tier therefore resolved and then cut its own\naction row in half: \"Уборка подтверждена\" was sliced through and \"В долг\"\n/ \"Простить\" never appeared at all.\n\nState the height the tier needs, so anything shorter degrades to\n`compact` rather than rendering truncated, and raise `defaultSize` to\n`{ w: 4, h: 8 }` (~413x310px) so a freshly-placed widget still clears it\n— the same fix shape as the earlier default-placement regression.\n\n* fix(ofelia): let the pinned day header cover the reserved ring strip\n\nThe history column reserves `--ofelia-viewer-ring` of inline-start room\nso the viewer ring is not clipped, and records paint into that strip. The\nsticky day header''s own box stops at the content edge, so the strip stayed\nuncovered: scrolling avatars slid past the pinned header through a 3px\ngap, measured against the column''s border box.\n\nStretch the header back over the strip with a negative inline-start margin\nand return the width as padding, so its background spans the full\nscrollport while nothing inside it moves.\n\n* feat(ofelia): scroll the history to the day picked in the week strip\n\nSelecting a day highlighted it in the strip and changed the panel, but\nleft the history where it was, so the records for that day had to be found\nby hand.\n\n`HistoryList` now keeps a node per duty day and scrolls the matching group\nto the top of its scrollport when the selection changes. Exactly one\nancestor scrolls in each layout — the column on desktop, the dialog body\non mobile — so the native `scrollIntoView` cannot drag two scrollports at\nonce. The first run only records the mounted day: scrolling on mount would\npull the panel above the history off screen in the mobile layout before\nthe reader touched anything.\n\nThe column subscribes to a newly exposed `view.selectedIso` rather than\n`view.selected`, so confirming or forgiving the selected day still does\nnot re-render the history.\n\n* fix(ofelia): scroll only a box the reader can scroll back\n\n`scrollIntoView` moves *every* scroll container in the ancestor chain, and\n`overflow: hidden` boxes are scroll containers too — they just have no\nscrollbar. The widget frame is one: measured at 149px of overflow in the\nmobile layout, `overflow-y: hidden`. Switching a day therefore displaced\nthe whole widget, and with no scrollbar there the reader could not put it\nback.\n\nWalk up to the innermost box that is genuinely user-scrollable\n(`auto`/`scroll` *and* overflowing) and scroll that one by hand, aligning\nthe group with the scrollport top where the sticky header pins. When\nnothing in the chain scrolls there is nothing to reveal, so the layout is\nleft alone rather than nudged.\n\n* fix(ofelia): keep the mobile tab pane from clipping the viewer ring\n\nBounding the tab pane needs `overflow: hidden`, which clips the inline axis\ntoo — `overflow-x` cannot stay `visible` once the other axis is not. So in\nthe mobile layout `.split` becomes a second clipper, outside the columns\nand at the same inline start, and it shaved off the 3px viewer ring that\nthe columns had already reserved room for. Measured at `cutL: 3` on the\ncomment and record avatars; on desktop `.split` is `overflow: visible`, so\nthis only ever showed on narrow screens.\n\nReach outward by the ring width there too, the same padding/negative-margin\npair the columns use.\n\n* docs(cron): rebase the cron spec and plan onto dev\n\ndev landed Node 26, the widget domain/ boundary and Ofelia''s server.ts with\naccount authorship, so those tasks are gone. Authorship for cron records is now\na system arm on the persisted createdBy union rather than a `by: ''system''`\nfield, and the plan drops from 12 tasks to 8.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(deploy): point the dev overlay back at dev\n\nThe overlay was temporarily aimed at this feature branch (5208c5c5) to test it on\nthe Pi before the `branch` environment existed. rpi.branch.toml now covers that\ncase per branch, so dev goes back to deploying dev — which is also what\nscripts/infra.test.ts asserts, and that test passes again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(widgets): add the cron job contract to widget server definitions\n\n* feat(server): validate widget cron schedules when the registry is built\n\n* feat(server): persist a per-job cron cursor in Valkey\n\n* feat(server): add the widget cron tick scheduler\n\n* fix(server): drain the cron scheduler on stop and pin the no-double-fire invariant\n\ninFlight was replaced on every interval fire instead of chained, so a tick\nthat outlived intervalMs was silently orphaned and stop() could return\nwhile it was still running. Chain onto the existing promise instead.\n\nAlso add a dueOccurrence test pinning that an occurrence already recorded\nin the cursor never re-fires.\n\n* style(server): rewrap the cron registry validation to the formatter''s width\n\n* fix(client): stop an edge cache from serving a stale host federation entry\n\n/remoteEntry.js is the one release file whose URL never changes while its\ncontents do: it names the hashed chunk that calls createInstance. Cached by URL,\nan intermediary keeps serving the previous release''s copy, whose chunk 404s in\nthe new release. The host then never initialises and every loadRemote fails with\n\"#RUNTIME-009 Please call createInstance first\" — a board that renders its shell\nand not one widget.\n\nObserved today on the branch stack: Cloudflare answered /remoteEntry.js with\ncf-cache-status HIT, age 6503, under its default max-age=14400, from a build\nnearly three hours older than the deployed one, while /index.html was DYNAMIC\nand the hashed assets were current. It survived a browser site-data clear, a\ncontainer recreate, a builder-cache prune and a cold rebuild, because none of\nthose touch the edge.\n\nWidget entries already carried no-cache for this exact reason; the host''s own\nentry never did. Give it the same header and pin both halves in the nginx smoke\ntest: the header, and that the chunk the entry names is one this release ships.\n\nThis is not specific to the throwaway branch host — production serves the same\nfile under the same TTL, so any release that changes the board bundle could\nshow an empty board for up to four hours.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W2bHRVE4dRMwQKQr82D6Zw\n\n* feat(server): start the cron scheduler with the app and expose a test tick\n\n* feat(ofelia): give server-written records a system author\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the harness and tests the required widget identity\n\nThe ofelia authored-ledger work made `identity` a required field of\nWidgetRuntimeProps; the passport-checker widget was written before that\nfield existed. Both landed on dev without a textual conflict, so the\nsemantic conflict only surfaced as four TS2741 errors — all in a dev\nharness and three test call sites, none of them part of the production\nbuild, which is why `pnpm build` kept passing.\n\nFollow the convention the widgets updated in that PR already use: the\nharness takes the live `runtime.identity`, the tests take the\n`makeStaticWidgetIdentity()` double. passport-checker never reads the\nroster, so the empty default keeps every assertion unchanged, and the\nstatic double issues no request — which matters in recovery-flow.test,\nwhere fetch is stubbed with a never-resolving promise.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01E1joL7m1hCFhj6XJUkvMvE\n\n* feat(ofelia): auto-approve unresolved duty days on a nightly cron\n\n* test(e2e): cover the auto-approve cron reaching an open board\n\n* test(server): cover the cron interval path and a retried partial run\n\nClose the three test-coverage gaps from the final whole-branch review:\nexercise makeCronScheduler''s start()/stop() interval path (the only\nproduction entry point, previously untested) with fake timers, cover a\nretry after a partially failed ledger append end to end in the ofelia\ncron suite, and assert append was never called in the widget''s cron\nread-error test to match its manual-path siblings.\n\n* fix(server): fence the generic storage routes off the auth keyspace\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`GET /api/storage?prefix=` passed an unvalidated prefix straight to `scanKeys`, and\n`session:<id>` embeds the session cookie''s own value in the key name, so any\nsigned-in device could enumerate live session tokens and then act as anyone —\nvoiding the server-stamped authorship this release is built on. PUT/DELETE were\nequally unbounded, which also left the new `cron:` cursor client-writable.\n\nRestrict all five generic storage routes to `root:`/`w:i:`/`w:t:`, rejecting a\npartial prefix (including the empty one) so enumeration cannot walk in sideways.\n\nAlongside: re-stamp `ip: ''''` on append as a one-release shim for pre-release\nreaders whose schema requires the field, log every `status >= 500` with its cause\nchain instead of only 500, and cover the dispatch route''s session→viewer wiring,\nwhich had no test at all — replacing it with a literal null left the suite green.\n\n* fix(ofelia): let the nightly auto-close actually discharge a debt\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`getDebtDays` scans forward from its origin, so every day it yields is >= today,\nwhile the cron only ever asks about days 1..7 in the past — `debtDayFor` was\nstructurally unreachable on that path. The auto-close therefore credited the\nrotation duty person instead of the debtor, omitted `onBehalfOf`, and `foldDebt`\nnever decremented: the debt walked forward to the next open day, forever, one\nmis-attributed record per night.\n\nAnchor the scan at `min(target, today)` behind an explicit `allowPastDebtDays`\nthat only `autoApproveDrafts` sets, so the interactive handlers stay anchored at\n`today` and keep matching what the week strip showed the user.\n\nAlongside: keep writing `by`/`author` for one release (pre-release readers require\nthem and their `z.array` fails wholesale on one missing field), drop unparseable\nelements instead of rejecting the whole array, skip the run when the ledger key is\nabsent so unused stacks stop accruing fabricated history, and re-read each day''s\nresolution before appending so a manual close landing mid-run is not overwritten.\n\n* fix(ofelia): surface failures the widget used to swallow\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\nEvery duty action captured its error into an atom nothing read, so a failed tap\nlooked identical to a successful one — and with the nightly cron now closing\nunresolved days, a silently failed «В долг» becomes a system-signed `cleaned`\nrecord at 00:05. A failed comment send additionally discarded the typed text and\nraised an unhandled rejection.\n\nTrack the last failure in write order rather than merging four per-action atoms in\na fixed order (the first non-null shadowed the rest forever), render it in Russian\nrather than leaking `WidgetApiError`''s internal message, and keep the composer''s\ntext on failure.\n\nAlongside: show a retry instead of an endless skeleton when the ledger read fails,\nstop attributing the previous week''s comments to the selected one, keep\nscroll-to-day inside the history column instead of scrolling the whole card, and\nstop the account avatar announcing a name the adjacent text already carries.\n\n* fix(board): migrate stale widget heights once, not on every read\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n`tiers.standard.minHeightPx` rose 200→300 and `defaultSize.h` 6→8, but nothing\nmigrated the `h` already persisted, so every placed ofelia card would render\nCompactTier after deploy and lose its week strip and debt chips.\n\nRun the bump once, recorded in a `root:`-scoped marker key a pre-release client\ncannot strip on write-back — clamping on every read instead would have made every\nwidget unshrinkable below its package default, since `minH` sits well under it.\n\nAlongside: preserve unknown keys on the shared board snapshot, so a client that\ndoes not know a field can no longer delete it when it writes the board back.\n\n* fix(client): stop the edge from serving a stale service worker\n\nThis release already gave `/remoteEntry.js` a `no-cache` header after Cloudflare\nwas caught serving a three-hour-old copy of it. `/sw.js` has the identical\nproperty — a stable URL whose contents change every release — and fell through to\nthe catch-all block with no Cache-Control at all, so an installed PWA kept getting\nthe previous release''s worker from the edge for up to four hours and never\ninstalled the new one. That is also what would have made the ofelia record-shape\nincompatibility outlast the deploy by hours rather than a reload.\n\nGive the worker its own location alongside the federation entries, and pin it in\nthe nginx smoke spec the same way the host entry is pinned.\n\nAlongside: raise `proxy_read_timeout` for widget dispatch above the automation\nbudget — the generic `/api/` block inherited nginx''s 60s default, which would have\ncut every long passport check before its own deadline could fire — and re-point\nthe two gate probes at allowlisted keys so they keep isolating the auth and CSRF\ngates instead of tripping the new storage allowlist.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): let the members directory recover from a failed first fetch\n\nThe roster was fetched once from a connect hook that fires only on the 0→1\nsubscriber edge. On the board the identity atoms stay connected for the life of\nthe page, so a transient failure at first paint — routine on the Pi, where the\nservice worker serves the shell while the server container is still starting —\npinned the roster at null for the whole session: no viewer avatar, no viewer ring,\nuntil someone reloaded. An account created later never appeared either.\n\nRetry with backoff on a transport failure, leave a 401 alone, and refresh so a\nlater-created account arrives without a reload. Every await carries its own\nrejection handler: the abort that `wrap` raises on teardown was otherwise an\nunhandled rejection on each disconnect.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* feat(passport-checker): add a delete control, and stop leaking recovery sessions\n\nThe board already hands every widget a `requestDelete` callback and clock and\nofelia both use it; passport-checker was the only widget with no way to remove it\nfrom the board. Wire it through the shared `WidgetControls`, in both tiers and in\nthe dev harness.\n\nAlongside, three fixes to the recovery and check paths:\n\n`teardown()` disposed the session but never invalidated an in-flight `start()`, so\nclosing the modal during the ~1s issue request still built the noVNC RFB against a\ndetached node. The recovery slot is a single global, so that orphan answered every\nlater attempt with 409 until its TTL expired.\n\nThe 25s client deadline sat far below the pipeline''s own budget (30s queue + 60s\nexecution, 100s at the board server), so on the Pi a normal check timed out in the\nUI while the invoke resolved later with a real result that nobody read — the\nstatus could never be persisted while the upstream site was slow. Raise it past\nthe server budget, keep a late success instead of dropping it, and ignore a\nstraggler from a superseded attempt so it cannot overwrite a newer result.\n\nThe SSH fallback hardcoded noVNC''s port 6080 after compose made it per-stack, so\nan operator recovering the dev stack tunnelled into production''s live Chromium —\npossibly mid check, with a real passport number on screen. It now uses the port\nthe stack actually published.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* fix(browser-automation): report the stack''s own noVNC host port\n\nThe widget renders an `ssh -L` command so an operator can take over a stuck\nbrowser session, and it reads the port from the rejection''s meta. Nothing ever\nput a port there: `UserInputRequiredError.publicMeta` carried only `sshTarget`,\nso the widget always fell back to 6080 — production''s published port — and an\noperator on dev or branch was told to tunnel into the wrong stack.\n\nThread `NOVNC_HOST_PORT` from the same compose variable the publish line uses\ninto the service config and out through `publicMeta`, so both ends of that\ncontract now agree and each is pinned by a test.\n\nAlso set `AUTOMATION_SSH_TARGET` in the dev and branch env templates: it was\nunset there, so the fallback the operator is meant to reach did not render at\nall on exactly the stacks that collide.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(backlog): record the findings this branch deliberately did not fix\n\nThe PR #27 review surfaced nine issues that were judged not worth acting on now:\nsome are contested (one review lens refuted them), some are the release''s own\ndocumented known gaps, and some are pre-existing shapes this branch only made\nvisible. They were about to be lost with the review transcript.\n\nEach entry states what exists today, why it is tolerable, the shape a fix would\ntake, and what would trigger doing the work — matching the two entries already\nhere. The facts in every entry were re-verified against the code rather than\ncopied from the review.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(rpi): move the env configs onto rpi 0.27 groups and ${git.branch}\n\nDeliver secrets through named groups instead of one bundle per environment:\n`prod` for production, `dev` for the passport-checker files the dev stack and\nthe branch stand share, `branch` for the stand''s own .env.branch. Both overlays\nclear [secrets].files, so nothing carries a second copy, and [secrets].env in\neach file is now documented as the local push source it has always been.\n\nResolve the branch stand''s source.branch through ${git.branch} and drop the\nBRANCH_NAME variable everywhere. Since 0.27 the environment key only gets a\nper-branch suffix when the configuration references ${env.slug}, so the stand is\none shared key by design; say so where the comments claimed the opposite.\n\nDelete scripts/deploy-branch.ts. It existed to push .env.branch before a deploy\ncould come up unconfigured, which a missing key bundle does silently. A declared\ngroup that is missing or empty instead fails the deploy at the layer-load step,\nnaming the group and the push that fixes it, so `rpi deploy --env branch` needs\nno wrapper.\n\nLock the layout with infra tests and refresh the docs that still said\n`rpi secrets send` and `--vars BRANCH_NAME=...`.\n\nVerified: rpi config show for base/dev/branch, oxfmt --check, oxlint and\ntsc --noEmit. The new infra assertions were run through an equivalent script —\nroot Vitest cannot start in this checkout, where rolldown fails to resolve any\nconfig entry, including untouched ones.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(rpi): drop the prod secret group and the dead PASSPORT_* variables\n\nProduction would be the only project attaching a `prod` group, and a keyless\npush always writes this file''s env + files into the deploy key''s own bundle,\nwhich is the last layer — so the group could only ever be an identical shadowed\ncopy, and a rotation pushed to it alone would silently never reach production.\nKeep the group mechanism where it earns its place: `dev`, shared by the dev\nstack and the branch stand.\n\nPASSPORT_SERIES/PASSPORT_NUMBER are gone from .env (untracked, so only its\ncomment header records this): nothing in docker-compose.yml reads them, the\nwidget takes those values from the file-backed secrets, and they were riding\ninto every stack''s containers through both groups.\n\nAgent state after this change: groups `dev` (0 keys, 2 files) and `branch`\n(7 keys, 0 files); production''s bundle 0 keys + 2 files; dev''s 7 keys + 0 files,\nwith the passport files finally resolving `<- dev` instead of a stale `<- key`\ncopy that was overriding the group.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: spec the unified widget card controls\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: plan the unified widget card controls\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: require codegen in the plan''s worktree setup\n\n* feat(widget-sdk): make WidgetControls the one widget chrome component\n\n* fix(widget-host): stop hiding card controls where there is no hover\n\n* test(client): assert the touch controls on the element that carries the style\n\n* docs: add the e2e typecheck the workspace gate does not cover\n\n* refactor(clock): take the card chrome from useWidgetChrome\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): take the delete control from useWidgetChrome\n\n* refactor(ofelia): drop OfeliaActionControls for the shared WidgetControls\n\n* refactor(passport-checker): mount the tiny tier chrome once\n\n* fix(widget-sdk): narrow the chrome policy and guard the tiny-tier switch\n\nWhole-branch code review fix wave:\n\n- useWidgetChrome now allows expand+delete only on mode === ''small'' and\n  falls through to close-only otherwise, instead of denying only on\n  ''large''. Equivalent today (WidgetMode is ''small'' | ''large''), but a\n  future third mode now defaults to no card-management chrome rather\n  than silently gaining full expand+delete.\n- TinyTier''s switch gains a `default: view satisfies never` branch.\n  ReactNode already includes undefined, so the prior bare `let body:\n  ReactNode` never tripped TS''s used-before-assignment check on a\n  missing case; the plan''s claim that it did was wrong and is corrected.\n- widget-interactions.spec.ts asserts the desktop half of the\n  hover-visibility rule (opacity 0 -> 1 on the overlay controls\n  container), which mobile-board.spec.ts cannot reach since it forces\n  hasTouch: true.\n- Corrects the spec/plan claim that a standalone dev/ harness shows\n  widget controls: every harness hardcodes mode: ''large'', so\n  useWidgetChrome() returns only onClose there and no controls render.\n\n* feat(widget-host): let a loading card name itself and be deleted\n\nA card whose widget is still loading showed an anonymous shimmer and no\ncontrols, so a component chunk that never resolved left a tile that could\nnot be removed. It now draws the widget''s icon and title — both known\nsynchronously from the codegen catalog before the remote lands — with the\nshared WidgetControls alongside them.\n\nThe controls are placement=\"inline\", not the board card''s usual overlay:\nan overlay is hover-revealed, and hover-hiding the only useful control on\na stuck card is the trap being fixed. Inline already means always-visible,\nso no new visibility rule was needed.\n\nAlso fixes a guard that never fired: the frame''s own onDelete is a\nuseEvent handle and so always truthy, which made the error card''s\n`{onDelete && ...}` unconditional. Both it and the error boundary now read\nthe raw prop.\n\n* fix(e2e): give the auto-approve cron test a ledger to close\n\nThe job returns early on an empty ledger by design — a stack where nobody\never used the widget must not grow a fabricated `cleaned` record a night\n(server.ts, F11). That guard landed in b1fffc0e, six hours after this test\nwas written in ecdd1cd0, and the test was never updated: it resets the\ndatabase, places the widget, touches nothing, and then expects the cron to\nclose a day. The ledger was empty, so the job no-opped and the plaque never\narrived.\n\nSeed one settled earlier day, the way the neighbouring forgive test already\ndoes. 2026-06-15 is a genuine Карина duty day and the entry closes it, so\nthe run skips it and auto-approves 2026-06-16 — the day the card shows.\n\nVerified: pnpm test:e2e:docker now reports 27/27, against 26/27 both on\nthis branch and on origin/dev before the change.\n\n* chore(lint): stop formatting markdown with oxfmt\n\nHand-written tables and wrapped prose in CLAUDE.md and the agent guides read\nbetter than oxfmt''s column padding, which also left the table separator row\nwider than the rows around it. docs/** and design/** were already ignored, so\nthis only extends the same treatment to the root markdown and the skill files.\n\nThe oxlint entry is symmetry: oxlint does not lint markdown today, but the two\nignore lists are maintained together.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* docs(agents): split CLAUDE.md into on-demand guides\n\nCLAUDE.md is read in full at the start of every session, and it had grown to\n~3.4k words. Move the parts that are only needed some of the time next to the\ncode they describe, and leave an index behind:\n\n- docs/agent/{deployment,test-troubleshooting,widget-server,radix-stacked-dialogs}.md,\n  each fronted by a thin .claude/skills wrapper so Claude Code loads it on its\n  own trigger, while Codex and the rest follow the table in CLAUDE.md\n- docs/architecture/overview.md for the descriptive architecture prose\n- the storage-key contract, including the incident that proves it, now lives in\n  the header comment of packages/shared/storage/scope.ts, so it reaches whoever\n  is actually editing the derivation\n\nAlso drop the generic rpi CLI mechanics already covered by the rpi:rpi-cli and\nrpi:rpi-toml skills, and fix what had drifted: pi.toml -> rpi.toml, the\nscopeWithColon normalization lives in host-runtime.ts and not widget-api.ts,\nbrowser-automation is no longer \"the future browser service\", the command list\nmatches package.json, and the hardcoded per-machine skill paths are gone.\n\nCLAUDE.md: 3368 -> 1382 words.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* fix(client): replace the deprecated apple PWA capability meta\n\nChrome flags <meta name=\"apple-mobile-web-app-capable\"> as deprecated and\nasks for the standard mobile-web-app-capable instead. Keeping both would\nkeep the warning, and standalone mode no longer depends on the apple tag:\nthe VitePWA manifest already declares display: standalone, which Safari\nhonours since iOS 16.4.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpBh5n7pVjk5W8YmNqxjq2\n\n* fix(passport-checker): load noVNC lazily so the widget can mount\n\n@novnc/novnc@1.7.0 ends core/util/browser.js in a module-scope top-level\nawait: it builds a real VideoDecoder, configures it for 1920x1080\navc1.42401f, decodes a frame and awaits decoder.flush(). That probe\nresolves in milliseconds on desktop Chrome and never settles at all on\nsome devices, and a top-level await that never settles parks every\nimporter in \"evaluating\" forever.\n\nui/PassportChecker.tsx imported model/rfb.ts statically for the prod RFB\nadapter, so the chain was mount-time: import(''./ui/PassportChecker'') never\nresolved, React.lazy never got a payload, and the board rendered this\nwidget''s loading card forever. Nothing was thrown, so no error boundary\nfired and the card was indistinguishable from a slow load.\n\nReach the adapter through model/load-rfb.ts''s dynamic import instead. The\nrecovery model now takes loadRfb and awaits it alongside transport.issue:\nthe store supersedes an unused capability on the next issue() and only\ncounts a live socket as busy, so running them in parallel costs nothing\nand keeps the modal''s time to first frame unchanged. A viewer that fails\nto load lands in the new viewerUnavailable state with a reconnect button\nrather than a silent spinner.\n\nVerified on the built output: avc1.42401f/EncodedVideoChunk now appear\nonly in rfb-*.js, and the widget chunk''s single reference to it is a\ndynamic import(). ui/passport-checker-novnc-graph.test.ts guards the\nchain — it goes red if the static import comes back.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(agent): record the machine and deploy failures that lived only in memory\n\nThe CLAUDE.md split moved detail into docs/agent/* but left ten machine- and\ndeploy-specific failure modes recorded nowhere in the repository. Each of them\nreads as a regression you just caused, and each has cost multiple sessions.\n\ntest-troubleshooting.md gains the worktree symlink failure, the rotten\nnode_modules in the main checkout, pnpm check''s kill-others-on-fail behaviour,\nthe docker e2e run that exits 0 without starting, and the stale vite preview\nthat serves another checkout''s build. It also corrects the Vitest filter: the\ndocumented `--filter client test -- <file>` does not scope, `exec vitest run`\ndoes.\n\ndeployment.md gains the three caches that sit between a build and what runs -\nthe service worker and IndexedDB that survive a redeploy on the shared branch\nhostname, the edge cache on URL-stable files like /remoteEntry.js and /sw.js,\nand the aborted deploy that strands a container on a dead network.\n\n* docs: stop CLAUDE.md repeating itself, and give it a worktree command that works\n\nTesting and Failure modes said nothing the file had not already said. The gate,\nthe PR rules, the commit style and the test locations were each stated twice;\nof the five failure modes, four restated the feature workflow or the global\nrules and the fifth is now in docs/agent/test-troubleshooting.md.\n\nThe worktree command in step 1 has never worked on this machine: AGENTS.md is a\ncommitted symlink Windows cannot materialize, so the documented command fails\noutright. It now carries both required git flags.\n\n* docs(backlog): record three follow-ups the shipped releases left behind\n\nOfelia''s tier thresholds now sit within 10px of their own frames, so any chrome\nadded to the card silently drops it to compact and takes five e2e tests with it.\nThe z.array record schemas mean one new field hangs every still-open client, and\nboth shipped releases papered over it with a compat shim. And an ofelia comment\nfrom an account outside DUTY_ROTATION loses its author on pre-release clients.\n\nAll three were only ever recorded in agent memory, which is not where a\nfollow-up survives.\n\n* docs(context): commit the session review snapshots\n\nThey have been untracked for three sessions running, so each review has had\nnothing to diff against.\n\n* docs: log the PWA update path in the backlog\n\nregisterType: ''autoUpdate'' does reload the page on its own, but the same\nbranch of vite-plugin-pwa''s register.js never calls onNeedRefresh and\nmakes updateServiceWorker() a no-op — so needRefreshAtom, applyUpdate and\nUpdateBanner are dead code, and an already-open client only re-checks once\nan hour. Records what exists, why it is tolerable, and what would trigger\nthe work.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: drop the typescript 7 migration benchmark report\n\nThe migration is done and the numbers have served their purpose. The\ngenerator, scripts/bench-typecheck-build.ts, stays — the report and its\nbenchmarks.json are reproducible from it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs(context): add the session-4 startup capture\n\nFollows 3d327e45, which committed the earlier snapshots.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: record the passport-checker loading-skeleton handoff\n\nRoot cause, the fix on feat/widget-controls, what is verified and what is\nnot, plus the device console probes and the list of dead ends not worth\nredoing. Delete it once that branch is released.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* docs: spec the fullscreen close control and back-gesture dismissal\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* docs: plan the fullscreen close control and back-gesture dismissal\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(passport-checker): give the tiny tier a way out of a restored success\n\nA card narrower than the widget''s 321px `standard` threshold renders\nTinyTier, and its `success` state drew a status chip and no action at all —\nthe only state in that tier without one. That was survivable while the\nwidget always started in `idle`. Once it began restoring the last\nsuccessful check on mount, a narrow card came up in `success` and stayed\nthere: passport-checker deliberately offers no expand affordance, so the\nonly button left on the card was \"Удалить\". Seen on the branch stand at\n314px, where 4 of 12 columns miss the threshold by 7px.\n\nThe chip is now the action rather than gaining one below it, so the state\ncosts no extra height and still fits the smallest tile. It keeps the\nstatus and timestamp it already carried, and its accessible name repeats\nthat text after the action rather than replacing it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* chore(docs): keep only agent and architecture guides in git\n\ndocs/context/ session logs and docs/superpowers/ plans, specs and\nartifacts churn every session and had nothing durable to diff against;\nuntrack them and ignore docs/* except docs/agent/ and docs/architecture/,\nthe canonical checked-in references.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* chore(docs): ignore docs/* except agent and architecture\n\nFollow-up to the previous commit — the .gitignore rule itself was left\nunstaged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013HLJLXQDcNjKUibianbiv7\n\n* doc: add .playwright-mcp to .gitignore\n\n* feat(widget-runtime): mirror open overlays onto browser history\n\n* chore(docs): drop this branch''s plan and spec from git\n\ndev now keeps only the agent and architecture guides in the repository and\nignores the rest of docs/ (0a17cdaf, 96b2c041). This branch predates that\nrule and still carried its own plan and design spec, which the merge left\ntracked because dev never had those two paths to delete. Untracked here;\nboth files stay on disk, now covered by the ignore rule.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* feat(widget-sdk): add the overlay back-dismiss hook\n\n* test(passport-checker): widen the noVNC graph test''s budget\n\nThe test''s only await is a cold import of the widget''s whole mount graph in\na fresh module registry, which overran the default 5s budget under\n`pnpm check` — five workspace gates running concurrently. It failed there\ntwice and passed every isolated run. Nothing in it waits on behaviour, so\n20s cannot mask a regression: a static noVNC import still fails the\nassertion, not the timer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* test(widget-sdk): add StrictMode and fallback tests for overlay back-dismiss hook\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): coalesce a dropped overlay''s traversal with the push behind it\n\nA history traversal is asynchronous. Measured in real Chromium,\nhistory.go(-1) lands 25-40ms later and a pushState issued in the same task\ndoes not cancel it — jsdom does, which is why the unit tests never caught\nthis. So dropOverlay''s eager go() fired after the following push, rolled the\nbrowser back past the new entry and delivered a spontaneous popstate at the\npre-overlay depth, which reconciled as \"close the overlay that opened 30ms\nago\". Both live call paths hit it: StrictMode''s mount -> cleanup -> mount,\nand any commit that closes one overlay and opens another.\n\ndropOverlay now records the depth history must land on and schedules the\ntraversal on the next task. A pushOverlay arriving first reuses one of the\nowed entries — depth taken from the debt rather than stack.length + 1, so it\ncannot collide with an overlay still waiting above a mid-stack drop — and\nthe flush is cancelled once the debt is spent. A drop with nothing behind it\ntraverses exactly as before.\n\nThe debt is a target depth, not a step count: two drops in one task each\nname the entry they want and the lower wins, where summing their steps\ntraversed past the pre-overlay entry and out of the application. Confirmed\nin Chromium against the module at HEAD, which left the probe page on\nabout:blank after one back press.\n\nThe new tests drive real navigation instead of dispatching a hand-built\nPopStateEvent, since a faked popstate cannot tell one registered entry from\ntwo that happen to carry the same depth.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* chore(widget-sdk): clear the pre-existing lint and type errors in the overlay tests\n\nBoth predate this branch''s overlay work and were failing `pnpm lint` and\n`pnpm typecheck` at HEAD: an unused `e2` binding in overlay-history.test.ts,\nand a `let capturedDismiss` that control-flow analysis narrows to `never`\nbecause it never sees the assignment made inside the component.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(widget-runtime): derive a pushed overlay''s depth from history, not the stack\n\n`stack.length + 1` is only correct while depth counts entries one for one. It\nstops doing so in the duplicate-depth mode a mid-stack drop can leave behind,\nand then the number it hands out skips one: from [C(1), E(2), B(2)] the next\npush is stamped 4 on an entry history places directly after the one stamped 2.\nflushOwedTraversal computes `readDepth() - target`, which counts entries, so a\nlater drop of E issues go(-3) where two entries separate it from its target and\ntakes C down with it.\n\n`pushState` inserts its entry immediately after the current one, so the depth is\n`readDepth() + 1` by construction. Identical whenever history and the stack\nagree, which is every ordinary case.\n\nAlso drops the `replaceState` from the coalescing branch — that branch runs only\nwhen the claimed depth equals the current one, so the spread rewrote identical\nstate. Confirmed in Chromium: the four-scenario probe is byte-identical with and\nwithout it. The claim path now writes nothing to history at all, which is what\nthe comments (here and on the hook) now say.\n\nThe two residuals move from the report into the module doc, where the tasks that\nwill wire real overlays to it can see them: two overlays mounted in the same\ncommit as a middle drop, and dropOverlay racing an eager dismissOverlay in one\ntask.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* feat(client): add the app-env registry, palette derivation and OKLCH conversion\n\n* feat(client): close the topmost dialog on the platform back gesture\n\n* feat(client): generate and commit per-environment app icons\n\n* test(client): pin the Escape close to history.back, not just the outcome\n\nwaitFor(() => expandedInstanceId() === null) alone cannot distinguish\n\"routed through requestDismiss/history\" from \"closed onOpenChange(false)\ndirectly\" -- both reach the same null. Spy on history.back and assert it\nwas called synchronously right after the Escape keydown, so a future\nchange that bypasses requestDismiss fails this test instead of staying\ngreen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* feat(client): brand the document title, tokens and icons per environment at build time\n\n* feat(client): show the environment badge in the board header\n\n* build: deliver the rpi environment name into the client image build\n\n* fix(client): serve the environment icons publicly so the login page is branded\n\n* feat(client): close the topmost popover on the platform back gesture\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* test(client): pin the popover Escape close to history.back, not just the outcome\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(client): pin the environment-branding wiring with tests and share the accent constants\n\nGuard the two things nothing in the gate actually exercised: the Header rendering\nEnvBadge, and both Vite configs registering appEnvBranding. Both were verified to\nfail when the guarded code is deleted.\n\nAlso: export ACCENT_L/ACCENT_C from registry.ts so the fixed accent lightness and\nchroma stop being copied across tokens.ts, generate-env-icons.ts and\nicons-lock.test.ts; derive generate-env-icons.ts''s PRODUCTION_FILL from them\ninstead of a fourth hardcoded #645fd1; give the env badge pill flex: none so it\ncan''t be squeezed by BoardSelect; and tighten a handful of comments/test anchors\n(dev-only activation branding limitation, a VITE_APP_ENV override caveat, a hook\ntier name, a looser Dockerfile layer-order assertion, three deployment.md claims).\n\n* feat(clock): give the fullscreen clock a close control\n\n* feat(passport-checker): give the fullscreen widget a close control\n\n* feat(passport-checker): dismiss the recovery modal on the back gesture\n\n* test(passport-checker): assert the async close mechanism, not just its effect\n\nThe three tests asserting a synchronous close on Escape/backdrop pointerdown\nwent stale the moment the recovery modal''s close paths started routing\nthrough history.back(), which is async in jsdom (and ~25-40ms later in real\nChromium). Update them to pin the mechanism (spy on history.back and assert\nit fired) rather than only awaiting the eventual state flip, and to wait for\nour own modal to actually close before checking the underlying Radix dialog\nsurvived, so the survivor assertion cannot pass on a race that hasn''t landed\nyet.\n\n* test: reset overlay-history between tests in the shared widget and client setups\n\nRecoveryModal.tsx''s useOverlayBackDismiss(true, close) now runs on every\nmount, so any test that mounts a Dialog/Popover root or RecoveryModal\ntouches overlay-history.ts''s file-scoped singletons (the entry stack, the\npopstate listener, a pending owed-traversal timer). recovery-flow.test.tsx\ngot its own beforeEach for this; RecoveryModal.test.tsx and\nrecovery-modal-radix-stack.test.tsx did not, and neither did any client test\nthat mounts a Dialog/Popover (AddWidgetMenu.test.tsx,\nBoardSchemaSelect.test.tsx, etc.) — a gap that let one test''s still-open\noverlay leak state into the next.\n\nReset once per package instead of scattering beforeEach hooks: widget-sdk''s\nshared `./test-setup` (used by every widget package via\ndefineWidgetVitestConfig) and the client package''s own vitest.setup.ts.\nBoth guard the reset to jsdom, since @vitest-environment node files in\nthese packages have no window/history at all.\n\nAdded a proof test per setup file with no reset of its own, so the shared\nhook''s effect is directly observable rather than inferred from a green\nsuite: one test leaks an overlay entry on purpose, the next asserts the\nsingleton started clean.\n\n* test(e2e): cover the fullscreen close control and the back gesture\n\n* fix(client): degrade the env badge to a dot, then hide it, on narrow viewports\n\n.brand has no overflow clipping, so once the header runs out of room it\nslides its content under .actions instead of clipping it. Give the badge\nthe same breakpoint ladder BoardSchemaSelect already uses: at <=640px\n(where the select goes full-width) drop the label text and shrink to a\ndot-only circle; at <=430px (where the select collapses to an icon) hide\nit entirely. The label now lives in its own span so CSS can hide it\nindependently of the dot, while the pill''s own aria-label keeps the\nenvironment announced in the dot-only state.\n\n* fix(client): collapse the theme toggle to one cycling button on narrow viewports\n\nThe three-item ToggleGroup is 102px; below 768px (MOBILE_BREAKPOINT, right\nat the measured collision onset) that alone accounts for a big share of\nthe header overflow. Render both controls and let CSS pick one via a\nmedia query, same approach the rest of the header''s responsiveness uses.\nThe cycle order (light -> dark -> system -> light) moves into the model\nas cycleThemeMode next to themeMode, since the order is domain knowledge,\nnot view glue; the view''s OPTIONS list is now derived from it instead of\nrepeating it. The view-transition DOM interop (--vt-x/--vt-y, startViewTransition)\nis factored into one applyWithViewTransition helper shared by both controls.\n\nThe cycling button''s accessible name (\"Сменить тему\") is deliberately\ngeneric and distinct from the ToggleGroupItem radio names, since jsdom\nrenders both controls at once (media queries don''t evaluate there) and a\ncolliding name would break the existing getByRole(''radio'', ...) tests.\n\nAdds an e2e spec covering the group/button swap at two viewports, the\nonly layer where the media query is real.\n\n* fix(client): let the board select fill the remaining header row, not the whole row\n\n.trigger (or .skeleton, while loading) is BoardSchemaSelect''s own top-level\nelement -- it has no wrapper -- so it''s a direct flex item of .brand\nalongside the logo and EnvBadge. Its <=640px width: 100% therefore resolved\nagainst .brand''s own content box, claiming the entire row for itself as if\nthose siblings weren''t there and pushing their content under .actions.\nflex: 1 1 auto fills only the space actually left after the siblings,\nwhich is what this rule always meant to do.\n\n(.root, the class that width: 100% previously also applied to at this\nbreakpoint, is never applied to any element in BoardSchemaSelect.tsx --\ndead CSS, not the flex item in question. Left untouched, out of scope.)\n\nVerified with a 900->320px real-Chromium sweep: the header now has zero\noverlap at every width down to 320px, closing the residual 545-435px band\nleft after the EnvBadge/ThemeToggle responsive work.\n\n* fix(client): let the board select shrink instead of overflowing the header row\n\nAbove 640px .trigger/.skeleton were width: 220px -- rigid. When the header\nrow ran short (the deployed stands render ''dev'' and ''branch'' in the env\nbadge; ''branch'' is the longest label the dev server never builds locally),\na rigid flex item has nowhere to give, so the surplus went past .actions.\n.triggerText already has overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis, so the\nselect was built to shrink -- nothing let it.\n\nSwitched to width: 220px + flex-shrink: 1 + min-width: 0. A bare\nflex-basis (flex: 0 1 220px) was tried first and doesn''t compose the same\nway: .brand''s own width is auto (content-based), and Chrome''s content-based\nsizing pass for an ancestor like that measures a flex-basis-only child by\nits intrinsic content size, not its flex-basis -- it silently discarded\nthe 220px preference and left the trigger permanently shrunk to ~123px\neven at 1280px with room to spare. An explicit width is what a\ncontent-based ancestor actually measures, so it''s kept alongside\nflex-shrink instead of being replaced by it.\n\nVerified this composes with the two media queries below it, unchanged:\n<=640px (flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0, from d4c7c1af) and <=430px (fixed\n36px icon) -- selectW is exactly 220 at 1280/900 and exactly 36 at every\nwidth <=430, for all three non-production env-badge labels (''local'',\n''dev'', ''branch'').\n\n* docs(widget-runtime): correct overlay-history and MyDevicesDialog comments\n\noverlay-history.ts''s module header claimed every close path routes through\ndismissOverlay and a traversal, but a widget''s fullscreen close control and\nAddWidgetMenu''s close button drop the overlay directly (open set to false,\nunmount) without ever calling dismissOverlay. Rewrite the header to name the\ntwo legitimate shapes -- dismiss and drop -- and clarify that the\n\"only popstate calls close\" invariant is about close specifically, not about\nevery close path routing through history.\n\nDocument two more constraints on callers in dropOverlay''s doc block,\nalongside the two already recorded: a push landing while a flush''s own\nhistory.go is still in flight reads a stale depth (unreachable today, but\nundocumented), and a dismiss cannot be vetoed by its caller once history has\nalready forwarded it to reconcile.\n\nMyDevicesDialog''s belt-and-braces setTimeout comment described a same-tick\nrace with Radix''s deferred pointerDownOutside check. Since the overlay-history\nwork, onOpenChange(false) no longer runs inside the click -- it arrives from\npopstate tens of milliseconds later -- so the described mechanism is stale,\neven though the guard it protects still holds (more strongly, in fact).\nCorrect the explanation without changing the guard.\n\nNo behavior change; comments and doc blocks only.\n\n* docs(agent): preserve the browser history traversal measurement\n\n.superpowers/ is git-ignored and gets deleted once this branch''s work\nfinishes, so the real-Chromium measurement behind overlay-history.ts''s\ndeferred-traversal design (browser-history-probe.md) would otherwise be lost\nentirely, leaving only the numbers already quoted in the module header with\nno reproducible method behind them.\n\nCapture the question, method, measured numbers, and the jsdom/Chromium\ndivergence in docs/agent/browser-history-traversal.md, written as durable\nguidance rather than a session log.\n\nPoint at it from CLAUDE.md. This worktree branched before the CLAUDE.md\nguide-table refactor landed on dev, so there is no existing table to add a\nrow to here; add a new one-row \"Related guides\" table in the same format,\nto be reconciled with dev''s table on the next merge.\n\n* docs: fold the history-traversal guide into the main guide table\n\nThe dev merge brought in the guide table this branch predated, leaving two\ntables side by side. One table, one row.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* fix(client): announce the compact theme button''s state and tighten the header tests\n\nThe compact theme button''s fixed aria-label (\"Сменить тему\") left the\ncurrent mode reaching a screen-reader user only through `title` (not\nreliably announced, least of all by touch screen readers on a <=768px\nviewport) -- a regression against the tri-state group it replaces, which\nannounces the active mode through the radio''s name plus aria-pressed.\nThe accessible name now composes the current mode in: `Тема: ${label}.\nСменить`. It names the state and the action, and can''t drift from\ncycleThemeMode since it describes the current mode, not the next one.\ntitle is kept for the hover affordance. Updated the two vitest queries and\nthe e2e HeaderPage locator that pinned the old fixed name to match on the\nconstant \"Сменить\" suffix instead (regex), since the name now changes\nwith every click.\n\nEnvBadge.test.tsx''s \"label in its own element\" guard couldn''t actually\nfail: getByText matches an element''s direct text-node children and\nreturns the closest such element, so reverting the label back to bare\ntext inside .badge would make getByText(''dev'') resolve to the pill\nitself -- still a <span>, still !== the dot, so both assertions passed on\nold and new markup alike. Rewrote it to compare against the outer pill\n(screen.getByRole(''status'')) instead, the one element getByText can only\nresolve to on the old markup. Verified by temporarily reverting the label\nto bare text: the rewritten test fails on it, passes once restored.\nFolded the now-redundant \"announces via aria-label\" test into the\nexisting \"labels itself for assistive technology\" test, which already\ncovered the same aria-label via a role query.\n\nDeleted BoardSchemaSelect.module.css''s dead .root class entirely (base\nrule and the <=430px block) instead of leaving two more live-looking\nreferences to a class the file''s own comment already called unused.\n\n@media (max-width: 768px) on the compact theme button disagreed by one\nwith the MOBILE_BREAKPOINT constant it''s commented as pairing with\n(grid-metrics.ts treats width < 768 as mobile, i.e. 768 itself is\ndesktop). Changed to max-width: 767px so both agree. Verified first: a\n1280->320px sweep (transitions disabled, 300ms settle) for all three\nnon-production env-badge labels (''local'', ''dev'', ''branch'') shows zero\noverlap at every width including exactly 768px, where the three-item\ngroup is still visible under the new threshold, so 767px doesn''t\nreintroduce the overlap 768px was chosen to avoid.\n\n* test(client): expect one Zakryt control in the fullscreen dialog, not zero\n\nClock now draws its own fullscreen close button (feat(clock): give the\nfullscreen clock a close control), so the \"widget can be expanded without\nduplicate fullscreen or close controls\" e2e test and the FullscreenOverlay\nheader comment -- both inherited unchanged from dev -- were asserting and\ndocumenting the old, no-longer-true premise that Clock draws none. Update\nthe assertion to expect exactly one such button (the widget''s own) and\ncorrect the comment''s example, while keeping the real guarantee both were\nprotecting: no *duplicate* chrome stacked on top of the widget''s.\n\n* chore(docs): drop this branch''s plan and spec from git\n\ndocs/* became local-only except the agent and architecture guides. These two\npredate that change on this branch; the files stay on disk.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q3xvMP5mPQsS9QBuPbfGHm\n\n* build: add a compose overlay for the local nginx e2e stack\n\ndocker-compose.yml defaults the WebAuthn triple to production\n(board.iiskelo.com, https) while playwright.nginx.config.ts drives\nhttp://localhost:8080. A stack started without overrides fails every passkey\njourney inside navigator.credentials.create(), surfacing only as the generic\nclient-side \"сбой процедуры регистрации\" -- register/options returns 200 and\nno verify call ever follows. README told readers to park the overrides in\n.env, which is unavailable here: rpi.toml uses that same file as its\n[secrets].env bundle, so localhost values would ride into production on the\nnext `rpi secrets push`.\n\nThe overlay carries those three plus ALLOW_TEST_DB_RESET, so\n`pnpm start:docker:nginx-e2e` is a single command that cannot be run half-way.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* fix(e2e): pin the gate logout journey to the logout response and navigation\n\n`page.waitForURL(''/'')` synchronized nothing: the board already IS at ''/'', so\nit resolved in the same tick as the logout click -- measured at 3 ms after it\nand ~20 ms before the model''s own navigation landed. Both halves of the\nresulting race were observed: the session probe reading the pre-logout 200,\nand page.reload() overtaking the navigation, which then re-entered the\nstill-controlling service worker and re-rendered the precached board shell\n(title \"myboard\" instead of \"myboard — активация\").\n\naccount.logout runs POST /api/auth/logout -> purge -> hard navigate, so arming\na waitForResponse and a framenavigated waiter before the click pins the whole\nsequence down.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n* fix(server): skip the create-invite entry test when its bundle is missing\n\nThe test guarded only one of its two preconditions. Valkey reachability was\nchecked; the built dist/scripts/create-invite.cjs it executes was not, even\nthough dist/ is gitignored and nothing in `pnpm test` builds it. So the test\nwas green only while nothing listened on 6379, and turned `pnpm check` red\nwith a MODULE_NOT_FOUND the moment any stack was up -- in a file the diff\nnever touched. `pnpm start:docker` publishes 6379 and `pnpm test:e2e:nginx`\nrequires that stack, so running the gate after the e2e suite hit it every time.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014t1jVWgGucabtmDCYdgJWv\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqaJHXCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA0eMQADhnCfLv1KcBx0PV794Fcq+T\nF4EmxIfJdUQsdlH6/K8JUtJSZWgqV8T0C5NvtfKZTIiy/K8r/KAGvGtiWY8Rf0Zg\nbkPGqDv7BuofrylZbSqyYYNigflWIhmA5QJPbGLAkh9AQrqJ5/+4srn2Vv+UQfgE\nxTr4cylsLy2DunRV1tYPyXkfVPnP5ZPpEFsGyi8xbi6JIWRgfgzDwSiCS8mpVHom\n7oI7qt5aYBPIa8m5yVR1Y6+SG4iKfX9jyYcAhsEywnNwiLwCViUzchqcxCnpBtM4\nu2wf0tn8g2v0aUzFS8nZxemCW6pFMXOCqrIpTlTTfcJt840KXtOEyOdZSaZxQ+8E\nKT0INr5A5eIaNpM6lgbUV6NNN691kcO3vExIRT9DRW85WOd8JUhGA3TS6pwgf6F2\n5RISCeB9pcbHl/RA4lUPessJCdPzOdiXPvdOCT6KyitnvchG1O9ECwaizLb4uOVO\nmouhx/PrqA622ctfMWbgHH9/G0t2U6wGPep9oy1ac/1wy1UW3GuV6mv3WBK7ex0z\nB50cWzA40pZBHRIIIoJagW85fu7HMmzGWTJf1D7NHxt8mH+YiS6Jh7oQmZUaqsy7\nKIY5hM/uPSy7/Hk8K9LHmW9OONIVZOGECAwY6b7sYgx3+x9JuwZu5EHMamdrHpdm\np16SP98etto75qoW2jAq\n=/OHB\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-07-28T11:26:16Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_